/* GENERATED by scripts/compose-full-mocks.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
window.ACE_FULL_MOCKS = {"version":"2026-10-01","rules":"five choices; key never the longest; even key letters per section; no item from The Climb or the Prometric mocks; every item blind re-solved","mocks":[{"id":"FULL_1","name":"Full-Length Test 1","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"bio:BIO_g011","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"An autosomal recessive disorder affects 1 in 10,000 individuals in a population that is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. What is the frequency of the recessive allele (q)?","opts":["0.0001","0.001","0.01","0.1","0.99"],"correct":2,"why":"This is a Hardy-Weinberg question wearing a sentence as a disguise. For a recessive condition, the word affected describes homozygous recessive individuals, so the number you are handed is q squared, not q. One in 10,000 is 0.0001, and the square root of that is 0.01. Any time a stem gives you the frequency of affected recessive individuals, take the square root first, then build p and the carrier frequency off that value.","traps":["This is q squared, the frequency of affected individuals, and the square root step still has to happen.","Moving the decimal three places is not the same operation as taking a square root, so this lands between the two real values.",null,"This is the square root of 1/100, so it would fit a disorder affecting 1 in 100 people rather than 1 in 10,000.","This is p, the frequency of the dominant allele, which is what you get after subtracting q from 1."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p044","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"A person at rest who breathes rapidly and deeply for several minutes will most likely develop which acid base disturbance?","opts":["Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis from a loss of hydrogen ions","No pH change","Respiratory alkalosis"],"correct":4,"why":"Two questions sort every acid base problem: which way did pH move, and what moved it. Blowing off carbon dioxide pulls the carbonic acid equilibrium backward, consumes hydrogen ions, and raises blood pH, and because ventilation caused it the label is respiratory. The choice reading respiratory alkalosis is the fit, and holding your breath gives you the mirror image, respiratory acidosis.","traps":["Metabolic acidosis comes from adding acid or losing bicarbonate, as with lactic acid or ketones, and it drives fast breathing rather than being caused by it.","That is what hypoventilation does: trapping carbon dioxide raises the hydrogen ion concentration and drops pH, the opposite of blowing it off.","The pH does rise here, but ventilation is what moved it, which makes it respiratory; metabolic alkalosis comes from losing stomach acid or gaining bicarbonate.","Carbon dioxide is the body's fastest pH lever, so changing how much of it you exhale shifts blood pH within a minute or two.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g064","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A woman who is red-green colorblind has children with a man who has normal color vision. Red-green color blindness is X-linked recessive. What is expected among their children?","opts":["All daughters affected and all sons unaffected","25% affected each","All sons normal","No effect","All sons affected, all daughters carriers"],"correct":4,"why":"An affected mother carries the colorblind allele on both of her X chromosomes, so every single child receives one copy from her. A son pairs that X with a Y and has nothing to mask it, so all sons are colorblind. A daughter pairs it with her father's normal X, so she sees color normally while carrying the allele. This criss-cross from mother to son is the classic X-linked recessive signature worth recognising on sight.","traps":["This reverses the pattern: the father gives each daughter a normal X that masks the allele, and he gives his sons only a Y.","Quarter fractions arise when each parent contributes an allele by chance, but here the mother's contribution is certain.","Sons take their only X chromosome from the mother, and both of hers carry the colorblind allele.","The mother is affected and passes an affected X to every child, so this cross certainly has consequences.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p090","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Sweat glands are distributed across most of the body surface, but they are most densely concentrated in which region?","opts":["Back","Palms, soles, axillae","Spread at the same density over the entire body surface","Eyelids","Lips"],"correct":1,"why":"Where sweat glands sit follows what each patch of skin is for. They are densest on the palms, soles, and axillae, which is why nervous sweat shows up in the hands first, and the palms and soles pair that with thick skin and friction ridges for grip. The choice naming palms, soles, and axillae is the concentrated set.","traps":["The back does carry sweat glands, but at a much lower density than the palms and soles.",null,"Density varies widely by region, from several hundred glands per square centimeter on the palm down to far fewer on the trunk and limbs.","Eyelid skin is the thinnest on the body and carries few sweat glands, with its glands mostly oil producing along the lid margin.","The lip margin has no sweat glands and no hair follicles, which is part of why lips dry out so easily."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev007","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In a developing bird embryo, which extraembryonic structure collects and stores nitrogenous waste?","opts":["Chorion","The albumen surrounding the embryo","Amnion","Allantois","Yolk sac"],"correct":3,"why":"A bird embryo is sealed inside a shell, so its waste has nowhere to go, and this question asks how it copes. Birds and reptiles excrete uric acid, which is nearly insoluble and low in toxicity, and they pack it into the allantois, a sac growing out of the gut that serves as a built in waste bin until hatching. Four membranes, four jobs: the amnion cushions, the chorion exchanges gas, the yolk sac feeds, and the allantois stores waste.","traps":["The chorion lines the inside of the shell and handles gas exchange with the outside air, working alongside the allantois but storing nothing.","Albumen is the egg white, a water and protein reserve supplied by the mother, not an embryonic structure that collects waste.","The amnion is the fluid filled sac that cushions the embryo and keeps it from drying out, so it holds water rather than waste.",null,"The yolk sac supplies food, delivering yolk nutrients to the growing embryo, which is traffic running in the opposite direction."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-p9zot1","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cytotoxic T cell presses Fas ligand against the Fas receptor on the surface of a target cell, and the target cell dies by apoptosis. Which pathway did that use, and which caspase is switched on first?","opts":["The extrinsic pathway, working through a death receptor, activating caspase-8","The extrinsic pathway, working through cytochrome c release, activating caspase-9","Neither pathway, since Fas binding produces necrosis rather than apoptosis","The intrinsic pathway, working through a death receptor, activating caspase-3 first","The intrinsic pathway, working through the mitochondrion, activating caspase-9"],"correct":0,"why":"Sort these by where the signal comes from. A ligand binding a death receptor on the outside of the cell is the extrinsic pathway, and it activates caspase-8 right at the membrane. Trouble arising inside the cell, such as DNA damage that cannot be repaired, is the intrinsic pathway, working through the mitochondrion and caspase-9. Both funnel into the same executioner caspases, so the only thing you have to hold separate is the trigger and the initiator.","traps":[null,"The pathway name is right and cytochrome c belongs to the other route, because the extrinsic pathway starts at the membrane rather than the mitochondrion.","Fas signaling is a controlled program with every hallmark of apoptosis, not the uncontrolled rupture of necrosis.","Death receptors do sit at the start, but that is what makes it extrinsic, and caspase-3 is an executioner activated further down either route.","The intrinsic pathway begins with a problem inside the cell, and this signal arrived from outside through a surface receptor."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d036","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is classified in which fungal phylum?","opts":["Zygomycota","Basidiomycota","Glomeromycota","Chytridiomycota","Ascomycota"],"correct":4,"why":"Yeasts are ascomycetes, the sac fungi, named for the ascus, the small sac in which their sexual spores form. The familiar members are the practical hooks: baker's and brewer's yeast, Penicillium, morels and truffles. Yeast is unusual for a fungus in being single celled and reproducing mostly by budding, so do not let the missing mushroom shape push you toward another group. Pair ascus with sac and basidium with club, and the two large phyla stay separate.","traps":["Zygomycetes are the bread molds such as Rhizopus, which form a thick-walled zygospore when two mating types fuse.","Basidiomycetes are the club fungi, the mushrooms, puffballs, shelf fungi and rusts, which make spores on basidia.","Glomeromycetes are the arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi that grow inside plant roots and do not live as free single cells.","Chytrids are mostly aquatic fungi with flagellated spores, the only fungi that swim.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-dh8m7c","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"Adenylyl cyclase makes the second messenger cAMP. What is its starting material?","opts":["PIP2","GTP","ATP","Phosphocreatine","Cholesterol"],"correct":2,"why":"cAMP is adenosine monophosphate closed into a ring, and it is cut straight out of ATP by adenylyl cyclase. Keep the pairs together: adenylyl cyclase takes ATP to cAMP, guanylyl cyclase takes GTP to cGMP, phospholipase C takes PIP2 to IP3 plus DAG. Match the first letter of the enzyme to the first letter of its substrate.","traps":["PIP2 is the membrane lipid that phospholipase C cleaves into IP3 and DAG.","GTP is the substrate for guanylyl cyclase, which makes cGMP.",null,"Phosphocreatine is a short-term phosphate reserve in muscle, not a signaling substrate.","Cholesterol is the precursor of steroid hormones, not of a cyclic nucleotide."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-2c5o89","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Insulin binds the outer portion of its receptor. What does the receptor do next?","opts":["Adds phosphate groups to tyrosines on its own cytoplasmic tails","Cleaves PIP2 into IP3 and DAG","Carries insulin with it into the nucleus","Opens as an ion channel and lets sodium in","Exchanges GDP for GTP on an attached G protein at the inner surface"],"correct":0,"why":"The insulin receptor is the classic receptor tyrosine kinase, and the word kinase is the whole mechanism. Once the hormone binds, the two halves of the receptor activate one another and hang phosphate groups on tyrosine side chains of their own cytoplasmic tails. Those phosphorylated tyrosines then act as docking sites for relay proteins that carry the message inward. Hormone binds, receptor phosphorylates itself, relay proteins dock.","traps":[null,"Cleaving PIP2 is the job of phospholipase C, further along a different pathway.","Insulin is a peptide and never enters the cell, that route belongs to steroids.","Ion channel receptors respond to neurotransmitters, not to insulin.","Loading GTP onto a G protein is what a G-protein-coupled receptor does."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-nd28q3","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A compound makes the thylakoid membrane freely permeable to protons. What is the immediate effect on an illuminated chloroplast?","opts":["Both electron transport and ATP synthesis speed up together","Electron transport continues while ATP synthesis collapses","Water splitting stops while NADPH production continues","Electron transport halts while ATP synthesis continues","The Calvin cycle speeds up because more protons reach the stroma"],"correct":1,"why":"Separate the two halves of chemiosmosis: the chain that builds the gradient and the turbine that spends it. Poking holes in the membrane lets protons slip back without turning ATP synthase, so the pumping continues and the payoff disappears. The same logic applies to any uncoupler in any membrane, including the mitochondrion.","traps":["Electron transport may run slightly faster with no gradient pushing back, but ATP synthesis cannot rise without one.",null,"Water splitting keeps feeding electrons into photosystem II, and NADPH production continues right along with it.","The carriers keep working as long as light is hitting them, because the leak destroys the gradient rather than the chain.","Protons that leak past ATP synthase do no work, and the Calvin cycle still needs the ATP it is no longer getting."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d039","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Chytrids (phylum Chytridiomycota) are set apart from all other fungi by which feature?","opts":["Flagellated spores","Photosynthesis","Cell walls built from cellulose instead of chitin","Multicellularity","Asexual only"],"correct":0,"why":"This question is pointing at the one fungal lineage that kept a swimming stage. Chytrids release zoospores that each carry a single posterior flagellum, something no other fungal group does, and it suits their life in water and saturated soil. Anchor it to the sound of the name: chytrids swim.","traps":[null,"No fungus photosynthesizes; all of them absorb nutrients from outside the body, which is why the swimming spore is the real distinction.","Fungal walls, chytrids included, are built of chitin, while cellulose walls belong to plants and to the fungus-like water molds.","Many fungi are multicellular, including molds and every mushroom former, so that cannot be what makes chytrids distinct.","Chytrids reproduce both sexually and asexually, and asexual reproduction is common across the entire kingdom anyway."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-x1d1j6","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Two cardiac muscle cells are joined so that ions and small molecules pass directly from the cytoplasm of one into the cytoplasm of the other. The structure responsible is:","opts":["A hemidesmosome","A gap junction","A tight junction","A plasmodesma","A desmosome"],"correct":1,"why":"The question turns on the word directly: no ligand, no receptor, just a pore joining two cytoplasms. Gap junctions are those pores in animal tissue, and they are why cardiac muscle contracts as one unit. Tight junctions seal the space between cells and desmosomes rivet cells together, so neither one passes a signal.","traps":["Hemidesmosomes anchor a cell to the extracellular matrix below it.",null,"Tight junctions seal the space between cells to stop leakage, they do not pass molecules between cytoplasms.","Plasmodesmata do exactly this job, but they are found in plant cells, and cardiac muscle is animal tissue.","Desmosomes are mechanical rivets that hold cells together against stress."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-194ncsd","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"An intestinal cell is treated with a drug that completely blocks the sodium potassium ATPase. Within minutes, glucose uptake across the apical membrane by the sodium glucose cotransporter falls off sharply. What is the best explanation?","opts":["The sodium gradient that powers the cotransporter runs down","The cotransporter needs ATP, and the drug has cut off its supply","The apical membrane depolarizes, which closes the carrier","The drug binds the glucose carrier directly and jams its binding site","Glucose is no longer phosphorylated once it arrives inside the cell"],"correct":0,"why":"The real question is what secondary active transport runs on. The cotransporter never touches ATP: it lets sodium fall downhill and drags glucose uphill with it, and that sodium gradient is built by the pump. Block the pump, sodium piles up inside, the gradient flattens, and the free ride ends. Any time a transporter is labeled secondary active, look upstream for the pump that stocked its gradient.","traps":[null,"Secondary active transporters do not hydrolyze ATP themselves, that is the defining difference from primary ones.","Voltage shifts here are small next to the loss of the sodium gradient itself.","The stem says the drug acts on the pump, and the delay of a few minutes points to a gradient draining rather than direct inhibition.","Trapping glucose by phosphorylation keeps the glucose gradient favorable, but it is not the energy source for this carrier."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-3vie62","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Under the microscope, a dividing cell shows a single tight line of chromosomes across the middle of the cell, each one gripped from both sides. Which stage is this, and what holds the chromosomes in that line?","opts":["Telophase, held by the two nuclear envelopes that are reassembling nearby","Interphase, held by the cell plate that is forming across the equator","Prophase, held together by a nuclear envelope that has not yet broken down","Metaphase, held by balanced pull from microtubules reaching to each pole","Anaphase, held in place by the contractile ring forming around the equator"],"correct":3,"why":"Metaphase is defined by the balance, not only by the position. Each chromosome is attached on one face to microtubules from one pole and on the other face to microtubules from the other pole, and the plate is exactly where those two pulls come out even. That balance is also what the spindle checkpoint is measuring. When you see one tidy line with attachments on both sides, that is metaphase every time.","traps":["In telophase the chromosomes are clustered at the two poles and unwinding, not lined up across the middle.","Interphase chromosomes are decondensed and invisible as separate bodies, and a cell plate belongs to plant cytokinesis after mitosis is over.","In prophase the chromosomes are condensed but scattered inside an intact nucleus, with nothing yet gripping them.",null,"In anaphase the chromatids have already left the middle and are on their way to the poles, so you would see two groups rather than one line."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"originals:ab-d8063fa866","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"An organism has a diploid number of 8. How many chromatids are present in one cell at metaphase I and in one cell at metaphase II?","opts":["16 at metaphase I and 8 at metaphase II","16 at metaphase I and 16 at metaphase II","32 at metaphase I and 16 at metaphase II","8 at metaphase I and 4 at metaphase II","16 at metaphase I and 4 at metaphase II"],"correct":0,"why":"Start with 8 chromosomes. After replication each has two chromatids, so metaphase I has 8 chromosomes and 16 chromatids. Meiosis I halves the chromosome number, so a metaphase II cell has 4 chromosomes, each still with two chromatids, for 8 chromatids.","traps":[null,"Meiosis I already split the cell, so a metaphase II cell holds half as much.","DNA is replicated once, so 8 chromosomes become 16 chromatids, not 32.","You counted chromosomes. Each chromosome is replicated here, so double it to count chromatids.","At metaphase II the sister chromatids are still joined, so 4 chromosomes means 8 chromatids."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1v7ag0l","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Apoptosis runs on a family of enzymes that sit in the cell at all times as inactive precursors and are switched on in a cascade once the decision to die is made. What type of enzyme are they, and how does the cascade work?","opts":["Proteases, switched on by cleavage, initiator caspases activating executioner ones","Nucleases, activated by a calcium surge, which cut the plasma membrane into blebs","Phosphatases, activated by cytochrome c, which strip phosphate off the mitotic spindle","Kinases, activated by cyclin binding, which phosphorylate the nuclear lamins","Ligases, activated by ATP binding, which crosslink the cytoskeleton into a rigid mesh"],"correct":0,"why":"Caspases are proteases, and the name is worth unpacking: they carry a cysteine in the active site and cut their targets after aspartate residues. They wait in the cytosol as inactive procaspases, which lets a cell commit to dying in seconds without having to make anything new. Initiator caspases are activated first and then cleave executioner caspases, which go on to cut hundreds of cell proteins. Cleaving is both how they get switched on and how they do their work.","traps":[null,"Nucleases do act later in apoptosis to chop up the DNA, but they are downstream, and a nuclease cannot cut a membrane.","Cytochrome c does belong to apoptosis, but what it triggers is a protease cascade, and stripping phosphates off the spindle is not part of cell death.","Lamin phosphorylation is how a cell takes its nuclear envelope apart for mitosis, which is a different process run by a different enzyme.","Ligases join molecules together, and apoptosis works by taking cell structures apart rather than stiffening them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-xm2ae3","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"RuBisCO attaches carbon dioxide to the five carbon sugar RuBP. What comes out of that first step?","opts":["One two carbon fragment and one three carbon acid","Two molecules of G3P, ready to leave the cycle","Two molecules of the three carbon acid 3-PGA","One stable six carbon sugar that leaves the cycle","One four carbon oxaloacetate"],"correct":2,"why":"Count carbons and the answer falls out on its own. Five carbons of RuBP plus one carbon from carbon dioxide makes six, and that six carbon intermediate is so unstable it immediately splits into two three carbon acids. Say the sentence every time: five plus one is six, and six breaks into two threes.","traps":["That pairing is what comes out when RuBisCO grabs oxygen instead of carbon dioxide, which is photorespiration.","G3P arrives two steps later, after ATP and NADPH have done their work on 3-PGA.",null,"A six carbon intermediate does form, but it lasts only an instant before splitting in half.","Oxaloacetate is what PEP carboxylase makes in a C4 plant's mesophyll cell, a different enzyme in a different place."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-a4a4xb","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A brightly lit leaf that is actively fixing carbon is suddenly moved into the dark, with carbon dioxide still available. In the first seconds, what happens to its RuBP and 3-PGA levels?","opts":["RuBP rises and 3-PGA falls","Both rise together","Both fall immediately","Neither changes until the leaf runs out of carbon dioxide","RuBP falls and 3-PGA rises"],"correct":4,"why":"Ask which step stalls first. Darkness cuts off ATP and NADPH, so the reduction step stops, while RuBisCO keeps attaching carbon dioxide to whatever RuBP is left. The molecule just before a blocked step piles up and the one just after it drains, and that rule reads any blocked pathway on any section of the exam.","traps":["That is the pattern for cutting off carbon dioxide instead of light, and it is the reversal students land on most often.","3-PGA does rise, but RuBP cannot rise while it is still being consumed and no longer being rebuilt.","Fixation keeps running for a few seconds in the dark, so 3-PGA is produced rather than lost.","The Calvin cycle depends on ATP and NADPH from the light reactions, so darkness shows up within seconds.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"originals:ab-5547ba43c3","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A person drinks alcohol, which inhibits the release of antidiuretic hormone. What is the expected result?","opts":["Less glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule","A higher filtration rate at the glomerulus with no change in urine concentration","More aquaporins in the collecting duct, so less urine is produced","Fewer aquaporins in the collecting duct, so more dilute urine is produced","More Na+ reabsorption in the distal tubule, so blood pressure rises"],"correct":3,"why":"ADH is the water saver. It puts aquaporin water channels into the collecting duct so water can leave the filtrate and return to the blood. Take ADH away and the water stays in the tubule, so urine is plentiful and dilute.","traps":["ADH does not control glucose handling.","The main ADH effect is on water permeability of the collecting duct, which changes urine concentration.","That is what ADH does when it IS present. Blocking ADH gives the opposite.",null,"That is the aldosterone effect, not the ADH effect."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e009","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In the nitrogen cycle, the process of nitrogen fixation converts:","opts":["NH₃ to NO₃⁻","NO₃⁻ to N₂","N₂ to NH₃","NH₄⁺ to amino acids in plant tissue","Organic to inorganic"],"correct":2,"why":"Nitrogen gas makes up most of the air, but the triple bond in N₂ puts it out of reach for nearly every organism, so the cycle has to start by breaking that bond. Fixation is that step: bacteria convert N₂ into ammonia, a form plants can actually use. Keep the order straight by following the atom: fixation brings nitrogen in from the air, nitrification oxidizes ammonia to nitrite and then nitrate, assimilation builds it into tissue, ammonification releases it from dead material, and denitrification returns it to the atmosphere as N₂.","traps":["This is nitrification, carried out by soil bacteria after fixation has already delivered the nitrogen.","This is denitrification, which sends nitrogen back to the atmosphere instead of bringing it in.",null,"This is assimilation, the step where a plant builds already fixed nitrogen into its own molecules.","This describes decomposition and ammonification, which recycle nitrogen that is already inside living things."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p019","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which cells of the stomach lining secrete hydrochloric acid?","opts":["Chief cells","Goblet cells","Parietal cells","Mucous neck cells","G cells"],"correct":2,"why":"Sort the stomach lining by what each cell hands over. Parietal cells pump out hydrochloric acid and also make intrinsic factor for vitamin B12 absorption, while chief cells release pepsinogen, the inactive form of the protein digesting enzyme. The acid from the parietal cells is what converts pepsinogen into active pepsin, so pair them as parietal for acid and chief for enzyme.","traps":["Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the enzyme precursor that the acid then activates.","Goblet cells secrete protective mucus and are found mainly in the intestine and the airways.",null,"Mucous neck cells produce the mucus layer that shields the stomach wall from its own acid.","G cells release the hormone gastrin, which tells the parietal cells to make acid rather than making it themselves."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_xp008","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"In a community with three trophic levels (producers, primary consumers, secondary consumers), the introduction of a top predator (a tertiary consumer) typically results in:","opts":["Trophic cascade; secondary-consumer numbers decline, primary consumers rebound, producer biomass decreases","Trophic cascade; secondary-consumer numbers decline, primary consumers also decline, producer biomass INCREASES","No effect; the new predator only eats producers","Loss of primary consumers across the food web","Linear decline in all populations"],"correct":0,"why":"Adding a tertiary predator triggers a top-down trophic cascade: the new predator suppresses secondary consumers, which releases primary consumers from predation pressure (primary consumers rebound), and the increased primary-consumer grazing then reduces producer biomass. Option A describes this classic four-level cascade correctly.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p057","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Approximately what percentage of the sodium filtered at the glomerulus is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule?","opts":["~25%","~45%","~5%","~65%","~95%"],"correct":3,"why":"The nephron reabsorbs sodium in a predictable order, and the proximal convoluted tubule is the workhorse. It reclaims about two thirds of the filtered sodium, with water and much else following along, then the thick ascending limb takes roughly a quarter and the distal tubule and collecting duct fine tune the last few percent. The choice sitting near two thirds matches that bulk reabsorption role.","traps":["Roughly a quarter is the thick ascending limb's share through its sodium potassium two chloride transporter, which makes it the second largest site.","Just under half undersells the proximal tubule, whose brush border and dense mitochondria let it reclaim about two thirds before the filtrate reaches the loop.","About 5 percent is the distal convoluted tubule's share, the fine tuning end of the nephron rather than the bulk.",null,"Nearly all filtered sodium is eventually reabsorbed across the whole nephron, but no single segment accounts for that much on its own."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev009","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The lens of the eye develops from which embryonic tissue?","opts":["Neural crest","Mesoderm and neural crest together","Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm"],"correct":2,"why":"This is the payoff of the classic induction experiment. The optic vesicle grows out from the forebrain and presses against the surface ectoderm of the head, and that contact instructs the surface ectoderm to thicken into a lens placode, fold inward, and pinch off as the lens. Hold the geometry in mind: the retina comes from the brain side, and the lens comes from the outer covering that the brain reached out and touched.","traps":["Neural crest does build parts of the eye, including much of the cornea and the stroma of the iris, which makes this tempting, but the lens comes from the surface layer instead.","Both of those tissues contribute to the eye, supplying muscles and corneal layers among other parts, but neither one gives rise to the lens.",null,"Mesoderm in the head forms the muscles that move the eye and the vessels that supply it, but not the clear lens itself.","Endoderm never reaches the surface of the head, since it is committed to the lining of the gut tube and the organs that bud from it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g077","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Early spontaneous abortion is most often associated with which kind of genetic abnormality?","opts":["Translocations","Point mutations","Inversions","Triploidy, from two sperm fertilizing one egg","Trisomies"],"correct":4,"why":"Most early pregnancy losses with an identified genetic cause are chromosomal, and among those the autosomal trisomies are the largest group. Think about why: an extra whole chromosome unbalances the dosage of hundreds of genes at once, which is far more disruptive than a single base change. When a stem asks what is most often behind early loss, reach for whole chromosome errors first.","traps":["Translocations do cause loss, particularly when a balanced carrier makes unbalanced gametes, but they account for far fewer cases than trisomies.","A single base change usually affects one protein, which is rarely enough on its own to end a pregnancy.","An inversion keeps all of the genetic material present, so carriers are often completely unaffected.","Triploidy does cause pregnancy loss, but it accounts for far fewer cases than the autosomal trisomies as a group.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1hzdpw4","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"After the Calvin cycle spends what the light reactions deliver, it sends two molecules back to the thylakoid. What goes back?","opts":["ADP and NADP+","Carbon dioxide and G3P","Oxygen and water","ATP and NADPH","Glucose and phosphate"],"correct":0,"why":"Think of it as a shuttle with a return trip. The thylakoid sends loaded carriers, ATP and NADPH, out into the stroma, and the stroma sends back the empties, ADP with phosphate and NADP+, to be recharged. Stall either direction and the other one stops within seconds.","traps":[null,"Carbon dioxide arrives from the air and G3P leaves to become sugar, so neither heads back to the thylakoid.","Oxygen leaves the leaf and water is a reactant for the light reactions, but neither is returned by the Calvin cycle.","Those are the two molecules the Calvin cycle receives and spends, not the ones it hands back.","Glucose is the end product of the whole process and never cycles back to the light reactions."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-i1qexy","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A researcher raises the outside glucose concentration step by step and measures how fast glucose enters a muscle cell. Uptake rises at first, then levels off at a maximum rate. What does that plateau tell you?","opts":["Glucose is entering by simple diffusion straight through the lipid bilayer","The cell has begun pumping glucose back out at an equal rate","Glucose crossing has switched over to active transport","The membrane became impermeable to glucose at high concentration","The carrier proteins are saturated, so transport has a ceiling"],"correct":4,"why":"A plateau is the fingerprint of a finite number of protein binding sites. Simple diffusion climbs in a straight line with the gradient because there is nothing to fill up, while a carrier has a limited number of seats, and once every seat is occupied, adding more glucose cannot speed anything up. Curve flattens means protein involved, whether the transport is facilitated or active.","traps":["Simple diffusion gives a straight line with no ceiling, since there is no protein to saturate.","Nothing in the data shows efflux, and growing efflux would bend the curve downward rather than flatten it.","Glucose entry down its gradient needs no energy input, and changing modes would not by itself create a ceiling.","Membranes do not switch off their permeability when solute concentration rises.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-jwpv4p","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Bread dough rises because the yeast in it is fermenting. Which products of that pathway explain both the rise and the alcohol that bakes off?","opts":["Lactate and water","Ethanol and carbon dioxide","Acetyl-CoA and carbon dioxide","Pyruvate and hydrogen gas","Ethanol and oxygen"],"correct":1,"why":"Alcoholic fermentation takes two steps. Pyruvate loses a carbon as carbon dioxide to become acetaldehyde, and then acetaldehyde accepts the electrons from NADH to become ethanol, which regenerates NAD+. The gas is what lifts the dough, and it is also the clean point of contrast with lactate fermentation, which releases no gas at all.","traps":["Lactate fermentation releases no gas, so nothing would inflate the dough.",null,"Acetyl-CoA is an aerobic intermediate that stays inside mitochondria and never piles up as a product.","Pyruvate is the starting material for fermentation rather than its end product, and yeast release no hydrogen gas.","Fermentation consumes no oxygen and produces none, since oxygen is exactly what the cell has run out of."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g015","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A single base pair in the coding region of a gene is changed, yet the resulting polypeptide has exactly the same amino acid sequence as before. This type of mutation is called:","opts":["Missense","Silent","Frameshift","Nonsense","Transversion"],"correct":1,"why":"This tests whether you can carry a DNA change through the genetic code and out to the protein. The code is redundant, so several different codons spell the same amino acid, and a base swap that lands on one of those synonyms leaves the protein untouched. Sort the names by what the protein ends up doing: same protein is silent, one amino acid swapped is missense, an early stop is nonsense, and a shifted reading frame is a frameshift.","traps":["A missense mutation swaps one amino acid for another, so the protein sequence does change.",null,"A frameshift comes from inserting or deleting bases in a number not divisible by three, which rewrites every codon downstream.","A nonsense mutation turns a codon into a stop signal and cuts the protein short, so the product is definitely altered.","Transversion describes the chemistry of the swap, a purine traded for a pyrimidine, and says nothing about whether the amino acid stayed the same."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e005","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In human populations, birth weight clusters tightly around about 7 pounds and the average has changed very little over time. Which type of natural selection does this pattern illustrate?","opts":["Directional","Drift","Sexual selection acting on a trait that improves mating success","Stabilizing","Disruptive"],"correct":3,"why":"Match the shape of the distribution to the mode of selection. Selection that favors the middle and trims both tails leaves a single narrow peak that stays put, which is exactly what birth weight looks like: very small infants and very large ones have both historically faced more difficulty than average sized ones. Directional selection would slide that peak to one side, and disruptive selection would split it into two.","traps":["Directional selection would move average birth weight steadily heavier or lighter over generations, and the average has held its position.","Drift is random and would not hold a trait so precisely at one value across many separate populations.","Sexual selection shapes traits that affect success in finding a mate, and birth weight is fixed long before an individual reaches reproductive age.",null,"Disruptive selection would favor very small and very large infants over average ones and produce two peaks, the opposite of one tight cluster."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-dmpc6","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"The first half of glycolysis costs the cell two ATP. Which pair of enzymes spends them?","opts":["Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase and phosphoglycerate kinase","Hexokinase and pyruvate kinase","Aldolase and triose phosphate isomerase","Phosphoglucose isomerase and enolase","Hexokinase and phosphofructokinase"],"correct":4,"why":"Two phosphorylations open glycolysis and both are paid for with ATP: glucose to glucose-6-phosphate, then fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate. Notice that both reactions add a phosphate onto the sugar, and that is the tell. Enzymes that spend ATP are the ones putting phosphates on, enzymes that make ATP are the ones taking phosphates off.","traps":["These are the two payoff-phase steps: one makes NADH and the other makes ATP.","Hexokinase does spend ATP, but pyruvate kinase is a payoff-phase enzyme that makes ATP instead.","Aldolase cuts the six-carbon sugar in two and the isomerase reshuffles one half, and neither reaction touches ATP.","These two rearrange the molecule, and the enolase step removes water rather than spending a phosphate.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-11djtx4","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A hormone activates phospholipase C in a smooth muscle cell. Which pair of second messengers is produced, and what does the water-soluble one do?","opts":["cAMP and cGMP, and cAMP activates protein kinase A in the cytoplasm of the cell","IP3 and DAG, and IP3 stays in the membrane to activate protein kinase C","DAG and calcium, and DAG travels to the nucleus to bind DNA","IP3 and DAG, and IP3 releases calcium from the smooth endoplasmic reticulum","cAMP and calcium, and cAMP opens calcium channels in the nucleus"],"correct":3,"why":"Phospholipase C splits the membrane lipid PIP2 into two pieces with different fates. IP3 is water soluble, so it leaves the membrane, travels to the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, and opens calcium channels there, while DAG is lipid soluble and stays in the membrane where it helps switch on protein kinase C. Sort the two by solubility and their destinations follow.","traps":["Those are the products of the two cyclases, not of phospholipase C.","The roles are swapped, DAG is the piece that remains in the membrane for protein kinase C.","DAG is not a transcription factor, and it does not leave the membrane.",null,"Phospholipase C does not make cAMP, and the calcium store being tapped is the smooth endoplasmic reticulum."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"originals:ab-8da5ad8639","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels. What is the direct effect on a neuron exposed to it?","opts":["Neurotransmitter release increases at the terminal","The resting potential immediately rises to 0 mV","The neuron fires action potentials continuously","Repolarization fails, so the membrane stays positive","The neuron can no longer fire action potentials"],"correct":4,"why":"No Na+ entry means no upstroke. The cell still has a normal resting potential, but it can never reach the rising phase, so no signal travels. That is why this toxin causes numbness and paralysis.","traps":["With no action potential reaching the terminal, release drops.","Resting potential depends mainly on the K+ leak and the pump, which the toxin leaves alone.","Continuous firing would need Na+ channels that stay open. The toxin BLOCKS them.","Repolarization is the K+ channel job, and it never gets the chance because the spike never starts.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-4-158brxy","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Two turns of the Krebs cycle, the amount one glucose supports, produce 6 NADH and 2 FADH2. Using the modern estimates of about 2.5 ATP per NADH and about 1.5 ATP per FADH2, how much ATP does oxidative phosphorylation get from those carriers alone?","opts":["12","15","18","20","24"],"correct":2,"why":"Run the two carriers on separate lines, then add. Six NADH at 2.5 each is 15, two FADH2 at 1.5 each is 3, and 15 plus 3 is 18. Keeping NADH and FADH2 on separate lines is what protects you from the most common slip here, which is quietly giving them the same value.","traps":["This gives every carrier the FADH2 value of 1.5, so all eight are counted at the lower rate.","This counts the 6 NADH correctly but leaves the 2 FADH2 out of the total entirely.",null,"This uses the older textbook values of 3 ATP per NADH and 1 ATP per FADH2.","This applies 3 ATP per NADH and then credits FADH2 with that same higher value."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p056","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Filtration of plasma from the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule is driven mainly by which of the following?","opts":["Colloid osmotic pressure of the plasma proteins","Active transport","Aquaporin gradient","Hydrostatic pressure","Diffusion only"],"correct":3,"why":"Filtration here is a plumbing question rather than a transport question. Blood enters the glomerulus through a wide afferent arteriole and leaves through a narrower efferent one, which holds capillary hydrostatic pressure high and pushes water and small solutes through the filtration barrier in bulk. Net filtration is that pushing pressure minus the opposing plasma colloid osmotic pressure and capsular pressure, so the choice naming hydrostatic pressure is the driving force.","traps":["Plasma proteins stay behind in the capillary and their osmotic pull works against filtration, holding fluid in rather than pushing it out.","Filtration at the glomerulus costs the kidney no ATP directly; active transport shows up later, during reabsorption along the tubule.","Aquaporins move water in the collecting duct under antidiuretic hormone control, well downstream of the filtration step.",null,"Diffusion moves individual solutes down their own gradients, while filtration pushes water and small solutes together through the filtration slits."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-0de0fec7bd","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A neuron receives a much stronger stimulus than before. How does it signal the greater intensity?","opts":["The resting potential becomes more negative","It fires action potentials more frequently","Each action potential becomes taller","The action potential travels faster down the same axon","Each action potential lasts longer"],"correct":1,"why":"A light switch is either on or off, so the neuron cannot shout louder with a bigger spike. Instead it shouts more often. Stimulus strength is coded as firing frequency.","traps":["Resting potential does not encode intensity. A more negative rest would make the cell HARDER to fire.",null,"Action potentials are all or none. Once threshold is crossed the spike is always the same height.","Speed depends on axon diameter and myelin, not on how hard you stimulate.","Duration is fixed by channel kinetics. It does not stretch with stimulus strength."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g061","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a three-point testcross, two of the three genes lie 25 centimorgans apart on the chromosome. What recombination frequency is expected between that pair of genes?","opts":["5%","12.5%","25%","50%","75%"],"correct":2,"why":"A centimorgan is defined as one percent recombination, so the conversion is direct and 25 centimorgans corresponds to about 25 percent recombinant offspring. The word approximate matters at larger distances, because a double crossover between two markers restores the parental arrangement and makes the observed frequency read slightly low. That is exactly why a three-point cross, with a marker sitting in the middle, gives better distances than a two-point cross.","traps":["Five percent would correspond to genes only about 5 centimorgans apart, a far tighter linkage than stated.","This halves the map distance, but centimorgans are already expressed as percent recombinants.",null,"Fifty percent is the value for genes that assort independently, whether unlinked or very far apart on the same chromosome.","Recombination frequency cannot exceed 50 percent, so any value above that is impossible."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1hr4sgo","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Oligomycin plugs the proton channel of ATP synthase while leaving the electron transport complexes themselves untouched. What happens to the rate of electron flow through the chain?","opts":["It increases, because protons are no longer being used up","It slows, because the gradient builds and pumping against it gets harder","It is unchanged, because the chain and ATP synthase work independently of each other","It slows, because oxygen can no longer accept electrons","It increases briefly and then stops as the matrix becomes acidic"],"correct":1,"why":"This is respiratory control, and the gradient is the link between the two halves. With the synthase plugged, protons have no route back in, the gradient grows steeper, and the pumps eventually cannot push against it, so electron flow and oxygen consumption both fall. Contrast that with an uncoupler, which drains the gradient and makes the chain race.","traps":["Protons piling up in the intermembrane space makes the next pump stroke harder, not easier.",null,"The two are coupled through the gradient, which is precisely what the phrase oxidative phosphorylation is telling you.","Oxygen is still present and complex IV is not the target, since the block sits downstream at the synthase.","Protons are pumped out of the matrix, so the matrix would become less acidic rather than more."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-ih7vz8","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Some bacteria living without oxygen still use an electron transport chain, passing electrons to sulfate or nitrate instead of to oxygen. How does that differ from fermentation?","opts":["Fermentation uses a chain too, but with oxygen as the final acceptor","Fermentation uses no electron transport chain and builds no proton gradient","It produces less ATP per glucose than fermentation does","Fermentation passes electrons to nitrate, while anaerobic respiration passes them to pyruvate","It is the same process under a different name"],"correct":1,"why":"Both happen without oxygen, which is why students blur them together, but only one of them uses a membrane. Anaerobic respiration still runs electrons through a chain and still builds a proton gradient, just with a different molecule waiting at the end, so it out-earns fermentation. Fermentation has no chain and no gradient: it hands NADH's electrons to an organic molecule so that glycolysis can carry on.","traps":["If oxygen were the final acceptor it would be aerobic respiration, and fermentation has no chain at all.",null,"Running a chain and a gradient yields more ATP per glucose than fermentation, not less.","This has the two acceptors swapped, since fermentation uses pyruvate or a molecule made from it, and nitrate or sulfate belongs to anaerobic respiration.","The two are distinct: one uses a membrane chain and a gradient while the other does not."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-104j1lu","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"In an illuminated chloroplast, which way do protons move through ATP synthase, and where does the ATP appear?","opts":["From the stroma into the lumen, with the ATP appearing in the lumen","From the cytosol into the stroma, with the ATP appearing in the stroma","From the lumen into the stroma, with the ATP appearing in the lumen","From the lumen into the stroma, with the ATP appearing in the stroma","From the stroma into the lumen, with the ATP appearing in the stroma"],"correct":3,"why":"Pumps push uphill and turbines run downhill. The electron carriers load protons into the lumen, so the lumen becomes the crowded acidic side, and ATP synthase lets that crowd stream back out into the stroma. The ATP shows up exactly where the Calvin cycle needs it, which makes a handy sanity check.","traps":["That is the direction the electron carriers pump protons, which is the uphill direction rather than the direction through the enzyme.","The cytosol sits outside the chloroplast entirely and takes no part in this gradient.","The direction is right, but the catalytic head of the enzyme faces the stroma, so the ATP is released there.",null,"Protons move down their gradient through the enzyme, which means out of the lumen, not into it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lw-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"Chlorine trifluoride (ClF₃) is a powerful fluorinating agent with a T-shaped molecular geometry and F-Cl-F angles near 87°. The central Cl has 7 valence electrons, forms 3 Cl-F σ bonds, and has remaining electrons distributed as lone pairs in equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid. How many lone pairs are on Cl?","opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":2,"why":"Cl contributes 7 e⁻; 3 are used in the three Cl-F σ bonds, leaving 4 e⁻ = 2 lone pairs. Steric number = 3 + 2 = 5 (sp³d, trigonal bipyramidal electron geometry). The two LPs go equatorial, producing the T-shaped molecular geometry.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#1xyppx6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.0693 min⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["28.9 min","0.1 min","10 min","14.4 min","0.048 min"],"correct":2,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.0693 = 10 min. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":["You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693.","Upside down. t½ = 0.693 / k, not k / 0.693.",null,"That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k.","You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#xz6wsz","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 16 g of CH₄? (molar mass 16 g/mol)","opts":["4","9.63 × 10²⁴","1.51 × 10²³","6.02 × 10²³","2.41 × 10²⁴"],"correct":4,"why":"16 g / 16 g/mol = 1 mol CH₄. Times 4 hydrogen per molecule = 4 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 2.41 × 10²⁴.","traps":["That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them.","You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.","You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.","That is the number of MOLECULES. Each CH₄ holds 4 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 4.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ph-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A 0.010 M Ba(OH)2 solution is prepared in water at 25 °C, where Ba(OH)2 dissociates completely to give 1 Ba(2+) and 2 OH- per formula unit. Therefore [OH-] = 0.020 M. Using pOH = -log[OH-] and pH + pOH = 14, what is the pH?","opts":["11.0","12.0","12.3","12.7","13.0"],"correct":2,"why":"[OH-] = 2 x 0.010 = 0.020 M; pOH = -log(0.020) = 1.70. Therefore pH = 14 - 1.70 = 12.30. Forgetting the factor of 2 (using 0.010 M) gives pH = 12.0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:nr3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"A concentration cell uses Cu electrodes in [Cu²⁺] = 0.01 M (left) and 1.0 M (right). E_cell?","opts":["0.118 V","−0.059 V","0","0.059 V","0.030 V"],"correct":3,"why":"Concentration cell → E° = 0. The 1.0 M side becomes the cathode (Cu²⁺ wants to deposit), the 0.01 M side becomes the anode. E = (0.0592/n)·log([Cu²⁺]_cathode/[Cu²⁺]_anode) = (0.0592/2)·log(1.0/0.01) = 0.0296 × 2 = 0.0592 V ≈ 0.059 V.","traps":["0.118 V leaves out the division by n: with 2 electrons the 0.0592 is halved before it multiplies log Q.","−0.059 V makes the dilute side the cathode; copper ions plate out on the concentrated side, so the cell voltage comes out positive.","Concentration cells DO produce voltage.",null,"0.030 V uses log(10) instead of log(100); the ratio here is 100, so the log is 2."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_nr-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"At 25 °C, a galvanic cell has E°_cell = 0.50 V and n = 2 electrons transferred per cell reaction. Using the relation between E° and K: log K = nE°/0.0592, calculate the equilibrium constant K for the cell reaction.","opts":["10^10","10","10^5","10^17","10^(-17)"],"correct":3,"why":"log K = nE°/0.0592 = (2)(0.50)/0.0592 = 16.9 ≈ 17. Therefore K ≈ 10^17. Forgetting n (using just E°/0.0592) gives ~10^8.5; sign errors give 10^(-17).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Argon (Z = 18) has the noble-gas configuration [Ne] 3s² 3p⁶ with 18 electrons. Among the following common ions, identify the one that is BOTH isoelectronic with Ar AND carries a +2 charge.","opts":["Ca²⁺ (Z = 20)","Sc³⁺ (Z = 21)","Cl⁻ (Z = 17)","S²⁻ (Z = 16)","K⁺ (Z = 19)"],"correct":0,"why":"Isoelectronic with Ar means 18 electrons. Ca has Z = 20 and protons = 20; Ca²⁺ has lost 2 electrons → 18 e⁻, matching Ar. K⁺ and S²⁻ are also 18-electron species but carry +1 and -2 charges respectively, failing the +2 requirement.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_pd5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"On the CO2 phase diagram, the triple point lies at 5.11 atm and 216.6 K, while the critical point is at 73 atm and 304 K. A sample of CO2 is held at 1.00 atm and 25°C (298 K), conditions that fall well below the triple-point pressure but above its temperature. What phase is the CO2 in?","opts":["Supercritical fluid","Mixture of solid and liquid","Solid","Gas","Liquid"],"correct":3,"why":"At 1 atm (below 5.11 atm triple-point pressure) liquid CO2 is unstable, so the diagram shows only solid below the sublimation curve and gas above. At 298 K and 1 atm we are above the sublimation curve, so CO2 exists as a gas.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bf-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A 1.00 L buffer of 0.10 M HA and 0.10 M A- (pKa 5.00) receives a 0.05 mol bolus of HCl, which converts 0.05 mol of A- into HA. New concentrations: [HA] = 0.15 M, [A-] = 0.05 M. Using Henderson-Hasselbalch, find the resulting pH.","opts":["4.00","4.52","4.74","5.00","5.48"],"correct":1,"why":"pH = pKa + log([A-]/[HA]) = 5.00 + log(0.05/0.15) = 5.00 + log(0.333) = 5.00 - 0.477 = 4.52. The buffer absorbed the proton and pH dropped only ~0.5 unit instead of crashing to ~1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_hb5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":3,"q":"Xenon tetrafluoride (XeF₄) is a square planar molecule with F-Xe-F angles of 90°. The central xenon has four Xe-F σ bonds and two lone pairs that occupy axial positions to minimize lone-pair/lone-pair repulsion. What hybridization is assigned to the xenon?","opts":["sp³d²","sp³","sp³d","dsp²","sp³d³"],"correct":0,"why":"Steric number at Xe = 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs = 6, requiring sp³d² hybridization (octahedral electron geometry). The two lone pairs sit trans to each other (axial), leaving the four fluorines in a square plane with 90° bond angles.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-limiting#6s11gt","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"N₂ + 3 H₂ → 2 NH₃\n\nIf 4 mol of N₂ reacts with 6 mol of H₂, how many moles of NH₃ can form?","opts":["10 mol","2 mol","8 mol","4 mol","5 mol"],"correct":3,"why":"N₂: 4 / 1 = 4. H₂: 6 / 3 = 2. The smaller one limits. Product = 2 × 2 = 4 mol.","traps":["You added the reactants. Moles are not conserved; use the mole ratio from the balanced equation.","You found the limiting amount but did not multiply by the product coefficient.","You built the answer from the reagent in EXCESS. The smaller moles-per-coefficient runs out first and sets the yield.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_rl-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"A reaction A -> products at 298 K is studied with several different starting concentrations [A]_0, and the measured half-life is found to be CONSTANT (independent of [A]_0) for all trials. Using the integrated rate-law dependence of t_(1/2) on [A]_0, what is the order of the reaction with respect to A?","opts":["Second order","Third order","Half order","Zero order","First order"],"correct":4,"why":"Only first-order reactions have a half-life t_(1/2) = ln(2)/k that is independent of [A]_0. Zero-order t_(1/2) is proportional to [A]_0; second-order t_(1/2) is inversely proportional to [A]_0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ek-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"For the gas-phase equilibrium 2 NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g) at 298 K, the equilibrium constant in terms of concentrations is K_c = 4.7 M⁻¹. Using K_p = K_c·(RT)^Δn with R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K and Δn = (1 − 2) = -1, calculate K_p at 298 K.","opts":["0.0085","0.19","4.7","41","115"],"correct":1,"why":"Δn = mol gas products - mol gas reactants = 1 - 2 = -1. K_p = K_c·(RT)^Δn = 4.7 × (0.0821 × 298)^(-1) = 4.7 / 24.5 ≈ 0.19. Negative Δn means K_p < K_c whenever RT > 1, as it always is at room temperature.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-combined-gas#1gsc28u","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A gas occupies 6 L at 1 atm and 127 °C. What is its volume at 4 atm and 27 °C?","opts":["1.13 L","2 L","18 L","0.319 L","1.5 L"],"correct":0,"why":"P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2 in Kelvin. V2 = 6 × (1/4) × (300/400) = 1.13 L.","traps":[null,"Temperature ratio is upside down. Hotter gas expands, so volume moves with temperature.","Pressure ratio is upside down. More pressure squeezes the gas, so volume moves opposite to pressure.","You used Celsius in the ratio. Convert both temperatures to Kelvin first.","You ignored the temperature change. Both P and T changed here."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#14hbxw7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"What is the pH of a 1.0 × 10⁻¹ M solution of NaOH at 25 °C?","opts":["1","7","12","13","15"],"correct":3,"why":"Strong base: [OH⁻] = 10⁻¹ M, pOH = 1, pH = 14 − 1 = 13.","traps":["That is the pOH. NaOH gives you [OH⁻], so finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","You assumed neutral. A strong base is well above 7.","Exponent slip. For 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ the pOH is exactly n.",null,"You added instead of subtracting from 14. A pH that high is a signal to recheck."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_en-q2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"The catalytic Ostwald oxidation of ammonia is 4 NH3 + 5 O2 -> 4 NO + 6 H2O(g). Standard enthalpies of formation (kJ/mol): NH3 = -46, NO = +90, H2O(g) = -242, O2 = 0. Use ΔH_rxn = sum(n·ΔH_f,products) - sum(n·ΔH_f,reactants) to compute ΔH_rxn for one mole of equation.","opts":["-184 kJ","-724 kJ","+908 kJ","-1092 kJ","-908 kJ"],"correct":4,"why":"Products: 4(+90) + 6(-242) = 360 - 1452 = -1092. Reactants: 4(-46) + 5(0) = -184. ΔH = -1092 - (-184) = -908 kJ. The reaction is highly exothermic.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-dilution#1tmuuzr","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"How many mL of water must be ADDED to 50 mL of 1 M HCl to make a 0.5 M solution?","opts":["25 mL","150 mL","50 mL","51 mL","100 mL"],"correct":2,"why":"M1V1 = M2V2, so V2 = (1 × 50) / 0.5 = 100 mL total. Water added = 100 − 50 = 50 mL.","traps":["You divided the volume. Diluting makes the volume bigger, not smaller.","You added the starting volume on top of the final volume. M1V1 = M2V2 already gives the total.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","That is the FINAL volume. The question asks how much water you add, so subtract the volume you started with."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_mc-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A pharmacist measures out 4.50 g of pure water (H₂O, MW = 18.0 g/mol). Avogadro's number is 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹. How many individual hydrogen atoms are present in this sample?","opts":["6.0 × 10²³","1.5 × 10²⁴","3.0 × 10²³","9.0 × 10²³","1.5 × 10²³"],"correct":2,"why":"Moles H₂O = 4.50/18.0 = 0.250 mol. Each H₂O contains 2 H atoms, so mol H = 0.500. Atoms H = 0.500 × 6.022×10²³ ≈ 3.0 × 10²³. The factor-of-2 stoichiometric step (H per H₂O) is the most common error point.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:col-q11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A real 0.10 m MgSO₄ solution shows ΔTf = 0.24 °C in water (Kf = 1.86). The \"ideal\" ΔTf for MgSO₄ assuming complete dissociation would be 0.372 °C. The effective van't Hoff factor i_eff is:","opts":["1.00","1.29","1.55","1.86","2.00"],"correct":1,"why":"i_eff = ΔTf_measured / (Kf · m) = 0.24 / (1.86 × 0.10) = 0.24 / 0.186 = 1.29. MgSO₄ is famously underdissociated - extensive ion pairing of Mg²⁺ with SO₄²⁻ in water means effective i is much less than the predicted 2.","traps":["i = 1 would mean MgSO₄ does not dissociate at all - that is wrong, it does dissociate, just incompletely.",null,"That is the ratio taken upside down, ideal over measured; i_eff is the measured depression divided by Kf times m.","1.86 is Kf, not i. You confused two different constants in the formula.","2.00 is the IDEAL i (full dissociation). The question asks for the actual measured i_eff, which is reduced by ion pairing."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#1nvtbkr","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 3 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 227 °C and 0.5 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["246 L","82.1 L","112 L","24900 L","61.6 L"],"correct":0,"why":"T = 227 + 273 = 500 K. V = nRT / P = (3)(0.0821)(500) / 0.5 = 246 L.","traps":[null,"You dropped the moles. n belongs on top with R and T.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273.","You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.","You multiplied by pressure. V = nRT / P, so pressure goes underneath."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"originals:ab-ec59a41c94","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Blood is buffered by carbonic acid and bicarbonate. If a person hyperventilates and blows off a large amount of CO2, what happens to blood pH?","opts":["It falls, because less bicarbonate is made","It does not change, because blood is buffered","It rises, because hyperventilating adds extra bicarbonate to the blood","It falls, because CO2 is a base","It rises, because removing CO2 lowers carbonic acid"],"correct":4,"why":"CO2 + H2O is in equilibrium with H2CO3, which is in equilibrium with H+ and HCO3-. Blow off CO2 and the chain shifts toward CO2, which uses up H+. Fewer H+ means a higher pH. That is respiratory alkalosis.","traps":["Losing CO2 pulls the equilibrium away from H+, which makes the blood less acidic, not more.","Buffers limit changes. A large loss of CO2 still shifts the pH.","Breathing faster does not add bicarbonate. Bicarbonate actually dips a little, because H+ and HCO3- combine to replace the CO2 that was lost. The pH rises because that shift uses up H+.","CO2 forms carbonic ACID in water. Removing it removes acid.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"generated:gc-molecular-formula#13jl0t8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A compound has the empirical formula CH₂O and a molar mass of 60 g/mol. What is its molecular formula?","opts":["C₅H₁₀O₅","C₂H₄O₂","CH₂O","C₄H₈O₄","C₃H₆O₃"],"correct":1,"why":"Empirical mass = 30. 60 / 30 = 2, so multiply every subscript by 2: C₂H₄O₂.","traps":["Wrong multiplier. Molar mass divided by empirical mass gives it exactly.",null,"That is the empirical formula again. Divide the molar mass by the empirical mass to get the multiplier.","You doubled the multiplier. Check the empirical formula mass.","Multiplier is one too high. Molar mass divided by empirical mass must come out to a whole number."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:b-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Why is HF's BP higher than HCl's, even though HCl is heavier?","opts":["Metallic bonding","HF has stronger London forces","Stronger H-bonding in HF","HCl is ionic","HF is denser"],"correct":2,"why":"F can H-bond (NOF rule). Cl cannot. H-bond strength > extra LDF gain.","traps":["Neither one is a metal, so there is no pooled electron sea here.","London forces grow with size and electron count, so HCl actually has the stronger ones, and hydrogen bonding more than makes up for that.",null,"HCl is a polar covalent molecule, and it stays a molecule in the liquid rather than separating into ions.","Density does not set a boiling point, and what sets it is how hard the molecules are to pull apart."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ek-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"For the gas-phase reaction A(g) ⇌ B(g) at 298 K, the equilibrium constant is K_1 = 5.0. The chemist now multiplies all stoichiometric coefficients by 2 to write 2A(g) ⇌ 2B(g) at the same temperature. What is the new equilibrium constant K_2?","opts":["K_1^2 = 25","1/K_1 = 0.20","K_1/2 = 2.5","K_1 = 5.0","2K_1 = 10"],"correct":0,"why":"Multiplying coefficients by n raises K to the n-th power: K_new = K_old^n. Here n = 2, so K_2 = (5.0)^2 = 25. Doubling K (giving 10) is the most common error.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:gl-e12","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Mole fraction of N₂ in mixture: 0.5 atm N₂ + 1.5 atm O₂?","opts":["0.20","0.25","0.33","0.50","0.75"],"correct":1,"why":"χ_N₂ = 0.5/(0.5+1.5) = 0.25.","traps":["Dividing by 2.5 counts the nitrogen once on top and twice underneath; the total is 0.5 plus 1.5, which is 2.0.",null,"Dividing nitrogen by oxygen, 0.5 over 1.5, gives the ratio between the two gases rather than nitrogen's share of the whole.","Reading the partial pressure off as the fraction gives 0.50; the fraction needs that pressure divided by the 2.0 atm total.","That is oxygen's share; nitrogen is the smaller piece here."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lf005","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A 5.00 L flask contains 0.250 mol N2 and 0.500 mol O2 at 27°C. Assuming ideal gas behavior, what is the partial pressure of O2 in atm?","opts":["3.69 atm","12.3 atm","1.23 atm","2.46 atm","4.92 atm"],"correct":3,"why":"P(O2) = n(O2)·R·T / V = (0.500)(0.08206)(300) / 5.00 = 2.46 atm. Or use mole fraction: x(O2)·P_total = (2/3)(3.69) ≈ 2.46.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sf-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"When multiplying or dividing measured values, the result must carry the same number of significant figures as the input with the FEWEST sig figs. Calculate 1.50 × 4.0 (where 1.50 has 3 sf and 4.0 has 2 sf) and express the answer with the correct number of significant figures.","opts":["6","6.0","6.00","6.000","6.0000"],"correct":1,"why":"Numerical product: 1.50 × 4.0 = 6.00 (raw). Sig-fig limit: minimum of (3 sf, 2 sf) = 2 sf. Therefore the answer must be reported as 6.0 (2 sig figs). Writing '6' alone implies 1 sig fig; '6.00' implies 3.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_en-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Two related reactions are tabulated at 298 K: (1) A -> B with ΔH1 = -50 kJ; (2) 2 B -> C with ΔH2 = +30 kJ. Apply Hess's law (multiply step 1 by 2 to match B stoichiometry, then add to step 2) to compute ΔH for the combined reaction 2 A -> C.","opts":["+70 kJ","-130 kJ","-20 kJ","+30 kJ","-70 kJ"],"correct":4,"why":"Multiply (1) by 2: 2 A -> 2 B, ΔH = 2(-50) = -100 kJ. Add (2): 2 B -> C, ΔH = +30 kJ. Sum: 2 A -> C, ΔH = -100 + 30 = -70 kJ.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ks-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"CaSO4 is sparingly soluble in water at 25 °C and dissociates as CaSO4(s) ⇌ Ca^(2+) + SO4^(2-) with Ksp = 4.9x10^(-5). Defining molar solubility s such that [Ca^(2+)] = [SO4^(2-)] = s, then Ksp = s^2. What is the molar solubility s of CaSO4?","opts":["2.4x10^(-5) M","4.9x10^(-5) M","1.4x10^(-2) M","2.4x10^(-3) M","7.0x10^(-3) M"],"correct":4,"why":"Ksp = s·s = s^2 = 4.9x10^(-5). s = sqrt(4.9x10^(-5)) ≈ 7.0x10^(-3) M. Forgetting the square root (using Ksp directly) gives 4.9x10^(-5) M.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A reaction has ΔH = -120 kJ/mol and ΔS = -150 J/(K·mol). Spontaneity requires ΔG = ΔH - T·ΔS < 0. Set ΔG = 0 to find the crossover temperature, then state the temperature range over which the reaction is spontaneous.","opts":["Below 800 K","Below 80 K","Spontaneous at all T","Below 120 K","Above 800 K only"],"correct":0,"why":"Set ΔG = 0: T = ΔH/ΔS = (-120,000 J)/(-150 J/K) = 800 K. Below 800 K, the favorable enthalpy dominates the unfavorable entropy. Above 800 K, T·|ΔS| exceeds |ΔH| and the reaction becomes nonspontaneous.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-1o9d4eq","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"The product trans-2-methylcyclopentan-1-ol is desired from the alkene shown (1-methylcyclopentene), with the new OH placed on the LESS substituted alkene carbon. Which reagent sequence gives this anti-Markovnikov, syn-addition outcome?","opts":["1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4","Dilute H3PO4, H2O","HBr, then NaOH","H2O, cat. H2SO4, heat","1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH"],"correct":4,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation adds H and OH across the double bond anti-Markovnikov (OH to the less substituted carbon) and syn (H and OH add to the same face), which on a ring sets the trans relationship between the new OH and the adjacent methyl. Acid hydration and oxymercuration are both Markovnikov and would place OH on the more substituted carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-13b7fabf1b","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"Which base is strong enough to deprotonate a terminal alkyne (pKa about 25) essentially completely?","opts":["Sodium bicarbonate, NaHCO3 (the pKa of carbonic acid is about 6)","Sodium acetate (the pKa of acetic acid is about 5)","Sodium hydroxide, NaOH (the pKa of water is about 16)","Sodium amide, NaNH2 (the pKa of ammonia is about 38)","Sodium ethoxide, NaOEt (the pKa of ethanol is about 16)"],"correct":3,"why":"Equilibrium favors the side with the WEAKER acid, the one with the higher pKa. Cross out the metal to find the real base. Amide ion gives ammonia at pKa 38, which is weaker than the alkyne at 25, so the reaction goes forward. Hydroxide would give water at 16, which is stronger than the alkyne, so it does not.","traps":["Bicarbonate is a very weak base. It is nowhere near strong enough.","Acetate is a very weak base.","Water is a STRONGER acid than the alkyne, so the equilibrium would favor the alkyne and hydroxide.",null,"Ethanol is a stronger acid than the alkyne, so ethoxide cannot pull that proton off to any useful extent."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-1lfvsj4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Salicylaldehyde (shown) has an intramolecular hydrogen bond between its phenol O-H and the adjacent aldehyde oxygen. Compared with the IR of a simple phenol, what is the expected effect of this intramolecular hydrogen bonding on the O-H stretch?","opts":["The O-H stretch becomes a sharp doublet, much like the two N-H bands of a primary aromatic amine","The O-H band moves to ~2250 cm-1, overlapping the nitrile region","The O-H stretch sharpens and shifts to HIGHER wavenumber (above 3600 cm-1)","The O-H stretch disappears entirely from the spectrum once the internal hydrogen bond forms","The O-H stretch is broadened and shifted to LOWER wavenumber (below ~3200 cm-1)"],"correct":4,"why":"Hydrogen bonding weakens the O-H bond, lowering its frequency and broadening the band. The intramolecular H-bond in salicylaldehyde persists even when dilute, so O-H is broad and shifted to lower wavenumber vs a free O-H (~3600).","traps":null,"qs":"O=Cc1ccccc1O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1eq5a2f","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Diethyl 2-methyladipate (shown) is treated with sodium ethoxide and then acid. Of the two possible Dieckmann ring closures, which beta-ketoester is the major product?","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCC(C)C1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CC(C)CC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1(C)CCCC1=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Dieckmann is reversible and is driven by deprotonation of the product's carbon flanked by both carbonyls. If the methyl-bearing alpha carbon attacks the far carbonyl, that carbon becomes quaternary (option B) with no acidic proton, so that path reverses. The favored product comes from the unsubstituted CH2 enolate attacking, leaving an acidic alpha proton on the ester-bearing ring carbon: option A (C9H14O3), verified to equal the path-B closure.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)C(C)CCCC(=O)OCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1vndb83","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Two carbonyls are available for reduction by NaBH4 in methanol: acetophenone (an aryl methyl ketone, drawn) and benzaldehyde. Considering electronic and steric effects on hydride addition, which statement about relative rate is correct?","opts":["Acetophenone is reduced faster because ketones are inherently more electrophilic than aldehydes at the carbonyl carbon in every solvent","Benzaldehyde is reduced faster than acetophenone because the aldehyde carbonyl is less hindered and more electrophilic","Both are reduced at identical rates because NaBH4 reacts only with esters","Neither reacts because NaBH4 cannot reduce conjugated aryl carbonyls","Acetophenone is reduced faster because the extra methyl group donates electron density that makes the carbonyl a better hydride acceptor"],"correct":1,"why":"Aldehydes are reduced faster than ketones by hydride: the aldehyde carbonyl carbon is less sterically hindered and more electrophilic (only one alkyl/aryl group donating electron density), so benzaldehyde reacts faster than the more hindered, more electron-rich acetophenone.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-18i1svf","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"1-Cyclobutylethan-1-ol is treated with acid. A ring-expansion (alkyl) shift relieves ring strain before elimination. Which is the major alkene product?","opts":["CC1(O)CCCC1","CC=C1CCC1","C=CC1CCC1","C1=CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"The initial secondary cation on the exocyclic carbon triggers a ring-bond migration that expands cyclobutane to a 1-methylcyclopentyl (tertiary) cation, relieving strain. Zaitsev E1 gives 1-methylcyclopentene (option A). option B/option C are unexpanded cyclobutane alkenes, option D is a substitution (cyclopentanol) product, option E (cyclohexene) has the wrong ring size. Single correct answer.","traps":null,"qs":"OC(C)C1CCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1mc2sbd","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Propanal (shown) is stirred with excess methanol and a trace of acid (HCl). Treating the aldehyde with two equivalents of alcohol under acid gives which product class?","opts":["CCC(O)O","CCC(=O)OC","CCC(OC)OC","CCC(O)OC","CCCO"],"correct":2,"why":"An aldehyde + 2 eq alcohol under acid forms an acetal with two OR groups on the original carbonyl carbon: CCC(OC)OC. CCC(O)OC is the hemiacetal (one equivalent, intermediate); CCC(=O)OC is an ester (wrong connectivity); CCC(O)O is the gem-diol hydrate; CCCO is a reduction product.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-dbrfsk","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"Propylbenzene (drawn) reacts with Br2 and light (hv). Which structure is the major monobromination product?","opts":["CCCc1ccccc1Br","BrCCCc1ccccc1","CCCc1ccc(Br)cc1","CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Br2/hv is radical halogenation; the most stable radical is benzylic (resonance-stabilized into the ring), so Br goes to the benzylic carbon, giving (1-bromopropyl)benzene (option A). The internal/terminal positions are non-benzylic, and ring substitution requires FeBr3 (ionic EAS), not radical conditions.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-11r58ig","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"2,2-Dimethylpropan-1-ol (neopentyl alcohol, shown) is treated with concentrated HBr. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)=CC","CC(C)(C)CCl","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(C)CBr"],"correct":3,"why":"Neopentyl alcohol with HBr cannot easily do SN2 (hindered) or form a primary cation, so a 1,2-methyl shift gives a stable tertiary cation; bromide traps it to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (option A). option B is the non-rearranged neopentyl bromide, option C a different rearranged skeleton, option D an alkene, option E the chloride. Neopentyl rearrangement is a classic textbook example.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)CO","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-qn8vo7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"Bromobenzene (drawn below) undergoes Friedel-Crafts acylation. Halogens are an unusual class of substituent in EAS. Which choice correctly classifies the bromine substituent and predicts the major product position?","opts":["Activating and meta directing; the acyl group enters mainly meta","Deactivating and ortho/para directing; the acyl group enters mainly ortho only","Deactivating but ortho/para directing; the acyl group enters mainly para","Deactivating and meta directing; the acyl group enters mainly meta","Activating and ortho/para directing; the acyl group enters mainly para"],"correct":2,"why":"Halogens are the classic exception: they are deactivating (electron-withdrawing by induction) yet ortho/para directing (lone-pair resonance donation stabilizes the ortho/para arenium intermediates). The para product dominates over ortho largely for steric reasons.","traps":null,"qs":"Brc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-anc6po","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"(E)-4-Phenylbut-3-en-2-one (benzalacetone) is treated with lithium dimethylcuprate (Me2CuLi), then aqueous workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(O)(C)/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)/C=C(C)/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)C(C)Cc1ccccc1","CC(O)CC(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Option B adds methyl at the alpha carbon, option C is the 1,2 allylic alcohol (organolithium behavior, alkene retained), option D shows extra carbonyl reduction, and option E retains the alkene (incorrect). Index 0 is correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"own-organic:ace-0002","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"3-methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr. Which product comes out of the flask in the largest amount?","opts":["BrCCC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(Br)C(C)C","CC=C(C)C"],"correct":2,"why":"The proton lands on the end carbon and leaves a secondary cation sitting right next to a tertiary carbon. A hydrogen slides over with its pair, the charge moves to the better spot, and the bromide finds it there. The skeleton you started with is not the skeleton you finish with.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)C","os":true,"src":"own-organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-zlk3b3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Heptane-2,6-dione is treated with NaOH and warmed (intramolecular aldol condensation). What is the major product?","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1=CC(=O)CCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC(=O)C1","CC1CCCC(=O)C1=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Intramolecular aldol closing the favored six-membered ring then dehydration gives conjugated 3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (C7H10O). Keyed option A is the conjugated enone (verified identical to O=C1CCCC(C)=C1). Distractors: a non-conjugated methylcyclohexenone regioisomer, plain cyclohexanone, a methyl 1,2-dione, and a 5-membered ring. All valid, unique.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1sm5f0m","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"3-Phenyl-1,5-hexadiene (drawn) is heated, allowing a Cope ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement to reach the more stable isomer. Which structure is that product?","opts":["C=CCC(C=C)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1CCC=C","C=CCCC=Cc1ccccc1","C=CC(c1ccccc1)C=CC","C1=CC=CC(c2ccccc2)C1"],"correct":2,"why":"The Cope [3,3] shift of the 1,5-diene breaks the C3-C4 sigma bond and forms a new C1-C6 bond, relocating the double bonds. The phenyl ends up conjugated with a newly formed alkene (styrene-type), giving 1-phenylhexa-1,5-diene (opt 0), which is thermodynamically favored. Option 1 is the unchanged starting material; the others have incorrect connectivity or form a ring.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(c1ccccc1)CC=C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1op9odj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"In a Gabriel synthesis using potassium phthalimide (shown) followed by hydrazinolysis, which alkyl halide will give the highest yield of the desired primary amine?","opts":["1-bromobutane (a primary alkyl halide)","bromobenzene (an aryl halide)","2-chloro-2-methylbutane (a tertiary halide)","2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide)","vinyl bromide (a vinyl halide)"],"correct":0,"why":"The phthalimide anion alkylates via SN2, so it needs an unhindered substrate; 1-bromobutane (primary) gives the best yield. Tertiary halides undergo E2 elimination, and aryl/vinyl halides are unreactive toward SN2.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1c2ccccc2C(=O)[N-]1.[K+]","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-vqku6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"The molecule drawn is cis-1,3-dichlorocyclohexane. Which single statement BEST classifies this molecule with respect to chirality and optical activity?","opts":["It is a meso compound: it has stereocenters but an internal mirror plane, so it is achiral and optically inactive","It is chiral and optically active, existing as a pair of non-superimposable enantiomers that each rotate plane-polarized light in opposite directions","It is a constitutional isomer of 1,4-dichlorocyclohexane and is optically active","It exists only as a single non-superimposable mirror image, so it has no achiral stereoisomer at all","It has no stereocenters at all, so chirality is not possible"],"correct":0,"why":"So cis-1,3-dichlorocyclohexane is meso, achiral, optically inactive - option A correct. option C wrongly denies stereocenters; option D confuses constitutional vs stereoisomerism.","traps":null,"qs":"C1C[C@H](Cl)C[C@H](Cl)C1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-8gdlsf","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Benzene is treated with 1-chloropropane (CH3CH2CH2Cl) and AlCl3. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CCc1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Friedel-Crafts alkylation goes through a carbocation; the primary propyl cation undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the secondary isopropyl cation, so cumene (isopropylbenzene, choice A) is major. B n-propyl (no-rearrangement trap), C tert-butyl and D ethyl (wrong carbon count), E isobutyl (wrong skeleton).","traps":null,"qs":"ClCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1v0wmr4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this E2 reaction?","opts":["C=C(C)C(C)C","CC(C)(C)C=C","CC(C)=C(C)C","CCOC(C)C(C)(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":1,"why":"E2 is concerted with no carbocation, so no rearrangement. The only beta-H is on the CH3 of the C-Br carbon (the other neighbor is the quaternary tert-butyl carbon, no H), giving 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (option A). option B (2,3-dimethyl-2-butene) is the rearranged Zaitsev trap requiring a hydride/methyl shift, which E2 cannot do.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)C(C)Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1mnmhwn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"2-Pentyne (drawn), an UNsymmetrical internal alkyne, is treated with H2O, HgSO4, and H2SO4. Which statement best describes the outcome?","opts":["A carboxylic acid forms by oxidative cleavage of the triple bond","A single aldehyde forms, because Markovnikov addition of water to an internal alkyne always delivers the oxygen to a chain-end carbon of the molecule","A roughly equal mixture of two regioisomeric ketones (2-pentanone and 3-pentanone) forms, because neither alkyne carbon is strongly favored","No reaction occurs, because internal alkynes are unreactive toward Hg(II)/acid","Only 2-pentanone forms, because Markovnikov addition always places the carbonyl oxygen on C2, the alkyne carbon carrying the shorter alkyl chain"],"correct":2,"why":"For an unsymmetrical INTERNAL alkyne, the two carbons of the triple bond are similar in substitution, so Markovnikov selectivity is poor and acid-catalyzed hydration gives a mixture of both possible ketones. Internal alkynes give ketones, never aldehydes; the reaction does occur; and no C-C cleavage happens under these conditions.","traps":null,"qs":"CC#CCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1boz2p7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Two keto acids are considered for thermal decarboxylation: the gamma-keto acid shown (4-oxopentanoic / levulinic acid) and 3-oxo-3-phenylpropanoic acid (a beta-keto acid). Which statement is correct?","opts":["The gamma-keto acid decarboxylates faster, because the extra distance between its carbonyl and its carboxyl relieves strain in the cyclic transition state that delivers the proton","Both decarboxylate equally well because both contain a ketone","Neither decarboxylates; a ketone cannot assist loss of CO2","Only the gamma-keto acid decarboxylates, because only its carbonyl and carboxyl are spaced to close a ring, and the product is a cyclic lactone rather than a simple ketone","Only the beta-keto acid decarboxylates readily on heating; the gamma-keto acid does not, because its carbonyl is too far for a six-membered cyclic transition state"],"correct":4,"why":"Facile thermal decarboxylation requires the second carbonyl to be beta to the carboxyl so a six-membered cyclic transition state can form. The beta-keto acid satisfies this; in the gamma-keto acid the ketone is one carbon too far, so no low-energy cyclic TS exists.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCC(=O)O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-8neusk","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with HBr. Which structure is the major product? (Consider carbocation stability carefully.)","opts":["BrCC(C)C(C)C","BrCCC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)CBr","CC(C)(Br)C(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation at C2 next to the quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives a more stable tertiary cation that Br- traps, yielding 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane (keyed option A, canonical CC(C)C(C)(C)Br). The classic trap (option B) is the unrearranged secondary bromide.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-yraq7h","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Three protonated nitrogen acids are compared: methylammonium (CH3NH3+, shown), anilinium (C6H5NH3+), and pyridinium (C5H5NH+). Which is the strongest acid (lowest pKa), reflecting the WEAKEST/most-stabilized conjugate base?","opts":["All three have identical pKa because each loses an N-H proton","Anilinium; its conjugate base aniline has the N lone pair delocalized into the benzene ring, making aniline a weak base and anilinium the strongest acid","Methylammonium; a primary amine cation is always the most acidic","Pyridinium; the aromatic ring feeds electron density to nitrogen and makes pyridine a stronger base than methylamine","Methylammonium; the alkyl group's electron donation makes methylamine the weakest base of the three, which leaves its cation as the strongest acid of the set by a wide margin"],"correct":1,"why":"Aniline is the weakest base (N lone pair delocalized into the ring), so its conjugate acid anilinium (pKa ~4.6) is the strongest acid, below pyridinium (~5.2) and methylammonium (~10.6).","traps":null,"qs":"C[NH3+]","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-p5414p","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n(E)-3-hexene is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","opts":["CC[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1CC","CCC1OC1CC","CCC(O)C(O)CC","CC[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CC","CC[C@H]1O[C@@H]1CC"],"correct":4,"why":"Epoxidation is syn/stereospecific, so E (trans) alkene gives trans-3,4-epoxyhexane, the (R,R)/(S,S) chiral pair. Two-concept (stereospecific syn + trans recognition).","traps":null,"qs":"CC/C=C/CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-csbizj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"Diethyl pimelate (diethyl heptanedioate, drawn) is subjected to NaOEt and then acidic workup. Predict the major Dieckmann condensation product.","opts":["O=C1CCCCC1C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","O=C1CCCC1C(=O)OCC","O=C1CCCCCC1C(=O)OCC","OC1CCCCC1C(=O)OCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Distractors: 5-ring, 7-ring, acyclic, beta-hydroxy ester.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(=O)OCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1olhj21","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"Aniline is dissolved in diethyl ether along with a neutral hydrocarbon impurity. To extract the aniline into the aqueous phase as a water-soluble salt, which reagent should be added during the acid/base extraction?","opts":["Aqueous NaHCO3","Aqueous HCl","Brine","Anhydrous MgSO4","Aqueous NaOH"],"correct":1,"why":"Aniline is a base; aqueous HCl protonates the amine to water-soluble anilinium chloride, pulling it into the aqueous layer. NaOH/NaHCO3 are basic and leave the neutral amine in ether; brine and MgSO4 do not ionize it.","traps":null,"qs":"Nc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-163l4so","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"The keto-aldehyde 4-oxopentanal (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CHO, drawn) is treated with exactly 1 equivalent of NaBH4 at 0 C. Which carbonyl is reduced preferentially, and what is the reason?","opts":["The aldehyde is reduced first because aldehydes are more electrophilic and less sterically hindered than ketones","The ketone is reduced first, because the two alkyl groups on a ketone make its carbonyl carbon more electrophilic than an aldehyde carbon","The aldehyde is reduced first because its carbonyl carbon is the more sterically hindered of the two and binds borohydride more tightly","Neither is reduced; NaBH4 only reduces esters","Both are reduced equally; NaBH4 shows no selectivity"],"correct":0,"why":"Aldehydes are both more electrophilic (one electron-donating alkyl group vs. two for the ketone) and less sterically hindered, so with a limited amount of NaBH4 the aldehyde is reduced selectively to give the hydroxy-ketone. Ketones are less, not more, electrophilic. The reasoning in the steric distractor is reversed.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCC=O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-9wwa4n","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Reaction of the cyclic alkene shown with O3 then Zn/AcOH gives a single dicarbonyl product. Classify the two carbonyl groups in that product.","opts":["Two aldehydes (a dialdehyde)","Two carboxylic acids (a diacid)","One ketone and one carboxylic acid","One ketone and one aldehyde","Two ketones (a diketone)"],"correct":4,"why":"1,2-dimethylcyclopentene is tetrasubstituted at the C=C (each sp2 carbon carries a methyl and a ring carbon, no H). Ring-opening cleavage places a ketone at both former alkene carbons, giving the open-chain diketone 2,6-heptanedione (C7H12O2). With no vinylic C-H, no aldehyde or acid forms.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=C(C)CCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1oitbuh","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Catalytic hydrogenation (H2/Pd) of the cyclic alkene shown is performed. What is the major organic product of this reaction?","opts":["Cyclohexanol","Benzene","1,2-dibromocyclohexane","Cyclohexane","Cyclohexanone"],"correct":3,"why":"H2/Pd adds two H across the single C=C of cyclohexene to give saturated cyclohexane. No O or halogen is introduced, and benzene (more unsaturated) is not formed by reduction.","traps":null,"qs":"C1CCC=CC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1rhxjv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"1-Hexyne is subjected to the following sequence: (1) NaNH2; (2) CH3CH2Br; (3) H2, Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the final major product?","opts":["C/C=C\\CCCCC","CCCCCCCC","CCC#CCCCC","CC/C=C\\CCCC","CC/C=C/CCCC"],"correct":3,"why":"Steps 1-2 alkylate the C6 acetylide with ethyl (C2) to give 3-octyne; step 3 (Lindlar) does syn reduction to cis (Z)-3-octene. Trans needs Na/NH3; octane is over-reduced; [3] is the alkyne (skipped Lindlar); [4] is cis-2-octene (wrong position).","traps":null,"qs":"C#CCCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-y8rsnv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"(S)-2-phenylpropanamide is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. The stereocenter is at the carbon alpha to the carbonyl. What is the major organic product, including correct configuration?","opts":["NC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","NC[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","NC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1C","O=C[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","OC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide to a primary amine (C=O becomes CH2, nitrogen retained), giving 2-phenylpropan-1-amine. Distractor 1 inverts the center (R), the alcohol comes from wrongly treating the amide like an acid, the aldehyde is an incorrect reduction, and the last option adds a ring methyl.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(N)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"qr-a:pct1163","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"A $318 tablet is marked down 45 percent. What is the sale price?","opts":["143.1","174.9","273","303.69","363"],"correct":1,"why":"New = 318 × (1 - 45/100) = 318 × 0.55 = 174.9.","traps":["That is 45 percent of 318, the discount itself rather than what is left to pay.",null,"You subtracted 45 dollars instead of 45 percent, and 45 percent of 318 is 143.10.","That takes off 4.5 percent; a 45 percent cut means multiplying by 0.55.","You added 45 dollars, and this price is coming down by 45 percent."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#ejj15","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A cyclist travels at 108 kilometers per hour. What is that speed in meters per second?","opts":["0.03 m/s","1.08 × 10⁵ m/s","1800 m/s","389 m/s","30 m/s"],"correct":4,"why":"108 km/h × 1000 m/km ÷ 3600 s/h = 30 m/s. Shortcut: divide km/h by 3.6.","traps":["That is kilometers per second. Multiply by 1000 to get meters.","That is meters per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour.","Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so kilometers and hours cancel.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1459","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"In the coordinate plane, how far apart are the points (3, 0) and (9, 5), to the nearest hundredth?","opts":["3.32","6","7.81","11","61"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((6)² + (5)²) = √61 = 7.81.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fam5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A delivery route runs 2/3 of a mile, then 5/6 of a mile further, then doubles back 1/2 of a mile. How many miles is the driver from the start?","opts":["7/6","2","5/6","3/4","1"],"correct":4,"why":"LCD = 6. 4/6 + 5/6 − 3/6 = 6/6 = 1.","traps":["You rescaled 1/2 as 2/6 instead of 3/6, leaving one sixth too much.","You added all three instead of subtracting the last one, 4/6 plus 5/6 plus 3/6.","You rescaled 1/2 as 4/6 instead of 3/6, taking away one sixth too many.","You drifted into fourths, but 3, 6 and 2 all fit inside sixths.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1107","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A race route is 37.8 kilometers long, and a runner has covered 15.6 kilometers of it. How many kilometers are left?","opts":["2.22","22.2","22.3","53.4","222"],"correct":1,"why":"Stack the numbers with the decimal points in line and subtract: 8 tenths minus 6 tenths is 2 tenths, then 37 minus 15 is 22, giving 22.2. Rounding to 38 minus 16 gives about 22, so the size checks out.","traps":["You placed the point one spot too far left; 37.8 and 15.6 differ by more than 22, not by about 2.",null,"You slipped in the tenths column; 8 tenths minus 6 tenths is 2 tenths.","You added the two numbers instead of subtracting them.","You dropped the decimal point entirely, which scales the answer up by ten."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1352","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A 36-mile delivery route is split between two drivers in a ratio of 2 to 7. How many miles does the shorter leg cover?","opts":["4","8","9","18","28"],"correct":1,"why":"Total parts = 9; one part = 36/9 = 4. Smaller = 2·4 = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1028","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A pump moves 4/7 of a tank in the first hour and 4/5 of a tank in the second. How many tanks were moved over the two hours?","opts":["11/7","16/35","8/12","8/35","48/35"],"correct":4,"why":"LCD = 35. 4/7 = 20/35, 4/5 = 28/35. Combine: 48/35 = 48/35.","traps":["You rounded 4/5 up to a whole; four fifths is 28/35, so the total is 48/35.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.","You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 8 over 12.","You put both 4s over 35 without rescaling; 4/7 is 20/35 and 4/5 is 28/35.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1417","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A kitchen mixes 200 mL of a 10% vinegar dressing with 250 mL of a 5% vinegar dressing. What is the approximate percent concentration of the mixture?","opts":["7.22","7.5","7.78","32.5","33.75"],"correct":0,"why":"Solute total = 20 + 12.5 = 32.5 mL in 450 mL → 7.22%.","traps":[null,"Averaged the two percents without weighting them by how much of each solution went in.","Paired each percent with the other solution's volume, putting the 10 percent on the 250 mL pour.","That is the total milliliters of solute, not the percent of the mixture.","Took 7.5 percent, the plain average of the two strengths, of the 450 mL total, which gives a volume rather than a concentration."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1285","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"Each of 4 clinics received 5 crates of supplies and returned 2 of them. How many crates did the clinics keep in total?","opts":["3","8","12","16","18"],"correct":2,"why":"Distribute: 4·5 - 4·2 = 20 - 8 = 12.","traps":["That is just the 5 - 2 inside the parentheses, so the 4 outside never got multiplied in.","That is 4 times 2 on its own, so the 4 times 5 piece got dropped.",null,"Only a single 4 came off the 20 instead of 4 times 2, which leaves 16.","Distributing the 4 only to the 5 gives 20 - 2 = 18, leaving the 2 unmultiplied."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#1wt0ylw","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the PRODUCT of the solutions of x² − 11x + 18 = 0?","opts":["18","−18","11","7","−11"],"correct":0,"why":"It factors as (x − 2)(x − 9), so the roots are 2 and 9. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = 11, product = c/a = 18.","traps":[null,"Sign slip. The product of the roots is c/a with its sign kept.","That is the SUM of the roots (−b/a). The product is c/a.","That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their product.","You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-compound-interest#1d9ugnl","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"$1000 is invested at 20% annual interest, compounded once a year. What is the balance after 2 years?","opts":["$440.00","$1200.00","$1400.00","$1440.00","$1728.00"],"correct":3,"why":"Balance = 1000 × (1.2)² = $1440.00.","traps":["That is the interest earned. The question asks for the BALANCE.","One year short. Count the compounding periods.","That is SIMPLE interest. Compounding earns interest on the interest, so multiply by the growth factor each year.",null,"One year too many. Count the compounding periods."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#b96vmp","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (5 × 10⁻⁶)(7 × 10⁵) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["1.2 × 10⁰","3.5 × 10⁻¹","3.5 × 10⁻²","3.5 × 10⁻³⁰","3.5 × 10⁰"],"correct":4,"why":"5 × 7 = 35 and 10⁻⁶ × 10⁵ = 10⁻¹. 35 × 10⁻¹ is not proper form, so write 3.5 × 10⁰. Answer: 3.5 × 10⁰.","traps":["You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You multiplied the exponents. Powers of ten ADD when you multiply.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1479","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A rinse cycle runs for 3.4 minutes. How many seconds is that?","opts":["0.06","20.4","63.4","184","204"],"correct":4,"why":"1 min = 60 sec, so 3.4 min × 60 = 204 sec.","traps":["You divided the 3.4 by 60 instead of multiplying, which sends the count the wrong way.","You multiplied by 6 instead of 60, dropping a zero.","You added 60 to the 3.4 instead of multiplying the 3.4 by it.","You read 3.4 minutes as 3 minutes and 4 seconds; the 0.4 is four tenths of a minute, which is 24 seconds.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dec1108","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A crate weighs 24.9 kilograms, and 1.1 kilograms of packing material is taken out. What does the crate weigh now in kilograms?","opts":["2.38","23.8","23.9","26.0","238"],"correct":1,"why":"Line the decimal points up and subtract: 9 tenths minus 1 tenth is 8 tenths, and 24 minus 1 is 23, so the answer is 23.8. Taking a little over 1 away from nearly 25 should land just under 24, which it does.","traps":["You placed the point one spot too far left; the gap between 24.9 and 1.1 is close to 24.",null,"You subtracted 1.0 rather than 1.1, leaving one tenth too much.","You added the two numbers instead of subtracting them.","You dropped the decimal point, which makes the answer ten times too big."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1184","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A monthly utility bill of $125 rises 10%, then falls 25% from that higher amount. What is the bill in dollars, to the nearest cent?","opts":["93.75","103.13","106.25","137.5","171.88"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply factors: 125 × 1.1 × 0.75 = 103.13. Naïvely adding gives 106.25, which is wrong.","traps":["That applies only the 25 percent cut and leaves the rise out.",null,"You added the two changes into a net 15 percent drop, and sequential percents multiply instead.","That is the price after the 10 percent rise, one step short of the answer.","That raises the second step by 25 percent instead of cutting by it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1720","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"What is the value of 5^1 × 5^6?","opts":["0","15625","15630","78125","6103515625"],"correct":3,"why":"Same base × adds exponents: 5^(1+6) = 5^7 = 78125.","traps":["Two positive powers multiplied together can never give zero.","You multiplied the exponents, so you landed back on 5^6.","You added the two values, 15625 and 5, instead of multiplying them.",null,"You multiplied the bases as well as adding the exponents, which gives 25^7."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rt3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Two cars start 200 miles apart, driving toward each other. One at 50 mph, the other 30 mph. When do they meet?","opts":["2.5 hr","5 hr","6.67 hr","2 hr","4 hr"],"correct":0,"why":"They close the gap at COMBINED speed: 50 + 30 = 80 mph. Time = 200 / 80 = 2.5 hours.","traps":[null,"Averaging the two speeds to 40 instead of adding them to 80.","Using only the slower car's 30 mph; both cars are eating into the gap.","You used 200/100 instead of 200/80.","You used one car's speed only."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1600","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"How many real roots does x² − 4x + 1 = 0 have?","opts":["no real roots","two imaginary roots","one real and one imaginary root","one real root","two real roots"],"correct":4,"why":"Δ = b² − 4ac = 16 − 4·1·1 = 12. > 0, so two.","traps":["A discriminant of 12 is positive, so both roots are real, at 2 + √3 and 2 − √3.","Imaginary roots require a negative discriminant, and this one is positive.","Complex roots come in conjugate pairs, so one real and one imaginary is not possible.","One root needs a discriminant of zero, and 16 − 4 is 12.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pem1303","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"What is the value of 3² + 6 × 5?","opts":["33","36","39","45","90"],"correct":2,"why":"Exponents go first, then multiplication, then addition: 3 squared is 9, 6 times 5 is 30, and 9 + 30 = 39. Watch the square itself, because 3 squared means 3 times 3, not 3 times 2. The line is two independent blocks that meet only at the final plus sign.","traps":["You dropped the square and used a plain 3, giving 3 + 30.","You read 3 squared as 3 times 2 = 6; squaring means 3 times 3 = 9.",null,"You added 3 and 6 first and then multiplied by 5, which skips both the square and the ranking.","You multiplied all three numbers and ignored the square entirely."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1332","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"Which inequality is equivalent to -3x < 0?","opts":["x = 0","x ≤ 0","x > 0","x < 0","x ≥ 0"],"correct":2,"why":"Divide both sides by -3 (negative) and FLIP: x > 0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp006","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A solution that is 25% acid by volume is mixed with a solution that is 60% acid. To produce 35 liters of a final solution that is 40% acid, how many liters of the 25% solution must be used?","opts":["12 L","10 L","25 L","15 L","20 L"],"correct":4,"why":"Let x = L of 25%, then 35 − x = L of 60%. 0.25x + 0.60(35 − x) = 0.40(35). 0.25x + 21 − 0.60x = 14. −0.35x = −7. x = 20 L.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1739","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"A thermostat is set to 10 degrees, and the room temperature of x degrees is exactly 4 degrees away from that setting. What are the possible values of x?","opts":["14 or 6","-6 or 6","14","4 or -4","6 or -14"],"correct":0,"why":"|x − 10| = 4 means x − 10 = ±4, so x = 14 or 6.","traps":[null,"That pairs 6 with its negative, and the other branch is 10 plus 4, which is 14.","You solved x - 10 = 4 and stopped, and the branch x - 10 = -4 gives 6 as well.","You set x equal to plus or minus 4 and never undid the minus 10 inside the bars.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp005","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A right triangle has legs of length 3 and 4 (and hypotenuse 5). It is inscribed in a CIRCLE such that the hypotenuse is a diameter. What is the area of the region INSIDE the circle but OUTSIDE the triangle?","opts":["(25π/4) − 6","(25π/2) − 6","6 − (25π/4)","5π − 12","25π − 6"],"correct":0,"why":"Hypotenuse = diameter = 5, so radius = 2.5. Circle area = π(2.5)² = 25π/4. Triangle area = (1/2)(3)(4) = 6. Region = 25π/4 − 6.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1255","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"What value of x satisfies -3x - 2 = x + 10?","opts":["-6","-4","-3","-2","3"],"correct":2,"why":"Move x: (-3-1)x = 10+2 → -4x = 12, so x = -3.","traps":["The x terms were combined as -3 - 1 = -2 instead of -4, leaving -2x = 12.","Substituting -4 gives 10 on the left and 6 on the right.",null,"The -2 was subtracted from the 10 instead of added, giving -4x = 8.","Dividing 12 by -4 gives -3, so this answer drops the minus sign."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dsm5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Four soil samples each weigh 3 grams. What is the variance of the four weights?","opts":["0","1","3","9","12"],"correct":0,"why":"All values equal mean, so all deviations = 0, so variance = 0. Zero spread.","traps":[null,"Identical values leave no deviation at all, so the variance is zero rather than one.","That's the mean.","Squared the mean instead of squaring the deviations from it.","That is the sum of the four values, not their spread."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1419","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A research assistant mixes 100 mL of a 25% sugar solution with 100 mL of a 10% sugar solution. What is the percent concentration of the mixture?","opts":["8.75","12.5","15","17.5","35"],"correct":3,"why":"Solute total = 25 + 10 = 35 mL in 200 mL → 17.5%.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:di3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"For what values of k does x² − 6x + k = 0 have two real solutions?","opts":["k < 9","k = 9","k < 36","k > 9","k ≥ 0"],"correct":0,"why":"Δ = 36 − 4k > 0, so k < 9.","traps":[null,"That is the single value where the discriminant hits zero and the two roots collapse into one.","You dropped the 4 in 4ac and solved 36 − k > 0.","You flipped the inequality, but a larger k shrinks 36 − 4k and kills the real roots.","You constrained the sign of k instead of comparing 4k with 36."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#y5q2s1","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 180 miles at 50 mph and returns the same 180 miles at 75 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["68.8 mph","61.2 mph","30 mph","62.5 mph","60 mph"],"correct":4,"why":"Time out = 180/50 = 3.6 h, time back = 180/75 = 2.4 h. Average = 360 / 6 = 60 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":["Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.","That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time.","You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d.","You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:cou1579","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Ten runners finish a race and the first three places are recorded in order. How many different orderings of the top three are possible?","opts":["13","30","120","720","1000"],"correct":3,"why":"nPr = 10!/(10-3)! = 720.","traps":["Added 10 and 3.","Multiplied 10 by 3.","That counts the groups with order ignored; each trio can be arranged 6 ways.",null,"Treated it as 10 choices for each of three slots, which lets an item repeat."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1638","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Daily visitors to a library average 100 with a standard deviation of 20. What is the z score of a day with 77 visitors?","opts":["-23","-1.15","-0.23","1.15","3.85"],"correct":1,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (77 − 100)/20 = -1.15.","traps":["This is the raw gap in points, stopping before the division by 20.",null,"Divided the 23-point gap by the mean of 100 instead of by the standard deviation of 20.","Right size, wrong side: 77 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative.","Divided 77 by 20 without first subtracting the mean."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#y9j3s6","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 7 runners, in how many ways can 1st through 3rd place be awarded?","opts":["6","21","35","210","343"],"correct":3,"why":"Order matters: 7P3 = 7 × 6 × 5 = 210.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 3 people. You still have to choose them from 7.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.","You used a combination. Places are different prizes, so ORDER matters: use a permutation.",null,"You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"written:qr-11-rn4znq","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"If cos θ = -0.28 and θ lies in the third quadrant, what is sin θ?","opts":["-0.96","-0.9216","-0.72","0.72","0.96"],"correct":0,"why":"Square and subtract: sin²θ is 1 minus 0.0784, which is 0.9216, and the square root of that is 0.96. The third quadrant has a negative y coordinate, so the sine is negative. The pair 0.28 and 0.96 is the 7-24-25 triangle scaled down by 25, which is a handy tell that the arithmetic came out clean.","traps":[null,"This is sin²θ, so the square root step has not happened yet.","This subtracts 0.28 from 1 instead of subtracting its square.","This subtracts the raw value and drops the sign as well.","This is the right size but the wrong sign, since sine is negative in the third quadrant."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:qr-11-1ntn2bl","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"Two sides of a triangle measure 5 and 7, and the angle between them is 60 degrees. How long is the third side?","opts":["2","√109, about 10.4","√39, about 6.2","12","√74, about 8.6"],"correct":2,"why":"The Law of Cosines gives the third side squared as 25 plus 49 minus 2 times 5 times 7 times cos 60. Cos 60 is 1/2, so the subtracted piece is 70 times 1/2, which is 35, leaving 74 minus 35, or 39, and the side is √39, about 6.2. A 60 degree angle is narrower than a right angle, so the third side has to come out shorter than the √74 a right angle would give, which checks the sign of that last term for you.","traps":["This multiplies by 1 instead of by cos 60, leaving 74 minus 70.","This adds the 35 rather than subtracting it, which is what a 120 degree angle would produce.",null,"This adds the two given sides, a length the third side can never reach.","This drops the cosine term entirely and treats the triangle as a right triangle."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1127","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"What is 35 percent of 476?","opts":["16.66","166.6","309.4","511","16660"],"correct":1,"why":"35% of 476 = 35/100 × 476 = 166.6.","traps":["You shifted the decimal one place too far, dividing the percent by 1000 rather than by 100.",null,"You found the 65 percent left over rather than the 35 percent the question asks for.","You added the 35 to the 476 instead of taking 35 percent of it.","You multiplied 35 by 476 and never divided by 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:men1234","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A commute is 23 kilometers each way. How many kilometers is the round trip?","opts":["36","40","46","56","146"],"correct":2,"why":"(20 + 3) × 2 = 40 + 6","traps":["That is ten light, since 40 plus 6 is 46.","That doubles the 20 and leaves the 3 times 2 out.",null,"That is ten over, since 40 plus 6 is 46.","An extra hundred appeared, since 20 times 2 is 40 and 3 times 2 is 6."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:vol1676","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A steel ball has a radius of 4 millimeters. To the nearest hundredth, what is its volume in cubic millimeters?","opts":["50.27","67.02","201.06","268.08","804.25"],"correct":3,"why":"V = (4/3)πr³ = 268.08.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1700","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"On a mapping grid, a straight cable runs between (-9,-12) and (-16,-14). What is its slope, to the nearest hundredth?","opts":["-9","-0.29","0.08","0.29","3.5"],"correct":3,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = -2/-7 = 0.29.","traps":["-9 adds the change in y and the change in x instead of dividing them.","-0.29 keeps one negative and drops the other, and two negatives divided give a positive.","0.08 comes from writing -16 minus 9 for the run instead of -16 minus -9, so the run lands at -25.",null,"3.5 divides the run by the rise, -7 over -2, which is the slope upside down."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1397","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"One student can enter a stack of survey forms in 5 hours and another can enter the same stack in 4 hours. Working together, how many hours does the stack take?","opts":["0.45","2.22","4","4.5","9"],"correct":1,"why":"1/T = 1/5 + 1/4 = 9/20. T = 20/9 = 2.22 hr.","traps":["Stopping at the combined rate of 9/20 of the job per hour instead of flipping it to get the hours.",null,"Reporting the faster worker's solo time; the help pulls it lower still.","Averaging the two times instead of combining the rates.","Adding the two times; together they finish sooner than either one alone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1160","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"A $37 water bill rises 33 percent. What is the new bill?","opts":["4","12.21","38.22","49.21","70"],"correct":3,"why":"New = 37 × (1 + 33/100) = 37 × 1.33 = 49.21.","traps":["You subtracted 33 dollars from 37, and this price is rising rather than falling.","That is 33 percent of 37 on its own, the increase before it joins the price.","That multiplies by 1.033, a 3.3 percent rise; 33 percent means multiplying by 1.33.",null,"You added 33 dollars instead of 33 percent."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pc3c","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"70% of students study. 90% of studiers pass. 30% of non-studiers pass. P(studied | passed)?","opts":["90%","60%","~88%","~72%","70%"],"correct":2,"why":"P(study & pass) = 0.7×0.9 = 0.63. Total P(pass) = 0.63 + 0.3×0.3 = 0.72. 0.63/0.72 ≈ 88%.","traps":["90% is the chance a studier passes, which is the conditional turned around.","60% is the gap between the two pass rates, 90 percent minus 30 percent, rather than a share of the passers.",null,"About 72 percent is the overall chance of passing, which is the denominator of the ratio, so this one stops a step early.","70% is the share of all students who study, before the passing information narrows the group."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261001"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s15"}},{"id":"FULL_2","name":"Full-Length Test 2","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"bio:BIO_p049","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Acetylcholine released by parasympathetic postganglionic neurons acts on which receptors at the target organ?","opts":["NMDA receptors","GABA receptors","Muscarinic receptors","Nicotinic receptors","Adrenergic receptors"],"correct":2,"why":"Parasympathetic traffic runs on acetylcholine at both of its synapses, so the receptor name is what tells the two apart. At the target organ, meaning heart, gut, and glands, the receptor is muscarinic, a G protein-coupled receptor that slows the heart and steps up digestion. Tie each word to its source: muscarine comes from a mushroom and acts on organs, while nicotine acts at the ganglia and at skeletal muscle.","traps":["NMDA receptors are glutamate receptors involved in central excitation and learning rather than in parasympathetic organ control.","GABA receptors handle inhibition inside the central nervous system and have no role in autonomic output to organs.",null,"Nicotinic receptors do bind acetylcholine, but they sit at the autonomic ganglia and at the neuromuscular junction, one synapse earlier than the target organ.","Adrenergic receptors bind norepinephrine and epinephrine, which is the sympathetic side of the autonomic system."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1btzdwa","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"Three turns of the Calvin cycle produce six G3P, and only one of them leaves. What becomes of the other five?","opts":["They are oxidized back to carbon dioxide to recover the ATP that was spent","They are rearranged, with three more ATP, into three molecules of RuBP","They move into the thylakoid lumen to feed the next round of light reactions","They are reduced further by NADPH into two molecules of glucose","They are exported to the cytosol and stored there as starch"],"correct":1,"why":"The cycle has to pay itself back before it pays anything out. Fifteen carbons sitting in five G3P get reshuffled into three five carbon RuBP, and that reshuffle costs three ATP, which is why the ATP bill runs higher than the NADPH bill. Count carbons on both sides, fifteen and fifteen, and regeneration stops feeling like sleight of hand.","traps":["Burning them would undo the entire point of fixing that carbon in the first place.",null,"Nothing from the Calvin cycle feeds back into the lumen; the traffic runs the other way, as ATP and NADPH into the stroma.","Five three carbon molecules cannot become two six carbon sugars, and glucose assembly happens outside the cycle anyway.","Starch is stored in the stroma, and only the single exported G3P ever leaves the cycle."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-c3e5a1","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"A cell releases a signal molecule that diffuses a short distance and acts on neighboring cells of a different type, without ever entering the bloodstream. This is:","opts":["Autocrine signaling","Endocrine signaling","Synaptic signaling","Direct contact through gap junctions","Paracrine signaling"],"correct":4,"why":"Sort these by how far the message travels and who receives it. Paracrine means the local neighborhood by diffusion, endocrine means the bloodstream to distant targets, autocrine means the signal comes back to the cell that sent it, and synaptic means across a narrow cleft to one named partner. Short trip, neighbors, no blood is paracrine.","traps":["Autocrine signaling acts on the same cell that released the molecule, not on its neighbors.","Endocrine signals travel through the bloodstream to distant targets.","Synaptic transmission crosses a specialized cleft to one target cell, not the general neighborhood.","Gap junction signaling passes molecules between joined cytoplasms with no trip through the extracellular space.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e027","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A symbiotic relationship in which one organism benefits while the other is neither helped nor harmed is called:","opts":["Commensalism","Amensalism (one harmed, one unaffected)","Competition","Mutualism","Parasitism"],"correct":0,"why":"Score each partner with a plus, a minus, or a zero and these names sort themselves out. Mutualism is plus plus, parasitism and predation are plus minus, competition is minus minus, and commensalism is plus zero. Barnacles riding on a whale are the standard picture: the barnacle gets transport and a feeding current while the whale carries on unaffected. Truly neutral partners are rare in nature, so exam items keep it clean by telling you outright that the second organism is unaffected.","traps":[null,"Amensalism is the mirror image of this relationship: one organism is harmed while the other is unaffected.","Competition costs both organisms, since each reduces what the other can obtain.","Mutualism has both partners gaining, the way plant roots and mycorrhizal fungi trade sugar for minerals.","Parasitism has one partner gaining at the other's expense, so the second organism is harmed rather than untouched."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-n4m8rq","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"What is the final electron acceptor of the mitochondrial electron transport chain, and what does it become?","opts":["NAD+, which becomes NADH","Ubiquinone, which becomes ubiquinol","Carbon dioxide, which becomes a carbohydrate","Oxygen, which becomes water","Cytochrome c, which becomes reduced cytochrome c"],"correct":3,"why":"The chain is a bucket brigade, and the question asks who is holding the bucket at the end. Oxygen takes the electrons at complex IV, picks up protons, and leaves as water, which is why blocking that last handoff stops the entire chain within seconds. Everything else listed is a carrier, and a carrier by definition passes the electrons along.","traps":["NAD+ collects electrons earlier in respiration and delivers them to the chain, so it is a supplier rather than the endpoint.","Ubiquinone shuttles electrons within the membrane and hands them to complex III, so it is a middleman too.","Carbon dioxide accepting electrons to build sugar describes photosynthesis, not respiration.",null,"Cytochrome c is a mobile carrier between two complexes, so it passes electrons on instead of keeping them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-hd8ex8","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A sprinting muscle cell is fermenting. What does it make from pyruvate, which enzyme does it, and is any gas released?","opts":["Acetyl-CoA, by pyruvate dehydrogenase, with CO2 released","Lactate, by pyruvate decarboxylase, with CO2 released","Lactate, by lactate dehydrogenase, with no gas released","Acetaldehyde, by pyruvate decarboxylase, with CO2 released","Ethanol, by alcohol dehydrogenase, with CO2 released"],"correct":2,"why":"Human muscle runs lactic acid fermentation: NADH hands its electrons straight to pyruvate, lactate dehydrogenase catalyzes the transfer, and NAD+ comes back ready for glycolysis. Nothing is decarboxylated, so no carbon dioxide comes off and lactate keeps all three of pyruvate's carbons. The carbon count is the cleanest way to tell the two fermentations apart: three carbons in and three carbons out means lactate.","traps":["This is aerobic pyruvate oxidation in the matrix, the pathway that shuts down when oxygen runs short.","The enzyme and the gas both belong to the alcoholic route, and making lactate removes no carbon at all.",null,"Acetaldehyde is the intermediate of alcoholic fermentation, again a yeast and plant pathway.","Ethanol is the yeast and plant route, and animal cells have no pyruvate decarboxylase to start it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev011","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The acrosome is the enzyme filled cap at the tip of a sperm cell. Which organelle does it come from?","opts":["Centrosome","Golgi apparatus","Mitochondrion","Lysosome","Rough endoplasmic reticulum"],"correct":1,"why":"This question rewards you for knowing the secretory pathway rather than a stray fact. Proteins headed for a membrane bound compartment are made at the rough endoplasmic reticulum, then sorted and packaged by the Golgi, which buds off the vesicles, and the acrosome is exactly such a vesicle, one enormous one sitting over the sperm nucleus. When the sperm meets the egg coat the acrosome empties its enzymes to clear a path, which is the same logic as a lysosome, another Golgi product.","traps":["The centriole a sperm carries goes on to organize the zygote's first spindle, so it is a microtubule organizing center rather than a membrane bound sac of enzymes.",null,"Mitochondria in a sperm are packed into the midpiece to power the tail, a different part of the cell with a different job.","The acrosome behaves like a giant lysosome because it is loaded with hydrolytic enzymes, which is why this is tempting, but lysosomes themselves also bud from the Golgi.","The hydrolytic enzymes are translated on the rough endoplasmic reticulum, but the vesicle that becomes the acrosome is packaged and pinched off by the Golgi."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g005","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Crossing a true-breeding red-flowered snapdragon with a true-breeding white-flowered snapdragon produces offspring with pink flowers. This result demonstrates:","opts":["Epistasis between two different genes","Codominance","Sex-linkage","Incomplete dominance","Linkage"],"correct":3,"why":"Look at the heterozygote and ask whether you are seeing both colors or an average of them. One working pigment allele makes only about half the usual amount of red pigment, so the petal lands between the two parents as a uniform pink rather than showing red and white separately. The proof that the alleles themselves stay intact is the next generation, which comes back as one red to two pink to one white.","traps":["Epistasis requires a second gene masking the first, but one pigment gene accounts for all three phenotypes on its own.","Codominance would show both colors at full strength at the same time, such as red and white streaks on one petal, rather than an even in-between shade.","Sex-linkage would make the result depend on which parent contributed which allele, and it does not here.",null,"Linkage describes two genes riding together on one chromosome, and only a single flower color gene is involved here."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-9webrs","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A human somatic cell has 46 chromosomes while it sits in G1. Consider that same cell at three moments: the end of G1, the end of G2, and the instant after anaphase has separated the sister chromatids but before the cytoplasm divides. How many chromosomes are present at each of those three moments?","opts":["46, then 46, then 92","23, then 46, then 92","46, then 92, then 92","46, then 92, then 46","92, then 92, then 46"],"correct":0,"why":"The trap is counting chromatid arms and calling them chromosomes. Count centromeres instead: after S phase the cell holds twice the DNA but still has 46 centromeres, so still 46 chromosomes, each now with two chromatids. The moment anaphase pulls those centromeres apart, every chromatid becomes a chromosome in its own right, so one undivided cell briefly holds 92. Cytokinesis then hands 46 to each daughter.","traps":[null,"23 is the haploid number that belongs to a gamete after meiosis, not to a somatic cell at any point in the ordinary cell cycle.","This counts chromatids at the end of G2, but replication leaves the two copies joined at a single centromere, so they still count as one chromosome.","This makes the same G2 error and then halves too soon, since the count per cell only returns to 46 once cytokinesis splits the cytoplasm.","This treats G1 as already replicated, and a G1 cell carries 46 chromosomes of one chromatid each."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p099","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which set of structures makes up the brainstem?","opts":["Thalamus, hypothalamus and epithalamus","Cerebellum","Cerebrum","Hypothalamus","Medulla, pons, midbrain"],"correct":4,"why":"The brainstem is the stalk connecting the brain to the spinal cord, and it is built from three pieces stacked top to bottom: midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata. Everything automatic that keeps you alive without thinking about it, breathing rate, heart rate, blood pressure, is run from here. A quick check is whether a structure sits on the stalk itself rather than hanging off it or resting above it.","traps":["Those three together form the diencephalon, which sits between the cerebrum and the brainstem.","The cerebellum attaches behind the brainstem and coordinates balance and smooth movement, but it is a separate structure rather than part of the stalk.","The cerebrum is the large upper brain handling thought, voluntary movement and sensation, sitting well above the brainstem.","The hypothalamus belongs to the diencephalon and controls the pituitary and homeostasis, just above the brainstem rather than within it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-f98995ebcf","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which hormone is released from the posterior pituitary but is actually made in the hypothalamus?","opts":["Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)","Prolactin","Growth hormone (GH)","Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)","Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)"],"correct":3,"why":"The posterior pituitary is a storage closet, not a factory. Hypothalamic neurons make ADH and oxytocin, ship them down their axons, and store them in the posterior lobe until release. The anterior pituitary makes its own hormones.","traps":["TSH is an anterior pituitary hormone.","Prolactin is made and released by the anterior pituitary.","GH is made AND released by the anterior pituitary.",null,"ACTH is an anterior pituitary hormone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-3-q3fw7z","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Compared with a peptide hormone acting on the same tissue, a steroid hormone usually produces a response that:","opts":["Begins faster and lasts longer","Begins faster and fades faster","Is identical in timing, since both work through second messengers","Begins more slowly and fades faster","Begins more slowly and lasts longer"],"correct":4,"why":"Timing follows mechanism. A peptide hormone flips switches that are already built, so cAMP rises and kinases fire within seconds, and the effect dies as soon as the messenger is broken down. A steroid changes which genes are transcribed, so new protein has to be made before anything happens, and that protein keeps working for hours after the hormone is gone. Gene transcription means slow on and slow off.","traps":["Steroid effects are not fast, transcription and translation both take time.","That is the peptide profile, since second messenger cascades rise quickly and quit quickly.","Steroids do not use second messengers, they act as transcription factors.","Steroid effects persist, because the newly made protein lingers after the signal ends.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1bz3wg9","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"In a resting neuron the inside of the membrane is negative relative to the outside, and sodium is far more concentrated outside than in. For sodium, the concentration gradient and the electrical gradient:","opts":["Oppose each other, with the concentration gradient winning","Both favor movement of sodium out of the cell","Both favor movement of sodium into the cell","Oppose each other, with the electrical gradient winning","Cancel exactly, so sodium already sits at equilibrium"],"correct":2,"why":"Two forces act on every ion, and they have to be read separately before they are combined. Sodium is more concentrated outside, so the concentration force pushes it in, and sodium is positive while the inside is negative, so the electrical force pulls it in as well. Both point the same way, which is why sodium floods in the instant a channel opens.","traps":["This assumes the two forces conflict, and for sodium at rest they both point inward.","Sodium is high outside rather than inside, so neither force sends it outward.",null,"This treats the negative interior as repelling sodium, but a negative interior attracts a positive ion.","Sodium sits far from equilibrium at rest, which is exactly what makes an action potential possible."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1of2hc4","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"Acetyl-CoA cannot start the Krebs cycle alone. It has to combine with a four-carbon molecule to form six-carbon citrate. Which molecule?","opts":["Pyruvate","Alpha-ketoglutarate","Succinyl-CoA","Citrate","Oxaloacetate"],"correct":4,"why":"The cycle is a loop, so the molecule greeting acetyl-CoA is the same one the previous turn regenerated. Four carbons plus the two arriving on acetyl-CoA gives the six of citrate, and the two CO2 released over the turn bring the count back to four. Tracking that carbon count, four to six to five to four, is the fastest way to keep the intermediates straight.","traps":["Pyruvate has three carbons and is converted to acetyl-CoA before the cycle even begins.","Alpha-ketoglutarate has five carbons and sits midway through the cycle, not at its start.","Succinyl-CoA is a four-carbon intermediate, but it appears later, after both decarboxylations.","Citrate is the product of that combination rather than one of the starting pieces.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p031","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Cardiac output equals stroke volume multiplied by which quantity?","opts":["Vessel diameter","Hematocrit","Heart rate","Ejection fraction","Blood pressure"],"correct":2,"why":"Cardiac output is how much blood the heart delivers each minute, so it has to combine how much leaves per beat with how many beats happen per minute. Stroke volume times heart rate gives volume per minute, and a typical resting value is about 70 milliliters times 70 beats per minute, close to 5 liters per minute. When a cardiac formula gets confusing, check the units, since the pairing that yields volume per minute is the right one.","traps":["Vessel diameter sets resistance downstream and says nothing about how much blood the heart ejects per minute.","Hematocrit describes what fraction of blood is red cells, which affects viscosity and oxygen carrying rather than volume pumped.",null,"Ejection fraction is the share of the filled ventricle that gets ejected, so it helps determine stroke volume rather than multiplying it.","Blood pressure is what results when output meets resistance in the vessels, not a factor inside the output formula."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p085","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In which structure of the male reproductive tract does spermatogenesis take place?","opts":["Vas deferens","Interstitial (Leydig) cells between the tubules","Epididymis","Seminiferous tubules","Prostate"],"correct":3,"why":"This is testing the map of the male tract: where sperm are made versus where they mature, are stored, or simply travel. Sperm are produced inside the coiled seminiferous tubules of the testis, with Sertoli cells nursing them through meiosis. Everything downstream is plumbing: the epididymis matures and stores them, the vas deferens carries them, and the accessory glands add fluid. Follow the sperm's path from the beginning and the first stop is the answer.","traps":["This is the tube that carries already mature sperm toward the urethra, a transport route rather than a production site.","Leydig cells sit between the tubules and make testosterone; the dividing sperm cells are inside the tubules, not in that interstitial tissue.","Sperm finish maturing and gain motility here and are stored here, but they arrive already made.",null,"This gland contributes fluid to semen and never houses developing sperm."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e022","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Allopatric speciation begins with which of the following events?","opts":["Hybrid zone","Part of the population shifting onto a new host plant while every individual stays in the same area","Random mating","Geographic barrier","Polyploidy"],"correct":3,"why":"The name carries the answer: allo means other and patria means homeland, so the two populations end up in different places. A river shifts its course, a mountain range rises, or a few individuals reach an island, and gene flow between the groups stops. Once gene flow stops, drift and different local selection pressures push the two groups apart until they can no longer interbreed even if they meet again.","traps":["A hybrid zone forms later, when separated populations come back into contact, so it is an outcome of the process rather than its trigger.","A shift onto a new host while everyone remains in the same area is the classic sympatric scenario, with no geographic separation involved.","Random mating keeps a population genetically mixed, which is what prevents a split rather than what starts one.",null,"Polyploidy creates a new species in one generation with no separation at all, which is the sympatric route instead."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-a1ldow","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Nitric oxide released by an endothelial cell reaches the smooth muscle of a blood vessel and relaxes it. Which feature of this signal is most unusual compared with a typical hormone?","opts":["It travels through the blood to reach distant target organs","It is stored in secretory vesicles and released slowly by exocytosis over several hours","It binds a G-protein-coupled receptor on the muscle cell surface","It acts as a transcription factor once it reaches the nucleus","It is a gas that crosses membranes directly and acts on an enzyme inside the cell"],"correct":4,"why":"Nitric oxide is a small uncharged gas, so it needs no surface receptor and no transporter: it diffuses straight through the membrane. Inside the muscle cell it activates guanylyl cyclase, cGMP rises, and the muscle relaxes so the vessel widens. It also breaks down within seconds, which is why its effect stays local.","traps":["It is far too short lived for long-distance travel, its action is local.","Nitric oxide is made on demand and breaks down in seconds rather than being stored.","Its receptor is the intracellular enzyme guanylyl cyclase, not a receptor at the surface.","Nitric oxide works through cGMP rather than by binding DNA.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-pw61ua","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Cholesterol sits wedged between phospholipids in an animal cell membrane. At body temperature it reduces fluidity, and at low temperature it keeps the membrane from stiffening. The best description of its role is:","opts":["It makes the membrane permanently more rigid","It anchors the membrane to the cytoskeleton beneath it","It makes the membrane permanently more fluid","It buffers fluidity against temperature change","It seals the membrane so that water cannot cross"],"correct":3,"why":"Cholesterol does not have one direction of effect, it has one job: holding fluidity steady. A warm membrane gets too loose and cholesterol restrains the tails, a cold membrane gets too ordered and cholesterol wedges in to stop tight packing. If a choice says cholesterol only increases or only decreases fluidity, half the temperature story is missing.","traps":["This captures only the warm-temperature half of the effect.","Anchoring is the job of membrane proteins that bind cytoskeletal filaments, not of cholesterol.","This captures only the cold-temperature half of the effect.",null,"Water still crosses the bilayer and moves through aquaporins, cholesterol does not seal it out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1f42c5x","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"FADH2 yields less ATP than NADH does. Why?","opts":["It is a weaker reducing agent because it carries no phosphate group","It is made in the cytosol and must be shuttled into the matrix first","It carries only one electron, while NADH carries two","It enters the chain at complex II, so it drives fewer proton pumps","It hands its electrons straight to oxygen, skipping the chain"],"correct":3,"why":"The yield difference comes down to how many pumps the electrons pass on the way down. NADH is oxidized at complex I and its electrons drive pumping at I, III, and IV, while FADH2 joins at complex II and only drives III and IV. Fewer protons pumped means a smaller contribution to the gradient, which is why NADH is worth about 2.5 ATP and FADH2 about 1.5.","traps":["Neither carrier uses a phosphate group to hold electrons, so that is not the distinction.","The FADH2 from the Krebs cycle is made inside the matrix, and shuttles are a separate issue affecting cytosolic NADH.","Both carriers deliver two electrons, so the electron count is not where they differ.",null,"Nothing reaches oxygen without passing through complexes III and IV first."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-yn3j9l","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Oxygen never appears as a reactant in any Krebs cycle reaction, yet the cycle stops almost immediately when a cell runs out of oxygen. Why?","opts":["Without oxygen acetyl-CoA is redirected into fatty acid synthesis by a direct oxygen sensor","Without oxygen the chain stops, so NAD+ is never regenerated for the cycle","Without oxygen the matrix pH drops far enough to denature the cycle's dehydrogenase enzymes","Without oxygen pyruvate can no longer cross the inner mitochondrial membrane","Without oxygen the cycle runs backward and consumes the ATP it previously made"],"correct":1,"why":"The link is the supply of oxidized carriers. Three cycle steps need NAD+ and one needs FAD, and the only way a cell restocks them in bulk is by dumping electrons onto oxygen at the end of the chain. No oxygen means no reoxidation, every carrier sits in its loaded form, and each dehydrogenase step has nothing left to hand electrons to.","traps":["Acetyl-CoA can feed other pathways, but no oxygen sensor redirects it, and that would not explain the cycle halting.",null,"The matrix does not acidify enough to denature enzymes, and the block is a missing reactant rather than protein damage.","The pyruvate transporter does not depend on oxygen; what runs short is oxidized carriers, not substrate.","The cycle's reactions are not simply reversed when oxygen disappears; the pathway stalls instead."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p082","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Vaccination protects a person against a later infection primarily through which mechanism?","opts":["Inducing memory B/T cells","Antibiotics","Strengthening innate barriers such as skin, mucus, and stomach acid","Killing pathogens directly","Direct antibody injection"],"correct":0,"why":"A vaccine is a rehearsal. It presents antigen without causing real disease, so the B and T cells that recognize that antigen expand and leave behind long lived memory cells, and a later real exposure then meets a secondary response that is faster, larger, and mostly IgG. Active immunity like this takes a week or two to build but it lasts, so the choice about inducing memory cells is what a vaccine actually does.","traps":[null,"Antibiotics treat a bacterial infection already underway and do nothing for viruses or for a future exposure.","Innate barriers are already in place and are not antigen specific, so strengthening them could not explain protection against one particular pathogen.","A vaccine contains nothing that kills a pathogen; it presents antigen so that your own cells learn the target.","Injecting antibodies is passive immunity, which works within hours but fades in weeks and leaves no memory behind."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e011","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following organisms shows a Type I survivorship curve?","opts":["Humans, large mammals","Most insects and other invertebrates","Oysters","Hydra","Annual plants"],"correct":0,"why":"A survivorship curve is a picture of when in life the deaths happen. Type I organisms produce few offspring and care for them, so most survive through early and middle life and the curve stays high before dropping late, which is the human and large mammal pattern. Type II is a straight diagonal, a roughly constant chance of dying at any age. Type III falls off a cliff at the start, the shape for organisms that release enormous numbers of eggs or seeds and invest nothing afterward.","traps":[null,"Most insects produce many offspring with very heavy early mortality, so they sit on the Type III curve.","Oysters release huge numbers of larvae and lose almost all of them early, which is the Type III shape.","Hydra face roughly the same chance of dying at any age, which gives the straight Type II line.","Annual plants make many seeds and lose most of them as seedlings, another Type III pattern."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-533cecbb82","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following does NOT contribute to genetic variation among gametes or offspring?","opts":["Random fertilization","Cytokinesis at the end of meiosis II","Mutation in a germ cell","Crossing over in prophase I","Independent assortment in metaphase I"],"correct":1,"why":"Cytokinesis only splits the cytoplasm around chromosomes that have already been sorted, so it does not shuffle or change any alleles. The shufflers are crossing over, independent assortment and random fertilization, and mutation is the original source of brand new alleles. One more fact worth keeping: DNA is copied once, before meiosis I, and never between meiosis I and meiosis II. That is how the chromosome number ends up halved.","traps":["Which sperm meets which egg DOES add variation.",null,"A mutation in a germ cell CAN be passed on, so it does add variation.","Crossing over DOES create new allele combinations.","Independent assortment DOES shuffle maternal and paternal chromosomes."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-5-18jhy06","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"A plant growing in dry soil closes its stomata in the middle of the day. What happens inside the guard cells to make that happen?","opts":["Potassium ions enter, water follows them in, and the cells swell","The guard cells divide and seal the pore with new cells","Carbon dioxide inside the leaf falls, and the drop pulls the pore shut","Potassium ions leave, water follows by osmosis, and the cells go limp","The guard cell chloroplasts stop absorbing blue light and the pore contracts"],"correct":3,"why":"Guard cells behave like two long balloons: pump solute in and they swell and bow apart, let solute out and they slump back together. Drought sends a hormone signal, abscisic acid, that opens channels for potassium to leave, water follows it out, and the pore closes. Water always follows the salt, and that single sentence rebuilds either direction on demand.","traps":["That is the opening mechanism, which runs in exactly the other direction when water is plentiful.","Guard cells do not divide to open or close a pore, since the pore is controlled by their shape.","Low carbon dioxide inside the leaf is a signal to open the pore, not to close it.",null,"Blue light is still hitting the leaf at midday, and the drought signal closes the pore in spite of it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d027","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Which set of features is present in all chordates at some point in the life cycle?","opts":["Vertebral column only","Bilateral symmetry only","Notochord, dorsal hollow nerve cord, pharyngeal slits, post-anal tail","Notochord, ventral solid nerve cord, pharyngeal slits and post-anal tail","Coelom"],"correct":2,"why":"The four chordate features are worth being able to recite, and the phrase at some point in the life cycle is doing real work in the stem. You had all four as an embryo: a notochord, later replaced by vertebrae, a dorsal hollow nerve cord that became your brain and spinal cord, pharyngeal slits that became structures of the jaw, ear and throat, and a post-anal tail. A sea squirt larva shows all four and then loses most of them as it settles into the adult form. When a chordate question appears, run that list of four in order.","traps":["A vertebral column belongs to the vertebrates, a subgroup within the phylum; tunicates and lancelets are chordates with no vertebrae.","Bilateral symmetry is shared with flatworms, molluscs and arthropods, so it cannot define chordates.",null,"A ventral solid nerve cord is the protostome pattern seen in earthworms and insects; the chordate nerve cord is dorsal and hollow.","A coelom is shared with annelids, molluscs and echinoderms, so it does not pick out chordates either."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g035","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a dihybrid cross where a homozygous recessive genotype at one gene masks the expression of a second gene, as in the Bombay phenotype, what phenotypic ratio is expected in the F2 generation?","opts":["9:6:1","9:3:3:1","13:3","12:3:1","9:3:4"],"correct":4,"why":"Start from the ordinary dihybrid 9:3:3:1 and ask which classes end up looking identical. In recessive epistasis, being homozygous recessive at the masking gene hides the second gene completely, so one of the 3 classes merges with the 1 class and becomes 4. That leaves 9:3:4, and the appearance of a 4 in any modified ratio is your signal that a recessive genotype is doing the masking.","traps":["This is duplicate gene action with an additive effect, where the two single-dominant classes look alike and combine into 6.","That is the unmodified dihybrid ratio, seen when two genes act independently with no interaction between them.","This comes from dominant suppression, where the 9, a 3 and the 1 all end up sharing one phenotype.","A 12 appears when a dominant allele at one gene does the masking, merging the 9 and one 3 class together.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p089","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which layer of the epidermis holds the dividing stem cells that replace keratinocytes lost from the skin surface?","opts":["Stratum basale","Stratum spinosum","Stratum corneum","Stratum granulosum","Stratum lucidum"],"correct":0,"why":"Skin replaces itself from the bottom up, so the dividing layer has to sit at the bottom. The stratum basale rests on the basement membrane and holds the keratinocyte stem cells along with melanocytes, and each daughter cell pushed upward fills with keratin and dies by the time it reaches the surface. Running the order from deep to superficial, basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum in thick skin, corneum, puts the choice naming the basale at the dividing end.","traps":[null,"The spinosum sits just above the basale and its cells are held together by desmosomes, but the dividing stem cells stay anchored to the basement membrane below it.","The corneum is the outermost layer of dead, flattened, keratin filled cells that are shed, so nothing divides there.","In the granulosum the cells are already filling with keratohyalin granules and losing their organelles on the way to dying.","The lucidum is a thin clear band of dead cells found only in thick skin such as the palms and soles."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p035","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The coronary arteries deliver blood to which tissue?","opts":["Liver","Heart muscle itself","Brain","Only the inner lining of the chambers, since the outer wall is fed by blood passing through","Lungs"],"correct":1,"why":"The heart is full of blood, yet the muscle cannot feed itself from it, because the wall is far too thick for oxygen to diffuse in from the chamber. So the first two branches off the aorta, just past the aortic valve, are the coronary arteries, and they loop back over the heart to supply the myocardium itself. Block one and that patch of muscle starves, which is what a heart attack is.","traps":["The liver is fed by the hepatic artery and the hepatic portal vein, both well downstream in the abdomen.",null,"The brain is supplied by the carotid and vertebral arteries, which branch further along the aortic arch.","Diffusion from the chambers reaches only a thin inner layer, which is why the coronary arteries supply the full thickness of the heart wall.","The lungs receive blood from the pulmonary arteries for gas exchange, and their own tissue is fed by the bronchial arteries."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1kxv9cz","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"A drug completely blocks triose phosphate isomerase, so dihydroxyacetone phosphate can no longer be turned into glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate. What happens to the net ATP yield of glycolysis per glucose?","opts":["It falls from 2 to 0","It stays at 2","It falls from 4 to 2","It falls from 2 to 1","It rises above 2"],"correct":0,"why":"Work the two halves of the pathway separately. The cell still pays 2 ATP to split the whole six-carbon sugar, but now only one of the two three-carbon pieces continues, so the payoff phase returns 2 ATP and 1 NADH instead of 4 and 2. Two spent against two returned leaves a net of zero, which is exactly why that isomerase step exists.","traps":[null,"Only half the carbon now reaches the payoff phase, so the output cannot be unchanged.","The 4 is the gross figure, and the question asks for net, which starts at 2.","Halving feels right, but the investment is not halved: the cell still spends 2 ATP up front on the whole sugar.","Blocking a step in the middle of the pathway does not undo what was already spent at the start."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p038","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Red blood cells from which blood type can be given to a recipient of any ABO and Rh type, making it the universal donor?","opts":["A+","AB+","O negative","B-","O+ (Rh positive)"],"correct":2,"why":"This is an antigen counting question, not an antibody one. Donor red cells are safe in anyone only when they display nothing for the recipient's antibodies to grab, and type O negative displays no A antigen, no B antigen, and no Rh antigen. Flip the logic for the universal recipient, which is the blood type whose plasma carries the fewest antibodies, AB positive.","traps":["Type A positive carries both the A antigen and the Rh antigen, so anti-A antibodies or Rh sensitization in the recipient could attack it.","AB positive is the universal recipient because its plasma holds no anti-A or anti-B antibodies, but as a donor it displays the most antigens of all.",null,"B negative avoids the Rh problem but still displays the B antigen, which the anti-B antibodies in a type A or type O recipient would attack.","O positive red cells carry no A or B antigen, but the Rh antigen is still there and an Rh negative recipient can respond to it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d007","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"An embryo develops by spiral, determinate cleavage. That pattern identifies the animal as which of the following?","opts":["Both","Protostome","A radially symmetric animal such as a jellyfish","Deuterostome","Neither"],"correct":1,"why":"Two words in the stem point the same direction. Spiral means the cells of the early embryo settle into the grooves between the cells below them instead of stacking straight up, and determinate means each cell's fate is locked in early, so a separated cell makes only part of an animal. Both belong to the protostome package, along with the blastopore becoming the mouth, as seen in molluscs, annelids and arthropods. Learn it as one phrase, spiral, determinate, mouth first, and the opposite package, radial, indeterminate, anus first, comes with it.","traps":["An embryo follows one cleavage pattern, not both; spiral determinate and radial indeterminate are alternatives.",null,"This mixes up cleavage geometry with body symmetry; spiral, determinate cleavage belongs to bilaterally symmetric protostomes, and a jellyfish is neither bilateral nor a protostome.","Deuterostomes cleave radially and indeterminately, so a cell separated early can still produce a whole animal.","Spiral, determinate cleavage is precisely the protostome signature, so the pattern does place the animal."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-y5m7wf","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Linear chromosomes lose a little sequence each round of replication, because the extreme end of the lagging strand has nowhere to place a final primer. Some cells avoid that loss using telomerase. What does telomerase do?","opts":["It joins the two chromosome ends together so that no sequence can be lost","It caps the end with protein so ordinary polymerase can place a primer there","It copies the missing end from the homologous chromosome, which serves as a template","It removes the last RNA primer and fills the gap with DNA, the way DNA polymerase I does","It carries its own RNA template and extends the 3' end with repeat sequence"],"correct":4,"why":"Telomerase is a reverse transcriptase that carries a short RNA template inside itself and uses it to add telomeric repeats onto the 3' overhang. That lengthened overhang gives the lagging strand machinery room to prime, so the chromosome stops getting shorter. Notice what it does not do: it never restores lost gene sequence, it extends the buffer at the tip. Look for it in germ cells, stem cells, and many cancer cells, and not in most somatic cells.","traps":["Joining chromosome ends is exactly the accident telomeres exist to prevent, and a fused chromosome tears apart at the next anaphase.","A protein cap does protect the end, but it cannot create the 3' hydroxyl that priming requires, so the shortening would continue.","Using the homolog as a template happens in some repair pathways, and telomerase needs no second chromosome because it brings its own template.","Removing and replacing a primer works in the middle of a strand, but at the very end there is no upstream 3' hydroxyl to extend from, which is the whole problem.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-iupkei","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Researchers grow algae in water whose oxygen atoms carry a heavy isotope label, and feed them ordinary unlabeled carbon dioxide. Where does the label first show up?","opts":["In the glucose the algae build","In the oxygen gas bubbling off the algae","In the phosphate groups of newly made ATP","In the carbon dioxide the algae take in from the air","In the chlorophyll of the algae's thylakoid membranes"],"correct":1,"why":"The real question is which reactant donates the oxygen atoms in the gas. Water is the molecule torn apart at photosystem II, so the oxygen released came from water, while carbon dioxide's oxygen ends up in sugar. When a labeling experiment confuses you, follow the atom rather than the molecule.","traps":["The oxygen atoms in glucose trace back to carbon dioxide, which was deliberately left unlabeled here.",null,"ATP is assembled from ADP and phosphate already sitting in the stroma, not from the atoms of the split water.","Carbon dioxide is a starting material taken from the air, not a product that could collect the label.","Chlorophyll was built long before the experiment began and is not remade out of the labeled water."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g009","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In a population at Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, 16 percent of individuals express a recessive disorder. What percentage of the population are heterozygous carriers?","opts":["16%","24%","36%","40%","48%"],"correct":4,"why":"Start from what you are handed: the affected group is the homozygous recessive genotype, so q squared equals 0.16 and q equals 0.4. Then p equals 1 minus q, which is 0.6, and the carriers are the heterozygotes, 2pq, or 2 times 0.6 times 0.4, which comes to 0.48. The order never changes: take the square root of the affected fraction first, subtract from one to get the other allele, then double the product of the two.","traps":["16 percent is the affected group you were given, the homozygous recessive fraction, and the question asks for the carriers instead.","24 percent is the product of p and q without doubling it, and since heterozygotes can form two ways, that product has to be multiplied by two.","36 percent is p squared, which is the homozygous dominant group rather than the carriers.","0.4 is the recessive allele frequency, the square root of the affected fraction, which stops one step short of the carrier calculation.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1wnmho1","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"DNA polymerase cannot begin a strand from bare template. Something else must lay down a short starter that polymerase can then extend. What is that starter made of, and what happens to it later?","opts":["A short RNA piece made by RNA polymerase II, later spliced out as an intron","A short protein tag made by helicase, later stripped off by the polymerase","A short RNA piece made by primase, later removed and replaced with DNA","A short DNA piece made by telomerase, later extended into the whole strand","A short DNA piece made by ligase, which is then left permanently in place"],"correct":2,"why":"The point here is that the starter is RNA and not DNA, and that is not an accident. Primase can begin a chain from nothing, polymerase cannot, so the RNA primer hands polymerase the free 3' hydroxyl it needs. The RNA is later excised, the gap filled with DNA, and the nick sealed. If a question mentions something that is put down during replication and then removed and replaced, it is the primer.","traps":["RNA polymerase II transcribes messenger RNA, and splicing removes introns from a transcript, which is a separate process from replication.","Helicase unwinds DNA and makes no starter of any kind, and polymerase extends nucleic acid rather than protein.",null,"Telomerase does extend DNA, but only at chromosome ends and only from its own RNA template, so it is not what starts an ordinary fragment.","Ligase joins pieces of DNA that already exist and cannot build a new one, and a DNA starter would not need removing in the first place."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g021","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Which description best fits an individual with Turner syndrome (45,X)?","opts":["Phenotypically female, with one Barr body in each cell","Phenotypically male","Phenotypically female; sterile, short stature","Phenotypically male; tall","Phenotypically female; tall"],"correct":2,"why":"Turner syndrome is a single X with no second sex chromosome, and without a Y there is no signal to build male structures, so the individual is phenotypically female. The exam features worth carrying are short stature, non-functional ovaries with infertility, and a webbed neck. The rule underneath is that the Y, not the number of X chromosomes, decides male development.","traps":["A 45,X cell has only one X and that single X stays active, so there is no inactivated X to form a Barr body.","Male development requires the Y chromosome, and a 45,X karyotype has none.",null,"This combines two features that do not apply here, since there is no Y to direct male development and stature is reduced rather than increased.","Tall stature points toward the extra X of triple X or the extra chromosome of Klinefelter, while Turner syndrome is characteristically short."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-4nakyd","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Both C4 and CAM plants keep carbon dioxide concentrated around RuBisCO. What separates the two strategies?","opts":["C4 separates the steps by place, CAM separates them by time","C4 plants use PEP carboxylase, CAM plants use RuBisCO alone","C4 plants close their stomata at night, CAM plants close theirs at noon","C4 skips RuBisCO entirely, while CAM still relies on it","C4 separates them by time of day, CAM separates them by place in the leaf"],"correct":0,"why":"Anchor each one to a picture. Corn is C4: two cell types side by side, so the separation is spatial. A cactus is CAM: one cell doing two jobs at different hours, so the separation is temporal, and cactus and clock both starting with C is a workable hook.","traps":[null,"Both pathways use PEP carboxylase for that first capture.","C4 plants open their stomata by day like most plants, and CAM plants keep theirs shut through the heat of the day.","Both pathways rely on RuBisCO for the actual Calvin cycle, and the extra enzyme only concentrates carbon first.","This is the reversal to watch for, since the cactus is the one working on a clock and the corn is the one working in two different cells."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-v3v9kk","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":1,"q":"A cell spends most of its life in interphase, which is divided into three phases, and then divides. Which sequence puts the interphase stages in order and correctly names the one in which the DNA is copied?","opts":["G1, then G2, then S, with the DNA copied during S","G1, then S, then G2, with the DNA copied during S","G0, then G1, then G2, with the DNA copied during G2","S, then G1, then G2, with the DNA copied during S","G1, then S, then G2, with the DNA copied during G2"],"correct":1,"why":"The names carry the answer. G1 and G2 are gaps, and they are numbered by which side of S phase they sit on, so the order can only be G1, S, G2. Synthesis here means DNA synthesis, so replication happens in S and nowhere else. G2 is when the cell grows and verifies the copy before it commits to mitosis.","traps":["The two gap phases are named for where they sit around S phase, so G2 cannot come before it.",null,"G0 is not a stage every cell passes through, it is a resting state that some cells step into out of G1.","This puts synthesis before the first gap, and a cell that has just divided needs to grow and check itself before it commits to copying its DNA.","The order is right but the copying is not, since G2 is the gap after replication, used for growth and for checking the copied DNA."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g023","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Red-green color blindness is X-linked recessive. A colorblind man has children with a woman who has normal color vision and is not a carrier. What percentage of their sons is expected to be colorblind?","opts":["0%","25%","50%","75%","100%"],"correct":0,"why":"Track the X chromosomes and the question answers itself. A son receives his single X from his mother and a Y from his father, so the father's affected X never reaches a son at all. This mother carries two normal alleles, so every son gets a normal X and normal color vision. The daughters are the interesting ones: each gets the father's affected X plus a normal X from the mother, making them unaffected carriers.","traps":[null,"One box out of four looks like a tidy Punnett square answer, but the affected allele is not present in the mother at all.","Half the sons being affected would require the mother to be a carrier so that she could pass an affected X to about half of them.","Three quarters of the sons could only be affected if the mother carried the affected allele, and she carries two normal ones.","This assumes the father hands his X chromosome to his sons, when in fact sons receive his Y."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sw-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"Four 0.10 M aqueous acid solutions at 25 °C are tested: HCl (strong), HF (Ka = 6.6x10^(-4)), HCN (Ka = 4.9x10^(-10)), and CH3COOH (Ka = 1.8x10^(-5)). Considering that pH depends on the extent of dissociation, which solution has the LOWEST pH?","opts":["All equal","HCN","CH3COOH","HF","HCl"],"correct":4,"why":"HCl is the only strong acid here; it fully dissociates to give [H+] = 0.10 M and pH = 1.0. The weak acids dissociate only partially, giving higher pH (HF ~ 2.1, CH3COOH ~ 3.4, HCN ~ 5.2).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:lw-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"In ClF₃, central Cl has how many lone pairs?","opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":2,"why":"Cl has 7 valence; 3 Cl−F bonds use 3, leaving 4 e⁻ = 2 LPs (T-shape geometry, expanded octet).","traps":["With no lone pairs the three bonds would spread into a flat triangle, and ClF₃ is T shaped, which is the tell that pairs are crowding it.","One lone pair comes from holding chlorine to a strict octet, and chlorine is period 3, so it can carry ten electrons here.",null,"Three lone pairs plus three bonds would need twelve electrons around chlorine, two more than it has.","Four leftover electrons make two pairs rather than four, since the leftover count still has to be halved."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_nr-q2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"A concentration cell at 25°C uses Cu electrodes in two Cu^2+ solutions: [Cu^2+] = 0.10 M (left) and 1.0 M (right). Both half-cells share E° = 0 (same reaction) so E_cell is determined entirely by the concentration difference via E = -(0.0592/n) log([dilute]/[concentrated]) with n = 2. Find E_cell.","opts":["0.25 V","0.059 V","0 V","0.030 V","0.118 V"],"correct":3,"why":"E_cell = (0.0592/2) log(1.0/0.10) = 0.0296 x 1 = 0.0296 V ~ 0.030 V. Concentration cells extract free energy from a concentration gradient until the two cells equalize.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A 0.20 m aqueous KCl solution (i = 2 for full dissociation) has Kb = 0.52°C·kg/mol. Pure water boils at 100.00°C at 1 atm. Using ΔTb = i·Kb·m, what is the boiling point of this solution?","opts":["100.21°C","100.10°C","100.00°C","101.04°C","100.52°C"],"correct":0,"why":"ΔTb = (2)(0.52)(0.20) = 0.208°C ≈ 0.21°C. New Tb = 100.00 + 0.21 = 100.21°C. Ignoring i = 2 gives 100.10; using i = 3 gives 100.31; the 1 m result (100.52) is the wrong-molality distractor.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sf-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"A laboratory report adds three independently measured volumes: 1.234 mL (4 sig figs), 5.6 mL (2 sig figs), and 12.34 mL (4 sig figs). The calculator returns 19.174. Apply the addition rule (smallest decimal places) to round the sum to its proper precision.","opts":["19","19.17","19.174","19.2","19.20"],"correct":3,"why":"Decimal places: 1.234 (3), 5.6 (1), 12.34 (2). The sum keeps the smallest = 1 decimal place. 19.174 rounds to 19.2.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ks-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Silver chromate (Ag₂CrO₄) dissolves slightly in pure water according to Ag₂CrO₄(s) ⇌ 2 Ag⁺(aq) + CrO₄²⁻(aq). Given K_sp = 1.1 × 10⁻¹² at 25°C, calculate the molar solubility s (mol/L) of Ag₂CrO₄ in pure water.","opts":["1.0 × 10⁻⁶","6.5 × 10⁻⁵","1.1 × 10⁻¹²","3.3 × 10⁻⁴","2.2 × 10⁻¹²"],"correct":1,"why":"If s = solubility, then [Ag⁺] = 2s and [CrO₄²⁻] = s, so K_sp = (2s)²(s) = 4s³. Solving: s³ = K_sp/4 = 2.75×10⁻¹³, s = (2.75×10⁻¹³)^(1/3) ≈ 6.5×10⁻⁵ M. Forgetting the (2s)² stoichiometric factor gives the much smaller 1.0×10⁻⁶ trap.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_xp009","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"10.0 g of N₂ and 4.00 g of H₂ are placed in a sealed reactor and heated until the reaction N₂(g) + 3 H₂(g) → 2 NH₃(g) goes to completion. Identify the limiting reagent and calculate the maximum mass of NH₃ formed.","opts":["H₂ limits; 22.4 g NH₃","Both consumed simultaneously; 14.0 g NH₃","N₂ limits; 12.1 g NH₃","H₂ limits; 11.3 g NH₃","N₂ limits; 17.0 g NH₃"],"correct":2,"why":"mol N₂ = 10/28 = 0.357. mol H₂ = 4/2 = 2.00. Stoichiometric ratio H₂:N₂ = 3:1, so H₂ needed for all N₂ = 3 × 0.357 = 1.07 mol < 2.00 available. N₂ limits, so mol NH₃ = 2 × 0.357 = 0.714, so mass = 0.714 × 17 = 12.1 g.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_en-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"Given ΔHf° (kJ/mol): CO2(g) = −394, H2O(g) = −242, CH3OH(l) = −239, and O2(g) = 0, calculate the standard enthalpy of combustion for CH3OH(l) + 1.5 O2(g) -> CO2(g) + 2 H2O(g) at 25 °C.","opts":["−639 kJ","−717 kJ","−400 kJ","+1117 kJ","+639 kJ"],"correct":0,"why":"ΔH_rxn = sum(ΔHf° products) − sum(ΔHf° reactants) = [−394 + 2(−242)] − [−239 + 1.5(0)] = −878 − (−239) = −639 kJ. Sign errors on reactants give −1117 or +639.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-e9","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Naturally occurring chlorine consists of two isotopes: ³⁵Cl with mass 34.97 amu at 75.0% abundance and ³⁷Cl with mass 36.97 amu at 25.0% abundance. Using the weighted-average formula AW = Σ(fᵢ · mᵢ), what is the average atomic mass of Cl reported on the periodic table?","opts":["36.5 amu","35.5 amu","35.0 amu","37.0 amu","36.0 amu"],"correct":1,"why":"Average atomic mass = (0.750 × 35) + (0.250 × 37) = 26.25 + 9.25 = 35.5 amu. The 75/25 abundance ratio places Cl's average mass closer to 35 (the more abundant) but not exactly at 35.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#12tb68e","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"What is the pH of a 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ M solution of Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C?","opts":["3","3.3","10","10.7","11"],"correct":4,"why":"[OH⁻] = 2 × 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.0 × 10⁻³ M. pOH = 3, pH = 14 − 3 = 11.","traps":["That is the pOH. Finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","That is the pOH of the undoubled concentration. Double [OH⁻], then subtract the pOH from 14.","You treated the concentration as 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ. Doubling 5.0 × 10⁻ⁿ moves you up a power of ten.","You forgot that Ba(OH)₂ releases TWO hydroxides. Double the concentration before taking the log.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-combined-gas#1hwah0q","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A gas occupies 6 L at 3 atm and 127 °C. What is its volume at 2 atm and 227 °C?","opts":["5 L","7.2 L","16.1 L","11.3 L","9 L"],"correct":3,"why":"P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2 in Kelvin. V2 = 6 × (3/2) × (500/400) = 11.3 L.","traps":["Pressure ratio is upside down. More pressure squeezes the gas, so volume moves opposite to pressure.","Temperature ratio is upside down. Hotter gas expands, so volume moves with temperature.","You used Celsius in the ratio. Convert both temperatures to Kelvin first.",null,"You ignored the temperature change. Both P and T changed here."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bf7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A physiology buffer contains 0.10 M HA and 0.20 M A- in 1.0 L of solution (pKa = 4.74). The buffer capacity for added strong acid is limited by the moles of A- available before all conjugate base is converted back to HA. Approximately how many mol/L of strong acid (H+) can be added before the buffer is exhausted?","opts":["~1.0 mol/L","~0.05 mol/L","~0.10 mol/L","~0.30 mol/L","~0.20 mol/L"],"correct":4,"why":"Each mole of added H+ converts one mole of A- to HA. Buffering ends when [A-] reaches zero, so the maximum H+ that can be neutralized equals the initial moles of A-, ~0.20 mol/L. Beyond that, pH crashes.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_m7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Combustion analysis of an unknown carbohydrate yields mass percentages of 40.0% C, 6.7% H, and 53.3% O. Assuming a 100 g sample (40.0 g C, 6.7 g H, 53.3 g O) and dividing each by atomic mass to find moles, what is the empirical formula?","opts":["C₃H₆O₃","CH₂O","CHO","C₆H₁₂O₆","C₂H₄O₂"],"correct":1,"why":"Moles: C 40.0/12 = 3.33; H 6.7/1 = 6.7; O 53.3/16 = 3.33. Dividing by smallest (3.33) gives 1:2:1 → CH₂O. C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose) is the molecular formula but not the empirical formula; the empirical formula is the simplest whole-number ratio.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"Pure water at 25°C has vapor pressure P° = 23.8 torr. A NaCl solution is prepared so the mole fraction of solvent water is chi_solvent = 0.90 (treating NaCl as a non-volatile, non-dissociating solute for this calculation). Apply Raoult's law P_solution = chi_solvent · P° to find the new vapor pressure.","opts":["19.0 torr","23.8 torr","21.4 torr","2.4 torr","26.4 torr"],"correct":2,"why":"P_solution = 0.90 x 23.8 = 21.42 torr ~ 21.4 torr. The 2.4 torr drop is the colligative vapor-pressure lowering. (A real NaCl solution would dissociate into 2 ions per formula unit, doubling the apparent solute fraction.)","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#1mruwsv","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 1 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 27 °C and 1 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["36.9 L","12.3 L","2.22 L","2490 L","24.6 L"],"correct":4,"why":"T = 27 + 273 = 300 K. V = nRT / P = (1)(0.0821)(300) / 1 = 24.6 L.","traps":["Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273.","You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-limiting#5o2mox","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"2 Na + Cl₂ → 2 NaCl\n\nIf 6 mol of Na reacts with 2.5 mol of Cl₂, how many moles of NaCl can form?","opts":["4 mol","5 mol","6 mol","2.5 mol","8.5 mol"],"correct":1,"why":"Na: 6 / 2 = 3. Cl₂: 2.5 / 1 = 2.5. The smaller one limits. Product = 2.5 × 2 = 5 mol.","traps":["Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.",null,"You built the answer from the reagent in EXCESS. The smaller moles-per-coefficient runs out first and sets the yield.","You compared raw moles. Divide each by its coefficient first, then pick the smaller.","You added the reactants. Moles are not conserved; use the mole ratio from the balanced equation."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ic-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A weak acid HA dissociates as HA(aq) ⇌ H+(aq) + A-(aq) at 25 °C, with Ka = 1.0x10^(-4) and [HA]_0 = 0.05 M, [H+]_0 ≈ 0, [A-]_0 = 0. Using the small-x approximation in an ICE table, calculate the equilibrium [H+].","opts":["2.2x10^(-3) M","5.0x10^(-3) M","1.0x10^(-2) M","7.1x10^(-4) M","1.4x10^(-3) M"],"correct":0,"why":"Ka = x^2 / (0.05 − x) ≈ x^2 / 0.05. So x^2 = (1.0x10^(-4))(0.05) = 5.0x10^(-6); x = sqrt(5.0x10^(-6)) ≈ 2.24x10^(-3) M. Forgetting to multiply by [HA]_0 gives 1x10^(-2).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:sr5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"You mix aqueous solutions and want to confirm sulfate (SO₄²⁻) is present in an unknown. Which cation should you add to make sulfate precipitate cleanly?","opts":["Ba²⁺","Mg²⁺","NH₄⁺","Na⁺","K⁺"],"correct":0,"why":"The classic sulfate confirmation: Ba²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ → BaSO₄(s), a white precipitate. Ba²⁺ is one of the sulfate exceptions to the \"sulfates are soluble\" rule. Na⁺, NH₄⁺, and K⁺ all form fully soluble sulfates (Group 1 + ammonium), so they cannot precipitate sulfate.","traps":[null,"MgSO₄ is soluble, which is why Epsom salts dissolve in a bath; a 2+ charge by itself does not precipitate sulfate.","(NH₄)₂SO₄ is soluble - ammonium sulfates are always soluble. No precipitate.","Na₂SO₄ is soluble - Group 1 sulfates are always soluble. No precipitate.","K₂SO₄ is soluble - same Group 1 rule. To precipitate sulfate you need Ba²⁺, Pb²⁺, or Sr²⁺."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:lr5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, starting from 28 g N₂ and 9 g H₂. How much H₂ is left over?","opts":["0 mol","1.5 mol","3 mol","0.5 mol","4.5 mol"],"correct":1,"why":"Used 1×3=3 mol H₂. Excess=4.5−3=1.5 mol.","traps":["0 mol would be right if hydrogen were limiting, but the nitrogen runs out first and leaves hydrogen behind.",null,"3 mol is the hydrogen consumed, not the hydrogen left over.","0.5 mol divides the 1.5 mol leftover by the coefficient 3 a second time, and the subtraction already handled that.","4.5 mol is what was there at the start, before 3 mol reacted away."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:x8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Rate doubles when temperature rises from 300 K to 310 K. Qualitatively, what does this tell you about the activation energy?","opts":["The 10 K doubling reports ΔH, not Eₐ","Eₐ is very small (a few kJ/mol)","Eₐ is moderate (around 50-60 kJ/mol)","Eₐ is large (>200 kJ/mol)","Eₐ cannot be inferred from rate vs T data"],"correct":2,"why":"The \"rate doubles per 10 K rise\" rule of thumb corresponds to a moderate Eₐ around 50-60 kJ/mol for most organic reactions near room temperature. Plugging into Arrhenius (ln 2 = Eₐ/R · ΔT/(T₁T₂)) gives Eₐ ≈ 54 kJ/mol, but the qualitative DAT version is \"rate doubles per 10 K, so moderate Eₐ.\" Very small Eₐ would give a much weaker T sensitivity; very large Eₐ would multiply rate by far more than 2.","traps":["ΔH is the energy gap between reactants and products, while the temperature sensitivity of the rate reports on the barrier Eₐ.","A few kJ/mol gives almost no temperature dependence - rate would barely change.",null,">200 kJ/mol would make rate jump by orders of magnitude per 10 K, not just double.","Eₐ can absolutely be inferred from rate vs T via Arrhenius. That is the whole point of the equation."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#qrgh47","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.0231 min⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["30 min","0.0333 min","43.3 min","86.6 min","0.016 min"],"correct":0,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.0231 = 30 min. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":[null,"Upside down. t½ = 0.693 / k, not k / 0.693.","That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k.","You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693.","You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_xp006","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A 2.50 L container holds N₂ at 1.00 atm and 25 °C. The container is sealed and the temperature is increased to 100 °C. Simultaneously, an additional 0.020 mol of N₂ is added (no leaks). What is the new pressure?","opts":["2.49 atm","4.00 atm","0.75 atm","1.50 atm","5.50 atm"],"correct":3,"why":"Initial: PV=nRT, so n = PV/RT = (1.00)(2.50)/(0.0821 × 298) = 0.1023 mol. Add 0.020 mol, so n_new = 0.1223 mol. New T = 373 K. P = nRT/V = (0.1223)(0.0821)(373)/2.50 ≈ 1.50 atm. (The +0.020 mol contributes substantially despite the higher T.) Re-check: actually 0.1223 × 0.0821 × 373 / 2.5 = 1.498 atm - answer is 1.50 atm.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"originals:ab-5f59e73687","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A gas-phase reaction is at equilibrium in a rigid container at constant temperature, and more of one reactant gas is suddenly added. Immediately afterward, how does Q compare with K, and which way does the reaction run?","opts":["Q is greater than K, so the reaction runs toward the products","Q is less than K, so the reaction runs toward the reactants","Q is greater than K, so the reaction runs toward the reactants","Q is less than K, so the reaction runs toward the products","Q equals K, so nothing happens"],"correct":3,"why":"Q is products over reactants. Add reactant and the bottom grows, so Q falls below K. The reaction runs forward, making products, until Q climbs back up to K.","traps":["If Q were above K the reaction would run backward.","When Q is below K there is too little product, so the reaction makes more product.","Reactants are in the DENOMINATOR of Q. Adding reactant makes Q smaller.",null,"The addition disturbed the system, so Q no longer equals K."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_v-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Among the molecules CO₂ (linear), BF₃ (trigonal planar), CH₂Cl₂ (tetrahedral), and CCl₄ (tetrahedral), each contains polar bonds. Yet only some are net polar molecules. Which of these molecules has individual polar bonds AND a non-zero net dipole moment?","opts":["CH₂Cl₂","CO₂","All are nonpolar","CCl₄","BF₃"],"correct":0,"why":"Symmetric arrangements (CO₂ linear, BF₃ trig planar, CCl₄ tetrahedral with identical substituents) cause bond dipoles to cancel - net nonpolar. CH₂Cl₂ has two different substituents (2 H and 2 Cl) on a tetrahedral C, so the dipoles do NOT cancel - net polar.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#y96ihy","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 36 g of H₂O? (molar mass 18 g/mol)","opts":["2.17 × 10²⁵","4","6.02 × 10²³","2.41 × 10²⁴","1.2 × 10²⁴"],"correct":3,"why":"36 g / 18 g/mol = 2 mol H₂O. Times 2 hydrogen per molecule = 4 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 2.41 × 10²⁴.","traps":["You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.","That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them.","You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.",null,"That is the number of MOLECULES. Each H₂O holds 2 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 2."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lw-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"The chlorate ion ClO3- is drawn for a Lewis structure exercise. Cl contributes 7 valence electrons, each O contributes 6, and the -1 charge adds 1 more electron. How many total valence electrons must appear in the completed structure?","opts":["24","25","26","28","32"],"correct":2,"why":"Total valence e- = 7 (Cl) + 3 x 6 (O) + 1 (charge) = 7 + 18 + 1 = 26. Distribute these as bonds and lone pairs to satisfy octets (with formal-charge minimization on Cl).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_bl3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Consider HCl ionizing in water (Bronsted) and BF3 accepting NH3's lone pair to form H3N-BF3 (Lewis). Comparing the two definitions: a Bronsted acid donates a proton (which has an empty 1s orbital), while a Lewis acid accepts an electron pair. Which statement correctly describes the relationship between Bronsted and Lewis acid categories?","opts":["Every Lewis acid is also a Bronsted acid","The two categories are mutually exclusive","Only Lewis theory applies to organic solvents","Only Bronsted theory applies in water","Every Bronsted acid is also a Lewis acid"],"correct":4,"why":"Donating an H+ is equivalent to having a proton accept an electron pair from the base, so all Bronsted acids meet the Lewis criterion. The reverse fails: Lewis acids like BF3 or AlCl3 contain no proton to donate. Lewis is the broader category and includes Bronsted as a subset.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gb-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A reaction has ΔH = +40.0 kJ/mol and ΔS = +100. J/(mol·K), with both values approximately temperature-independent. Setting ΔG = ΔH − T·ΔS = 0 to find the crossover temperature above which the reaction becomes spontaneous, what is T_cross?","opts":["1000 K","400 K","300 K","200 K","500 K"],"correct":1,"why":"Set ΔG = 0: T = ΔH/ΔS = 40.0 kJ / 0.100 kJ/K = 400 K. Above 400 K the reaction is spontaneous. Forgetting J->kJ conversion (using 100 directly) gives 0.4 K.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:nr-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"For Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, [Cu²⁺] = 0.001 M, n = 2, E° = +0.34 V. Find E.","opts":["0.43 V","0.16 V","0.34 V","0.31 V","0.25 V"],"correct":4,"why":"Q = 1/[Cu²⁺] = 1000. E = 0.34 − (0.0296)(3) = 0.34 − 0.0888 ≈ 0.25 V.","traps":["0.43 V flips the sign of the log term: Q is 1/[Cu²⁺] = 1000, so log Q is +3 and the correction comes off E°.","0.16 V uses the full 0.0592 instead of 0.0592 divided by the 2 electrons, so the correction is twice as large as it should be.","0.34 V is the standard-state value, which only holds when Cu²⁺ sits at 1 M.","0.31 V subtracts the 0.0296 term once and leaves out the log Q = 3 multiplier.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:cal7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Why use Styrofoam in a coffee-cup calorimeter rather than glass or metal?","opts":["It keeps the solution from evaporating during the run","Easier to seal","Insulator - minimizes heat lost to surroundings","Reacts less with hot solutions","Cheaper"],"correct":2,"why":"Calorimetry assumes the system is isolated (q_lost = q_gained inside). Styrofoam is a poor thermal conductor, so minimal heat exchange with the surroundings, so measured ΔT reflects only the chemistry, not heat sink to the cup.","traps":["An open cup does not stop evaporation; what foam does is refuse to conduct heat out to the room.","A coffee-cup calorimeter sits open to the air, so sealing is not the point.",null,"Foam is fairly inert, but so is glass, and inertness is not what keeps the heat in.","Foam cups are cheap, but cost is not what makes the measurement trustworthy."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-1fquwbw","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"(R)-2-bromobutane (shown) is treated with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMSO. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["C[C@H](C#N)CC","C[C@@H](C#N)CC","C[C@@H](O)CC","C[C@H](Br)CC","CCC(C)C#N"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary substrate + strong CN- in polar aprotic DMSO = SN2 with backside inversion. 3D inversion of (R)-2-bromobutane gives canonical CC[C@H](C)C#N = the (S)-nitrile (option A). The descriptor flips R to S because CN outranks ethyl; option B is retention, option C is achiral/flat, option D is unreacted, option E is wrong nucleophile.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H](Br)CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-jpxwdt","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":1,"q":"A student claims the carbonyl carbon of methyl vinyl ketone (but-3-en-2-one, drawn below) is sp3 with ~109.5 degree angles. Which statement is correct?","opts":["Incorrect; the carbonyl carbon is sp2 but its angles are ~109.5 degrees","Correct; the carbonyl carbon is sp2 but its angles are ~180 degrees","Correct; the carbonyl carbon is sp3 with ~109.5 degree bond angles","Incorrect; the carbonyl carbon is sp2 with ~120 degree bond angles","Incorrect; the carbonyl carbon is sp with ~180 degree bond angles"],"correct":3,"why":"The carbonyl carbon has three sigma regions (vinyl, methyl, plus C=O) = three electron regions = sp2, trigonal planar, ~120 degrees. A double-bonded carbon is never sp3. Option 4 pairs right hybridization with wrong angle.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC(C)=O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-146gkua","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Cyclopentene oxide (drawn) is treated with sodium azide (NaN3) in DMF. Which structure correctly shows the major product, including its stereochemistry?","opts":["O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","OC1CCC(O)C1","[N-]=[N+]=NC1CCCC1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]"],"correct":1,"why":"Azide opens the epoxide by anti (backside) SN2, placing azide and OH trans: trans-2-azidocyclopentanol. C is a diol (no nitrogen), D has no hydroxyl (ring not opened to an alcohol), E is a six-membered ring (wrong ring size).","traps":null,"qs":"C1CC2OC2C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1x7pi97","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"An unknown gives a 1H NMR with three signals: a 3H singlet near 2.5 ppm and two 2H doublets in the aromatic region (~7.4 and ~8.1 ppm). Of the two candidate structures, the one drawn is p-nitrotoluene; the contrast candidate is tert-butanol (CC(C)(C)O). Which structure is consistent with the spectrum?","opts":["The drawn structure (p-nitrotoluene): CH3 singlet plus two aromatic doublets from the para ring","tert-Butanol, because its 9H singlet matches the 3H methyl peak","Neither structure fits, because p-nitrotoluene is para-disubstituted and would give four separate aromatic signals rather than two doublets","tert-Butanol, because it produces two aromatic doublets","The drawn structure (p-nitrotoluene), except that its methyl signal must integrate for 9H rather than 3H against the two aromatic doublets"],"correct":0,"why":"p-Nitrotoluene has a benzylic CH3 (3H singlet, ~2.5 ppm) and a para-disubstituted ring giving an AA'BB' pattern that reads as two 2H doublets in the aromatic region (the H ortho to NO2 shifted downfield ~8.1 ppm). tert-Butanol has no aromatic H and would show only a 9H singlet plus OH, so it cannot match.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-2iy0wn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"The alkene shown is converted into 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (the tertiary alcohol, with OH on the ring carbon that bears the methyl group). Which set of reagents accomplishes this transformation?","opts":["BH3-THF, then H2O2 with aqueous NaOH (anti-Markovnikov hydration)","1. OsO4; 2. NaHSO3","Dilute H2SO4, H2O (acid-catalyzed hydration)","1. O3; 2. Zn, H2O (reductive ozonolysis that cleaves the double bond)","Br2 in CCl4"],"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration (and oxymercuration) follow Markovnikov: water adds to the more substituted carbon, giving the tertiary alcohol via the more stable tertiary carbocation/mercurinium opening. BH3 then H2O2/NaOH is anti-Markovnikov and would give the secondary alcohol. The other reagents do not install a single OH.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1raxpuc","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,3-dibromopropane (BrCH2CH2CH2Br), then heated in aqueous acid. The dibromide drawn is the alkylating agent. Which structure is the major final product?","opts":["O=C(O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCCC(C)=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Double intramolecular alkylation of the acetoacetate carbon by the 1,3-dibromide closes a ring: the central carbon plus the three-carbon tether forms a four-membered (cyclobutane) ring still bearing the acetyl and ester. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation leave the methyl ketone, giving 1-cyclobutylethanone/acetylcyclobutane (ring size 4 confirmed). The cyclopropyl ketone (option B, 2-C tether) and cyclopentyl ketone (option C, 4-C tether) are the wrong ring size; the open diketone (option D) skips cyclization; cyclobutanecarboxylic acid (option E) is the acid class (malonic route). Verified correct=0.","traps":null,"qs":"BrCCCBr","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1k54mp1","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"(S)-3-chloro-3-methylhexane (drawn, a tertiary chloride) is stirred in methanol (CH3OH) at room temperature. Which structure best represents the substitution product?","opts":["CCC[C@](C)(CC)OC","CCCC(C)(O)CC","CCCC(C)(CC)OC","CC=C(C)CCC","CCC[C@@](C)(CC)OC"],"correct":2,"why":"Tertiary substrate + weak neutral nucleophile (MeOH) + polar protic = SN1 via planar carbocation, attacked from both faces = racemic. option D is the alcohol (wrong nucleophile), option E an elimination alkene.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC[C@@](C)(Cl)CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1juifa8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"The molecule below is 2-bromo-3-chlorobutane drawn with both stereocenters defined. Assign the (C2, C3) configuration. (Number from the bromine-bearing carbon as C2.)","opts":["(2R, 3S)","(2S, 3S) - and the molecule is meso","(2S, 3R)","(2R, 3R)","(2R, 3R) - and the molecule is meso"],"correct":3,"why":"(2R,3R). It is not meso because the two ends carry different halogens (Br vs Cl), so no internal mirror plane. Keyed correct=0 is right; the meso distractors are good traps.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Cl)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-2sknr7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":1,"q":"Cyclopentanone is treated with KCN and aqueous HCl. Predict the major product.","opts":["N#CC1CCCC1","N#CC1(O)CCCCC1","OC1CCCC1","N#CC1(N)CCCC1","N#CC1(O)CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to the ketone followed by protonation gives the cyclopentanone cyanohydrin (a quaternary carbon bearing both OH and CN). The nitrile without OH, the aminonitrile, the simple alcohol (reduction), and the six-membered ring (wrong skeleton) are incorrect.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-qoccz7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"Furan is shown below. The oxygen bears two lone pairs. How many pi electrons does the aromatic system contain, and where do they come from?","opts":["6 pi electrons, but they come entirely from three C=C bonds within the ring","6 pi electrons: 4 from the two C=C bonds plus 2 from ONE oxygen lone pair in a p orbital; the other O lone pair stays in an sp2 in-plane orbital","4 pi electrons from the two C=C bonds only; the oxygen lone pairs are not involved","8 pi electrons: 4 from the two C=C bonds plus 4 from both oxygen lone pairs held in p orbitals, which pushes the ring past the sextet and makes furan antiaromatic rather than aromatic","6 pi electrons: 4 from the two C=C bonds plus 2 contributed jointly by BOTH oxygen lone pairs, one electron from each"],"correct":1,"why":"Furan O is sp2: one lone pair in a p orbital donates 2 e-, the other stays in-plane. option E is impossible (furan has only two ring C=C, not three). correct=0.","traps":null,"qs":"c1ccoc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-duu60b","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"5-Aminopentanal (drawn) is treated with NaBH3CN under mildly acidic conditions, where the molecule undergoes INTRAMOLECULAR reductive amination. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["C1CCNC1","C1CCNCC1","CCCCC=N","C1CCCNCC1","NCCCCCO"],"correct":1,"why":"The -NH2 and aldehyde condense to a cyclic iminium reduced to a saturated cyclic amine. Distractors: pyrrolidine (5-membered) and azepane (7-membered) wrong ring sizes, the acyclic amino alcohol, and an acyclic imine that also lost a carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"O=CCCCCN","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-2335q9","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with O3, then Zn/AcOH (reductive workup). Which structure is the product?","opts":["CC1CCCCC1=O","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","O=CCCCCC=O","CC(O)CCCCCO","CC(=O)CCCCC=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the endocyclic C=C and OPENS the ring to an acyclic chain. Reductive (Zn/AcOH) workup makes the more-substituted (methyl-bearing) carbon a methyl ketone and the CH carbon an aldehyde: 6-oxoheptanal (CC(=O)CCCCC=O, C7H12O2). Option 2 omits the methyl, option 3 is oxidative over-oxidation to an acid, option 4 is a diol, and option 5 wrongly keeps the ring intact (impossible for an endocyclic alkene). Index 0 correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-14m1cd6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"5-Hydroxypentanoic acid (drawn) is warmed with a trace of acid. Considering ring-size preference for intramolecular esterification, which lactone forms preferentially?","opts":["O=C1CCCCO1","O=C1CCCCCO1","O=C1CCCC1","O=C1CCCO1","O=C(O)CCCCOC(=O)CCCCO"],"correct":0,"why":"Keyed option A is the 6-membered delta-valerolactone (C5H8O2, ring size 6 confirmed), the favored product from a 5-hydroxy acid. The 5-membered option B (C4) would need a 4-hydroxy acid, option C is 7-membered, option E is linear dimeric ester, option 5 is a cyclopentanone (not a lactone).","traps":null,"qs":"OCCCCC(=O)O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-5m4ttp","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"(E)-1-phenylpropene (shown) undergoes O3 followed by H2O2 (oxidative workup). What are the two products?","opts":["Benzaldehyde and acetaldehyde","Acetophenone and formic acid","Benzoic acid and CO2","Benzoic acid and acetic acid","Benzaldehyde and acetic acid"],"correct":3,"why":"Each alkene carbon bears one hydrogen. Oxidative workup (H2O2) converts both CH carbons to carboxylic acids: the ring side gives benzoic acid and the methyl side gives acetic acid. Reductive workup would give benzaldehyde + acetaldehyde. Neither carbon is a terminal =CH2, so no CO2 is lost.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fiyxzm","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"The alkyne shown is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst (Pd poisoned with lead, quinoline). Which best describes the alkene product that forms?","opts":["trans-2-butene (the E alkene)","cis-2-butene (the Z alkene)","An equal mix of cis- and trans-2-butene","Butane (full reduction to the alkane)","A racemic mixture of 2-butanol"],"correct":1,"why":"2-butyne + H2/Lindlar gives syn addition that stops at the alkene, producing cis (Z)-2-butene. Lindlar does not over-reduce, and dissolving-metal would be needed for trans.","traps":null,"qs":"CC#CC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1gntirq","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"(2S,3S)-2,3-dimethyloxirane (a single enantiomer of trans-2,3-epoxybutane) is treated with dilute aqueous acid (H3O+). What is the major product, including stereochemistry?","opts":["CCC(O)CO","CC(O)C(C)O","C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","C[C@H](O)[C@H](C)O","C[C@@H](O)[C@H](C)O"],"correct":4,"why":"Acid-catalyzed anti (backside) opening inverts one stereocenter. From (2S,3S) trans-epoxide, inverting one center gives (2R,3S) = meso-2,3-butanediol. Options 1/2 are the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) diols.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H]1O[C@H]1C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-15vr1kg","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nCH3CH2CH2CH2-Br + NaCN (in DMSO)\n\nWhich structure is formed?","opts":["CCCCOC#N","CCCCC#N","C=CCC","CCCC#N","C=NCCCC"],"correct":1,"why":"Cyanide is a good nucleophile and a primary alkyl halide in a polar aprotic solvent favors SN2. The carbon of CN attacks, displacing bromide, giving pentanenitrile (CCCCC#N), one carbon longer. Distractors: isonitrile (N-attack, minor), one-carbon-short skeleton, a cyanate, and the (disfavored) elimination alkene.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCCBr","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-b7gq59","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated sequentially: (1) NaOEt then CH3I; (2) NaOEt then CH3CH2I; then (3) dilute H2SO4 and heat. What is the major product?","opts":["CCC(CC)C(C)=O","CCC(C)C(=O)CC(C)=O","CC(=O)CC(C)C","CCCCC(C)=O","CCC(C)C(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Sequential methylation then ethylation puts a methyl and an ethyl on the central carbon; hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives CH3CO-CH(CH3)-CH2CH3 = 3-methyl-2-pentanone. opt[0] matches. Distractors: 2-hexanone (one alkylation), non-decarboxylated diketone, 3-ethyl-2-pentanone (wrong group, two ethyls), and 4-methyl-2-pentanone (wrong substitution position).","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1o3v4s0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"1-Hexyne (shown) is treated with NaNH2, then with 1-bromopropane, and finally with Na in liquid NH3. Which statement correctly describes the final product?","opts":["trans-2-nonene, an internal E alkene","4-nonyne, an internal alkyne (no reduction occurs)","1-nonyne, a terminal alkyne","trans-4-nonene, an internal E alkene","cis-4-nonene, an internal Z alkene"],"correct":3,"why":"Acetylide alkylation of 1-hexyne with 1-bromopropane gives 4-nonyne; Na/NH3 then reduces that internal alkyne by anti addition to trans-4-nonene. Numbering: propyl(C3)-C#C-butyl(C4) places the bond at C4.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CCCCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1nqrfbl","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"The alkene shown (1-ethylcyclohexene) is treated with BH3-THF followed by H2O2/NaOH. Considering BOTH regiochemistry (anti-Markovnikov) and stereochemistry (syn addition), which description matches the major product?","opts":["OH adds to the less substituted ring carbon (the CH), and H and OH add to the same face (syn), giving trans-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol","The ethyl group is oxidized to a carboxylic acid","OH adds to the more substituted ring carbon that bears the ethyl group (Markovnikov), with H and OH delivered to the same face of the ring","Two OH groups add cis across the double bond","OH adds to the less substituted ring carbon, but H and OH add to opposite faces of the ring (anti), giving cis-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol"],"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov (OH to the less substituted ring CH, not the carbon bearing the ethyl) and syn (H and OH add to the same face). On the ring, syn addition of H and OH places the new OH and the boron-derived H on the same face, which makes the OH trans to the ethyl substituent, giving trans-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC1=CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1boltky","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"What is the major product of hydroboration-oxidation of 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (1. BH3; 2. H2O2, NaOH)?","opts":["CC(C)(C)CCO","CC(C)(C)C(O)C","CC(C)C(O)CC","CC(C)(C)C=CO","CCC(C)(C)CO"],"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration is concerted/anti-Markovnikov with NO carbocation rearrangement, so the neopentyl skeleton is preserved and OH goes to the terminal carbon -> 3,3-dimethyl-1-butanol (primary). Keyed option A correct. option B = Markovnikov alcohol, option C = rearranged skeleton (the acid-hydration lure), option D = enol (retains unsaturation), option E wrong isomer.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)C=C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-nymgh3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is treated with excess acetaldehyde and NaOH, then heated. Acetaldehyde is in excess so it provides the enolate. Which structure is the major crossed product, and what governs its alkene geometry?","opts":["O=C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)CC=O","O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=CCCc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Benzaldehyde (no alpha H) is the electrophile; the acetaldehyde enolate is the nucleophile. option B minor Z, option C the beta-hydroxy addition product, option D (phenylacetaldehyde)/option E (3-phenylpropanal) wrong connectivity. Geometry rationale is qualitative (conjugation/sterics), not FMO. Keyed answer correct.","traps":null,"qs":"O=Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1xw84k9","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"Ethylbenzene is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (hv). What is the major monobromination product?","opts":["CC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","CCc1ccc(Br)cc1","CC(Br)(Br)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1Br"],"correct":0,"why":"NBS/hv abstracts the most stabilized H. The benzylic C-H gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic radical, so Br goes there: (1-bromoethyl)benzene (opt[0]). opt[1] brominates the terminal primary non-benzylic carbon, opt[2]/opt[4] are ring (EAS) products, opt[3] is a geminal dibromide (over-bromination).","traps":null,"qs":"CCc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-185lgi6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"1-Methoxy-1,3-butadiene reacts with acrolein (prop-2-enal) thermally. Predict the major product (apply the 'ortho' regiochemistry rule).","opts":["COC=CC=CCCC=O","COC1C=CC1C=O","COC1C=CCCC1C=O","COC1C=CC(C=O)CC1","COC1C=CCC(C=O)C1"],"correct":2,"why":"A 1-substituted (terminal) diene with a mono-substituted dienophile follows the 'ortho' rule: the methoxy and the CHO end up on adjacent (1,2) carbons of the cyclohexene, with the new ring double bond between the former diene C2-C3. option A is the 1,2 product. option B is the 1,3 ('meta') and option C the 1,4 ('para') isomer (wrong regiochemistry), option D is a 4-membered ring (wrong skeleton), option E never cyclized.","traps":null,"qs":"COC=CC=C.C=CC=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1odc4g1","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene) is heated with excess KMnO4, then treated with acid. What is the major product?","opts":["CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"The isopropyl group has one benzylic C-H, so KMnO4 degrades the whole side chain to a single COOH carbon on the ring, giving benzoic acid (the two methyls are lost as CO2/carbonate). Benzylic alcohol, acetophenone, and 2-phenylpropanoic acid wrongly retain side-chain carbons; KMnO4 does not stop at those stages.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1n62b5h","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"1,2-Dimethylcyclopentene (shown) undergoes catalytic hydrogenation with H2/Pt. Because the reaction proceeds by syn addition of H2, which stereochemical descriptor applies to the 1,2-dimethylcyclopentane product?","opts":["The product is achiral and planar, so no descriptor applies","An equal mixture of cis and trans because the catalyst is not stereospecific","cis (the two methyl groups end up on the same face of the ring)","trans (the two methyl groups end up on opposite faces of the ring)","A racemic mixture of two enantiomers with no defined cis/trans relationship"],"correct":2,"why":"Both H atoms add to the same face (syn). The two ring-bound methyls on C1/C2 end up on the same face relative to each other, giving cis-1,2-dimethylcyclopentane (a meso compound). Classic textbook syn-addition stereochemistry result.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=C(C)CCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fp66n0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A benzenediazonium salt (C6H5N2+, from aniline + NaNO2/HCl at 0-5 C) is added to a cold basic solution of phenol (drawn). Predict the major azo-coupling product.","opts":["Oc1ccc(Nc2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1O","Oc1ccccc1/N=N/c1ccccc1","Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1","O=C1C=CC(=Nc2ccccc2)C=C1"],"correct":3,"why":"Diazonium salts are weak electrophiles that couple at the para position of the strongly activated phenol via EAS, giving 4-(phenylazo)phenol (Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1). The ortho isomer, the reduced N-H (diarylamine) bridge, an extra-OH catechol, and a quinone-imine are wrong.","traps":null,"qs":"Oc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-349b912165","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Phenol is far more acidic than cyclohexanol. What is the main reason?","opts":["Phenol has a higher molar mass, and larger molecules ionize more completely in aqueous solution","The O-H bond in phenol is longer","The negative charge of the phenoxide ion is delocalized into the ring by resonance","The ring carbons of phenol are more electronegative than oxygen, so they pull the proton off the hydroxyl group","Cyclohexanol cannot form hydrogen bonds"],"correct":2,"why":"Always look at the conjugate base. In phenoxide the lone pair on oxygen sits next to a pi system, so the negative charge is shared with the ring carbons. In cyclohexanol the charge is stuck on one oxygen. A more stable conjugate base means a stronger acid.","traps":["Molar mass has nothing to do with acidity.","The decisive factor is the stability of the conjugate base, not the bond length.",null,"Oxygen is more electronegative than carbon. The key is that the charge can spread out over the ring.","Both compounds form hydrogen bonds. That does not explain the difference in acidity."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-fe4b8d","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (drawn) is heated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu). Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["C=CC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)OC(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)=C","CCC(C)(C)O"],"correct":3,"why":"Tertiary halide + bulky strong base (tert-butoxide) gives E2 with Hofmann selectivity (less-substituted, more accessible beta-H), yielding 2-methyl-1-butene. The Zaitsev alkene 2-methyl-2-butene is disfavored by the bulky base; bulky ether/tertiary alcohol are substitution products; last is wrong skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC(C)(C)Br","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-pm2a7","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with LDA, then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br) is added. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["O=C1CCCC(Cc2ccccc2)C1","C(c1ccccc1)OC1=CCCCC1","OC1(Cc2ccccc2)CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1(Cc1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","O=C1CCCCC1Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"C-alkylation dominates over O-alkylation (option B enol ether), the group goes on the alpha not beta carbon (option D), controlled stoichiometry gives mono not the 2,2-dibenzyl product (option E), and no carbonyl addition (option 5 alcohol).","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"generated:qr-successive-percent#x565wy","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $50 item is marked UP by 25% and then the new price is marked DOWN by 50%. What is the final price?","opts":["$25.00","$31.25","$37.50","$46.88","$62.50"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply the factors: 50 × 1.25 × 0.5 = $31.25. Percents stack by multiplying, never by adding.","traps":["You skipped the mark-up and only applied the discount.",null,"You added and subtracted the percents. The second percent is taken of the NEW price, so multiply the two factors.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You stopped after the mark-up. Apply the mark-down to that new price."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#wdx15q","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A cyclist travels at 54 kilometers per hour. What is that speed in meters per second?","opts":["900 m/s","0.015 m/s","54000 m/s","194 m/s","15 m/s"],"correct":4,"why":"54 km/h × 1000 m/km ÷ 3600 s/h = 15 m/s. Shortcut: divide km/h by 3.6.","traps":["You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour.","That is kilometers per second. Multiply by 1000 to get meters.","That is meters per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so kilometers and hours cancel.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp002","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"If log₂(x) + log₂(x − 6) = 4, what is the value of x?","opts":["x = 12","x = 16","x = 8","x = 4","x = −2"],"correct":2,"why":"log₂(x(x−6)) = 4, so x(x−6) = 16, so x² − 6x − 16 = 0 → (x − 8)(x + 2) = 0. x = 8 or x = −2. Reject negative (log undefined). x = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"generated:qr-mixture#1ytvgkf","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"3 gallons of a 20% acid solution is mixed with 2 gallons of a 80% acid solution. What is the concentration of the mixture?","opts":["36.7%","44%","50%","56%","220%"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid: 20% of 3 plus 80% of 2 = 2.2 gallons. Total volume = 5 gallons. Concentration = 44%.","traps":["You divided by the product of the volumes. Divide by the SUM: total volume.",null,"You averaged the two percents. The mixture leans toward whichever solution you used MORE of, so weight by volume.","You swapped the volumes. Pair each concentration with its own volume.","You found the total acid but never divided by the total volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-combined-work#fuu075","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One printer finishes a print run in 5 hours. A second printer does the same job in 20 hours. Working together, how long do they take?","opts":["4 hours","15 hours","12.5 hours","0.25 hours","25 hours"],"correct":0,"why":"Rates add: 1/5 + 1/20 = 1/4 of the job per hour. Time = 4 = 4 hours. Sanity check: it must be less than 5 hours.","traps":[null,"You subtracted the times. Rates add when both are working.","You averaged the times. Two working together must be FASTER than the faster one alone.","That is the combined RATE (jobs per hour). Flip it to get the time.","You added the times. Adding helpers cannot make the job take longer. Add the RATES: 1/a + 1/b."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1133","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"What is 35 percent of 225?","opts":["7.88","78.75","146.25","260","7875"],"correct":1,"why":"35% of 225 = 35/100 × 225 = 78.75.","traps":["You shifted the decimal one place too far, dividing the percent by 1000 rather than by 100.",null,"You found the 65 percent left over rather than the 35 percent the question asks for.","You added the 35 to the 225 instead of taking 35 percent of it.","You multiplied 35 by 225 and never divided by 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1024","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A commute takes 2/5 of an hour by bus and 1/4 of an hour on foot. How many hours is the whole commute?","opts":["13/20","12/20","2/20","3/9","9/10"],"correct":0,"why":"LCD = 20. 2/5 = 8/20, 1/4 = 5/20. Combine: 13/20 = 13/20.","traps":[null,"You scaled 1/4 by 4 instead of by 5; a quarter is 5/20.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.","You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 3 over 9.","You rescaled 1/4 as 10/20, which is a half; a quarter is 5/20."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1715","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"A rack holds 3^2 trays and each tray holds 3^1 samples. How many samples fit in the rack?","opts":["3","9","12","27","729"],"correct":3,"why":"Same base × adds exponents: 3^(2+1) = 3^3 = 27.","traps":["You subtracted the exponents, which is what division does.","You multiplied the exponents, so you stayed at 3^2.","You added the two values, 9 and 3, instead of multiplying them.",null,"You multiplied the bases as well as adding the exponents, which gives 9^3."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ps5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Rent on a storage unit is $200 a month and rises 25 percent of its current amount in each of the next two years. What is the monthly rent after both increases?","opts":["$250","$256","$275.00","$300","$312.50"],"correct":4,"why":"×1.25 × 1.25 = ×1.5625. 200 × 1.5625 = $312.50.","traps":["That is one 25 percent step, and the second rise still has to be applied.","Nothing in the two steps lands at 256: 1.25 times 1.25 is 1.5625.","You added 25 dollars on the second step instead of 25 percent, which on 250 is 62.50.","You added the two rises into a single 50 percent, and sequential percents multiply instead.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1695","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A drainage run on a plan goes from (3,-2) to (6,-4). What is its slope, to the nearest hundredth?","opts":["-1.5","-0.67","-0.22","0.67","1"],"correct":1,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = -2/3 = -0.67.","traps":["-1.5 divides the run by the rise, 3 over -2, which is the slope upside down.",null,"-0.22 adds the two x values for the run, 3 plus 6, and the run is their difference, 6 minus 3.","0.67 has the right size and the wrong sign, since y drops from -2 to -4.","1 adds the change in y and the change in x instead of dividing them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1053","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A crew used 2/4 of a can of stain to cover 3/7 of a deck. What fraction of the deck would a full can cover?","opts":["5/28","6/28","6/11","6/7","7/6"],"correct":3,"why":"Flip the divisor and multiply: 3/7 times 4/2 gives 12/14. Both numbers are even, so in lowest terms that is 6/7. A quick sanity check is that dividing by something smaller than 1 has to make the answer bigger, and 6/7 is indeed above 3/7.","traps":["You added the tops (3 + 2) over the multiplied bottoms, treating the division sign as a times sign.","You multiplied straight across without flipping the divisor first.","You multiplied straight across without flipping, then added the bottoms (7 + 4).",null,"That flips the first fraction instead of the second. The one being divided by is the one that turns over."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-1idv8g5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"Given that sin 25° = 0.423, what is cos 65°?","opts":["-0.423","0.179","0.423","0.577","0.906"],"correct":2,"why":"The two angles add to 90 degrees, and the cosine of an angle equals the sine of its complement, so cos 65 is exactly sin 25. That makes the value 0.423 with no calculation at all. Any time a problem pairs an angle with its complement and switches between sine and cosine, the number carries straight across.","traps":["This attaches a negative sign, but 65 degrees is acute so every basic ratio there is positive.","This squares 0.423, which gives sin squared of 25 rather than any cosine.",null,"This subtracts the sine from 1, but the Pythagorean identity works on squares, not on the raw values.","This is sin 65, which pairs the wrong two values, and it is also what you get by running the Pythagorean identity on 25 degrees instead of using the complement."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:pfl1515","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A clinic uses a fair coin flip to send each of five patients to one of two waiting rooms. What is the probability that exactly one flip lands heads?","opts":["0.03","0.08","0.16","0.31","0.84"],"correct":2,"why":"C(5,1) × (1/2)^5 = 5 × 0.0313 = 0.1563.","traps":["0.03 is the chance of one particular pattern, 1/32, and the single head has five places it can sit.","0.08 carries one extra factor of 1/2, which stretches the run to six flips instead of five.",null,"0.31 is the chance of exactly two heads, ten of the thirty-two patterns, while exactly one head has five of them.","0.84 is the complement, the chance of anything other than exactly one head."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#bt630n","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (4 × 10⁻¹)(3 × 10⁻¹) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["1.2 × 10⁻¹","7 × 10⁻¹","1.2 × 10¹","1.2 × 10⁻²","1.2 × 10⁻³"],"correct":0,"why":"4 × 3 = 12 and 10⁻¹ × 10⁻¹ = 10⁻². 12 × 10⁻² is not proper form, so write 1.2 × 10⁻¹. Answer: 1.2 × 10⁻¹.","traps":[null,"You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","You multiplied the exponents. Powers of ten ADD when you multiply.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:cou1580","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"From 6 items, how many different ordered arrangements of 2 can be formed?","opts":["8","12","15","30","36"],"correct":3,"why":"nPr = 6!/(6-2)! = 30.","traps":["Added 6 and 2.","Multiplied 6 by 2 instead of 6 by 5.","That counts unordered pairs; when order matters each pair counts twice.",null,"Treated it as 6 choices for both slots, which allows the same item twice."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:dis1282","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"A crate holds 4 large vials and 8 small vials. How many vials are in 5 such crates?","opts":["12","28","55","60","65"],"correct":3,"why":"Distribute: 5·4 + 5·8 = 20 + 40 = 60.","traps":["That is just the 4 + 8 inside the parentheses, so the 5 outside never got multiplied in.","Distributing the 5 only to the 4 gives 20 + 8 = 28, leaving the 8 unmultiplied.","That is 5 times 11, so the sum inside came out one short of 4 + 8 = 12.",null,"Adding the outside 5 after multiplying counts it twice, since 5 times 12 is already 60."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1185","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"An 80 gram sample loses 25% of its mass in a first drying step and 10% of what is left in a second. What is its mass in grams at the end?","opts":["52","54","60","66","72"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply factors: 80 × 0.75 × 0.9 = 54. Naïvely adding gives 52, which is wrong.","traps":["You added the two cuts into a single 35 percent, and sequential percents multiply instead.",null,"That is the price after the 25 percent cut, one step short of the answer.","That raises the second step by 10 percent instead of cutting by it.","That applies only the 10 percent cut and leaves the 25 percent out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1458","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"A museum floor is mapped on a grid, with one exhibit at (-8, 7) and another at (0, 12). In a straight line, about how far apart are they?","opts":["3.61","8","9.43","13","89"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((8)² + (5)²) = √89 = 9.43.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pem1308","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A delivery contains 8 loose vials plus 7 trays holding 4 vials each. How many vials is that in total?","opts":["19","28","36","39","60"],"correct":2,"why":"Multiply before adding: 7 × 4 = 28, then 8 + 28 = 36.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-znbg69","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In triangle ABC, side a = 8, angle A = 30°, and angle B = 45°. What is the length of side b?","opts":["4√2, about 5.7","8√3, about 13.9","12","8√2, about 11.3","16"],"correct":3,"why":"The Law of Sines says a over sin A equals b over sin B. Here 8 divided by sin 30 is 8 divided by 1/2, which is 16, so b equals 16 times sin 45, and sin 45 is √2/2, leaving 8√2. Since angle B is bigger than angle A, side b has to be longer than side a, which rules out anything under 8 before you even compute.","traps":["This inverts the ratio, dividing by sin 45 and multiplying by sin 30.","This uses sin 60 where sin 45 belongs.","This scales the side by the ratio of the angles, but sides scale with the sines of the angles, not with the angles themselves.",null,"This is the shared ratio of 8 over sin 30, the number you still have to multiply by sin 45."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1640","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A data set has a mean of 75 and a standard deviation of 5. What is the z score of a value of 70?","opts":["-5","-1","-0.07","1","14"],"correct":1,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (70 − 75)/5 = -1.","traps":["This is the raw gap in points, stopping before the division by 5.",null,"Divided the 5-point gap by the mean of 75 instead of by the standard deviation of 5.","Right size, wrong side: 70 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative.","Divided 70 by 5 without first subtracting the mean."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1353","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A team of 22 volunteers is assigned to two sites in a ratio of 4 to 7. How many volunteers are at the smaller site?","opts":["2","4","8","11","14"],"correct":2,"why":"Total parts = 11; one part = 22/11 = 2. Smaller = 4·2 = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-empirical-rule#1n90no","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Scores are normally distributed with mean 500 and standard deviation 100. About what percent of scores fall above 600?","opts":["16%","32%","34%","68%","84%"],"correct":0,"why":"Empirical rule: 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD. Halves: 34% and 47.5% per side, tails 16% and 2.5%. Here that gives 16%.","traps":[null,"That is BOTH tails together. Only the upper tail is asked for: halve it.","That is the slice between the mean and one SD. The question asks for the TAIL beyond it.","That is the middle band within one SD. The tail is what is left over, split in two.","That is everything BELOW the cutoff. The question asks for above."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:int1439","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A deposit of $1000 earns 7.5% interest compounded annually for 5 years. To the nearest cent, what is the total amount in dollars after 5 years?","opts":["375","1000.38","1075","1375","1435.63"],"correct":4,"why":"A = P(1+r)^t = 1000·(1.075)^5 = 1435.63.","traps":["That is the simple interest for five years, not the ending amount.","Added rt as about thirty-eight cents rather than as 37.5 percent of the 1000.","Grew the balance for one year instead of five.","Used simple interest, so the later years never earned interest on the earlier interest.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#saaxqm","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the PRODUCT of the solutions of x² − 15x + 56 = 0?","opts":["15","−15","−56","56","1"],"correct":3,"why":"It factors as (x − 8)(x − 7), so the roots are 8 and 7. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = 15, product = c/a = 56.","traps":["That is the SUM of the roots (−b/a). The product is c/a.","You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a.","Sign slip. The product of the roots is c/a with its sign kept.",null,"That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their product."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1738","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"If x differs from 6 by exactly 1, what are the possible values of x?","opts":["7","-5 or -7","-5 or 5","5 or -7","7 or 5"],"correct":4,"why":"|x − 6| = 1 means x − 6 = ±1, so x = 7 or 5.","traps":["You solved x - 6 = 1 and stopped, and the branch x - 6 = -1 gives 5 as well.","You subtracted the 6 instead of adding it back on both branches.","That pairs 5 with its negative, and both branches here land above zero, at 7 and 5.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1075","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A shift log records 5/6 of an hour of machine downtime. To three decimal places, how many hours is that?","opts":["0.167","0.25","0.333","0.5","0.833"],"correct":4,"why":"5/6 = 0.833. Long-divide if you don't have it memorized.","traps":["That is the 1/6 left over, not the 5/6 the question asks for.","That is 1/4, well under five sixths of a whole.","That is 1/3, which is two sixths.","That is 1/2, which is three sixths.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1056","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A machine fills 2/8 of a crate in 6/9 of a minute. What fraction of a crate does it fill in one minute?","opts":["3/8","8/72","12/17","8/3","12/72"],"correct":0,"why":"Flip the divisor and multiply: 2/8 times 9/6 gives 18/48, which reduces by 6 to 3/8. Because 6/9 is less than 1, dividing by it has to push 2/8 upward, and 3/8 is larger, so the size is right. Flip, multiply, reduce, in that order.","traps":[null,"You added the tops (2 + 6) over the multiplied bottoms, treating the division sign as a times sign.","You multiplied straight across without flipping, then added the bottoms (8 + 9).","That flips the first fraction instead of the second, which inverts the whole answer.","You multiplied straight across without flipping the divisor first."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1495","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Five trays each hold one full set of instruments, but 5 instruments are missing overall, leaving 70 in total. How many instruments are in a full set?","opts":["13","14","15","16","20"],"correct":2,"why":"'Five less than 5 times a number' becomes 5x minus 5 = 70. Add the 5 to both sides for 5x = 75, then divide by 5 for 15. Undo the addition or subtraction first and the multiplication second.","traps":["Moved the 5 the wrong way: 70 minus 5 is 65, and 65 split five ways gives this.","Divided 70 by 5 and stopped there, leaving the subtracted 5 unaccounted for.",null,"Added the 5 back twice: 70 plus 5 plus 5 is 80, and 80 split five ways gives this.","Five 20s make 100, well past the 75 that 5x has to equal."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1377","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A freight truck averages 60 miles per hour for 4.5 hours. How many miles does it cover?","opts":["13.33","210","240","270","330"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 60 × 4.5 = 270 mi.","traps":["Dividing 60 by 4.5 instead of multiplying.","Using 3.5 hours instead of 4.5.","Dropping the half hour and using 4 hours flat.",null,"Using 5.5 hours instead of the 4.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#1la5g4q","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 7 students, in how many ways can a committee of 2 be chosen?","opts":["2","14","21","42","49"],"correct":2,"why":"Order does not matter: 7C2 = 42 / 2! = 21.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 2 people. You still have to choose them from 7.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.",null,"You used a permutation. A committee has no positions, so order does NOT matter: divide out the orderings.","You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:rt5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"You drive 60 mph for 2 hr, then 40 mph for 3 hr. Average speed?","opts":["48 mph","45 mph","50 mph","100 mph","52 mph"],"correct":0,"why":"Average speed is never the average of the speeds; it is total distance over total time. The legs cover 60 times 2, or 120 miles, and 40 times 3, or another 120 miles, so 240 miles in 5 hours gives 48 mph. The tell is that the times differ, so the 3 hours at 40 mph drag the average below the halfway point of 50.","traps":[null,"Estimating between the two speeds instead of dividing 240 miles by 5 hours.","Averaging 60 and 40 straight across, which ignores the extra hour spent at 40.","Adding the two speeds together.","Pairing each speed with the other leg's time, 60 for 3 hours and 40 for 2."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1735","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"A target reading is 10, and an actual reading of x differs from the target by exactly 7. What are the possible values of x?","opts":["-3 or -17","-3 or 3","17","17 or -3","17 or 3"],"correct":4,"why":"|x − 10| = 7 means x − 10 = ±7, so x = 17 or 3.","traps":["You moved the 10 to the wrong side and subtracted it instead of adding it back.","That pairs 3 with its negative, and the other branch is 10 plus 7, which is 17.","You solved x - 10 = 7 and stopped, and the branch x - 10 = -7 gives 3 as well.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1356","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A 44-gram sample is separated in a ratio of 2 to 9. How many grams are in the smaller portion?","opts":["4","8","11","22","36"],"correct":1,"why":"Total parts = 11; one part = 44/11 = 4. Smaller = 2·4 = 8.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1659","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Three tent poles measure 17, 14, and 3 units. Can they be joined end to end to form a triangle?","opts":["Cannot determine","Yes","Only if it is obtuse","Only if right triangle","No"],"correct":4,"why":"|17−14| = 3 and 17+14 = 31. 3 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":["The three lengths settle it: 14 plus 3 equals 17, and the sum has to be greater than the longest side, not equal to it.","14 and 3 add to exactly 17, so they lie flat along the longest side instead of rising above it.","There is no corner to open: 14 and 3 lie flat along the 17 rather than rising above it.","There is no angle to open up: the two shorter sides lie flat along the 17, tying it rather than beating it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:g2d5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A square has side 4. If you double the side, the area becomes…","opts":["×16","×64","×2","×8","×4"],"correct":4,"why":"Area scales as length squared. Double the side, so area × 2² = ×4.","traps":["Raised the factor of 2 to the fourth power, scaling the figure twice over.","That is the new area in square units, not the factor the area grew by.","Linear scaling, but area is squared.","That's cubed, for volume.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:est1210","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A car odometer reads 1,906 miles at the start of a trip that covers 44 miles. What does it read at the end?","opts":["1862","1890","1940","1950","1990"],"correct":3,"why":"Work it out: 1906 + 44 = 1950. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 1906 minus 44, so the plus got read as a minus.","That is below 1906, and adding 44 has to move the total up.","That is 1900 plus 40, the rounded estimate rather than the exact 1,950.",null,"That adds about 85, nearly double the 44 on the page."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1414","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A technician combines 300 mL of a 20% alcohol solution with 100 mL of a 5% alcohol solution. What is the percent concentration of the mixture?","opts":["12.5","16.25","21.67","50","65"],"correct":1,"why":"Solute total = 60 + 5 = 65 mL in 400 mL → 16.25%.","traps":["Averaged the two percents without weighting them by how much of each solution went in.",null,"Divided the 65 mL of solute by the original 300 mL instead of by the new 400 mL total.","Took 12.5 percent, the plain average of the two strengths, of the 400 mL total, which gives a volume rather than a concentration.","That is the total milliliters of solute, not the percent of the mixture."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lgm4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"A buffer is measured at pH 8. Using pH = −log[H⁺], what is [H⁺]?","opts":["10⁻⁸","8","−8","10⁸","10⁻⁶"],"correct":0,"why":"pH = −log[H⁺]. So [H⁺] = 10⁻ᵖᴴ = 10⁻⁸.","traps":[null,"You returned the pH itself instead of converting it back to a concentration.","You negated the pH but never put it back as a power of 10.","You dropped the minus sign in the pH formula, and the exponent has to be negative.","You used the pOH, 14 − 8 = 6, and that exponent belongs to the hydroxide concentration."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1333","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"For which values of x is -5x < 3 true?","opts":["x > -0.6","x = -0.6","x > 0.6","x < 0.6","x < -0.6"],"correct":0,"why":"Divide both sides by -5 (negative) and FLIP: x > -0.6.","traps":[null,"You solved the boundary equation -5x = 3 instead of the range of values.","You flipped the direction but dropped the negative on 3/-5.","You divided by 5 rather than -5, so neither the sign nor the direction changed.","You kept the inequality pointing the same way after dividing by a negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261002"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s14"}},{"id":"FULL_3","name":"Full-Length Test 3","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"bio:BIO_g013","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"After meiosis I is complete but before meiosis II begins, what is the chromosome content of each daughter cell?","opts":["Diploid (2n), 2 chromatids","Haploid (n), 1 chromatid each","Diploid (2n), 1 chromatid each","Haploid (n), with each homologous pair still attached at a chiasma","Haploid (n), 2 sister chromatids each"],"correct":4,"why":"This is testing when the chromosome number is halved and when sister chromatids separate, which are two different events. Meiosis I pulls the homologous pairs apart, so the count drops to haploid, but the two sister chromatids of each chromosome are still joined at the centromere. Picture the pair splitting up while each partner keeps its own twin, so the choice naming a haploid cell with two sister chromatids per chromosome is the one that matches.","traps":["This is the cell as it entered meiosis I, right after DNA replication, rather than the cell that comes out of it.","The ploidy is right, but one chromatid per chromosome describes the product of meiosis II, after the sisters have finally been pulled apart.","This treats meiosis I like mitosis, splitting sister chromatids and keeping the full chromosome count, but meiosis I separates homologs and the chromatids stay joined.","Homologs do pair and form chiasmata in prophase I, but they are pulled completely apart in anaphase I, so nothing holds them together once meiosis I is over.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-a90f3aa0fb","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which statement about cortisol is correct?","opts":["It is an amino acid derivative from the adrenal medulla that raises heart rate within seconds","It is a steroid from the adrenal cortex that raises blood glucose","It strengthens the immune response during stress","It is a steroid from the adrenal cortex that lowers blood glucose","It is released in response to TSH from the anterior pituitary"],"correct":1,"why":"Cortisol is the long-haul stress hormone. ACTH from the anterior pituitary tells the adrenal cortex to release it. It keeps fuel available by raising blood glucose and it quiets the immune system.","traps":["That describes epinephrine. Cortisol is a CORTEX steroid and acts over hours.",null,"Cortisol suppresses inflammation and immunity, which is why related drugs are used as anti-inflammatories.","Cortisol RAISES blood glucose by promoting new glucose production.","The signal for cortisol is ACTH, not TSH."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e043","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"In speciation, what does the process called reinforcement describe?","opts":["Hybrid sterility decreasing","Selection favoring the hybrids until the two populations merge back into one","Identical mating behavior","Reduced fitness in hybrids, which strengthens prezygotic isolation","Random drift"],"correct":3,"why":"When two diverging populations meet again and their hybrids do poorly, any individual that avoids mating across the line leaves more surviving offspring than one that does not. Selection therefore builds up traits that prevent the cross from happening in the first place, such as different songs, different flowering times or different courtship displays. Reinforcement is prezygotic isolation being strengthened precisely because postzygotic isolation is already costly.","traps":["Reinforcement does not repair the hybrids, it reduces how often they are produced, so hybrid sterility is not what changes.","If hybrids did well, the two populations could blend back into one, which is the alternative outcome when reinforcement does not take hold.","Identical courtship behavior would make interbreeding more likely, which is the opposite of what reinforcement builds up.",null,"Drift is random change in allele frequencies, while reinforcement is selection responding to a real cost paid by hybrids."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d016","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"An adult marine animal is radially symmetric, but its larva is bilaterally symmetric. The animal most likely belongs to which group?","opts":["Sponge","Echinoderm","A flatworm such as a planarian","Cnidarian","Mollusc"],"correct":1,"why":"Symmetry is worth checking at both ends of the life cycle, because one group changes partway through. Sea stars, urchins and sand dollars hatch as bilateral swimming larvae and only take on five-part radial symmetry as adults, which tells you the radial look is secondary, acquired later. That is also why echinoderms group with chordates as deuterostomes despite how the adult looks. When a stem hands you a bilateral larva with a radial adult, it is pointing at the echinoderm choice.","traps":["Sponges are mostly irregular in shape and have no true tissues, so neither half of the description fits.",null,"Flatworms are bilateral as larvae and as adults, with a definite head end, and never become radial.","Cnidarians are radial animals and are not bilaterians at all, so a bilateral larval stage is not part of their story.","Molluscs stay bilateral as adults; even a snail is a bilateral animal whose shell coils, rather than a radial one."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-17676nh","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"Bacteria are grown for many generations in medium whose only nitrogen source is the heavy isotope 15N, so all of their DNA is heavy. They are then moved into medium containing only light 14N and allowed to divide exactly twice. What fraction of the DNA molecules now contain one heavy strand and one light strand?","opts":["One half of them","One quarter of them","Three quarters of them","All of them","None of them, because every molecule is now fully light"],"correct":0,"why":"This is semiconservative replication counted out rather than recited: you start with one molecule holding two heavy strands, and those two strands survive every round intact. After one round there are two molecules and both are hybrid. After two rounds there are four molecules, and the two heavy strands still sit in two of them, so half are hybrid. The shortcut worth keeping is that the number of hybrid molecules stops changing after the first round while the total keeps doubling, so the hybrid fraction halves each generation.","traps":[null,"One quarter is the fraction of individual strands that are heavy after two rounds, which is not the same as the fraction of molecules carrying one.","Three quarters is the fraction of individual strands that are light after two rounds, again a strand count rather than a molecule count.","All hybrid is the picture after one round, and the second round dilutes it, because each hybrid molecule gives one hybrid daughter and one fully light daughter.","This assumes the old strands are discarded, but replication keeps and reuses every parental strand, so the two original heavy strands are still somewhere."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e013","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Dogs that repeatedly heard a bell just before receiving food eventually began to salivate at the sound of the bell alone. This result illustrates:","opts":["Insight","Habituation","Operant conditioning","An unlearned reflex present from birth","Classical conditioning"],"correct":4,"why":"Classical conditioning takes a reflex the animal already has and attaches it to a new trigger. Food causes salivation with no learning at all, so food is the unconditioned stimulus and that salivation is the unconditioned response. Pairing the bell with food often enough turns the bell into a conditioned stimulus that draws out the same salivation, now a conditioned response. The test is that the animal is passive: nothing it chooses to do changes the outcome, and that is what separates this from operant conditioning.","traps":["Insight is sudden problem solving without trial and error, and a reflex response to a tone is not problem solving.","Habituation is a response fading away to a repeated harmless stimulus, the opposite of a new response being built.","Operant conditioning requires the animal's own action to produce a consequence, but the dogs were not doing anything to earn the food.","Salivating to food is the unlearned reflex, but salivating to a bell appears only after the bell has been paired with food, which makes it learned.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d008","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Echinoderms and chordates are placed together in which major group of bilaterian animals?","opts":["Protostomes","Lophotrochozoa","Ecdysozoa","Radiata, the radially symmetric animals","Deuterostomes"],"correct":4,"why":"The question is testing the top-level split of bilaterian animals, and the split is made on how the embryo builds its gut. In deuterostomes the first opening of the gastrula becomes the anus and the mouth forms second, and cleavage is radial and indeterminate. Echinoderms and chordates both develop this way, which is why a sea star is a closer relative of yours than a snail is. The name is the memory hook, since deuteros means second, and it is the mouth that comes second.","traps":["Protostomes are the other branch, the molluscs, annelids and arthropods, where the blastopore becomes the mouth.","Lophotrochozoans are a protostome subgroup, the animals with a lophophore or a trochophore larva, such as molluscs and annelids.","Ecdysozoans are the molting protostomes, the arthropods and roundworms.","Adult sea stars are radially symmetric, but their larvae are bilateral and chordates are never radial, so radial symmetry is not what unites these two.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1thf01r","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"C4 plants fix carbon first with PEP carboxylase in their mesophyll cells. What makes that extra step worth the trouble?","opts":["It will not bind oxygen, so it keeps working when carbon dioxide runs low","It fixes carbon without costing the plant any ATP anywhere in the pathway","It produces G3P directly, so the Calvin cycle can be skipped altogether","It works inside the thylakoid, closer to the ATP the light reactions make","It runs backwards in the dark, releasing the carbon it fixed during the day"],"correct":0,"why":"The advantage comes down to what the enzyme will and will not bind. PEP carboxylase has no oxygenase side and holds carbon dioxide tightly, so it keeps collecting carbon even when the stomata are mostly shut. The four carbon acid it makes is a delivery truck that unloads concentrated carbon dioxide right where RuBisCO is waiting.","traps":[null,"Regenerating PEP costs ATP, which is exactly the price C4 plants pay for the extra step.","PEP carboxylase makes a four carbon acid, and the Calvin cycle still does the sugar building in the bundle sheath cells.","PEP carboxylase works in the cytosol of the mesophyll cell, nowhere near the inside of a thylakoid.","Nothing in this pathway runs backwards at night, and CAM plants that fix carbon in the dark use the same forward reaction."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p046","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The central chemoreceptors in the medulla oblongata adjust breathing rate primarily in response to changes in which of the following?","opts":["Glucose","Arterial blood pressure","CO₂ / pH","Temperature","O₂"],"correct":2,"why":"This asks what sets your breathing rate minute to minute. Carbon dioxide diffuses from blood into cerebrospinal fluid, forms carbonic acid, and releases hydrogen ions, and the central chemoreceptors read that pH drop and speed breathing up until the excess is blown off. Oxygen is the backup signal, watched by the carotid and aortic bodies, so the choice naming carbon dioxide and pH is the primary driver.","traps":["Glucose sensing belongs to the pancreatic islet cells and the hypothalamus, and it drives insulin and glucagon rather than breathing.","Pressure is read by baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch, which feed the cardiovascular centers rather than setting respiratory rate.",null,"The hypothalamus handles core temperature; the medullary respiratory centers are tuned to the chemistry of the fluid around them.","Oxygen is monitored by the peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies, and only once it falls quite low."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1lljp1j","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Assuming every salt dissociates completely, which of these solutions has the highest osmolarity?","opts":["0.1 M glucose","0.15 M KCl","0.1 M CaCl2","0.3 M glucose","0.2 M NaCl"],"correct":4,"why":"Convert each solution to particles per liter, then compare. Glucose stays as one particle, so 0.3 M glucose is 300 mOsm, calcium chloride releases three particles so 0.1 M is 300 mOsm, potassium chloride releases two so 0.15 M is 300 mOsm, and sodium chloride releases two so 0.2 M is 400 mOsm. Spotting that three of them tie at 300 is the fastest way through.","traps":["This is only 100 mOsm, the lowest of the group.","Potassium chloride releases two particles, so 0.15 times 2 gives 300 mOsm.","Calcium chloride releases three particles, so 0.1 times 3 gives 300 mOsm.","Glucose does not dissociate, so this one is 300 mOsm.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1qjf2kq","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"Which enzyme sets the pace of the Krebs cycle, and what turns it down?","opts":["Aconitase, slowed by acetyl-CoA","Succinate dehydrogenase, slowed by a shortage of FAD","Isocitrate dehydrogenase, slowed by NADH and ATP","Citrate synthase, slowed by oxygen","Malate dehydrogenase, slowed by oxaloacetate"],"correct":2,"why":"The cycle is regulated the same way glycolysis is, by the products that signal a full tank. Isocitrate dehydrogenase is the rate-limiting enzyme, and rising NADH and ATP slow it while ADP speeds it back up. If a question tells you a cell is energy-rich and asks what the Krebs cycle does, expect the pathway to throttle at this step.","traps":["Aconitase only rearranges citrate into isocitrate, and acetyl-CoA feeds the cycle rather than slowing it.","Succinate dehydrogenase needs FAD as its electron acceptor, so FAD drives that step rather than blocking it.",null,"Citrate synthase is regulated, but oxygen is not its signal, since the cycle never uses oxygen directly.","Oxaloacetate is the product of that step and citrate synthase consumes it immediately, so it does not build up as a brake."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1v1yzon","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Which molecule of ATP or GTP in cellular respiration is made with no help at all from the proton gradient?","opts":["The ATP made when NADH is oxidized at complex I","The GTP made as succinyl-CoA becomes succinate","The ATP made when cytochrome c passes electrons onward","The ATP made as protons spin the rotor of ATP synthase","The ATP made as oxygen is reduced to water at the end of the chain"],"correct":1,"why":"This is the split between substrate-level phosphorylation and oxidative phosphorylation. In substrate-level phosphorylation a phosphate moves straight from a high-energy substrate onto ADP or GDP, with no membrane required, and the Krebs cycle does it once per turn at the succinyl-CoA step. Glycolysis uses the same trick twice in its payoff phase, which is why glycolysis still works when the mitochondria are shut down.","traps":["Complex I oxidizing NADH pumps protons, so any ATP that follows still rides on the gradient.",null,"Cytochrome c moving electrons between complexes drives proton pumping rather than making ATP directly.","This is the definition of chemiosmosis, where the gradient is the entire energy source.","Reducing oxygen is the last electron handoff, and the energy released there goes into the gradient."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-13rd0ee","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"Aquaporins in a kidney tubule cell let water cross the membrane far faster than it otherwise could. What kind of transport is this?","opts":["Passive transport through a channel protein","Secondary active transport driven by the sodium gradient","Primary active transport driven by ATP","Movement against the osmotic gradient","Endocytosis of small droplets of water"],"correct":0,"why":"An aquaporin is a pore, not a pump. Water still goes only where osmosis sends it, from the more dilute side toward the more concentrated side, and the channel just widens the road so the same trip happens much faster. A protein in the path does not mean energy is being spent, so check whether anything is moving against its gradient before calling a process active.","traps":[null,"Aquaporins do not couple water to sodium, that pattern describes a cotransporter.","No ATP is hydrolyzed by an aquaporin, it has no pump activity at all.","Water through an aquaporin still follows osmosis, it never climbs the gradient.","Taking in fluid by vesicle is pinocytosis, which is slow and nonselective next to a water channel."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p024","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which class of antibody crosses the placenta and gives a newborn passive immunity?","opts":["IgD","IgM","IgG","IgA","IgE"],"correct":2,"why":"Match each antibody class to its job. IgG is the most abundant class in blood, the workhorse of the secondary response, and the one built to be carried across the placenta, so a newborn starts life protected by the mother's IgG. IgA is the class delivered after birth in breast milk, a different route for the same idea.","traps":["IgD sits on the surface of naive B cells as part of their receptor and is barely present in circulation.","IgM is the first antibody made in a new infection, and as a large pentamer it is far too bulky to cross the placenta.",null,"IgA guards mucous surfaces and reaches the infant through breast milk rather than through the placenta.","IgE binds mast cells and drives allergic and antiparasitic responses, and it stays with the mother."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1n24d6b","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"Trace one electron through noncyclic (linear) electron flow. Which order is correct?","opts":["Water, photosystem II, ferredoxin, plastocyanin, cytochrome complex, plastoquinone, photosystem I, and finally NADP+","Water, photosystem I, ferredoxin, cytochrome complex, plastoquinone, plastocyanin, photosystem II, NADP+","NADP+, ferredoxin, photosystem I, plastocyanin, cytochrome complex, plastoquinone, photosystem II, water","Water, photosystem II, plastoquinone, cytochrome complex, plastocyanin, photosystem I, ferredoxin, NADP+","Water, photosystem I, plastoquinone, cytochrome complex, plastocyanin, photosystem II, ferredoxin, NADP+"],"correct":3,"why":"The trap in this one is the numbering. Photosystem II fires first even though it carries the higher number, because it was discovered second. Hold the shape in your head as the letter Z: water at the bottom left, an uphill jump at photosystem II, a slide down through the carriers, a second uphill jump at photosystem I, then NADP+ waiting at the top right.","traps":["Ferredoxin sits on the far side of photosystem I, not between photosystem II and the cytochrome complex.","Photosystem I appears first here and ferredoxin appears far too early, since ferredoxin only receives electrons after the second light hit.","This runs the whole chain backwards, with NADP+ donating electrons instead of accepting them at the end.",null,"The photosystems are numbered by discovery order, not working order, so starting at photosystem I flips the chain."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p047","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"What is the approximate resting membrane potential of a typical neuron?","opts":["+30 mV","0 mV","-70 mV","+70 mV","-55 mV"],"correct":2,"why":"At rest the inside of a neuron is negative relative to the outside, landing near -70 millivolts. Two things put it there: potassium leak channels let potassium drift out down its gradient and leave negatively charged proteins behind, and the sodium-potassium pump exports three sodium for every two potassium it imports. Lock in the sign first, negative inside, then the size.","traps":["Positive 30 millivolts is roughly the peak reached at the top of an action potential, not the resting state.","Zero would mean no charge separation across the membrane, leaving the neuron with no gradient to work with.",null,"Positive 70 millivolts has about the right magnitude but the wrong sign, and a resting neuron is negative on the inside.","Around -55 millivolts is the threshold a neuron must reach in order to fire, which sits above the resting value rather than at it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-867ilc","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Two electron carriers in the chain are mobile rather than locked inside a complex. Which pair?","opts":["Oxygen and water","Cytochrome c and cytochrome a3","Ubiquinone and cytochrome c","Ubiquinone and ATP synthase","NADH and FADH2"],"correct":2,"why":"Four complexes sit fixed in the membrane, so something has to ferry electrons across the gaps between them. Ubiquinone, also called coenzyme Q, is lipid-soluble and drifts within the membrane from complexes I and II over to complex III, while cytochrome c is a small protein that skims the intermembrane space face from complex III to complex IV. Remember the pattern as one carrier inside the membrane and one riding on its surface.","traps":["Oxygen is the endpoint and water is the product, so neither shuttles electrons between complexes.","Cytochrome a3 is built into complex IV rather than being free to move.",null,"ATP synthase spends the gradient the chain builds and carries no electrons at all.","These two deliver electrons to the chain from the matrix, but neither is part of the chain itself."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1d8y656","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"How many turns of the Calvin cycle does a chloroplast need to build one six carbon glucose?","opts":["Eighteen","Two","Twelve","Three","Six"],"correct":4,"why":"Each turn fixes exactly one carbon, and glucose holds six of them. The longer route to the same answer: three turns release one exportable G3P, and two G3P join to form glucose, so double the three. Counting carbons in against carbons out settles every Calvin cycle bookkeeping question.","traps":["Eighteen is the ATP bill for one glucose, not a count of turns.","Two is the number of G3P molecules that combine into glucose, not the number of turns.","Twelve is how many G3P molecules are made across those turns, and most of them go straight back into RuBP.","Three turns yields a single G3P, which is only half a glucose.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g006","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A pedigree shows that affected individuals are predominantly male, and that no affected father ever passes the condition to his sons. Which inheritance pattern best fits this pedigree?","opts":["X-linked recessive","X-linked dominant","Y-linked","Autosomal recessive","Mitochondrial"],"correct":0,"why":"Two clues do all the work here. No father to son transmission tells you the gene is not on the Y and is not being carried through males on an autosome, because a father gives his son a Y chromosome rather than an X. A strong male excess tells you one copy is enough to show the trait, which is what happens when the gene sits on the X and males have only one X to begin with. Put those together and you land on X-linked recessive every time.","traps":[null,"X-linked dominant also blocks father to son transmission, but it usually produces more affected females than males rather than a male excess.","A Y-linked trait would pass from every affected father to every one of his sons, which is exactly what this pedigree never shows.","Autosomal recessive conditions strike males and females at about the same rate, and affected fathers can pass the allele to sons.","Mitochondrial conditions come only from the mother and affect sons and daughters alike, with no male bias."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-ndmaar","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A few thousand epinephrine molecules reaching a liver cell can release hundreds of millions of glucose molecules. The main reason is that:","opts":["Each hormone molecule is used over and over again by a single receptor protein","The hormone is stored and then released again by the target cell","Each step in the cascade is an enzyme that activates many copies of the next","The hormone enters the cell and binds DNA directly","The receptor is a channel that admits glucose from outside"],"correct":2,"why":"Amplification is the payoff of using enzymes as relay runners. One active G protein switches on an adenylyl cyclase that makes many cAMP molecules, each protein kinase A phosphorylates many targets, and the numbers multiply at every stage. When a question marvels at how small the input was, the answer is almost always the enzyme cascade.","traps":["Even repeated binding by one molecule gives nothing like a millionfold gain, the multiplication happens downstream.","Storing and rereleasing a hormone does not multiply the size of the response.",null,"Epinephrine is not lipid soluble and works through a surface receptor, and direct DNA binding would not amplify this way.","The glucose released comes from the cell's own glycogen stores, not from outside."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-10j9q5x","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Why is oxygen able to interfere with carbon fixation at all?","opts":["Oxygen binds a separate regulatory site that switches RuBisCO off","Oxygen blocks carbon dioxide from crossing the chloroplast envelope","Oxygen oxidizes the magnesium ion in the enzyme and inactivates it","Both gases compete for the same active site on RuBisCO","Oxygen reacts with NADPH before the Calvin cycle can put it to use"],"correct":3,"why":"One site, two gases, and whichever is more plentiful wins the spot. The enzyme's full name, ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase oxygenase, spells out both jobs in a single word. That competition is the whole reason C4 and CAM plants evolved ways to keep carbon dioxide concentrated around the enzyme.","traps":["There is no off switch involved, because oxygen is taken in as a substrate at the working site.","Gases diffuse across the envelope freely, and neither one blocks the other's entry.","The magnesium in the active site stays put, and photorespiration is not an enzyme poisoning event.",null,"NADPH is not what oxygen intercepts, since the competition happens at the enzyme that grabs the gas."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1ae3if3","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":1,"q":"The chromatids have finished arriving at the two poles of the cell. Which set of events comes next, before the cytoplasm is divided?","opts":["Kinetochores capture microtubules coming from both of the spindle poles","Cohesin is cleaved at the centromere so the sister chromatids can separate","A fresh round of DNA replication begins at each of the spindle poles","Chromosomes condense further while the nucleoli disassemble","Nuclear envelopes reform, chromosomes decondense, nucleoli reappear"],"correct":4,"why":"Telophase is prophase run backward. The envelope rebuilds around each cluster of chromosomes, the chromosomes unwind so their genes can be read again, the nucleoli reappear as ribosome production restarts, and the spindle comes apart. Cytokinesis overlaps with it but is a separate event, dividing the cytoplasm rather than the nucleus. If you can describe prophase, you already know telophase in reverse.","traps":["Kinetochore capture belongs to prometaphase, back when the chromosomes still had to be gathered to the middle.","Cohesin cleavage is what started anaphase, so it has already happened by the time the chromatids reach the poles.","Replication happens in S phase of the next interphase, only after the cell has divided and passed its G1 checks.","Condensing and losing the nucleoli is prophase, the opening of mitosis rather than its close.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev008","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In a vertebrate embryo, the notochord signals to the ectoderm lying directly above it. What does that signal induce?","opts":["Heart","Somites","Limb buds","Neural tube","The lens of the eye"],"correct":3,"why":"Induction means one tissue instructing a neighboring tissue about what to become, and the notochord is the textbook example. It runs down the midline beneath the dorsal ectoderm and signals that strip to thicken into the neural plate, which then folds up into the neural tube that becomes the brain and spinal cord. Remember the pairing as the notochord speaking upward to ectoderm, and note that the notochord itself mostly disappears, leaving the nucleus pulposus inside each intervertebral disc.","traps":["The heart forms from lateral plate mesoderm under its own signals, well away from the midline rod of the notochord.","Somites are blocks of paraxial mesoderm that form beside the notochord on a segmentation clock, and while the notochord helps pattern them it does not call them into existence.","Limb buds arise from lateral plate mesoderm and are patterned by their own signaling centers rather than by midline induction.",null,"The lens is a famous induction as well, but the inducer there is the optic vesicle reaching out from the forebrain and touching the overlying surface ectoderm."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g024","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Hemophilia is an X-linked recessive condition. A man's mother is a known carrier. Before any testing is done, what is the probability that this man has hemophilia?","opts":["Cannot be determined without knowing the father's genotype","100%","25%","50%","0%"],"correct":3,"why":"A man has exactly one X chromosome and it came from his mother, so she alone sets the odds. She carries a normal allele on one X and the hemophilia allele on the other, and she passes one of the two at random, which makes this a straight coin flip at one half. The general rule worth keeping is that for an X-linked trait in a son, the father is not part of the calculation, because what he contributes is the Y.","traps":["The father contributes a Y chromosome to a son, so his X-linked genotype has no bearing on his son's risk.","Certainty would require the mother to carry the allele on both of her X chromosomes, which would make her affected rather than a carrier.","One quarter would be right if two independent one-half events both had to occur, but only a single transmission event is involved here.",null,"A carrier mother does have the allele available to pass on, so a son is genuinely at risk."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p094","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the best example of a hinge joint?","opts":["Vertebrae","Elbow, knee","Thumb base","The joint between the atlas and the axis at the top of the neck","Hip"],"correct":1,"why":"Joint questions ask you to match the shape of the joint surfaces to the motion they allow. A hinge joint is a cylinder sitting in a trough, so it opens and closes in a single plane like a door, and the elbow and the knee are the classic pair. When a joint name shows up on the exam, picture the motion first: bending in one plane points to a hinge, motion in every direction points to ball and socket, and spinning around a long axis points to a pivot.","traps":["The small facet joints between vertebrae glide on each other and the discs between the bodies are cartilaginous, so neither one works like a hinge.",null,"The base of the thumb is a saddle joint, which is what lets the thumb swing across the palm and oppose the fingers.","The atlas and axis form a pivot joint, which rotates the head side to side rather than bending it open and closed.","The hip is a ball and socket joint, so it swings in every direction rather than being limited to one plane of motion."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g014","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A cell with a diploid number of 2n = 8 undergoes meiosis. How many chromosomes does each resulting gamete contain?","opts":["4","2","16","It cannot be determined without knowing how many crossovers occurred","8"],"correct":0,"why":"Meiosis runs one round of DNA replication followed by two divisions, which halves the chromosome number exactly once. If 2n equals 8, then n equals 4, so every normal gamete carries 4 chromosomes. When a stem hands you 2n, write n beside it before you look at the choices and the arithmetic stops being a trap.","traps":[null,"This divides by four, probably because meiosis yields four cells, but four products does not mean four halvings of the chromosome number.","This doubles instead of halving; replication in S phase makes sister chromatids, not extra chromosomes.","Crossing over swaps segments between homologs and changes which alleles travel together, but it never changes how many chromosomes a gamete receives.","This is the parent cell's diploid count, which is what you get if the halving step is left out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e045","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the equation for logistic population growth?","opts":["N = K","dN/dt = rN(K-N)/K","dN/dt = rN(N-K)/K","N = N₀ × e^(rt)","dN/dt = rN"],"correct":1,"why":"Logistic growth is exponential growth with a brake bolted on. The rN piece is the same engine that drives exponential growth, and the (K minus N) over K term is a fraction between 0 and 1 measuring how much room is left. When N is small that fraction sits near 1 and growth looks exponential; as N approaches K it shrinks toward 0 and growth stops, producing the S-shaped curve. Read the brake term that way and you never have to memorize the formula blind.","traps":["This states that the population equals carrying capacity, which is the endpoint rather than an equation for growth.",null,"This reverses the sign, which would make the population shrink whenever it sits below carrying capacity and grow once it passes it.","This is the integrated form of exponential growth, giving population size over time rather than a logistic rate of change.","This is exponential growth, with no ceiling at all, so the population would never level off."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-19awm96","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"At a synapse, calcium entering the axon terminal triggers vesicles of neurotransmitter to release their contents into the cleft. During that release, the vesicle membrane:","opts":["Is pumped out by the sodium potassium pump","Fuses with the plasma membrane and becomes part of it","Pinches off and floats away into the synaptic cleft as a free bubble","Passes intact through the plasma membrane","Is digested by lysosomal enzymes"],"correct":1,"why":"Exocytosis is fusion, not ejection. The vesicle membrane joins the plasma membrane, the inside of the vesicle opens to the outside world, and the cell surface grows by exactly that patch, which is later recovered by endocytosis. Contents go out, membrane stays.","traps":["That pump moves ions only and has nothing to do with vesicle traffic.",null,"Membrane does not survive as a free bubble in the cleft, it merges into the cell surface.","A whole vesicle cannot pass through an intact bilayer.","Lysosomal digestion is how a cell breaks down internalized material, not how it secretes."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p067","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Melatonin, the hormone whose release rises in darkness and helps set the sleep and wake cycle, is secreted by which structure?","opts":["Liver","Anterior pituitary","Thymus","Hypothalamus","Pineal gland"],"correct":4,"why":"The exam checks that you can attach each hormone to its gland. Melatonin comes from the pineal gland, a small structure sitting deep in the brain behind the thalamus, and its output climbs in darkness and falls in light. Picture the pineal as the brain's light meter and melatonin as the night signal it sends.","traps":["The liver clears and modifies many hormones, and clearing melatonin is not the same as making it.","The anterior pituitary releases growth hormone and the tropic hormones, and melatonin is not among them.","The thymus is where T cells mature, an immune organ rather than a source of melatonin.","The hypothalamus sets many rhythms and drives the pituitary, but it does not produce melatonin itself.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p106","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Schwann cells form the myelin sheath around which axons?","opts":["Only the axons of motor neurons, not sensory neurons","PNS axons","Dendrites only","Both equally","CNS axons"],"correct":1,"why":"Two different cells do the same insulating job in two different places. Schwann cells wrap axons in the peripheral nervous system, each one wrapping a single segment of a single axon, while oligodendrocytes do the job in the brain and spinal cord and one of them can serve segments of several axons. If the question is about a nerve out in a limb, think Schwann.","traps":["Schwann cells wrap peripheral axons whether the neuron carries information in or out, so sensory axons are myelinated too.",null,"Myelin insulates axons so the signal can jump between the gaps in the sheath, and dendrites are not myelinated.","Each cell type stays in its own territory, so Schwann cells are not found wrapping central axons.","Myelin in the brain and spinal cord is laid down by oligodendrocytes, not by Schwann cells."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g007","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a family pedigree, every child of an affected mother is affected, both sons and daughters, while no child of an affected father is ever affected. Which inheritance pattern does this describe?","opts":["Mitochondrial","X-linked dominant","Autosomal dominant","X-linked recessive","Y-linked"],"correct":0,"why":"This combination is the fingerprint of mitochondrial inheritance. Mitochondria travel in the cytoplasm of the egg, so a mother hands her mitochondrial DNA to every child she has, while sperm contribute essentially none. The pattern to recognise is all children of affected mothers affected, zero transmission from affected fathers, and no sex bias at all.","traps":[null,"An X-linked dominant mother passes the allele to only about half her children, and an affected father would pass it to all of his daughters.","Under autosomal dominance a heterozygous parent of either sex transmits to roughly half the children, so affected fathers would have affected children too.","An affected mother would give every son the condition, but her daughters would be unaffected carriers unless the father also carried the allele.","Y-linked traits move from father to son only, and daughters can never be affected."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p036","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Mature red blood cells (erythrocytes) circulating in a mammal are best described as which of the following?","opts":["Square-shaped","Have many mitochondria","Able to divide to produce new red blood cells","Multinucleate","Anucleate"],"correct":4,"why":"This is really asking what a mammalian red blood cell gives up to become a better oxygen carrier. During maturation it ejects its nucleus and loses its mitochondria, leaving a flexible biconcave bag of hemoglobin with the maximum room for cargo. When a question describes a cell built for one job at the cost of everything else, the choice saying it has no nucleus is the one that fits.","traps":["The mature cell is a biconcave disc, a shape that adds surface area and lets it fold through narrow capillaries.","Red blood cells drop their mitochondria along with the nucleus and run on anaerobic glycolysis, which keeps them from burning the oxygen they are carrying.","With no nucleus there is no DNA to copy, so these cells cannot divide; replacements come from stem cells in the bone marrow.","Multiple nuclei are a skeletal muscle fiber feature; a red blood cell pushes out the one nucleus it had rather than gaining extras.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev006","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"The blastopore forms during gastrulation. In which group of animals does the blastopore become the mouth?","opts":["Neither","Deuterostomes","Humans and other vertebrates","Protostomes","Both equally"],"correct":3,"why":"The names carry the answer once you translate them. Protostome means first mouth, so the blastopore, the first opening, becomes the mouth and the anus forms second. Deuterostome means second mouth, so the blastopore becomes the anus instead. Attach the rest of the pattern to each name: protostomes show spiral determinate cleavage and schizocoely, while deuterostomes show radial indeterminate cleavage and enterocoely and include the echinoderms and all chordates.","traps":["The blastopore always becomes one of the two openings of the gut, either the mouth or the anus depending on the lineage.","In deuterostomes the blastopore becomes the anus and the mouth breaks through second, which is what the prefix deutero, meaning second, is telling you.","Vertebrates, humans included, are deuterostomes, so the blastopore becomes the anus and the mouth forms later as a second opening.",null,"The fate of the blastopore is one of the defining splits between these two groups, so it cannot come out the same way in both."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-7-1cnibqa","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cell holds at the boundary between metaphase and anaphase and will not proceed. What is the checkpoint at that boundary actually monitoring?","opts":["That every kinetochore is attached to microtubules from opposite poles","That the cell has grown large enough and has the nutrients to divide","That the cleavage furrow has already begun pinching the cytoplasm inward","That the nuclear envelope has completely reassembled around both sets of chromosomes","That the DNA has finished replicating and carries no unrepaired damage"],"correct":0,"why":"Match each checkpoint to the disaster it prevents. The spindle checkpoint exists to keep a daughter cell from getting the wrong number of chromosomes, so the only thing it can be watching is attachment. Any kinetochore still unattached keeps sending a wait signal, and anaphase begins only when the last one is captured with pull coming from both poles. G1 asks about resources, G2 asks about the copy, and this one asks about the grip.","traps":[null,"Size and nutrients are the G1 checkpoint's business, because that is where the cell decides whether to commit to a whole division.","Cytokinesis follows chromosome separation rather than permitting it, and a furrow forming early would cut the cell before the chromosomes were sorted.","The nuclear envelope reassembles during telophase, which comes after anaphase, so it cannot be a condition for starting anaphase.","That is what the G2 checkpoint asks, and by metaphase the replication question has long since been settled."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-10vm1b1","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"Where are the four complexes of the electron transport chain located in a eukaryotic cell?","opts":["Cytosol, alongside the glycolytic enzymes","Intermembrane space","Inner mitochondrial membrane","Mitochondrial matrix","Outer mitochondrial membrane"],"correct":2,"why":"A proton gradient needs a membrane that protons cannot leak across, and only one mitochondrial membrane qualifies. Its folds, the cristae, pack far more chain complexes and ATP synthase into the same volume. Picture the pumps set into that folded wall, pushing protons out into the intermembrane space.","traps":["Glycolysis is cytosolic, and the chain needs a sealed membrane to build a gradient across.","Protons are pumped into this space and cytochrome c rides along its face, but the complexes sit in the membrane beside it.",null,"The matrix holds the Krebs enzymes and receives protons returning through ATP synthase, but the complexes themselves are membrane-bound.","The outer membrane is studded with porins that let small molecules pass, and it holds no chain complexes."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e006","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Whales have small pelvic bones embedded in the body wall that are not attached to any hind limb. The presence of such vestigial structures is evidence for which of the following?","opts":["Structures shrink through disuse during an animal's lifetime and the reduction is passed to its offspring","Common ancestry","Ongoing adaptation","Convergent evolution","Random mutation"],"correct":1,"why":"A vestigial structure is a reduced version of something that was fully functional in an ancestor, and the only reason it is still present is that it was inherited. Whale pelvic bones make sense if whales descend from four limbed land mammals, a conclusion that fossils such as Ambulocetus and shared skeletal anatomy support independently. Whenever a structure exists in a form that does not match any current job, think inheritance from an ancestor in which it did.","traps":["Changes an animal acquires during its own lifetime are not written back into its DNA, so disuse in one generation cannot be inherited by the next.",null,"Adaptation describes a trait being shaped for a role it plays now, while a vestigial structure is a reduced remnant of an ancestral one.","Convergent evolution explains similar structures in unrelated lineages doing the same job, and a reduced bone with no locomotor job is a different situation entirely.","Mutation supplies the raw variation evolution works with, but on its own it does not explain why a whale carries a reduced version of a land mammal's hip."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-oggnpm","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Exactly one reaction in glycolysis reduces NAD+ to NADH. Which one?","opts":["Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate to 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate split into two three-carbon sugars","1,3-bisphosphoglycerate to 3-phosphoglycerate","Glucose to glucose-6-phosphate","Phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate"],"correct":0,"why":"You are being asked to find the oxidation step. Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate is oxidized as an inorganic phosphate is attached, and the electrons pulled off land on NAD+. Look for the aldehyde turning into an acyl phosphate: that is where the electrons leave, and because one glucose gives two of these molecules, one glucose makes two NADH here.","traps":[null,"This is the cut made by aldolase, a rearrangement with no oxidation involved.","This step hands a phosphate to ADP, so what it makes is ATP rather than NADH.","This is the opening phosphorylation, which spends ATP and moves no electrons.","This is the final step of the pathway, and it also makes ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p074","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Salivary amylase released into the mouth begins the chemical digestion of which class of nutrient?","opts":["Cellulose (plant fiber)","Nucleic acids","Starch (carbs)","Proteins","Fats"],"correct":2,"why":"Chemical digestion of one nutrient class starts before food even leaves the mouth. Salivary amylase, also called ptyalin, cuts the alpha bonds in starch into shorter chains and maltose, then stops once stomach acid denatures it, and pancreatic amylase finishes the job in the small intestine. Carbohydrate is the only class with a mouth stage, so the choice naming starch is the one to take.","traps":["Cellulose is glucose linked by beta bonds, and no human enzyme can cut those, which is why it passes through as fiber.","Nucleases come from the pancreas and work on nucleic acids in the small intestine.",null,"Protein digestion begins in the stomach with pepsin once acid unfolds the protein; nothing in saliva attacks peptide bonds.","Fat digestion is mostly a small intestine job handled by bile and pancreatic lipase."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1d483j","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Glucose taken into an intestinal epithelial cell across the apical membrane then leaves across the basolateral membrane into the blood, moving down its concentration gradient through a GLUT carrier. That exit step is:","opts":["Exocytosis","Primary active transport","Simple diffusion","Facilitated diffusion","Secondary active transport"],"correct":3,"why":"The same sugar uses two different mechanisms on two faces of one cell, and that contrast is the point. Entry at the apical side is uphill and borrows the sodium gradient, while exit at the basolateral side is downhill through a carrier with no energy spent, which is facilitated diffusion. At every step ask two things: which way does the gradient run, and is a protein involved.","traps":["Glucose leaves through a carrier protein, not packaged inside a vesicle.","No ATP is hydrolyzed at this step, the pump spending ATP in this cell is working on sodium.","Glucose is polar and bulky, so it needs a carrier rather than the bare bilayer.",null,"That describes the apical entry step, the exit has no gradient to borrow from."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-yh7561","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cell takes DNA damage during G1. The p53 protein accumulates and the cell halts before it can enter S phase. What does p53 switch on to produce that halt?","opts":["Cyclin D, which pushes the cell through the restriction point sooner","Telomerase, which lengthens the chromosome ends before replication starts","Separase, which cleaves the cohesin holding the sister chromatids together","p21, an inhibitor of cyclin-CDK complexes, which holds the cell in G1","The anaphase-promoting complex, which strips cyclin B out of the cell"],"correct":3,"why":"This tests whether you can follow a stop signal down to the machine it actually stops. Cyclin-CDK complexes are the accelerator, so any checkpoint arrest has to work by blocking one of them. Damaged DNA stabilizes p53, p53 turns on p21, and p21 binds and inhibits the cyclin-CDK that would otherwise carry the cell into S phase. Repair then happens, or the cell is steered into apoptosis, which is why p53 is lost in so many cancers.","traps":["Cyclin D drives the cell forward past the restriction point, which is the opposite of what a damage response needs.","Lengthening telomeres does nothing about damage in the middle of a chromosome, and telomerase is not part of the damage checkpoint.","Cohesin cleavage happens at the start of anaphase, far downstream of a G1 decision about whether to replicate at all.",null,"The anaphase-promoting complex acts during mitosis to bring it to an end, so it cannot be what blocks entry into S phase."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A 5.0% (w/w) NaCl solution has a measured density of 1.04 g/mL at 20 °C, and the molar mass of NaCl is 58.4 g/mol. Assuming the solution is at the same temperature and well mixed, what is the molarity of NaCl in this solution?","opts":["0.89 M","0.45 M","1.04 M","1.78 M","5.0 M"],"correct":0,"why":"Per 1.00 L: mass = 1040 g, mass NaCl = 0.050 x 1040 = 52.0 g, mol = 52.0/58.4 = 0.890 mol. Therefore M = 0.89 mol/L. Skipping the density factor gives ~0.86, while doubling gives 1.78.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gv-e4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In an operating galvanic cell at 25 °C, electrons flow externally from anode to cathode, while ions flow through the salt bridge to maintain electrical neutrality in each half-cell. Cations from the salt-bridge electrolyte (e.g., K+) migrate to balance charge buildup. In which direction do these cations move?","opts":["Toward the anode","They do not move","Both directions equally","Toward the cathode","Toward the more dilute side"],"correct":3,"why":"At the cathode, reduction consumes positive ions (e.g., Cu^(2+) -> Cu), creating a buildup of negative charge. Cations from the salt bridge flow toward the cathode to neutralize this; anions flow to the anode.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"originals:ab-9b3bacee93","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A buffer made from a weak acid with pKa 4.76 has a pH of 3.76. What is the ratio of conjugate base to weak acid?","opts":["1 to 2","1 to 10","1 to 1","1 to 100","10 to 1"],"correct":1,"why":"pH minus pKa is -1, so log(base/acid) = -1 and base/acid = 0.1. A pH below the pKa means the acid form dominates, and each pH unit is a factor of ten.","traps":["The relationship is logarithmic. One pH unit below the pKa means ten times more acid.",null,"Equal amounts would give a pH equal to the pKa.","One pH unit is a factor of ten, not one hundred.","That ratio would put the pH one unit ABOVE the pKa. A pH below the pKa means there is more acid than base."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"gchem-a:x7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A 0.10 M weak acid HA has Ka = 1×10⁻⁴. % dissociation?","opts":["0.032%","0.32%","3.2%","10%","32%"],"correct":2,"why":"x = √(Ka·C) = √(10⁻⁵) = 3.16×10⁻³. %diss = (3.16×10⁻³ / 0.10)×100 = 3.16% ≈ 3.2%.","traps":["That is the fraction dissociated written as though it were already a percent, so the factor of one hundred never got applied.","That comes from dividing the 3.2×10⁻³ by 1.0 M instead of by the 0.10 M actually given.",null,"Ten percent comes from taking the square root of Ka alone and leaving the concentration out of the root.","Those digits are ten times too large, which is the root of 10⁻³ rather than 10⁻⁵."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:b-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Which has the SHORTEST C−C bond?","opts":["Cyclopropane (strained ring)","Ethene (C=C)","Ethyne (C≡C)","Benzene (1.5 order)","Ethane (C-C)"],"correct":2,"why":"Bond length follows bond order: the more pairs shared between two atoms, the harder they are pulled together. Ethyne's triple bond comes in near 1.20 Å, ethene's double near 1.34 Å, benzene's bonds near 1.40 Å at an order of 1.5, and ethane's single near 1.54 Å. Count the pairs between the two carbons first, then rank: the shortest bond is always the one with the highest order.","traps":["The ring is strained by its 60 degree angles rather than by short bonds, and its C-C sits near 1.51 Å.","The double bond pulls in to about 1.34 Å, shorter than ethane but not the shortest here.",null,"Benzene's bonds average about 1.40 Å, longer than ethene's double bond and well past ethyne's triple.","Ethane's single bond is the longest of the set at about 1.54 Å."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:nc4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Approximate molar solubility of MX with Ksp = 4 × 10⁻⁹ (no calculator).","opts":["~2 × 10⁻⁵ M","~6 × 10⁻⁵ M","~2 × 10⁻⁴ M","~4 × 10⁻⁹ M","~10⁻³ M"],"correct":1,"why":"For a 1:1 salt, Ksp = s², so s = √Ksp. √(4 × 10⁻⁹) = 2 × √(10⁻⁹). Note √(10⁻⁹) ≈ √(10⁻¹⁰) × √10 ≈ 10⁻⁵ × 3.16 ≈ 3 × 10⁻⁵. So s ≈ 2 × 3 × 10⁻⁵ = 6 × 10⁻⁵ M. Estimate within a factor of 2 of the exact answer.","traps":["2 × 10⁻⁵ M comes from rounding 10⁻⁴·⁵ down to 10⁻⁵, and that half power is closer to 3 × 10⁻⁵.",null,"2 × 10⁻⁴ M would give Ksp = 4 × 10⁻⁸ - one order of magnitude too high.","Ksp is NOT solubility. Ksp = s², so you must take the square root to get s.","10⁻³ M would mean Ksp = 10⁻⁶ - three orders of magnitude too high."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:gb-q1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"ΔH = −120 kJ, ΔS = −150 J/K. Spontaneous BELOW what T?","opts":["Never spontaneous at any T","120 K","Always spontaneous","800 K","80 K"],"correct":3,"why":"(−,−) quadrant: spont when T < ΔH/ΔS. T_cross = (−120,000)/(−150) = 800 K.","traps":["A negative ΔH can carry this on its own while T stays small, so there is a range below 800 K where it does run.","120 K is ΔH read off as a temperature, and the crossover needs ΔH divided by ΔS.","The pair with negative ΔH and negative ΔS has a ceiling temperature, above which the entropy penalty turns ΔG positive.",null,"80 K comes from a units slip, and 120,000 J divided by 150 J/K gives 800 K."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#1u8ztlk","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"What is the pH of a 5.0 × 10⁻³ M solution of Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C?","opts":["2","2.3","11","11.7","12"],"correct":4,"why":"[OH⁻] = 2 × 5.0 × 10⁻³ = 1.0 × 10⁻² M. pOH = 2, pH = 14 − 2 = 12.","traps":["That is the pOH. Finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","That is the pOH of the undoubled concentration. Double [OH⁻], then subtract the pOH from 14.","You treated the concentration as 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ. Doubling 5.0 × 10⁻ⁿ moves you up a power of ten.","You forgot that Ba(OH)₂ releases TWO hydroxides. Double the concentration before taking the log.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ca5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"A 0.500 g sample of benzoic acid is combusted in a sealed bomb calorimeter (constant volume) with heat capacity C_cal = 5.00 kJ/°C. The combustion releases 2.50 kJ of heat, all absorbed by the calorimeter assembly. What temperature change ΔT does the calorimeter experience?","opts":["2.5°C","5.0°C","25°C","12.5°C","0.50°C"],"correct":4,"why":"For a bomb calorimeter, q_rxn = -C_cal·ΔT. Solving for ΔT: ΔT = q_absorbed/C_cal = 2.50 kJ / 5.00 kJ/°C = 0.500°C. Note that constant-volume calorimetry uses the calorimeter constant C_cal directly, not m·c, since C_cal already lumps in all components.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-combined-gas#1h2bnxt","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A gas occupies 4 L at 2 atm and 27 °C. What is its volume at 3 atm and 127 °C?","opts":["3.56 L","12.5 L","2 L","8 L","2.67 L"],"correct":0,"why":"P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2 in Kelvin. V2 = 4 × (2/3) × (400/300) = 3.56 L.","traps":[null,"You used Celsius in the ratio. Convert both temperatures to Kelvin first.","Temperature ratio is upside down. Hotter gas expands, so volume moves with temperature.","Pressure ratio is upside down. More pressure squeezes the gas, so volume moves opposite to pressure.","You ignored the temperature change. Both P and T changed here."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ks4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"Two sparingly soluble salts at 25°C are compared: AgCl (Ksp = 1.8 x 10^-10, dissolves as Ag+ + Cl-) and PbI2 (Ksp = 9.8 x 10^-9, dissolves as Pb^2+ + 2 I-). Note the different stoichiometries: s for AgCl satisfies Ksp = s^2, while s for PbI2 satisfies Ksp = 4s^3. Which salt is MORE soluble in pure water?","opts":["Equal because Ksp values are similar","Both are completely insoluble","AgCl","Cannot determine without comparing s values","PbI2"],"correct":4,"why":"AgCl: s = sqrt(1.8e-10) = 1.3 x 10^-5 M. PbI2: s = (9.8e-9/4)^(1/3) = 1.34 x 10^-3 M. PbI2 is ~100x more soluble, even though Ksp values look 'close' on a log scale. Ksp alone cannot rank solubility when stoichiometries differ.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gl-e11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A fixed sample of ideal gas initially has P1 = 2.0 atm, V1 = 10. L, and T1 = 300. K. The gas is then warmed and allowed to expand to a final volume V2 = 20. L at a final temperature T2 = 600. K. Using the combined gas law, what is the final pressure P2?","opts":["2.0 atm","3.0 atm","4.0 atm","1.0 atm","0.50 atm"],"correct":0,"why":"Combined gas law: P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2, so P2 = P1 x (V1/V2) x (T2/T1) = 2.0 x (10/20) x (600/300) = 2.0 atm. The volume doubles (halving P) but T doubles (doubling P), so P stays the same.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#173pz7a","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 0.5 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 27 °C and 1 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["12.3 L","1250 L","18.5 L","24.6 L","1.11 L"],"correct":0,"why":"T = 27 + 273 = 300 K. V = nRT / P = (0.5)(0.0821)(300) / 1 = 12.3 L.","traps":[null,"You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You dropped the moles. n belongs on top with R and T.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_el-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"An electroplating bath of CuSO4 deposits Cu via Cu^2+ + 2 e- -> Cu(s) at a constant current of 0.50 A. To deposit exactly 1.0 g of Cu (M = 63.5 g/mol) using F = 96485 C/mol, compute the time required from t = nFm/(IM) and convert to minutes.","opts":["50 min","200 min","400 min","100 min","25 min"],"correct":3,"why":"Moles Cu = 1.0/63.5 = 0.01575; moles e- = 0.0315; charge Q = 0.0315 x 96485 = 3039 C; t = Q/I = 3039/0.50 = 6078 s ~ 101 min ~ 100 min.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_rl-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction A -> products at 298 K has [A]_0 = 2.0 M and rate constant k = 0.10 s^(-1). Using the first-order half-life formula t_(1/2) = 0.693/k (which is independent of [A]_0), how long does it take for [A] to reach 1.0 M?","opts":["6.93 s","10 s","0.693 s","3.5 s","20 s"],"correct":0,"why":"t_(1/2) = ln(2)/k = 0.693/0.10 s^(-1) = 6.93 s. First-order half-life is independent of [A]_0. Confusing 1/k with the formula gives 10 s.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ek4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"The reaction 2A(g) ⇌ B(g) has K_forward = 4.0 at 350 K. Reversing the reaction inverts K; halving the coefficients takes the square root of K. For the REVERSE reaction B ⇌ 2A (same coefficients, opposite direction), what is the new K?","opts":["16","0.25","4.0","2.0","−4.0"],"correct":1,"why":"K_reverse = 1/K_forward = 1/4.0 = 0.25. Squaring would correspond to doubling coefficients; taking square root would correspond to halving coefficients. Equilibrium constants are never negative.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-e10","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Mix 25 mL 0.20 M HCl + 50 mL 0.10 M NaOH. Final pH?","opts":["0.7","1","7","11","13"],"correct":2,"why":"Count moles, not molarity: 0.025 L times 0.20 M gives 0.0050 mol of HCl, and 0.050 L times 0.10 M gives 0.0050 mol of NaOH. Equal moles of a strong acid and a strong base cancel completely, so nothing is left but NaCl, and neither of its ions tugs on water. That is why the answer is neutral, and it is also why a smaller volume of a stronger acid can still be an exact match for a larger volume of a weaker one.","traps":["0.7 is the pH of the 0.20 M HCl on its own, before the base arrived and before the volume tripled.","A pH near 1 assumes the acid survives the mixing, and every mole of HCl here meets a mole of NaOH.",null,"A pH of 11 is mildly basic, which would take a small excess of NaOH, and the two are matched mole for mole.","A pH of 13 assumes leftover hydroxide, and the base does not outnumber the acid in this flask."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lr-e12","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"For Mg(s) + 2 HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g), a strip of magnesium (24.0 g, MW = 24 → 1.00 mol Mg) is added to 36.5 g HCl (MW = 36.5 → 1.00 mol HCl). Compute mole/coefficient ratios to identify the limiting reactant.","opts":["Cannot determine","Both simultaneously","H₂ is limiting","HCl is limiting","Mg is limiting"],"correct":3,"why":"Ratios: Mg → 1.0/1 = 1.0; HCl → 1.0/2 = 0.5. Smaller ratio = HCl limits. With HCl limiting, only 0.5 mol Mg reacts (consuming 0.5 mol Mg), leaving 0.5 mol Mg unreacted in excess.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-freezing-point#x56iha","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"What is the freezing point of a 1.5 m aqueous solution of KBr? (Kf for water = 1.86 °C/m; assume ideal behavior)","opts":["-8.37 °C","-2.79 °C","-1.54 °C","-5.58 °C","5.58 °C"],"correct":3,"why":"ΔTf = i Kf m = (2)(1.86)(1.5) = 5.58 °C. Freezing point = 0 − 5.58 = -5.58 °C.","traps":["You counted one ion too many. Write the dissociation out and count the particles.","You forgot the van't Hoff factor. KBr splits into 2 ions, so i = 2.","You used Kb (boiling). Freezing uses Kf = 1.86.",null,"Sign slip. Solute LOWERS the freezing point, so the answer is below 0 °C."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ar-e2","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"An Arrhenius experiment varies the temperature of a first-order reaction from 300 K to 350 K while holding the activation energy constant at E_a = 75 kJ/mol. According to k = A·exp(−E_a/RT), how does the rate constant k change as T increases?","opts":["k decreases with T","k inversely proportional to T","k oscillates with T","k stays the same regardless of T","k increases with T"],"correct":4,"why":"As T increases, −E_a/(RT) becomes less negative, so exp(−E_a/RT) increases. Therefore k increases with temperature for any positive E_a.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-molecular-formula#14xj1a3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A compound has the empirical formula CH₂ and a molar mass of 84 g/mol. What is its molecular formula?","opts":["C₁₂H₂₄","C₆H₁₂","CH₂","C₇H₁₄","C₅H₁₀"],"correct":1,"why":"Empirical mass = 14. 84 / 14 = 6, so multiply every subscript by 6: C₆H₁₂.","traps":["You doubled the multiplier. Check the empirical formula mass.",null,"That is the empirical formula again. Divide the molar mass by the empirical mass to get the multiplier.","Multiplier is one too high. Molar mass divided by empirical mass must come out to a whole number.","Multiplier is one too low. Compute the empirical formula mass carefully, then divide."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A 0.10 mol methane sample combusts in a bomb calorimeter (C_cal = 5.0 kJ/°C). T rises 18°C. ΔH per mol?","opts":["−180 kJ/mol","−900 kJ/mol","−1800 kJ/mol","−450 kJ/mol","−90 kJ/mol"],"correct":1,"why":"q = C_cal × ΔT = 5.0 × 18 = 90 kJ released. Per mol: 90/0.10 = 900 kJ. ΔH = −900 kJ/mol (matches the actual ~890 kJ/mol for CH₄ combustion).","traps":["That divides by 0.50 mol instead of 0.10 mol; only a tenth of a mole burned.",null,"That divides by 0.050 mol, half the sample; 0.10 mol burned.","That uses half the heat, or 0.20 mol; the calorimeter took in 90 kJ and 0.10 mol reacted.","That is the total heat released in the bomb, stopping before it is spread over the 0.10 mol."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-limiting#5y28dw","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"2 Na + Cl₂ → 2 NaCl\n\nIf 2 mol of Na reacts with 4 mol of Cl₂, how many moles of NaCl can form?","opts":["3 mol","1 mol","2 mol","6 mol","8 mol"],"correct":2,"why":"Na: 2 / 2 = 1. Cl₂: 4 / 1 = 4. The smaller one limits. Product = 1 × 2 = 2 mol.","traps":["Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You found the limiting amount but did not multiply by the product coefficient.",null,"You added the reactants. Moles are not conserved; use the mole ratio from the balanced equation.","You built the answer from the reagent in EXCESS. The smaller moles-per-coefficient runs out first and sets the yield."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_hb4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Acetylene (H−C≡C−H) is the linear hydrocarbon used in welding torches that produces flames near 3100°C in oxygen. Counting the C≡C triple bond and both C−H bonds, how many π bonds are present in one molecule of acetylene?","opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":2,"why":"Each triple bond consists of one σ + two π bonds, while each single bond is purely σ. Acetylene therefore has 3 σ bonds (2 C-H + 1 C-C σ) and 2 π bonds, both within the C≡C unit. The carbons are sp hybridized, leaving two perpendicular 2p orbitals to overlap into the two π bonds.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:bf6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A buffer is made from 0.20 M NH₃ + 0.20 M NH₄Cl (pKb of NH₃ = 4.74). What is the pH?","opts":["4.74","4.98","7.00","9.26","11.26"],"correct":3,"why":"For an NH₃/NH₄⁺ buffer: pH = pKa(NH₄⁺) + log([NH₃]/[NH₄⁺]). pKa(NH₄⁺) = 14 − pKb(NH₃) = 14 − 4.74 = 9.26. With equal concs: log(1) = 0. pH = 9.26.","traps":["That's pKb, not pKa.","That treats the solution as ammonium chloride alone, ignoring the ammonia that makes it a buffer.","Equal amounts of a base and its conjugate acid do not land at neutral, since the pH sits at the pKa of the ammonium ion.",null,"That is the pH of 0.20 M ammonia by itself, which ignores the ammonium ion already in the flask."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-dilution#1siwg7v","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"How many mL of water must be ADDED to 100 mL of 6 M HCl to make a 1.5 M solution?","opts":["300 mL","400 mL","301 mL","500 mL","25 mL"],"correct":0,"why":"M1V1 = M2V2, so V2 = (6 × 100) / 1.5 = 400 mL total. Water added = 400 − 100 = 300 mL.","traps":[null,"That is the FINAL volume. The question asks how much water you add, so subtract the volume you started with.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You added the starting volume on top of the final volume. M1V1 = M2V2 already gives the total.","You divided the volume. Diluting makes the volume bigger, not smaller."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#yt5pvw","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 48 g of CH₄? (molar mass 16 g/mol)","opts":["1.81 × 10²⁴","12","4.52 × 10²³","2.89 × 10²⁵","7.22 × 10²⁴"],"correct":4,"why":"48 g / 16 g/mol = 3 mol CH₄. Times 4 hydrogen per molecule = 12 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 7.22 × 10²⁴.","traps":["That is the number of MOLECULES. Each CH₄ holds 4 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 4.","That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them.","You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.","You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:lw-e3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Lone pairs on central P in PCl₃?","opts":["0","1","2","3","10"],"correct":1,"why":"P has 5 valence; 3 P−Cl bonds use 3, leaving 2 e⁻ = 1 LP.","traps":["With no lone pair, PCl₃ would be a flat triangle, and the pyramid shape is the tell that phosphorus is holding a pair.",null,"Two leftover electrons make one pair, so the leftover count still has to be halved.","Three is what each chlorine carries, while phosphorus spent three of its five valence electrons on bonds and keeps one pair.","Ten is every lone pair in the molecule, nine on the chlorines plus one on phosphorus, and the question asks about the central atom."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:nu6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A radioactive sample of 80 g decays to 5 g in 24 days. What is its half-life?","opts":["3 days","12 days","4 days","8 days","6 days"],"correct":4,"why":"80 → 40 → 20 → 10 → 5 = 4 half-lives. 24 days / 4 = 6 days per half-life.","traps":["A 3 day half-life fits eight halvings into 24 days, which would take the sample far below 5 g.","A 12 day half-life allows just two halvings, which would leave 20 g.","A 4 day half-life gives six half-lives in 24 days, leaving about 1.25 g.","An 8 day half-life gives only three half-lives, leaving 10 g rather than 5 g.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ph-e11","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"At 25 °C, 50.0 mL of 0.10 M HCl is mixed with 50.0 mL of 0.10 M NaOH in a beaker, producing 100.0 mL of solution containing only NaCl and water. Considering that HCl + NaOH -> NaCl + H2O fully neutralizes (equimolar strong acid + strong base), what is the final pH?","opts":["1.0","3.0","7.0","10","13"],"correct":2,"why":"Equal moles of strong acid and strong base produce a salt of a strong acid and strong base (NaCl), which is pH-neutral. The resulting solution is pure NaCl(aq) at pH = 7.0 at 25 °C.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-1xnyxrx","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"Classify the alkene geometry drawn below using CIP (E/Z) nomenclature.","opts":["R","S","Z","Neither E nor Z (no geometric isomerism)","E"],"correct":2,"why":"Each sp2 carbon has methyl > H; the two methyls are cis (same side), so Z. Keyed correct=0 (Z) is right.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C\\C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-c74e4a1474","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"Which substituent in the para position makes a phenol the most acidic?","opts":["A hydrogen, meaning unsubstituted phenol","A nitro group (NO2)","A methoxy group (OCH3)","An amino group (NH2)","A methyl group (CH3)"],"correct":1,"why":"Ask what the substituent does to the phenoxide. A para nitro group pulls the negative charge all the way onto its own oxygens by resonance, so the anion is far more stable and the phenol is more acidic. Groups that push electron density in do the opposite.","traps":["An electron-withdrawing group beats no substituent.",null,"A methoxy group DONATES electron density by resonance, which makes the phenoxide less stable.","An amino group is a strong electron donor by resonance, which makes the phenol less acidic.","An alkyl group donates electron density, which makes the phenol slightly LESS acidic."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-15myqzk","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"Benzamide (C6H5CONH2) is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide carbonyl to a methylene while retaining the C-N bond, converting C(=O)NH2 into CH2NH2 to give benzylamine (NCc1ccccc1). Distractors: benzyl alcohol (C-N cleavage), aniline (wrong skeleton), benzaldehyde (incomplete), and toluene (full deoxygenation). Exactly one correct.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(N)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"own-organic:ace-0003","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"A secondary cation becomes a tertiary cation partway through an addition. What actually moved?","opts":["A bromide ion arriving from the solvent","Nothing moved, the positive charge simply spread out on its own","A hydrogen carrying both of its bonding electrons","A hydrogen carrying none of its electrons","A methyl group that moved without taking its bonding electrons"],"correct":2,"why":"The group that migrates carries its pair with it, so the carbon it left is the one that ends up positive. That is the whole reason the charge lands one carbon over and lands somewhere better.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"own-organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1jzd8nw","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Menthyl chloride (shown) undergoes E2 with sodium ethoxide. The leaving Cl is equatorial in the stable chair, so the ring must flip to an all-axial chair before elimination. Which alkene is the MAJOR (essentially only) product?","opts":["CC1CC=C(C(C)C)CC1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(O)C1","C=C1CCC(C(C)C)CC1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)CC1","CC1C=CC(C(C)C)CC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Verified: classic menthyl chloride E2. With Cl axial only the C6 H is anti-periplanar (the isopropyl-bearing C2 H is equatorial), so the less-substituted 2-menthene (option A, double bond between unsubstituted ring carbons) is the sole product, not the Zaitsev menthene at the iPr carbon (option B).","traps":null,"qs":"CC1CC[C@@H](C(C)C)[C@@H](Cl)C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ykycfl","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A student wants to make a six-membered cyclic beta-ketoester by a Dieckmann condensation. Of the diesters below, which gives a six-membered ring (cyclohexanone) product, while diethyl succinate (shown) fails to give a useful Dieckmann product?","opts":["Diethyl pimelate (1,7-diester)","Diethyl malonate (1,3-diester)","Diethyl oxalate (1,2-diester)","Diethyl succinate (1,4-diester)","Diethyl adipate (1,6-diester)"],"correct":0,"why":"Ring size tracks chain length: a 1,7-diester (pimelate) closes to a favorable six-membered ring. Succinate (drawn, 1,4) would form a strained three-membered ring and fails, adipate (1,6) gives a five-membered ring, and malonate/oxalate are too short to cyclize. Only pimelate gives the six-membered cyclohexanone beta-ketoester.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)OCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1xtkdzx","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"Cumene (isopropylbenzene) is to be converted to benzoic acid in one oxidative step. Which reagent/condition accomplishes the side-chain (benzylic) oxidation to -COOH?","opts":["Cold dilute KMnO4 (Baeyer test conditions)","H2 / Pd-C","Br2 / FeBr3","Hot KMnO4, then H3O+","NaBH4 / CH3OH"],"correct":3,"why":"Hot (vigorous) KMnO4 performs benzylic oxidation: any alkyl group bearing a benzylic C-H is degraded to a ring-attached -COOH, so isopropyl becomes -COOH (benzoic acid). H2/Pd reduces, Br2/FeBr3 brominates the ring, NaBH4 reduces carbonyls only, and cold dilute KMnO4 only dihydroxylates alkenes (none present).","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-f00uzq","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"1,3-Butadiene (drawn) reacts with 1 equivalent of HBr. Protonation gives a resonance-stabilized allylic cation, and under thermodynamic control the 1,4-addition product dominates. Which structure is that major 1,4-product (note its alkene geometry)?","opts":["C/C=C/CBr","CCC(Br)C","C=CC(C)Br","C/C=C\\CBr","C=CCCBr"],"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives allylic cation CH3-CH(+)...CH=CH2; bromide capture at the 1,4-terminus gives 1-bromo-2-butene, with the more stable (E) alkene under thermodynamic control: C/C=C/CBr. (Z) is wrong geometry; C=CC(C)Br is the kinetic 1,2-product; CCC(C)Br (2-bromobutane) is wrong skeleton; C=CCCBr (4-bromo-1-butene) is anti-Markovnikov.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC=C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1bw83st","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene) is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (hv), limited to monobromination. What is the major product?","opts":["CC(C)c1ccc(Br)cc1","BrCC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1Br","CC(C)(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(Br)c1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":3,"why":"NBS/hv abstracts the most stabilized H. Cumene's single benzylic C-H is on the central carbon; the resulting radical is both tertiary AND benzylic (doubly stabilized), far weaker than the primary methyl C-H. Br installs there: (2-bromopropan-2-yl)benzene (opt[0]). opt[1] brominates a primary methyl (non-benzylic, minor), opt[2]/opt[3] are ring (EAS) products, opt[4] is a dibrominated over-reaction.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-11y5yyy","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Diethyl oxalate (drawn) is treated with ethyl acetate and sodium ethoxide, followed by acidic workup. What is the major crossed-Claisen product?","opts":["CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","O=CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)OCC"],"correct":4,"why":"Diethyl oxalate has no alpha H and acts only as the electrophilic acceptor. Distractors: self-Claisen product, aldehyde variant, beta-hydroxy diester, double-addition over-condensation. All valid, exactly one correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)C(=O)OCC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-4hs54s","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"Methyl benzoate (drawn below) is heated with a large excess of ethanol and a trace of H2SO4, converting it to ethyl benzoate. What name best describes this reaction?","opts":["Fischer esterification","Transesterification","Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis","Saponification","Claisen condensation"],"correct":1,"why":"Converting one ester into another by swapping the alkoxy group (-OCH3 to -OCH2CH3) under acid catalysis with excess alcohol is transesterification. Fischer starts from an acid; saponification needs hydroxide; hydrolysis gives the acid; Claisen forms a beta-keto ester.","traps":null,"qs":"COC(=O)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1ix28dz","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Consider the bromonium-ion mechanism of Br2 addition to an alkene. Which statement BEST describes the curved arrow for the ring-opening step that sets the stereochemistry (substrate shown: cyclohexene)?","opts":["Bromide attacks a bromonium-ion carbon from the face opposite the C - Br+ bond (backside), giving anti (trans) addition","Bromide attacks the same face of the ring that the bromonium bridge occupies, so both bromines end up on one side, giving syn (cis) addition","Bromide adds to the less hindered carbon by a concerted syn pathway, giving the cis product","A free, planar carbocation forms first, so the bromide can add to either face equally (no stereochemical preference)","The pi electrons attack bromide directly, expelling Br+ from the molecule and giving a Markovnikov rearranged monobromide product"],"correct":0,"why":"The stereochemistry-setting step is SN2-like backside attack of bromide on the bridged bromonium carbon, inverting that center and placing the two bromines anti (trans). option A is correct; option B/option 5 give the wrong (syn/cis) outcome, option D reverses the electron flow, option E wrongly describes an open carbocation. Identifies a single arrow only (no full mechanism), substrate is drawn, in DAT scope.","traps":null,"qs":"C1CCC=CC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-jr6ldy","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"1,3-Butadiene reacts with one equivalent of p-benzoquinone in a Diels-Alder reaction. Which structure is the major mono-adduct?","opts":["O=C1C=CC(=O)C=C1.C=CCC=C","O=C1CCC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1C=CC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","OC1=CC=C(O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1C=CC(=O)c2ccccc21"],"correct":2,"why":"p-Benzoquinone is a strong dienophile: butadiene adds across ONE of its C=C bonds, leaving the other enedione double bond intact and forming a new fused cyclohexene ring (mono-adduct, formula C10H10O2). option B is over-reduced (the retained enedione C=C is gone), option C shows no reaction, option D is the hydroquinone (wrong tautomer/aromatization), option E is a wrong (naphthoquinone) skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC=C.O=C1C=CC(=O)C=C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-7d9eh6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"(Bromomethyl)cyclopentane (shown) is solvolyzed in water, giving a single rearranged alcohol as the major product. The primary cation undergoes ring expansion. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CC1=CCCC1","C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","OCC1CCCC1","C=C1CCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Ionization gives a primary exocyclic cation; a ring C-C bond migrates (ring expansion) to give a secondary cyclohexyl cation, trapped by water to give cyclohexanol (OC1CCCCC1). Cyclopentylmethanol (OCC1CCCC1) is the unrearranged product; methylenecyclopentane, 1-methylcyclopentene, and cyclohexene are elimination products, not the alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"BrCC1CCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1miej84","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"2-methylpropene (isobutylene, shown) is treated with dilute aqueous H2SO4. Which product predominates, and by what rule?","opts":["methylpropene oxide (an epoxide)","tert-butanol (2-methylpropan-2-ol), by Markovnikov addition","a 1,2-diol, because dilute acid is an oxidant","isobutane, because the alkene is reduced","isobutanol (2-methylpropan-1-ol), by anti-Markovnikov addition"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration is Markovnikov: protonation gives the more stable tertiary carbocation at the central carbon, and water adds there to give tert-butanol. Anti-Markovnikov (giving isobutanol) would require hydroboration-oxidation. Dilute acid does not oxidize, reduce, or epoxidize the alkene.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)=C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1k5glo1","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"In a crossed aldol reaction, ethyl acetoacetate (drawn) is often chosen as the nucleophilic partner instead of a simple ketone. Which statement best explains its behavior under mild base?","opts":["It undergoes dehydration before any C-C bond forms","Its central CH2 is flanked by two carbonyls, so it is readily and selectively deprotonated to a stabilized enolate that acts as the nucleophile","Mild base removes a proton from the terminal ester oxygen to give an alkoxide, and that oxygen, not the central carbon, is what attacks the partner carbonyl","Mild base reduces its ketone to an alcohol before the aldol step","It has no alpha hydrogens between its two carbonyl groups, so it can serve only as the electrophilic partner in the crossed aldol and never as the nucleophile"],"correct":1,"why":"The central CH2 sits between a ketone and an ester carbonyl, so it is selectively deprotonated to a doubly stabilized enolate (the active-methylene nucleophile). option B is false (it clearly has acidic alpha H), option C misplaces deprotonation, option D/option E invent reduction/pre-dehydration.","traps":null,"qs":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1bxsg2d","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"4-Methyl-1-pentene is treated with HBr and a peroxide (radical conditions). Identify the major product.","opts":["CC(C)CC(Br)CBr","CC(C)CC(C)Br","CCC(Br)C(C)C","CC(C)CCCBr","CCCC(C)(C)Br"],"correct":3,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov (peroxide) addition: Br radical adds to the terminal CH2 to give the more stable secondary radical at C2, then H caps C2, so Br is on the primary terminal carbon (1-bromo-4-methylpentane). The isopropyl branch is a spectator. The Markovnikov option puts Br on C2; the rest are rearranged, falsely tertiary, or dibromide structures.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCC(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-75v5a0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"(S)-2-phenylpropanoyl chloride (drawn) is treated with methanol. The bond to the stereocenter is never broken. What is the major product?","opts":["COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Nucleophilic acyl substitution occurs only at the carbonyl carbon; the alpha stereocenter is a spectator, so configuration is retained. Recognition-level stereo, in DAT scope (no mechanism drawn).","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(Cl)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1cv6f2u","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: (E)-1-phenylpropene (trans-beta-methylstyrene) is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H2O. Which set of products forms?","opts":["CC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","CCC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)O.O=C(O)c1ccccc1","C=O.CC(=O)c1ccccc1","C=O.O=Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the internal C=C. The phenyl-bearing carbon becomes benzaldehyde and the methyl-bearing carbon becomes acetaldehyde.","traps":null,"qs":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1cv2c5c","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"A mixture of acetone (bp 56 C), water (bp 100 C), and toluene (bp 111 C) is purified by simple distillation (no azeotrope effects assumed). Which component distills over and collects in the receiving flask FIRST?","opts":["Toluene (bp 111 C)","Water and toluene co-distill together first","Acetone (bp 56 C)","Water (bp 100 C)","The component with the largest molar mass"],"correct":2,"why":"In distillation the lowest-boiling component vaporizes most readily and is collected first; acetone at 56 C boils well below water and toluene, so it distills over first.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fhb3ww","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":1,"q":"In the Wittig reaction of phenylacetaldehyde (drawn) with a phosphorus ylide, the first carbon-carbon bond-forming step is the nucleophilic addition of the ylide. Which single curved arrow correctly represents this first bond formation?","opts":["From a lone pair on the carbonyl oxygen to the ylide carbon, making oxygen the nucleophile","From the carbanionic ylide carbon to the carbonyl carbon of the aldehyde","From the aldehyde C-H bond to the ylide carbon","From the phosphorus lone pair to the carbonyl carbon, forming the new bond at phosphorus","From the C=O pi bond to the phosphorus atom"],"correct":1,"why":"The ylide carbon is the nucleophile; the first bond-forming arrow goes from that carbanionic carbon to the electrophilic carbonyl carbon, forming the betaine/oxaphosphetane C-C bond. Phosphorus binds oxygen only later, and the carbonyl carbon (not P) is the first electrophile attacked.","traps":null,"qs":"O=CCc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1gnnsax","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"The molecule drawn is the cyclopropenyl (cyclopropenium) cation. Apply Huckel's rule: count the pi electrons and classify the ring.","opts":["0 pi electrons; non-aromatic","2 pi electrons (4n+2 with n=0); aromatic","4 pi electrons (4n with n=1); antiaromatic","2 pi electrons; antiaromatic","6 pi electrons; aromatic"],"correct":1,"why":"One C=C provides 2 pi electrons and the cationic carbon is an empty p orbital completing cyclic overlap. 2 pi electrons = 4n+2 with n=0 -> aromatic, the textbook reason this tiny cation is unusually stable. Option 0 is correct; the 4-, 0-, and 6-electron counts are wrong, and 2 pi electrons cannot be antiaromatic.","traps":null,"qs":"[CH+]1C=C1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-mnw0d4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Cyclopentene (drawn) is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic). The two new oxygens add to the same face of the ring. Which structure is the product?","opts":["O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","C1CC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O","OC1CCCC1","O=C1CCCC1O"],"correct":2,"why":"Cold dilute KMnO4 performs syn-dihydroxylation; same-face addition on the ring gives the cis-1,2-diol (meso cis-cyclopentane-1,2-diol), opt 0. C is the epoxide (peracid product), D is a mono-ol, E is an over-oxidized alpha-hydroxy ketone.","traps":null,"qs":"C1=CCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-qjs2wp","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"Neopentyl bromide (1-bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane, drawn) is treated with sodium iodide in acetone. Which statement best explains the observed outcome?","opts":["It undergoes fast E2 elimination because acetone removes a beta-hydrogen, giving the more substituted Zaitsev alkene as the major product","It reacts rapidly by SN1 because the neighboring tert-butyl group stabilizes the primary carbocation by hyperconjugation from all three of its adjacent methyl groups","It reacts rapidly by SN2 because primary substrates always favor SN2","It rearranges to a tertiary iodide by a hydride shift from the neighboring quaternary carbon before iodide attacks, so substitution occurs at the rearranged center","The reaction is extremely slow because the adjacent quaternary carbon sterically blocks SN2 backside attack, even though the carbon bearing Br is primary"],"correct":4,"why":"Neopentyl is the textbook 'primary but SN2-resistant' case: the neighboring quaternary carbon's methyls block backside approach (Finkelstein/NaI-acetone are SN2 conditions). SN1 (option B) fails for a primary cation; option C ignores sterics; the carbon bearing Br has no beta-H positioned for E2 and NaI is not a base (option D); the carbocation/hydride-shift path (option E) does not operate under SN2 conditions.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)CBr","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-17mk9ys","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with O3, then with dimethyl sulfide (Me2S). What is the major product?","opts":["CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","O=CCCCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC(C)=O","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":3,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C, replacing each carbon with a carbonyl and opening the ring to a chain; reductive Me2S workup gives carbonyls (not acids). The methyl-bearing (disubstituted) carbon becomes a ketone, the H-bearing carbon becomes an aldehyde: 6-oxoheptanal (option A, C7H12O2 verified). option B (heptane-2,6-dione, same formula) wrongly makes both ends ketones; option C wrongly makes both aldehydes (ignores the methyl); option D is the over-oxidized acid (oxidative workup); option E is a non-cleavage alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-xtm96d","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"You need to convert the internal alkyne shown into the corresponding cis (Z) alkene WITHOUT over-reducing it to the alkane. Which reagent should you choose?","opts":["Excess H2 with Raney nickel (a highly active sponge-nickel catalyst)","H2 with Pd/C at one atmosphere in ethanol","Na metal in liquid NH3 (dissolving-metal reduction through a vinyl radical anion)","H2 with PtO2 (Adams' catalyst, a finely divided platinum oxide surface)","H2 with Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with Pb and quinoline)"],"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar's poisoned Pd catalyst stops at the alkene and delivers both H syn, giving the cis (Z) alkene. Pd/C, PtO2, and Raney Ni over-reduce to the alkane; Na/NH3 dissolving-metal gives the trans alkene.","traps":null,"qs":"CC#CC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-dp6bvf","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Hexane-2,5-dione is treated with aqueous NaOH (intramolecular aldol condensation). What is the major product?","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1C","O=C1CCCC1=O","CC1=CCC(=O)C1","CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1CCC(=O)C1=O"],"correct":3,"why":"An enolate at a terminal methyl attacks the other carbonyl intramolecularly to close a favorable five-membered ring; dehydration gives conjugated 3-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-one (C6H8O). Keyed structure correct. Distractors: cyclopentane-1,2-dione (no aldol), a regioisomeric enone, a methyl 1,2-dione, and an extra-methyl ring. All valid, unique.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CCC(C)=O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1jgftmx","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol (shown) is treated with hot concentrated H2SO4. Which alkene is the major product?","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","C=C(C)C(C)(C)C","C=CC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Loss of water gives a secondary cation adjacent to a quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift gives a more stable tertiary cation, then Zaitsev elimination gives tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (option A). option C is the non-rearranged terminal alkene, option B is a rearranged less-substituted alkene, option D is substitution (no Cl), option E is saturated.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1nni7r4","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (shown) is converted to a product whose IR shows a strong carbonyl absorption near 1715 cm-1 and whose 1H NMR shows a 3H singlet near 2.1 ppm but NO aldehydic proton near 9-10 ppm. Which reagents produce this compound?","opts":["H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","1. Disiamylborane, 2. H2O2/NaOH","1. BH3, 2. H2O2/NaOH","1. O3, 2. (CH3)2S","H2, Lindlar catalyst"],"correct":0,"why":"A 3H singlet near 2.1 ppm plus a carbonyl band and no aldehyde proton identifies a methyl ketone (CH3-C=O), i.e. 1-cyclohexylethanone, which is the Markovnikov hydration product made with Hg2+/H3O+. Hydroboration (options 2,3) would give an aldehyde with a 9-10 ppm CHO; ozonolysis cleaves; Lindlar only gives an alkene.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CC1CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-17kba84","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"3-Hexyne is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2, which adds anti across the triple bond. Which structure is the major product, including geometry?","opts":["CCC(Br)(Br)C(Br)CC","CCCC(Br)(Br)CC","CCC(Br)C(Br)CC","CC/C(Br)=C(\\Br)CC","CC/C(Br)=C(/Br)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"Anti Br2 addition places the two bromines trans. On each internal carbon Br>ethyl, so trans-Br => E. Others are over-brominated or saturated wrong skeletons. (E)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene is the answer.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC#CCC","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"qr-a:uni1477","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A race course is 7.8 kilometers long. What is that distance in meters?","opts":["0.01","78","780","1007.8","7800"],"correct":4,"why":"1 km = 1000 m, so 7.8 km × 1000 = 7800 m.","traps":["You divided by 1000 instead of multiplying; the smaller unit always gives the bigger count.","You moved the decimal one place instead of three, multiplying by 10 rather than 1000.","You multiplied by 100 instead of 1000; kilometers to meters moves three places.","You added 1000 to the 7.8 instead of multiplying the 7.8 by it.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1499","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"One delivery route has 45 stops, which is three more than twice the number of stops on a second route. How many stops does the second route have?","opts":["20","21","22","24","42"],"correct":1,"why":"'Three more than twice a number' is 2x plus 3, and it equals 45. Take the 3 off for 2x = 42, then halve for 21. Constants come off first, then you undo the coefficient.","traps":["Twice 20 is 40, and 3 more is 43, just short of the 45 given.",null,"Halved the 45 and rounded down, which never uses the 3.","Added the 3 instead of taking it off: 48 halved gives this.","Stopped one step short at 2x = 42 without halving it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fa4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"One box of tiles weighs 3 1/4 kilograms and another weighs 2 2/3 kilograms. How many kilograms do they weigh together?","opts":["5 11/12","5 3/12","5 3/7","6","6 11/12"],"correct":0,"why":"Convert: 3 1/4 = 13/4 = 39/12. 2 2/3 = 8/3 = 32/12. Sum = 71/12 = 5 11/12.","traps":[null,"You moved to twelfths but kept the original tops, 1 and 2, giving 3/12 instead of 11/12.","You added the whole numbers, then added the fraction parts straight across, 1 plus 2 over 4 plus 3.","You rescaled 1/4 as 4/12 instead of 3/12, which pushes the fraction parts up to a full extra unit.","You carried an extra whole number; 71/12 is 5 and 11/12."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp008","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"An item is marked up 20% from its cost, then discounted 25% from the marked-up price. The final price is $90. What was the ORIGINAL cost?","opts":["$72","$90","$100","$108","$120"],"correct":2,"why":"Cost C, so markup 1.20C, so discount 0.75 × 1.20C = 0.90C. 0.90C = 90 → C = 100.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1457","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Two weather stations sit at (-12, 2) and (-6, 8) on a survey grid. Measured in a straight line, about how many units apart are they?","opts":["3.46","6","8.49","12","72"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((6)² + (6)²) = √72 = 8.49.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1371","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A moving truck averages 40 miles per hour for 4.5 hours. How many miles does it cover?","opts":["8.89","140","160","180","220"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 40 × 4.5 = 180 mi.","traps":["Dividing 40 by 4.5 instead of multiplying.","Using 3.5 hours instead of 4.5.","Dropping the half hour and using 4 hours flat.",null,"Using 5.5 hours instead of the 4.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lg5","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the value of log(40) − log(4)?","opts":["log(36)","10","1","log(160)","2.66"],"correct":2,"why":"Quotient law: log(a) − log(b) = log(a/b). So log(40) − log(4) = log(10) = 1.","traps":["You subtracted the inputs instead of using the log law. log(a)−log(b) ≠ log(a−b).","You divided 40 by 4 and stopped there, and the log of that quotient still has to be taken.",null,"You used the product law and multiplied 40 by 4 instead of dividing.","That divides one log by the other. Subtracting logs divides the numbers inside them, it does not divide the logs themselves."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1261","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"What value of x satisfies 9x + 3 = x - 8?","opts":["-2.38","-1.38","-0.63","-0.38","1.38"],"correct":1,"why":"Move the x terms together and the numbers together. Subtract 1x from both sides for 8x + 3 = -8, then subtract 3 for 8x = -11. Divide by 8 and x is -11/8, which is -1.375 and rounds to -1.38. Keep the fraction exact through the work and round only at the finish, so the rounding never leaks into an earlier step.","traps":["That is -19 divided by 8, but the constants combine to -11, not -19.",null,"The 3 was added to the -8 instead of subtracted, leaving 8x = -5.","That is -3 divided by 8, so the -8 on the right never made it across.","Dividing 11 by 8 gives 1.38, but 8x = -11 makes x negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:le7","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"What value of x satisfies 5(2x − 4) = 30?","opts":["x = 7","x = 1","x = 2","x = 5","x = 3"],"correct":3,"why":"Distribute: 10x − 20 = 30. Add 20: 10x = 50. Divide: x = 5. (Or divide both sides by 5 first: 2x − 4 = 6 → 2x = 10, so x = 5.)","traps":["Both terms got multiplied by 10, but the 4 only takes the 5 out front, giving 10x − 20 = 30.","Distributing gave 10x + 20, but 5 times −4 is −20.","Substituting 2 gives 5(4 − 4) = 0, not 30.",null,"Dividing by 5 first gives 2x − 4 = 6, and stopping at 2x = 6 skips adding the 4 back."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-mixture#1sypefl","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"4 L of a 20% acid solution is mixed with 1 L of a 80% acid solution. What is the concentration of the mixture?","opts":["32%","40%","50%","68%","160%"],"correct":0,"why":"Acid: 20% of 4 plus 80% of 1 = 1.6 L. Total volume = 5 L. Concentration = 32%.","traps":[null,"You divided by the product of the volumes. Divide by the SUM: total volume.","You averaged the two percents. The mixture leans toward whichever solution you used MORE of, so weight by volume.","You swapped the volumes. Pair each concentration with its own volume.","You found the total acid but never divided by the total volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:rat1378","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"A boat runs at 30 miles per hour for 3.5 hours. How many miles does it travel?","opts":["8.57","75","90","105","135"],"correct":3,"why":"d = rt = 30 × 3.5 = 105 mi.","traps":["Dividing 30 by 3.5 instead of multiplying.","Using 2.5 hours instead of 3.5.","Dropping the half hour and using 3 hours flat.",null,"Using 4.5 hours instead of the 3.5 given."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1158","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $245 conference registration goes up 55 percent. What is the new price?","opts":["110.25","134.75","190","300","379.75"],"correct":4,"why":"New = 245 × (1 + 55/100) = 245 × 1.55 = 379.75.","traps":["That takes 55 percent off instead of putting it on, so the price moved the wrong way.","That is 55 percent of 245 on its own, the size of the increase before it goes back on the price.","You subtracted 55 dollars, and the price here is going up by 55 percent.","You added 55 dollars instead of 55 percent, and 55 percent of 245 is 134.75.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1026","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A storeroom holds 6/15 of a pallet of gloves and 2/5 of a pallet of masks. How many pallets of stock is that?","opts":["4/5","12/75","1","8/15","8/20"],"correct":0,"why":"6/15 reduces to 2/5, so this is two fifths plus two fifths, which is 4/5. Spotting that the two fractions are the same size saves the whole conversion.","traps":[null,"You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.","You reduced 6/15 to 3/5 instead of 2/5, which pushes the total up to a whole.","You put 2/5 over 15 but left the 2 on top; two fifths is 6/15.","You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 8 over 20."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_pfl1517","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A quality check flips a fair coin for each of eight cartons. What is the probability that all eight flips land heads?","opts":["0.004","0.01","0.03","0.125","1"],"correct":0,"why":"A half to the eighth power is one over two hundred and fifty six, which is about 0.004. Every flip is independent, so the chances multiply rather than add. Watch for the answer that rounds the run away to zero: rare is not the same as impossible, and on this exam the difference is usually the point.","traps":[null,"0.01 is (1/2) to the seventh rounded off, one flip short of eight.","A probability never falls below 0, so this one can go before any arithmetic starts.","0.125 reads eight flips as a 1 in 8 chance, and each flip halves the run, so eight of them give 1/256.","1 would make the run certain, and that sits much closer to the chance of at least one head across eight flips."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"written:qr-11-1oihzm9","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A 20 foot ladder leans against a wall and makes a 60 degree angle with the ground. How high up the wall does the top of the ladder reach?","opts":["40√3/3 ft, about 23.1","10√2 ft, about 14.1","10 ft","20√3 ft, about 34.6","10√3 ft, about 17.3"],"correct":4,"why":"The ladder is the hypotenuse and the height is the side opposite the 60 degree angle, so sine is the ratio that connects them. Height equals 20 times sin 60, and sin 60 is √3/2, which gives 10√3, a little over 17 feet. Draw the triangle and label the ladder as the hypotenuse before picking a ratio, because the leaning object is never a leg.","traps":["This divides by sin 60 instead of multiplying, which treats the 20 foot ladder as a leg.","This uses the 45 degree value √2/2, which belongs to an isosceles right triangle rather than this one.","This is 20 times cos 60, the distance from the wall out to the foot of the ladder rather than the height.","This multiplies by √3 instead of √3/2, so the 2 in the denominator got dropped.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1453","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A trail map marks a trailhead at (-1, -3) and a lookout at (5, 1). In a straight line, about how many units apart are they?","opts":["3.16","6","7.21","10","52"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((6)² + (4)²) = √52 = 7.21.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1694","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A line passes through (-8,8) and (-5,5). What is its slope?","opts":["-3","-1","0","1","3"],"correct":1,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = -3/3 = -1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1474","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"How many centimeters are in 2.4 meters?","opts":["0.02","24","102.4","240","2400"],"correct":3,"why":"1 m = 100 cm, so 2.4 m × 100 = 240 cm.","traps":["You divided by 100 rather than multiplying, which sends the count the wrong way.","You multiplied by 10 instead of 100; meters to centimeters moves two places.","You added 100 to the 2.4 instead of multiplying the 2.4 by it.",null,"You multiplied by 1000, the kilometer-to-meter step, when meters to centimeters needs 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pa3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Two fair coins are flipped. What is the probability that at least one of them lands heads?","opts":["1","1/4","1/3","1/2","3/4"],"correct":4,"why":"OR: P(H1) + P(H2) − P(both). 1/2 + 1/2 − 1/4 = 3/4. Or flip-to-none: 1 − 1/4 = 3/4.","traps":["Treated at least one head as a sure thing; both coins land tails once in every four tries.","That is the chance that both land heads, not the chance of at least one.","Counted two heads, two tails and one of each as equally likely; one of each happens twice as often as either other.","That is the chance for one coin on its own; two coins give four equally likely outcomes, three of which hold a head.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1021","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A vial is 6/8 full, and 1/4 of a vial is drawn out of it. What fraction of the vial is left?","opts":["3/4","7/12","6/32","5/8","1/2"],"correct":4,"why":"Quarters fit inside eighths, so 1/4 becomes 2/8. Then 6/8 minus 2/8 is 4/8, which is 1/2. Reducing first works just as well, since 3/4 minus 1/4 is a half.","traps":["You reduced 6/8 to 3/4 and stopped before subtracting the quarter.","You moved into twelfths, but eighths and quarters both live comfortably in eighths.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of subtracting them.","You put 1/4 over 8 but left the 1 on top; a quarter is 2/8.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1027","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"What is the sum of 9/15 and 10/14?","opts":["97/70","50/35","46/35","19/29","90/210"],"correct":2,"why":"LCD = 210. 9/15 = 126/210, 10/14 = 150/210. Combine: 276/210 = 46/35.","traps":["One of the rescalings slipped; in seventieths the pieces are 42 and 50, which total 92/70.","You multiplied each top by its own bottom instead of by the other fraction's bottom.",null,"You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 19 over 29.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#8hauoz","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (4 × 10³)(3 × 10²) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["7 × 10⁶","1.2 × 10⁴","1.2 × 10⁵","12 × 10⁵","1.2 × 10⁶"],"correct":4,"why":"4 × 3 = 12 and 10³ × 10² = 10⁵. 12 × 10⁵ is not proper form, so write 1.2 × 10⁶. Answer: 1.2 × 10⁶.","traps":["You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.","Right size, wrong form. In proper scientific notation the front number sits between 1 and 10.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1737","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"A tank is meant to hold 6 liters, and its actual level of x liters is off by exactly 5 liters. What are the possible values of x?","opts":["11 or 1","-1 or 1","5 or -5","1 or -11","11"],"correct":0,"why":"|x − 6| = 5 means x − 6 = ±5, so x = 11 or 1.","traps":[null,"That pairs 1 with its negative, and the other branch is 6 plus 5, which is 11.","You set x equal to plus or minus 5 and never undid the minus 6 inside the bars.","That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.","You solved x - 6 = 5 and stopped, and the branch x - 6 = -5 gives 1 as well."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:bn4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"A square storage room measures 19 feet on each side. What is its floor area in square feet?","opts":["341","361","379","381","441"],"correct":1,"why":"(20−1)² = 400 − 40 + 1 = 361.","traps":["You made the middle term 60 rather than 40, which lands 20 below 361.",null,"You subtracted 20 and then 1 as well, instead of subtracting 40 and adding 1.","You used 20 for the middle term instead of 2 times 20 times 1, which is 40.","You added the middle term instead of subtracting it, which squares 21, not 19."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-compound-interest#1b1xn3t","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"$2000 is invested at 5% annual interest, compounded once a year. What is the balance after 2 years?","opts":["$205.00","$2100.00","$2200.00","$2205.00","$2315.25"],"correct":3,"why":"Balance = 2000 × (1.05)² = $2205.00.","traps":["That is the interest earned. The question asks for the BALANCE.","One year short. Count the compounding periods.","That is SIMPLE interest. Compounding earns interest on the interest, so multiply by the growth factor each year.",null,"One year too many. Count the compounding periods."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:int1438","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A lab equipment fund of $5000 earns 8.75% interest compounded annually for 3 years. To the nearest cent, what is the total amount in dollars at the end?","opts":["1312.5","5000.26","5437.5","6312.5","6430.69"],"correct":4,"why":"A = P(1+r)^t = 5000·(1.0875)^3 = 6430.69.","traps":["That is the simple interest for three years, and the question asks for the ending amount.","Added rt as about twenty-six cents instead of applying the rate to the balance each year.","Grew the balance for one year instead of three.","Used simple interest, so years two and three never earned interest on the earlier interest.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:vs3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":1,"q":"A sphere has a radius of 3, and its volume is (4/3)πr³. Approximating π as 3, what is its volume?","opts":["27","108π","36","9","108"],"correct":4,"why":"(4/3)(3)(27) = 4 × 27 = 108. (Or with π: 36π.)","traps":["Cubed the radius and stopped, without the 4/3 or the π.","Used 3 for π and then kept a π on the end as well, counting it twice.","Left π out and stopped at 4/3 times 27.","Squared the radius and stopped there.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1718","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"What is the value of 2^1 ÷ 2^1?","opts":["1","4","0","1/2","2"],"correct":0,"why":"Same base ÷ subtracts: 2^(1-1) = 2^0 = 1.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#1ylorxc","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A car travels at 45 miles per hour. What is its speed in feet per second? (1 mile = 5280 ft)","opts":["66 ft/s","3960 ft/s","30.7 ft/s","0.0125 ft/s","2.38 × 10⁵ ft/s"],"correct":0,"why":"45 mi/h × 5280 ft/mi ÷ 3600 s/h = 66 ft/s. Handy anchor: 60 mph is 88 ft/s.","traps":[null,"You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour.","Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so miles and hours cancel.","That is miles per second. Multiply by 5280 to get feet.","That is feet per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-perm-vs-comb#znh491","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"From 9 runners, in how many ways can 1st through 3rd place be awarded?","opts":["6","27","84","504","729"],"correct":3,"why":"Order matters: 9P3 = 9 × 8 × 7 = 504.","traps":["That is just the number of ways to order 3 people. You still have to choose them from 9.","You multiplied n by r. Count the choices for each slot: n, then n − 1, and so on.","You used a combination. Places are different prizes, so ORDER matters: use a permutation.",null,"You let the same person be picked twice. Once someone is chosen they are out of the pool."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1136","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"What is 42 percent of 476?","opts":["19.99","199.92","276.08","518","19992"],"correct":1,"why":"42% of 476 = 42/100 × 476 = 199.92.","traps":["You shifted the decimal one place too far, dividing the percent by 1000 rather than by 100.",null,"You found the 58 percent left over rather than the 42 percent the question asks for.","You added the 42 to the 476 instead of taking 42 percent of it.","You multiplied 42 by 476 and never divided by 100."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1502","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"The sum of a number and 2 times the number is 6. Find the number.","opts":["1","2","3","4","6"],"correct":1,"why":"A number plus 2 times the number is 3 of them, because the number itself counts as one. That gives 3x = 6 and x = 2. Count the pieces first, then divide.","traps":["One plus twice 1 comes to only 3, half of the 6 the stem asks for.",null,"Divided by 2 instead of 3; the number itself is one of the three pieces.","That is 2 times the number, the second piece of the sum, rather than the number.","That is the total from the stem, copied down before the 6 was split three ways."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#sua54k","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the SUM of the solutions of x² + 2x − 3 = 0?","opts":["3","−3","−2","4","2"],"correct":2,"why":"It factors as (x − 1)(x + 3), so the roots are 1 and −3. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = −2, product = c/a = −3.","traps":["You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a.","That is the PRODUCT of the roots (c/a). The sum is −b/a.",null,"That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their sum.","Sign slip. The sum of the roots is the OPPOSITE of the x coefficient: −b/a."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1262","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"To the nearest hundredth, what value of x satisfies 11x + 10 = -3x - 9?","opts":["-2.36","-1.36","-0.36","0.07","1.36"],"correct":1,"why":"Add 3x to both sides for 14x + 10 = -9, then subtract 10 for 14x = -19. Divide by 14 and x is -19/14, which works out to -1.357 and rounds to -1.36. When every choice carries two decimals, that rounding step is part of the answer, so do the division in full before you trim it.","traps":["Substituting -2.36 leaves about -16 on the left and about -1.9 on the right.",null,"Substituting -0.36 leaves about 6 on the left and about -7.9 on the right.","The 10 was added to the -9 instead of subtracted, leaving 14x = 1.","Dividing 19 by 14 gives 1.36, but 14x = -19 makes x negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1054","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"What is 5/8 multiplied by 2/8?","opts":["10/16","5/64","7/64","5/32","40/16"],"correct":3,"why":"Multiply across: (5·2)/(8·8) = 10/64 = 5/32.","traps":["You multiplied the tops but added the bottoms (8 + 8) instead of multiplying them.","You halved the top of 10/64 while leaving the bottom alone; reducing has to act on both.","You added the tops (5 + 2) over the multiplied bottoms instead of multiplying across.",null,"You flipped the second fraction and divided: 5/8 divided by 2/8 is 40/16."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:psm2","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A textbook's price is raised 30 percent and then cut 10 percent from the raised price. What is the net percent change?","opts":["+40%","+17%","−40%","+20%","+43%"],"correct":1,"why":"×1.30 × 0.90 = ×1.17. Net +17%.","traps":["You added the 10 percent drop on as another rise instead of taking it off.",null,"You added the sizes of the two changes and pointed the result downward, and the rise is the bigger move here.","You added the 30 up and the 10 down, and sequential percents multiply instead.","That raises the second step by 10 percent instead of cutting by it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-q46gn4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"The terminal side of an angle θ in standard position passes through the point (-3, 4). What is cos θ?","opts":["4/5","3/5","-3/5","-4/5","-4/3"],"correct":2,"why":"Treat the point as a right triangle hanging off the origin: the horizontal leg is -3, the vertical leg is 4, and the distance from the origin is the square root of 9 plus 16, which is 5. Cosine is the x coordinate over that distance, so it is -3/5. The distance is always positive, so the sign of the answer comes entirely from the coordinate on top.","traps":["This is sin θ, using the y coordinate where the x coordinate belongs.","This drops the negative sign, but the point sits to the left of the y axis.",null,"This uses the y coordinate and attaches the sign that belongs to the x coordinate.","This is tan θ, the y coordinate over the x coordinate."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:ds1698","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A line passes through (12,-7) and (13,-2). What is its slope?","opts":["-9","-5","0.2","5","6"],"correct":3,"why":"m = Δy/Δx = 5/1 = 5.","traps":["-9 comes from writing -2 minus 7 for the rise instead of -2 minus -7, over a run of 1.","-5 has the right size and the wrong sign, since y rises from -7 to -2.","0.2 divides the run by the rise, 1 over 5, which is the slope upside down.",null,"6 adds the change in y and the change in x instead of dividing them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:ds3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Q: What is the area of the rectangle? (1) Length is 4. (2) Perimeter is 14.","opts":["Both together","Neither","(1) alone","(2) alone","Either one alone"],"correct":0,"why":"(1) alone: no width. (2) alone: many shapes. Together: 2(4)+2w=14, so w=3, so area=12.","traps":[null,"Together works.","Knowing the length is 4 still leaves the width open, so the area could be anything.","A perimeter of 14 fits a 4 by 3 rectangle and a 5 by 2 rectangle, and those areas differ.","Choosing this treats the perimeter as fixing the rectangle, and 4 by 3 and 5 by 2 share that perimeter with areas of 12 and 10."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-combined-work#csydkg","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One painter paints a fence in 6 hours. A second painter does the same job in 12 hours. Working together, how long do they take?","opts":["18 hours","6 hours","4 hours","9 hours","0.25 hours"],"correct":2,"why":"Rates add: 1/6 + 1/12 = 1/4 of the job per hour. Time = 4 = 4 hours. Sanity check: it must be less than 6 hours.","traps":["You added the times. Adding helpers cannot make the job take longer. Add the RATES: 1/a + 1/b.","You subtracted the times. Rates add when both are working.",null,"You averaged the times. Two working together must be FASTER than the faster one alone.","That is the combined RATE (jobs per hour). Flip it to get the time."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261003"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s13"}},{"id":"FULL_4","name":"Full-Length Test 4","sections":{"bio":[{"id":"bio:BIO_p018","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which ion binds troponin to start muscle contraction?","opts":["Cl-","Ca²⁺","Na+","Mg²⁺","K+"],"correct":1,"why":"This is the trigger question. Calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin, troponin changes shape and drags tropomyosin off the myosin binding sites on actin, and the heads can then attach and pull. Keep the two ion jobs separate: sodium and potassium carry the action potential along the membrane, while calcium converts that electrical signal into an actual pull.","traps":["Chloride helps stabilize the resting membrane and has no role in uncovering the myosin binding sites.",null,"Sodium entry carries the action potential along the muscle fiber membrane, which is the arriving signal rather than the trigger inside the cell.","Magnesium partners with ATP in the myosin head's chemistry, but the binding site on troponin is built for calcium.","Potassium leaving the cell repolarizes the membrane after the impulse, ending the signal rather than starting the pull."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g004","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A person who inherits one I^A allele and one I^B allele has type AB blood. This is an example of:","opts":["Incomplete dominance","Codominance","Complete dominance","Recessive epistasis","Polygenic inheritance"],"correct":1,"why":"The question is asking you to separate codominance from incomplete dominance, and the test is always the same: look at what the heterozygote actually shows. If both parental products appear at full strength, side by side and unchanged, that is codominance. An AB individual builds whole A antigen and whole B antigen on the same red blood cells, with no blending and no intermediate antigen in between, so both alleles are fully expressed at once.","traps":["Incomplete dominance would produce a blended, intermediate product, but the A and B antigens both show up intact and unmodified.",null,"Complete dominance would hide one allele entirely, so the person would type as A only or B only.","Recessive epistasis is one gene masking a different gene, which is the Bombay situation, not two alleles of the single ABO gene.","Polygenic inheritance means many genes each adding a small amount to one trait, while everything here comes from a single gene."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p091","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"In a long bone such as the femur, the elongated cylindrical shaft between the two ends is called which of the following?","opts":["Metaphysis","Endosteum","Periosteum","Diaphysis","Epiphysis"],"correct":3,"why":"Long bone terms map onto one picture: a tube with a knob at each end. The diaphysis is that tube, a cylinder of compact bone wrapped around the marrow cavity and built to resist bending, while each epiphysis is a knob of spongy bone under articular cartilage and the metaphysis joins the two. The choice naming the diaphysis is the shaft, and holding that picture keeps the other terms in place.","traps":["The metaphysis is the transition zone between shaft and end, and it contains the growth plate in a growing bone.","The endosteum is the thin membrane lining the marrow cavity inside the shaft, so it is a lining rather than the shaft.","The periosteum is the fibrous sheath wrapping the outside of the bone rather than a region of the bone itself.",null,"The epiphysis is the expanded end of the bone that forms the joint surface and holds mostly spongy bone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g036","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"In a dihybrid cross showing dominant epistasis, where a dominant allele at one gene masks the expression of a second gene, what F2 phenotypic ratio is expected?","opts":["13:3","9:3:4","12:3:1","9:3:3:1","15:1"],"correct":2,"why":"Work from 9:3:3:1 again and ask which classes become indistinguishable. Under dominant epistasis, every genotype carrying the masking dominant allele shows the same phenotype, so the 9 and one of the 3 classes fuse into 12 and the ratio reads 12:3:1. Use the leading number as your tell: a 12 means a dominant allele is doing the hiding, while a 4 means a homozygous recessive genotype is.","traps":["This is dominant suppression, in which three of the four classes collapse into one, a different interaction from straightforward dominant epistasis.","The 4 in that ratio signals that a homozygous recessive genotype is doing the masking, which is recessive epistasis.",null,"That is the baseline ratio for two genes acting independently, with neither one masking the other.","That is duplicate dominant gene action, where a dominant allele at either gene alone is enough to produce the same phenotype."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-d1bcg6","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"A macrophage extends its membrane around a whole bacterium and takes it in inside a large vesicle. Compared with pinocytosis, this process:","opts":["Requires no membrane at all, since the bacterium dissolves in the cytoplasm on contact","Brings in a large solid particle rather than a droplet of extracellular fluid","Uses a carrier protein in place of a vesicle","Moves material out of the cell instead of into it","Consumes no ATP, because the membrane simply flows around the target"],"correct":1,"why":"Both are endocytosis, so both build a vesicle out of the plasma membrane and both cost energy. The split between them is the cargo: phagocytosis takes a large solid particle, pinocytosis takes a gulp of surrounding fluid with whatever is dissolved in it. Cell eating versus cell drinking is the pairing to keep.","traps":["The bacterium is wrapped in membrane inside a phagosome, it is not dissolved into the cytoplasm.",null,"Carrier proteins handle single small solutes, a whole bacterium goes nowhere near one.","Both phagocytosis and pinocytosis bring material in, exocytosis is the outward one.","Both forms of endocytosis need ATP for the cytoskeletal work of reshaping the membrane."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g020","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"How many Barr bodies would you expect in a somatic cell from a person with Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY)?","opts":["0","3","The number varies from cell to cell, because X inactivation is random","1","2"],"correct":3,"why":"A Barr body is an inactivated X chromosome, and a cell keeps exactly one X active no matter how many it carries. So the count is always the number of X chromosomes minus one, which for XXY is one. Run that subtraction on any karyotype you are given and this whole family of questions becomes a single step.","traps":["Zero Barr bodies fits a typical 46,XY male or a 45,X female, where there is only one X to begin with and nothing to inactivate.","Three Barr bodies would require four X chromosomes, which is not what this karyotype describes.","Which X gets silenced is random, and that is why females are mosaics, but how many get silenced is fixed at all but one.",null,"Two Barr bodies would require three X chromosomes, as in a 47,XXX karyotype."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-b115107d15","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Epinephrine is classified as which type of hormone, and how does it signal?","opts":["An amino acid derivative that crosses the membrane like thyroid hormone","A steroid released from the adrenal cortex","A steroid that binds a receptor inside the nucleus","A peptide made of many amino acids that enters the cell through a channel","An amino acid derivative that binds surface receptors"],"correct":4,"why":"Both epinephrine and thyroid hormone come from tyrosine, but they behave differently. Epinephrine is water soluble, so it binds a surface receptor and a second messenger such as cAMP carries the signal. That is why fight or flight switches on in seconds.","traps":["Thyroid hormone is the lipid soluble exception. Epinephrine behaves like a peptide and uses surface receptors.","Epinephrine comes from the adrenal MEDULLA and is not a steroid.","Epinephrine is not made from cholesterol. It is water soluble and stays outside the cell.","It is derived from a SINGLE amino acid and it does not enter the cell.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1yx1bd4","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Two membranes are identical except that one is built largely from phospholipids with unsaturated fatty acid tails. At a cool temperature, that membrane will be:","opts":["Solid, because unsaturated tails crystallize first","Completely impermeable to dissolved gases","More fluid, because the kinks keep the tails from packing tightly","Less fluid, because the double bonds lock neighboring tails tightly together","Identical in fluidity, since only cholesterol matters"],"correct":2,"why":"Picture the tails. A double bond puts a permanent kink in the chain, and kinked tails cannot stack neatly, so they keep sliding past one another as the temperature drops. Saturated tails are straight, pack tight, and solidify first. Kink means loose, straight means tight.","traps":["Saturated tails solidify first, unsaturated tails stay mobile longer as temperature falls.","Small nonpolar gases cross any bilayer readily, whatever the tails look like.",null,"A double bond kinks the chain and prevents tight packing rather than locking chains together.","Tail saturation is a major determinant of fluidity right alongside cholesterol."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p025","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Midway through the menstrual cycle, luteinizing hormone rises sharply in what is called the LH surge. What does this surge trigger?","opts":["Ovulation","Implantation","Menstruation","Fertilization","Recruitment and early growth of the ovarian follicles"],"correct":0,"why":"This question is testing which hormone spike does which job in the cycle. Estrogen from the growing follicle climbs until it flips the pituitary into positive feedback, which fires a sharp surge of luteinizing hormone, and that surge ruptures the mature follicle and releases the egg. The same surge then converts the leftover follicle into the corpus luteum. Hold the surge as the trigger that pops the follicle and the rest of the cycle lines up around it.","traps":[null,"Implantation happens about a week after the egg is released and depends on progesterone from the corpus luteum, not on the spike itself.","Bleeding follows the fall of progesterone at the very end of the cycle, which is the opposite hormonal situation from a mid-cycle spike.","Fertilization depends on sperm meeting the egg in the oviduct; the surge only makes an egg available to be met.","Early follicle growth is driven by follicle-stimulating hormone at the start of the cycle, before this mid-cycle spike happens."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p017","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"During muscle contraction, which region of the sarcomere stays the same length?","opts":["Sarcomere","H-zone","The zone where thick and thin filaments overlap","A-band","I-band"],"correct":3,"why":"Sliding filament means the filaments themselves never shorten, they slide past each other. The A band is defined by the length of the thick myosin filaments, and since myosin does not change length, the A band does not either. Everything that shrinks is a gap: the I band is thin filament with no myosin over it and the H zone is myosin with no actin over it, so both narrow as the actin slides inward.","traps":["The whole sarcomere shortening is what contraction is, since the Z discs are pulled toward each other.","The H zone is the middle stretch of myosin with no actin over it, and it shrinks as actin is pulled inward.","The overlap region grows during contraction as more thin filament slides in alongside the myosin.",null,"The I band is the stretch of thin filament not yet overlapped by myosin, and it narrows as the filaments slide together."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-4tojjq","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"A leaf running linear flow starts building up NADPH faster than the Calvin cycle can spend it. Why does routing some electrons into cyclic flow help?","opts":["It raises oxygen output, which the leaf needs for its own respiration at night","It lets the leaf fix carbon in the dark using electrons stored during the day","It converts the surplus NADPH back to NADP+ by handing its electrons straight to carbon dioxide in the stroma","It rebuilds water molecules from the protons in the lumen, so the leaf loses less water","The Calvin cycle needs more ATP than NADPH, so extra ATP with no extra NADPH balances the books"],"correct":4,"why":"This is a stoichiometry question wearing a physiology costume. Three turns of the Calvin cycle burn nine ATP but only six NADPH, so a plant running linear flow alone slowly falls short on ATP. Cyclic flow is the top up valve: pure ATP, no NADPH, called for whenever that ratio drifts.","traps":["Cyclic flow bypasses photosystem II completely, so it produces no oxygen at all.","Cyclic flow still needs light striking photosystem I, so it is not a pathway for darkness.","Carbon dioxide is reduced by Calvin cycle enzymes in the stroma, not by a photosystem handing electrons straight to it.","Nothing in the light reactions puts water back together, and the protons in the lumen are spent driving ATP synthase.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e025","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Gause's competitive exclusion principle states that:","opts":["Competition leads to extinction","Two species can always coexist","Mutualism wins","Two species whose niches overlap at all cannot coexist in the same habitat","Two species with identical niches cannot coexist long-term"],"correct":4,"why":"This principle is about a limiting resource, not about species disliking each other. If two species need exactly the same thing and one is even slightly better at obtaining it, that small edge compounds generation after generation until the other is pushed out of the habitat. What you usually observe in nature instead is resource partitioning, where the two shift to slightly different niches, different perch heights, feeding times, or prey sizes, and both persist. So when a stem shows two species coexisting on an overlapping resource, look for how they divide it.","traps":["The loser is excluded from that habitat, which usually means local displacement rather than the species going extinct everywhere.","This ignores what happens when two species depend on the same limiting resource and nothing separates them.","Mutualism is a separate kind of interaction and does not determine the outcome of competition for one resource.","Partial overlap is common and stable; the principle only bites when the niches are effectively identical on the limiting resource, and partitioning handles the rest.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"originals:ab-abf0966668","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"During the absolute refractory period no stimulus, however strong, can fire a second action potential. Why?","opts":["The K+ channels are closed","The voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated","The cell has run out of Na+","Neurotransmitter has been used up at the synapse","The Na+/K+ pump has stopped working"],"correct":1,"why":"Think of the inactivation gate as a door that locks itself after use. Until the membrane gets back down near rest, the lock stays on and no amount of stimulus can open those Na+ channels. That lock is also why the signal travels one way down the axon.","traps":["Closed K+ channels would not stop a spike, and for most of this window (the falling phase) the voltage-gated K+ channels are actually OPEN. The block is on the Na+ side.",null,"Only a tiny fraction of the ions move during one spike. The gradients are barely dented.","That is a synapse issue. The refractory period is a property of the axon membrane channels.","The pump keeps running the whole time. It is not what gates excitability."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1gazair","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Three turns of the Calvin cycle fix three molecules of carbon dioxide. What does that cost in ATP and NADPH?","opts":["3 ATP and 3 NADPH","6 ATP and 6 NADPH","9 ATP and 6 NADPH","6 ATP and 9 NADPH","18 ATP and 12 NADPH"],"correct":2,"why":"Two steps in the cycle spend ATP and only one spends NADPH, which is why ATP is always the bigger number. The six three carbon molecules each take one ATP and one NADPH during reduction, then three more ATP go into rebuilding RuBP, giving nine and six. Lock in the per carbon ratio of three ATP to two NADPH and you can scale it to any number of turns.","traps":["One of each per turn undercounts badly, since the reduction step alone works on six three carbon molecules.","Six and six gets the NADPH right but misses the three extra ATP spent regenerating RuBP.",null,"The numbers are right but swapped, and ATP is the one spent in two separate places, so it is always the larger of the two.","Those are the totals for a full glucose, which takes six turns rather than three."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-4-11y9km5","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"Mature human red blood cells have no mitochondria at all. What does that mean for how they make ATP?","opts":["They import ATP from neighboring cells through gap junctions","They run the Krebs cycle in the cytosol instead","They rely on glycolysis and convert pyruvate to lactate to keep NAD+ coming","They use the oxygen they carry to run an electron transport chain in the cytosol","They make no ATP and live on stored reserves until they are cleared"],"correct":2,"why":"No mitochondria means no pyruvate oxidation, no Krebs cycle, and no electron transport chain, so glycolysis is the whole energy budget. To keep glycolysis turning, the cell has to regenerate NAD+, and lactate fermentation is how it does that, which is why red blood cells put out lactate even in a well-oxygenated person. It is a useful case to keep on hand, because it cleanly separates carrying oxygen from using it.","traps":["Red blood cells do not maintain gap junctions with other cells, and ATP is not traded around that way.","Krebs enzymes live in the mitochondrial matrix, so a cell without mitochondria has nowhere to run them.",null,"The oxygen a red blood cell carries is cargo bound to hemoglobin, and the chain needs membrane complexes this cell does not have.","These cells work for roughly 120 days, which no fixed ATP store would cover."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g033","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-10","topic":"Beyond Mendel and molecular tools","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Roan cattle have coats containing both fully red hairs and fully white hairs intermixed, and they result from crossing red cattle with white cattle. This coat pattern is an example of:","opts":["Random X-inactivation, as in calico cats","Incomplete dominance","Epistasis","Codominance","Polygenic"],"correct":3,"why":"The test for codominance is whether the heterozygote displays both parental products at full strength rather than an average of them. Look closely at a roan coat and it is not pink: it is red hairs and white hairs side by side, each individual hair committed to one allele's product. Compare that with a pink snapdragon, where every cell makes an in-between amount of a single pigment, and the difference between codominance and incomplete dominance stops being slippery.","traps":["That mechanism creates the patchwork coat of calico cats and applies to X-linked genes in females, while roan appears in bulls and cows alike.","Incomplete dominance would give one uniform intermediate shade across the whole coat, not two distinct hair colors on the same animal.","Epistasis requires a second gene masking the first, and a single coat color gene accounts for this cross.",null,"Polygenic inheritance produces a smooth range of shades across many offspring, rather than two discrete hair colors within one animal."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-3-fiwnsa","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"Epinephrine binds a receptor on a liver cell and the cell starts breaking down glycogen. After the receptor changes shape, put the middle of the pathway in order:","opts":["A G protein binds GTP, cAMP rises, adenylyl cyclase is activated, and protein kinase A is activated","A G protein binds GTP, adenylyl cyclase is activated, cAMP rises, protein kinase A is activated","Adenylyl cyclase is activated, a G protein binds GTP, cAMP rises, protein kinase A is activated","Protein kinase A is activated, cAMP rises, a G protein binds GTP, adenylyl cyclase is activated","cAMP rises, a G protein binds GTP, protein kinase A is activated, adenylyl cyclase is activated"],"correct":1,"why":"Order is the entire question, so walk the relay one handoff at a time. The activated receptor loads the G protein with GTP, the G protein switches on adenylyl cyclase, that enzyme makes cAMP from ATP, and cAMP frees protein kinase A to phosphorylate its targets. Receptor, G protein, enzyme, second messenger, kinase is the shape of every version of this pathway.","traps":["cAMP is made by adenylyl cyclase, so the enzyme has to be active before the messenger rises.",null,"Adenylyl cyclase cannot switch on before the G protein that activates it.","The kinase is the last step rather than the first, since cAMP is what releases it.","cAMP is a product of this pathway, so it cannot appear before the enzyme that makes it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-5-jthhks","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"A cactus opens its stomata only after dark. How does it still fix carbon in daylight?","opts":["It stores carbon dioxide as a gas in large vacuoles until morning","It takes up carbon dioxide through its roots while the stomata are shut","It locks the carbon into organic acids overnight and releases it by day","It runs the light reactions at night and the Calvin cycle during the day","It ships the night's carbon to neighboring bundle sheath cells for daytime use"],"correct":2,"why":"CAM is a night shift for gas exchange. Stomata open in the cool dark, carbon dioxide gets locked into malic acid and parked in the vacuole, then by day the acid hands that carbon back to RuBisCO while the stomata stay shut. Same trick as C4, concentrate carbon dioxide near the enzyme, but staged in time instead of in space.","traps":["Gas cannot be warehoused that way, since the carbon has to be locked into a molecule first.","Roots take up water and minerals, not the carbon dioxide used in photosynthesis.",null,"The light reactions need light, so they cannot run in the dark no matter how the carbon is stored.","Handing carbon to bundle sheath cells is the C4 solution, which separates the steps by place rather than by time."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"originals:ab-1275ae4cf5","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-14","topic":"Control systems: nerves and hormones","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Which change would increase the conduction speed of an axon the most?","opts":["More neurotransmitter stored in the terminal","A larger axon diameter together with a myelin sheath","A smaller axon diameter together with a myelin sheath","A more negative resting potential","A larger axon diameter with the myelin removed"],"correct":1,"why":"Two levers set speed. A wide axon is a wide pipe with low internal resistance. Myelin stops current from leaking out and lets the signal skip between nodes. Pull both levers and you get the fastest axon.","traps":["That affects the synapse, not the speed along the axon.",null,"A narrow axon has more internal resistance, which slows current. Bigger is faster.","That moves the cell further from threshold. It does not speed the signal along the axon.","Losing myelin forces the signal to regenerate at every patch of membrane, which is slow."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"written:bio-4-zfom9x","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"One Krebs cycle enzyme is not floating in the matrix. It is built into the inner mitochondrial membrane and doubles as a complex of the electron transport chain. Which reaction does it catalyze?","opts":["Isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate","Succinate to fumarate","Citrate to isocitrate","Malate to oxaloacetate","Succinyl-CoA to succinate"],"correct":1,"why":"The enzyme is succinate dehydrogenase, which is also complex II of the electron transport chain. Because it is wedged into the membrane, the electrons it strips off go onto FAD and straight into the chain rather than onto NAD+. That membrane address is the reason the cycle's only FADH2 comes from this one step.","traps":["This is the rate-limiting decarboxylation, run by a matrix enzyme that reduces NAD+.",null,"This isomerization belongs to aconitase, a soluble matrix enzyme that just relocates a hydroxyl group.","This last oxidation reduces NAD+ in the matrix and regenerates oxaloacetate for the next turn.","This is the substrate-level phosphorylation that makes GTP, and it takes place in the matrix."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-16zwvy5","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Helicase has just pried the two parental strands apart at the fork. Left alone, those exposed single strands would snap back together or fold back on themselves before polymerase could use them. What keeps them available?","opts":["Histones wrap each single strand into a nucleosome until polymerase arrives there","Ligase coats the exposed strands and seals them against re-pairing with each other","Topoisomerase clamps the two strands apart by cutting and holding the backbone open","Primase stays bound along the entire length of each exposed template strand","Single-strand binding proteins coat the separated strands and hold them open"],"correct":4,"why":"This is about keeping the template accessible, which is a job of holding rather than cutting. Single-strand binding protein coats the separated strands, blocks them from re-annealing or folding into hairpins, and keeps them straight enough for polymerase to read. Sort the fork proteins by verb and they stop blurring together: helicase opens, single-strand binding protein holds, topoisomerase relieves twist, primase starts, polymerase builds, ligase seals.","traps":["Nucleosomes package double-stranded DNA and would hide the template rather than expose it.","Ligase seals nicks in a backbone, has no role in holding template apart, and does not coat DNA.","Topoisomerase does cut and reseal DNA, but it works ahead of the fork on supercoiling rather than on the separated strands behind it.","Primase touches down only where a primer is needed and then leaves, so it cannot hold a whole strand open.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e003","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A horse and a donkey mate and produce a mule, which grows into a healthy adult but cannot reproduce. This illustrates which type of reproductive isolation?","opts":["Postzygotic - hybrid sterility","Prezygotic - gametic","Temporal isolation, in which the two species breed at different times of year","Postzygotic - inviability","Prezygotic - behavioral"],"correct":0,"why":"Split the timeline at fertilization. Anything that stops a zygote from forming, such as different habitats, different breeding seasons, incompatible courtship, or gametes that cannot fuse, is prezygotic. Here a zygote clearly formed and grew into an adult, so the barrier acted afterward, and the specific failure is in meiosis: a horse has 64 chromosomes, a donkey has 62, and the mule's 63 cannot pair up evenly to make working gametes.","traps":[null,"Gametic isolation means sperm and egg never fuse, yet fertilization plainly succeeded in this cross.","Temporal isolation would keep the two species from ever breeding together, but this cross produced offspring.","Hybrid inviability means the hybrid dies as an embryo or before reaching maturity, while a mule is healthy and long lived.","Behavioral isolation means the two species never court each other successfully, and these two mated."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-5-1f9fdnq","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-5","topic":"Photosynthesis","nights":[5],"d":3,"q":"In linear electron flow, each electron is boosted once at photosystem II and again at photosystem I. What is the minimum number of photons absorbed per molecule of oxygen released?","opts":["Twelve","Eight","Two","Six","Four"],"correct":1,"why":"Build the number up from the electrons. One oxygen molecule comes from two waters, which release four electrons between them, and each of those four gets lifted twice, so four times two is eight. Any photon counting question on the light reactions starts by counting electrons first.","traps":["Twelve overshoots, because each electron takes two uphill jumps rather than three.",null,"Two is the number of water molecules split, not the number of photons absorbed.","Six does not match any step in the count of electrons or of boosts.","Four counts the electrons, or one boost each, and forgets the second excitation at photosystem I."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-glmnle","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Acetylcholine released at the neuromuscular junction binds a receptor on the muscle fiber that is itself an ion channel. The immediate result is:","opts":["Calcium pumped out of the fiber by an ATPase","Transcription of new receptor genes","A rise in cAMP inside the fiber","Autophosphorylation of the receptor on several tyrosine side chains","Sodium flowing into the fiber and depolarizing the membrane"],"correct":4,"why":"This is a ligand-gated ion channel, so binding and response are the same event: the channel opens and ions move down their electrochemical gradients. Sodium is high outside and the inside is negative, so sodium rushes in and the membrane depolarizes within milliseconds. No second messenger is needed, which is why this is the fastest receptor type there is.","traps":["Pumping calcium out costs ATP and is not what this ligand binding triggers.","Gene transcription runs on the steroid timescale, not the millisecond response of a channel.","A rise in cAMP needs a G-protein-coupled receptor and takes far longer than a channel opening.","Autophosphorylation on tyrosines belongs to receptor tyrosine kinases such as the insulin receptor.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_e007","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-12","topic":"Ecology and behavior","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"The producers in a grassland food chain capture 10,000 kcal of energy. Using the ten percent rule for energy transfer between trophic levels, approximately how much of that energy reaches the tertiary consumers?","opts":["100 kcal","9000 kcal","1 kcal","10 kcal","1000 kcal"],"correct":3,"why":"This is the ten percent rule, and the only real trap is counting the steps. Producers pass about a tenth up to primary consumers, leaving 1,000 kcal, those pass a tenth to secondary consumers, leaving 100 kcal, and those pass a tenth to tertiary consumers. Count the arrows rather than the animals: three transfers means dividing by ten three times. The energy that does not move up leaves as heat from respiration and in parts that are never eaten.","traps":["This is the secondary consumer level, which stops one transfer short of the tertiary consumers.","This flips the rule: about ten percent moves up and about ninety percent is lost, so each step divides by ten rather than shaving off a tenth.","This divides by ten four times, counting one more transfer than this chain contains.",null,"This is the primary consumer level, one transfer down from the producers."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1u7pjce","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"The very first thing a cell does to glucose is attach a phosphate to it. What does that accomplish?","opts":["It releases the energy later captured as ATP","It supplies the phosphate that is eventually handed to oxygen","It converts glucose into fructose","It gives the sugar a charge so it cannot leave the cell","It tags the sugar for transport into the mitochondrion"],"correct":3,"why":"Phosphate groups carry negative charge, and charged molecules do not slide back through a lipid membrane or ride the glucose transporter. Phosphorylating glucose therefore locks it inside the cell and commits it to being used. The same trick shows up all over metabolism: add a phosphate, trap the molecule.","traps":["That step costs energy rather than releasing it, and the payoff does not arrive until later in the pathway.","Oxygen never receives a phosphate. It receives electrons and protons and leaves as water.","An isomerase does that conversion, and it happens in a separate step after the phosphate is already on.",null,"Glucose-6-phosphate is not what crosses into the mitochondrion; pyruvate is, and it uses its own transporter."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-3-1rvt47w","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-3","topic":"The membrane and what crosses it","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"A cell with an internal osmolarity of 300 mOsm is placed in a 0.15 M solution of NaCl, which dissociates completely. What happens to the cell volume?","opts":["It stays the same, because the outside is also 300 mOsm","It swells, because sodium enters and water follows it in","It shrinks, because 300 mOsm of NaCl outdraws the cytoplasm","It swells, because 150 mOsm outside is well below the 300 mOsm inside","It shrinks, because 0.15 M NaCl comes to 600 mOsm"],"correct":0,"why":"The whole question turns on counting particles rather than molecules. Each NaCl unit splits into two particles in solution, so 0.15 M times 2 gives 0.30 osmol per liter, which is 300 mOsm and matches the cell exactly. Equal osmolarity on both sides means no net water movement and no volume change. Multiply molarity by the number of ions a salt releases before comparing anything.","traps":[null,"Sodium does not freely leak in here, and water moves by osmosis rather than chasing a solute that stayed outside.","The outside is 300 mOsm, which equals the inside rather than exceeding it.","This forgets that NaCl splits into two particles, so it uses 150 mOsm instead of 300 mOsm.","This multiplies by four, 0.15 M NaCl yields 0.30 osmol per liter."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p079","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Natural killer (NK) cells patrol the body and destroy virus infected and tumor cells. They are best classified as part of which of the following?","opts":["T cell line","B cell line","The myeloid cell line","Adaptive immunity","Innate immunity"],"correct":4,"why":"Sort immune cells with one question: does this cell have to learn its target first. Natural killer cells do not; they watch for cells that have lost their class I major histocompatibility complex display, which is exactly what many viruses and tumors do to hide from cytotoxic T cells, and they kill on contact with perforin and granzymes. That missing self trigger, plus the absence of memory, places them in innate immunity even though they develop along the lymphoid line.","traps":["Cytotoxic T cells also kill infected cells, but they need antigen displayed on class I major histocompatibility complex, which is nearly the opposite of what triggers a natural killer cell.","B cells make antibodies and carry antigen specific receptors, while natural killer cells kill without any antigen specific receptor at all.","Natural killer cells develop along the lymphoid line with B and T cells, even though they behave like an innate first responder.","Adaptive cells need prior exposure and build memory, while natural killer cells attack on first contact using general danger signals.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1xq2vux","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A liver cell is flush with ATP and has very little AMP. What happens to glycolysis, and through which enzyme?","opts":["It slows down, because ATP inhibits pyruvate kinase, the committed step","It speeds up, because ATP is the substrate of the investment phase","It slows down, because ATP inhibits phosphofructokinase","It speeds up, because ATP activates phosphofructokinase","It is unaffected, because glycolysis is controlled only by how much glucose is available"],"correct":2,"why":"This is feedback inhibition asked in a cell-biology costume. Phosphofructokinase catalyzes the committed step, so it is the valve the cell turns: ATP and citrate close it while AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate open it. Any time a question hands you the energy state of a cell and asks about glycolysis, go straight to that enzyme.","traps":["Right logic, wrong enzyme: the committed, rate-limiting step of glycolysis is the one phosphofructokinase runs.","ATP is consumed in the investment phase, but being a substrate there does not set the pathway's rate.",null,"ATP does bind phosphofructokinase at a regulatory site, but it turns the enzyme down rather than up.","Substrate supply matters, but the pathway also reads the cell's energy state through feedback inhibition."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_dev003","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-16","topic":"Reproduction and development","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Skeletal muscle develops from which germ layer?","opts":["Mesoderm","Neural crest","Ectoderm","Multiple layers","Endoderm"],"correct":0,"why":"Germ layer questions get much simpler once you hold one sentence for each layer: ectoderm makes the outer covering and the nervous system, endoderm makes the lining of the gut tube and the organs that bud from it, and mesoderm makes everything in the middle. Muscle of all three types, bone, blood, kidney and heart are all middle layer structures. Skeletal muscle in particular traces back to the myotome portion of the somites, which are blocks of paraxial mesoderm.","traps":[null,"Neural crest supplies pigment cells, sensory and autonomic neurons and much of the connective tissue of the head, but it does not produce muscle fibers.","Ectoderm makes the nervous system and the outer covering, so it supplies the nerve that commands the muscle rather than the muscle itself.","Skeletal muscle has a single origin in mesoderm, specifically the myotome of the somites in the trunk and limbs.","Endoderm lines the gut tube and buds off its glands, so it makes the lining while the muscle wrapped around the gut comes from mesoderm."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-1yqv1yp","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"One glucose goes all the way through glycolysis. Counting what the cell spent as well as what it made, what is the net haul?","opts":["2 ATP, 2 NADH, 2 pyruvate","4 ATP, 2 NADH, 2 pyruvate","2 ATP, 4 NADH, 1 pyruvate","2 ATP, 2 FADH2, 2 pyruvate","4 ATP, 4 NADH, 2 pyruvate"],"correct":0,"why":"The whole question is gross versus net. The payoff phase makes 4 ATP and 2 NADH, but 2 ATP went in during the investment phase, so the cell clears 2. Say it as one sentence every time you see this: spend two, make four, keep two, plus two NADH and two pyruvate.","traps":[null,"That is the gross ATP from the payoff phase, before subtracting the 2 ATP invested at the start.","Glucose is split into two three-carbon pyruvates, and each three-carbon branch yields one NADH, not two.","Glycolysis never makes FADH2; the only FADH2 in respiration comes from succinate dehydrogenase in the Krebs cycle.","This takes the gross ATP and doubles the NADH at the same time, counting the payoff phase twice."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g010","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-11","topic":"Evolution","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"The Hardy-Weinberg model applies only when a specific set of conditions is met. Which of the following is NOT one of those conditions?","opts":["Infinite population","No migration into or out of the population","Random mating","No mutation","Equal sex ratio"],"correct":4,"why":"The model asks what allele frequencies do when nothing disturbs them, so its conditions are simply a list of the five things that do disturb them: mutation, non-random mating, gene flow, drift in a small population, and selection. Test each choice by asking whether it would change allele frequencies from one generation to the next. The ratio of males to females is not on that list, because the model tracks alleles rather than counts of each sex.","traps":["An effectively infinite population is required so that chance sampling between generations, which is genetic drift, does not shift frequencies.","No gene flow is one of the required conditions, since migrants arriving with different allele frequencies would shift the population's own.","Non-random mating such as inbreeding or assortative mating changes genotype proportions, so random mating is genuinely required.","Mutation creates new alleles and shifts frequencies, so ruling it out really is one of the required conditions.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"written:bio-4-idaha0","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-4","topic":"Cellular respiration","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A yeast culture is kept strictly anaerobic and fed 5 molecules of glucose. Every glucose runs through glycolysis and then alcoholic fermentation. How much ATP does the culture net?","opts":["10 ATP","20 ATP","5 ATP","150 ATP","35 ATP"],"correct":0,"why":"Fermentation contributes no ATP of its own, so the entire yield is the net 2 ATP per glucose from glycolysis. Five glucose at 2 ATP each is 10. When a question puts a cell in anaerobic conditions, reset your count to glycolysis alone and leave the electron carriers out of it.","traps":[null,"This uses the gross figure of 4 ATP per glucose and forgets the 2 invested up front.","This counts one ATP per glucose, but the net from glycolysis is 2 per glucose.","This is roughly the aerobic yield of about 30 per glucose, which requires oxygen and the full chain.","This cashes in the 2 NADH per glucose at 2.5 ATP each, but with no oxygen there is no chain to cash them in on."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-yiltle","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":3,"q":"The outer mitochondrial membrane is the gate for the intrinsic pathway, and two opposing families of proteins compete over it. A tumor cell is found to make far more Bcl-2 protein than normal. What is the effect on that cell?","opts":["It arrests the cell in G1, so the tumor ends up growing more slowly over time","It forces the cell into the extrinsic pathway, so death receptors take the decision over","It punches pores in the mitochondrial membrane, so the cell dies more readily than usual","It blocks cytochrome c release, so the cell survives damage that should kill it","It activates caspase-8 through the death receptor, which speeds apoptosis up"],"correct":3,"why":"Two families, one gate. Bax and Bak open the outer mitochondrial membrane and let cytochrome c out, and Bcl-2 is the antiapoptotic partner that holds them in check. Too much Bcl-2 means the gate never opens, so a cell carrying damage that should have ended it survives and keeps dividing, which is why this protein is overproduced in certain cancers. Sort the family by the first letters: Bax breaks the gate open, Bcl-2 bolts it closed.","traps":["Bcl-2 acts on cell death rather than on the cell cycle, and a cell that resists dying accumulates rather than slows down.","Blocking one pathway does not redirect a cell into the other, and the extrinsic pathway needs a ligand from outside to start it.","Pore formation is the job of the opposing family, Bax and Bak, and Bcl-2 exists specifically to keep those proteins in check.",null,"Caspase-8 belongs to the extrinsic pathway at the cell surface, and Bcl-2 acts at the mitochondrion instead."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"written:bio-7-t3v9co","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-7","topic":"Replication and mitosis","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A cell with DNA damage it cannot repair commits to the intrinsic pathway of apoptosis. Which molecule leaves the mitochondrion to set the cascade going, and where does it go once it is out?","opts":["Cytochrome c, into the cytosol, where it helps assemble the apoptosome","Calcium, into the nucleus, where it condenses the chromatin all by itself","Fas ligand, into the extracellular space, where it binds the neighboring cell","ATP synthase, into the cytosol, where it goes on to activate death receptors","Bcl-2, into the cytosol, where it directly cleaves the executioner caspases"],"correct":0,"why":"The elegant part of the intrinsic pathway is that it borrows a respiratory protein. Cytochrome c normally shuttles electrons in the intermembrane space, and when the outer membrane is permeabilized it spills into the cytosol, binds Apaf-1, and builds the apoptosome, which activates caspase-9. Finding cytochrome c in the cytosol is the classic sign that a cell has committed. Remember it by its address: inside the mitochondrion it means respiration, outside it means death.","traps":[null,"Calcium is stored mainly in the endoplasmic reticulum rather than released as the apoptotic trigger, and chromatin condensation happens downstream of the caspases.","Fas ligand is made by another cell entirely and belongs to the extrinsic pathway, not to anything released from a mitochondrion.","ATP synthase is anchored in the inner mitochondrial membrane and stays there, and death receptors are activated by ligands arriving from outside the cell.","Bcl-2 sits on the mitochondrial membrane holding this release shut, so it is the brake rather than the signal, and it is not a protease."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p021","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Bile is produced by which organ?","opts":["Gallbladder","Pancreas","Small intestine","Liver","Spleen"],"correct":3,"why":"Two organs share the bile story and the exam checks which does which. The liver's hepatocytes make bile continuously, then the gallbladder stores and concentrates it and squeezes it out when a fatty meal arrives. Made in the liver, kept in the gallbladder, used in the small intestine.","traps":["The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile between meals, and storing is not the same as producing.","The pancreas sends digestive enzymes and bicarbonate into the same stretch of intestine, which is easy to blur with bile, but it does not make bile.","The small intestine is where bile does its work on fat, not where it is made.",null,"The spleen recycles worn out red blood cells and releases bilirubin, which the liver then takes up and puts into bile."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d011","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A group that contains an ancestral species together with every one of its descendants is described as:","opts":["Monophyletic","Paraphyletic","Syngenetic","Polyphyletic","A sister group"],"correct":0,"why":"Phylogeny questions come down to three shapes, and it helps to draw them. Cut one branch cleanly off the tree and everything on that piece, the ancestor plus every descendant, is a clade, which is the monophyletic choice. Cut the branch but leave a twig behind on the tree and you have a paraphyletic group. Pick two twigs from separate branches because they look alike and you have a polyphyletic one.","traps":[null,"A paraphyletic group keeps the ancestor but drops some descendants, the classic case being reptiles with the birds left out.","This is not a standard phylogenetic term for a complete ancestor plus descendants group.","A polyphyletic group gathers species that resemble one another but leaves out their common ancestor, as grouping bats with birds because both fly would do.","A sister group is the lineage branching off next to the one you are looking at, a relative position on the tree rather than a rule about including all descendants."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_g059","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-8","topic":"Meiosis","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A newborn is diagnosed with Edwards syndrome. Which chromosomal abnormality accounts for it? In the choices below, T followed by a number means trisomy of that chromosome.","opts":["T13","A deletion on chromosome 18","XXY","T21","T18"],"correct":4,"why":"Edwards syndrome is three copies of chromosome 18. The fastest way to keep this set straight is to memorize the three numbers as a block, 21 for Down, 18 for Edwards, 13 for Patau, and then read the number in each choice rather than the name. The choice naming trisomy 18 is the one.","traps":["Trisomy 13 is Patau syndrome, the most severe of the three.","This has the right chromosome but the wrong mechanism, since Edwards syndrome is an extra whole copy of 18 rather than a missing segment of it.","The 47,XXY karyotype is Klinefelter syndrome, a sex chromosome condition rather than an autosomal trisomy.","Trisomy 21 is Down syndrome.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_d002","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-13","topic":"Diversity of life","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Fungi share a distinctive set of structural and nutritional features. Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of fungi?","opts":["Glycogen storage","Chitin walls","Cellulose walls","Hyphae that grow into a mycelium","Heterotrophic absorbers"],"correct":2,"why":"This question is really asking you to separate the fungal wall from the plant wall. Fungi work like animals on the inside, storing carbohydrate as glycogen and feeding by absorption, but they wall themselves in with chitin, the same material in an insect exoskeleton. Cellulose is the plant wall polymer, so the choice naming cellulose walls is the one that does not belong. Hold on to fungus equals chitin, plant equals cellulose, and this whole family of questions falls out.","traps":["Fungi store carbohydrate as glycogen, the same polymer animals use, so this is a fungal feature.","Chitin is exactly what a fungal cell wall is built from, so this belongs on the fungal list rather than being the exception.",null,"A mycelium of branching hyphae is the standard fungal body plan, so this is a fungal feature, not the exception.","Fungi secrete enzymes onto their food and absorb the digested product, so absorptive heterotrophy is a fungal feature."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"},{"id":"bio:BIO_p092","sub":"Biology","stop":"bio-15","topic":"Body systems at work","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is the basic repeating structural unit of compact bone?","opts":["Lamellae","Perforating (Volkmann) canals","Osteon (Haversian system)","Lacunae","Trabeculae"],"correct":2,"why":"Compact bone is organized around its blood supply. Each osteon is a cylinder of concentric lamellae built around a central Haversian canal carrying a vessel and a nerve, with osteocytes sitting in lacunae between the rings and reaching one another through canaliculi. Ask which structure contains all of the others and the choice naming the osteon is the repeating unit, while spongy bone uses trabeculae instead.","traps":["Lamellae are the concentric rings of matrix inside an osteon, which makes them a component of the unit rather than the unit itself.","Perforating canals run crosswise to connect the central canals of neighboring osteons, carrying vessels in from the periosteum.",null,"Lacunae are the small spaces in the matrix where individual osteocytes sit, linked to each other by canaliculi.","Trabeculae are the strut network of spongy bone, arranged along lines of stress rather than into cylinders."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"bio"}],"gc":[{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lc4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"An equilibrium mixture of N2(g) + 3 H2(g) ⇌ 2 NH3(g) sits in a sealed 5.00 L vessel at 500 K. A chemist injects 1.0 mol of inert argon while keeping the volume constant at 5.00 L. Considering that K_p depends only on partial pressures of N2, H2, and NH3 (not on Ar), which way does the equilibrium shift?","opts":["Equilibrium dissociates","Shifts reverse (toward N2 + H2)","Shifts forward, then reverse","No shift","Shifts forward (toward NH3)"],"correct":3,"why":"Adding inert gas at constant V does not change the partial pressures or molar concentrations of N2, H2, or NH3, so Q is unchanged and Q = K still holds. Le Chatelier's principle predicts no shift. (At constant pressure, dilution would shift toward more moles of gas.)","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_mc-e16","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Ethane combusts in air according to the balanced equation 2 C₂H₆(g) + 7 O₂(g) → 4 CO₂(g) + 6 H₂O(g). Starting with exactly 1.00 mol of C₂H₆, use the mole ratio of O₂ : C₂H₆ from the balanced equation to determine how many moles of O₂ are required.","opts":["1.0","2.0","3.0","3.5","7.0"],"correct":3,"why":"Stoichiometric ratio = 7 mol O₂ / 2 mol C₂H₆ = 3.5. So 1.00 mol C₂H₆ needs 1.00 × 3.5 = 3.5 mol O₂. Forgetting to halve the ratio gives the 7.0 trap; reading 7:2 inverted gives 3.0 or 2.0.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-cell-potential#tl00x0","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":2,"q":"Standard reduction potentials:\n\nZn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Zn E° = −0.76 V\nNi²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ni E° = −0.25 V\n\nWhat is E°cell for the galvanic cell built from these two half-cells?","opts":["+0.77 V","+0.51 V","−1.01 V","−0.51 V","+1.02 V"],"correct":1,"why":"Cathode = higher reduction potential (Ni). Anode = Zn. E°cell = (-0.25) − (-0.76) = +0.51 V. Red cat, an ox: reduction at the cathode, oxidation at the anode.","traps":["Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.",null,"You added the two table values. E°cell = E°cathode MINUS E°anode, both read as reductions.","A galvanic cell runs by itself, so E°cell must be POSITIVE. The higher reduction potential is the cathode.","You multiplied E° by the electron coefficients. Potential is intensive: never scale it when you balance."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_sf-e3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-1","topic":"The two questions: measurement and the lab","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"When ADDING measured values, the result must be rounded to the same DECIMAL PLACE as the input with the fewest decimals (not fewest sig figs). Compute 25.34 + 1.2 + 0.123 and report with correct precision.","opts":["26.66","26.663","26.6630","26.7","27"],"correct":3,"why":"Sum = 26.663. The least precise addend is 1.2 (one decimal place), so the sum must be rounded to one decimal place: 26.663 → 26.7. Addition uses decimal places (not sig figs); multiplication uses sig figs.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-first-order-half-life#1x4qwu9","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"A first-order reaction has a rate constant k = 0.347 s⁻¹. What is its half-life?","opts":["0.501 s","5.76 s","2.88 s","2 s","0.24 s"],"correct":3,"why":"First order: t½ = 0.693 / k = 0.693 / 0.347 = 2 s. It does not depend on the starting concentration.","traps":["Upside down. t½ = 0.693 / k, not k / 0.693.","You used 2 instead of ln 2 = 0.693.","That is 1/k. The half-life carries ln 2: t½ = 0.693 / k.",null,"You multiplied. A bigger k means a FASTER reaction and a SHORTER half-life, so k goes underneath."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:ph-q3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Mix 50 mL of 0.10 M HCl with 50 mL of 0.20 M NaOH. Final pH?","opts":["1.30","7.00","12.70","13.00","13.30"],"correct":2,"why":"mol H⁺ = 0.005, mol OH⁻ = 0.010. Excess OH⁻ = 0.005 mol in 100 mL. [OH⁻] = 0.05 M. pOH = −log(0.05) = 1.30. pH = 14 − 1.30 = 12.70.","traps":["That is the pH of the acid by itself, and the base here is present in twice the moles.","Equal moles would give 7; not equal here.",null,"That leaves the extra 0.005 mol in the original 50 mL instead of the combined 100 mL.","That uses the full 0.20 M hydroxide, ignoring both the acid it neutralized and the doubled volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:lw-e7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Lone pairs on central O in H₂O?","opts":["0","1","2","3","6"],"correct":2,"why":"O has 6 valence; 2 bonds use 2, leaving 4 e⁻ = 2 LP.","traps":["With no lone pairs water would be linear, and the bend near 104.5 degrees comes from pairs pushing the bonds down.","One pair leaves two of oxygen's six valence electrons unaccounted for.",null,"Three lone pairs plus two bonds would need ten electrons around oxygen, and a second row atom stops at eight.","Six is oxygen's valence electron count: two of those go into the O−H bonds, and the remaining four pair up into two lone pairs."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_gv-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-14","topic":"The charge ledger","nights":[14],"d":3,"q":"The Daniell cell Zn|Zn²⁺(1 M)‖Cu²⁺(1 M)|Cu has a standard cell potential E°cell = +1.10 V at 25°C, with n = 2 electrons transferred per mole of reaction. Using ΔG° = -n·F·E°cell with F = 96,485 C/mol, calculate ΔG° in kJ/mol.","opts":["+424 kJ/mol","−424 kJ/mol","−54 kJ/mol","+212 kJ/mol","−212 kJ/mol"],"correct":4,"why":"ΔG° = -n·F·E°cell = -(2)(96,485 C/mol)(1.10 V) = -212,267 J/mol ≈ -212 kJ/mol. The negative sign confirms spontaneity, consistent with E°cell > 0. Sign errors and forgetting the factor of n are the main error modes.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ti-h1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A 25.0 mL aliquot of 0.100 M acetic acid (CH₃COOH, K_a = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵) is titrated with 0.100 M NaOH. At the half-equivalence point, exactly half the acid has been converted to its conjugate base, so [HA] = [A⁻]. What is the pH of the solution at this point?","opts":["2.87","4.74","7.00","8.72","9.26"],"correct":1,"why":"At half-equivalence, the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation pH = pK_a + log([A⁻]/[HA]) reduces to pH = pK_a since [A⁻] = [HA] makes log(1) = 0. pK_a = -log(1.8×10⁻⁵) = 4.74, so pH = 4.74.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-dilution#1quyu21","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"How many mL of water must be ADDED to 100 mL of 3 M HCl to make a 1.5 M solution?","opts":["100 mL","101 mL","200 mL","300 mL","50 mL"],"correct":0,"why":"M1V1 = M2V2, so V2 = (3 × 100) / 1.5 = 200 mL total. Water added = 200 − 100 = 100 mL.","traps":[null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","That is the FINAL volume. The question asks how much water you add, so subtract the volume you started with.","You added the starting volume on top of the final volume. M1V1 = M2V2 already gives the total.","You divided the volume. Diluting makes the volume bigger, not smaller."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_lf006","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":3,"q":"For the combustion of methane: CH4(g) + 2 O2(g) → CO2(g) + 2 H2O(l), ΔH° = −890 kJ/mol and ΔS° = −243 J/(mol·K). At what temperature (approximate) does the spontaneity of the reaction reverse? (Assume ΔH° and ΔS° are temperature-independent.)","opts":["The reaction is always spontaneous; spontaneity never reverses","About 3,660 K - far above any practical temperature","About 27 K - only at cryogenic temperatures","The reaction is never spontaneous at any temperature","About 273 K (0°C)"],"correct":1,"why":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = 0 when T = ΔH/ΔS = 890,000 / 243 ≈ 3663 K. Below this T, ΔG < 0 (spontaneous); above, ΔG > 0. In practice, methane combustion is always spontaneous at attainable temperatures.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:x12","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"Combust 0.5 mol propane (C₃H₈), ΔH = −2220 kJ/mol. How much heat released?","opts":["25 kJ","4440 kJ","2220 kJ","1110 kJ","555 kJ"],"correct":3,"why":"q = 0.5 × 2220 = 1110 kJ. (Sign: |q| since asking heat released; ΔH is negative for exo combustion.)","traps":["Reading the 0.5 as grams rather than moles gives about 25 kJ; half a mole of propane is 22 g.","Doubling instead of halving gives 4440 kJ, which is the heat from 2 mol.","That is the heat from a full mole, and only half a mole burned here.",null,"Halving twice gives 555 kJ; the 0.5 mol calls for one halving."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:if5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-6","topic":"Shape and the sticky forces","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Which liquid has the LOWEST vapor pressure at 25°C?","opts":["Acetone ((CH₃)₂CO)","Diethyl ether ((C₂H₅)₂O)","Isopropyl alcohol ((CH₃)₂CHOH)","Ethylene glycol (HOCH₂CH₂OH)","Pentane (C₅H₁₂)"],"correct":3,"why":"Two -OH groups, so strongest H-bonding, so hardest to vaporize, so lowest VP.","traps":["Acetone is polar through its C=O, and with no O-H it cannot donate a hydrogen bond, so it evaporates readily.","Ether has an oxygen but no O-H, so it can accept hydrogen bonds without donating any, and it is famously volatile.","This one does hydrogen bond, and a single O-H per molecule holds far less than the two on ethylene glycol.",null,"Pentane has only dispersion forces, so it is the easiest of these to push into the vapor."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_mc-e18","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"Glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, MW = 180.0 g/mol) is the primary blood sugar. A 36.0 g sample contains 12 H atoms per glucose molecule. Use n_H = (m / MW) × 12 to compute the moles of hydrogen.","opts":["0.40","1.2","2.4","4.8","12"],"correct":2,"why":"n_glucose = 36/180 = 0.200 mol; n_H = 0.200 × 12 = 2.40 mol. Forgetting the ×12 stoichiometry per molecule gives the 0.20 trap; mistakenly multiplying by 6 (for C₆) gives 1.2.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"originals:ab-77b0d210b1","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"0.10 mol of a weak acid HA (pKa 5.00) is mixed with 0.050 mol of NaOH in 1.0 L of water. What is the pH of the resulting solution?","opts":["1.30","4.70","5.00","5.30","7.00"],"correct":2,"why":"Do the reaction first, then the equilibrium. NaOH converts 0.050 mol of HA into A-, leaving 0.050 mol HA and 0.050 mol A-. Equal amounts means pH equals pKa, so 5.00. Half neutralizing a weak acid is a classic way to make a buffer.","traps":["You treated 0.050 M as strong acid. The NaOH is consumed and what remains is a mix of HA and A-.","You used a base to acid ratio of 0.5. After the reaction there is 0.050 mol of HA and 0.050 mol of A-, a ratio of 1.",null,"You used a base to acid ratio of 2. Half of the HA remains, so the two are equal.","Partial neutralization of a weak acid does not give a neutral solution. It gives a buffer."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ks3","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"Silver chromate dissociates as Ag₂CrO₄(s) ⇌ 2Ag⁺(aq) + CrO₄²⁻(aq). Letting molar solubility = s, the ion concentrations become [Ag⁺] = 2s and [CrO₄²⁻] = s. Building the Ksp expression with stoichiometric coefficients as exponents, what is Ksp in terms of s?","opts":["Ksp = 27s⁴","Ksp = s²","Ksp = s³","Ksp = 2s²","Ksp = 4s³"],"correct":4,"why":"Ksp = [Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻] = (2s)²(s) = 4s²·s = 4s³. Forgetting the coefficient 2 in (2s)² gives s³; the 27s⁴ form applies to MX₃ (one M³⁺ and three X⁻) salts.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_at-e4","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-3","topic":"The quantum address, plus nuclear","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"Neutral iron (Fe, Z = 26) has a ground-state electron configuration of [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶. The six 3d electrons distribute across five degenerate 3d orbitals according to Hund's rule (maximize parallel spins before pairing). How many unpaired electrons does Fe have?","opts":["0","2","4","5","6"],"correct":2,"why":"Distribute 6 d-electrons in 5 orbitals: ↑↓ ↑ ↑ ↑ ↑. After all five are singly occupied (5 unpaired), the 6th electron pairs with one of them, leaving 4 unpaired electrons. The 4s² electrons are all paired and contribute 0 unpaired.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_m8","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A 0.500 mol sample of liquid water at 25°C contains both H and O atoms. Each H₂O molecule has 3 atoms (2 H + 1 O). Using Nₐ = 6.02×10²³ molecules/mol, how many TOTAL atoms (H plus O) are in the sample?","opts":["9.03 × 10²³ atoms","6.02 × 10²³ atoms","1.8 × 10²⁴ atoms","3.01 × 10²³ atoms","1.5 × 10²³ atoms"],"correct":0,"why":"Molecules = 0.500 × 6.02×10²³ = 3.01×10²³. Each molecule has 3 atoms, so total atoms = 3 × 3.01×10²³ = 9.03×10²³. Forgetting the ×3 for atoms-per-molecule gives 3.01×10²³ (just molecules).","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-combined-gas#1i6a2pp","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A gas occupies 6 L at 3 atm and 27 °C. What is its volume at 1 atm and 127 °C?","opts":["18 L","84.7 L","2.67 L","13.5 L","24 L"],"correct":4,"why":"P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2 in Kelvin. V2 = 6 × (3/1) × (400/300) = 24 L.","traps":["You ignored the temperature change. Both P and T changed here.","You used Celsius in the ratio. Convert both temperatures to Kelvin first.","Pressure ratio is upside down. More pressure squeezes the gas, so volume moves opposite to pressure.","Temperature ratio is upside down. Hotter gas expands, so volume moves with temperature.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_s-e6","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A 50.0 mL aliquot of 4.0 M sucrose solution is poured into a graduated cylinder, and water is added to dilute the solution to a final concentration of 2.0 M. Assuming volumes are additive and temperature is constant, what volume of water must be added?","opts":["200 mL","50 mL","150 mL","25 mL","100 mL"],"correct":1,"why":"M1V1 = M2V2 gives V2 = (4.0)(50.0)/(2.0) = 100. mL final volume. The water added is V2 - V1 = 100 - 50 = 50 mL. Forgetting the subtraction step gives 100 mL.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"generated:gc-limiting#xoxn1s","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"4 Fe + 3 O₂ → 2 Fe₂O₃\n\nIf 6 mol of Fe reacts with 3 mol of O₂, how many moles of Fe₂O₃ can form?","opts":["2 mol","9 mol","4 mol","3 mol","1 mol"],"correct":0,"why":"Fe: 6 / 4 = 1.5. O₂: 3 / 3 = 1. The smaller one limits. Product = 1 × 2 = 2 mol.","traps":[null,"You added the reactants. Moles are not conserved; use the mole ratio from the balanced equation.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You built the answer from the reagent in EXCESS. The smaller moles-per-coefficient runs out first and sets the yield.","You found the limiting amount but did not multiply by the product coefficient."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:ca5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-9","topic":"The energy ledger","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"Bomb calorimeter (constant V): combust gives 2.5 kJ heat. C_cal = 5.0 kJ/°C. ΔT?","opts":["0.5°C","5.0°C","12.5°C","2.0°C","2.5°C"],"correct":0,"why":"q = C_cal × ΔT → ΔT = 2500 J / 5000 J/°C = 0.5°C. (Note: bomb calorimetry uses heat capacity of whole apparatus, not m·c separately.)","traps":[null,"That is the calorimeter's heat capacity, the kilojoules needed per degree, not the degrees produced.","That multiplies the 2.5 kJ by the 5.0 kJ/°C; the heat capacity belongs underneath, since a bigger calorimeter warms less.","That divides 5.0 by 2.5 and flips the ratio; degrees come from kilojoules divided by kilojoules per degree.","That is the heat released in kilojoules read off as a temperature; divide it by the 5.0 kJ per degree first."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-gibbs#12ayr68","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-10","topic":"Downhill: entropy and free energy","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"For a reaction, ΔH = -120 kJ and ΔS = 200 J/K. What is ΔG at 400 K?","opts":["-120 kJ","-80100 kJ","-40 kJ","-80 kJ","-200 kJ"],"correct":4,"why":"TΔS = 400 × 200 J/K = 80 kJ. ΔG = -120 − (80) = -200 kJ, so the reaction is spontaneous at this temperature.","traps":["You forgot to multiply ΔS by the temperature.","Unit trap. ΔS is in J/K but ΔH is in kJ. Divide TΔS by 1000 before subtracting.","Sign slip. ΔG = ΔH MINUS TΔS.","You dropped ΔH. Both terms matter.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-ph#1xvu5bk","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-13","topic":"Proton traffic","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"What is the pH of a 5.0 × 10⁻² M solution of Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C?","opts":["1","1.3","12","12.7","13"],"correct":4,"why":"[OH⁻] = 2 × 5.0 × 10⁻² = 1.0 × 10⁻¹ M. pOH = 1, pH = 14 − 1 = 13.","traps":["That is the pOH. Finish with pH = 14 − pOH.","That is the pOH of the undoubled concentration. Double [OH⁻], then subtract the pOH from 14.","You treated the concentration as 1.0 × 10⁻ⁿ. Doubling 5.0 × 10⁻ⁿ moves you up a power of ten.","You forgot that Ba(OH)₂ releases TWO hydroxides. Double the concentration before taking the log.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-a:col-q12","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-8","topic":"States and solutions","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"You compute osmotic pressure with π = i·M·R·T. For the answer to come out in atm with M in mol/L and T in K, R must have units:","opts":["L·atm/(mol·K)","J/(mol·K)","atm·mol/(L·K)","cal/(mol·K)","mmHg·L/(mol·K)"],"correct":0,"why":"π [atm] = i · M [mol/L] · R · T [K]. Solving for R's units: atm / (mol/L · K) = atm·L/(mol·K) = L·atm/(mol·K). That is the gas constant 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K). Picking R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) gives π in J/L (not atm).","traps":[null,"R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) is the SI value, but it does not give atm directly. Use 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K) when the answer must be in atm.","Units do not balance: this would give π in (atm·mol/(L·K)) × (mol/L) × K = atm·mol²/L² - not a pressure unit.","Calorie units are obsolete for this and would produce the wrong magnitude - π in cal/L is not standard.","The structure is right but the pressure unit is not; R = 62.4 mmHg·L/(mol·K) returns the answer in mmHg, and this question asks for atm."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"generated:gc-ideal-gas#1nltpvs","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-7","topic":"Gases","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"What volume does 1 mol of an ideal gas occupy at 27 °C and 4 atm? (R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K)","opts":["624 L","6.16 L","98.5 L","9.24 L","0.554 L"],"correct":1,"why":"T = 27 + 273 = 300 K. V = nRT / P = (1)(0.0821)(300) / 4 = 6.16 L.","traps":["You used R = 8.314, which is for joules. With atm and liters use 0.0821.",null,"You multiplied by pressure. V = nRT / P, so pressure goes underneath.","Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You used Celsius. Gas laws only work in Kelvin: add 273."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:gc-atoms-count#xp7b40","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-2","topic":"The mole bridge","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"How many hydrogen ATOMS are in 51 g of NH₃? (molar mass 17 g/mol)","opts":["6.02 × 10²³","1.81 × 10²⁴","5.42 × 10²⁴","3.07 × 10²⁵","9"],"correct":2,"why":"51 g / 17 g/mol = 3 mol NH₃. Times 3 hydrogen per molecule = 9 mol atoms. Times 6.02 × 10²³ = 5.42 × 10²⁴.","traps":["You divided by the subscript. There are MORE atoms than molecules, so multiply.","That is the number of MOLECULES. Each NH₃ holds 3 hydrogen atoms, so multiply by 3.",null,"You multiplied grams by Avogadro. Convert grams to moles first.","That is moles of atoms. Multiply by 6.02 × 10²³ to count them."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"gchem-b:GC_ek-e5","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-12","topic":"Balance and the push-back","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"For the gas-phase reaction A(g) ⇌ B(g) at 298 K, the equilibrium constant is K_forward = 5.0. A chemist now writes the reverse reaction B(g) ⇌ A(g) at the same temperature. Using the rule that reversing a reaction inverts K, what is K_reverse?","opts":["0.20","5.0","0.50","25","−5.0"],"correct":0,"why":"Reversing a reaction gives K_reverse = 1/K_forward = 1/5.0 = 0.20. Squaring K (giving 25) corresponds to doubling stoichiometry, not reversing.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-b"},{"id":"gchem-a:b7","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-5","topic":"Why atoms bond","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Which compound has both ionic AND covalent bonds?","opts":["CO₂","NaCl","HCl","CH₃OH","NH₄Cl"],"correct":4,"why":"NH₄Cl: NH₄⁺ has covalent N - H bonds INSIDE the cation, then ionic to Cl⁻ between cation and anion. NaCl is purely ionic; HCl and CO₂ are purely covalent.","traps":["Carbon dioxide is covalent throughout, two double bonds and no charged pieces.","Sodium chloride is ionic all through, with no shared pairs anywhere in the lattice.","A single shared pair between hydrogen and chlorine is all there is here, polar but covalent.","Methanol's OH is a covalent O−H bond inside the molecule rather than a hydroxide ion, and nothing in it carries a full charge.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"},{"id":"gchem-a:xx-e13","sub":"Gen Chem","stop":"gc-11","topic":"How fast, not how far","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"Catalyst lowers Eₐ by 15 kJ/mol at 300 K. Approx rate ratio (k_new/k_old)?","opts":["~5","~400","~1","~6000","~50"],"correct":1,"why":"k_cat/k_old = exp(15000/(8.314×300)) = exp(6.01) ≈ 408.","traps":["That is far too small: an exponent near 6 multiplies the rate constant by hundreds, not by a handful.",null,"That comes from putting 15 into the exponent instead of 15000, leaving the barrier in kilojoules while R is in joules.","That is closer to e⁸·⁷, which would need a cut of about 22 kJ/mol.","That is roughly e⁴, which would correspond to a barrier cut near 10 kJ/mol instead of 15."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"gchem-a"}],"ochem":[{"id":"organic:oc-1iyt40m","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Optically pure (R)-3-phenylbutan-2-one (shown) is stirred in aqueous NaOH and then re-isolated. The alpha carbon bearing the phenyl group is the stereocenter. What is the stereochemical outcome and why?","opts":["The compound is destroyed by a haloform-type C-C cleavage at the stereocenter","A single diastereomer forms because a new stereocenter is created at the carbonyl carbon","The product is racemic (R and S in equal amounts) because base-promoted enolization at that alpha carbon makes it sp2 and planar, and reprotonation occurs equally on both faces","The (R) configuration is fully retained, because enolization occurs only toward the methyl group on the far side of the carbonyl, so the stereocenter is never flattened and never reprotonated","It is converted entirely to the (S) enantiomer, because hydroxide attacks the stereocenter from the face opposite the proton and displaces it with clean backside inversion of configuration"],"correct":2,"why":"The stereocenter is the alpha carbon and bears an alpha H. Aqueous NaOH (no halogen present) enolizes it, making it sp2/planar and erasing chirality; reprotonation on either face gives a 50:50 (R)/(S) racemate. option A correct. Not an SN2 inversion, no new stereocenter at the carbonyl, and haloform cleavage is impossible here because there is no halogen and the stereocenter is not a methyl-ketone CH3.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C(C)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-o828tv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":2,"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (ethynylcyclohexane, drawn) is treated with (1) BH3 then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","opts":["O=CC1CCCCC1","O=CCC1CCCCC1","CC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OCCC1CCCCC1","OC=CC1CCCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"This is a terminal alkyne (C#C-) bearing a cyclohexyl group; hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov, so the carbonyl ends up on the terminal carbon, giving cyclohexylacetaldehyde (a two-carbon aldehyde side chain). The methyl ketone is the Markovnikov product; cyclohexanecarbaldehyde (ring-CHO) has lost a carbon and the wrong skeleton; the alcohol is over-reduction; the enol is the intermediate.","traps":null,"qs":"C#CC1CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1usu1iz","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-7","topic":"The hungry carbon: aldehydes and ketones","nights":[7],"d":1,"q":"You want to convert benzaldehyde (drawn) into benzyl alcohol (PhCH2OH), a primary alcohol, without forming any new carbon-carbon bond. Which reagent set accomplishes this?","opts":["KMnO4, heat","1) CH3MgBr, ether; 2) H3O+","1) CH3Li, ether; 2) H3O+","1) PhMgBr, ether; 2) H3O+","1) NaBH4, MeOH; 2) H3O+"],"correct":4,"why":"Reducing the aldehyde with NaBH4 adds H to the carbonyl carbon, giving the primary alcohol PhCH2OH with no new C-C bond. Grignard/organolithium reagents (CH3MgBr, CH3Li, PhMgBr) all form a new C-C bond, changing the carbon skeleton. KMnO4 oxidizes rather than reduces.","traps":null,"qs":"O=Cc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-ym5nrn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":1,"q":"Propene is treated with HBr in the presence of organic peroxides (ROOR). What is the major product?","opts":["CCCBr","CC(Br)CBr","C=CCBr","CC(Br)C","CCCO"],"correct":0,"why":"HBr/peroxides is a radical chain (anti-Markovnikov) addition. Br radical adds to the less substituted carbon (forms the more stable secondary radical), so Br ends on the terminal carbon: 1-bromopropane (opt[0]). opt[1] is the Markovnikov product (ionic HBr), opt[2] is the Br2 dibromide, opt[3] is allyl bromide (allylic substitution), opt[4] is an alcohol.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CC","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1g5ezpj","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"A chemist runs a competitive hydrogenation comparing the relative rates of H2/Pd uptake for the three alkenes below. Based on alkene substitution, which ranks them CORRECTLY from FASTEST to SLOWEST hydrogenation? I = monosubstituted (1-hexene), II = disubstituted (cyclohexene), III = tetrasubstituted (2,3-dimethyl-2-butene).","opts":["III > II > I","I > II > III","I > III > II","II > I > III","III > I > II"],"correct":1,"why":"Heterogeneous hydrogenation rate decreases with increasing alkene substitution (more steric hindrance to flat adsorption on the catalyst; also slightly greater thermodynamic stability). Least-substituted reacts fastest: monosubstituted (I) > disubstituted (II) > tetrasubstituted (III).","traps":null,"qs":"CCCCC=C.C1CCC=CC1.CC(C)=C(C)C","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-yuv7z3","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"Styrene is shown below. Without any spectra, how many degrees of unsaturation (index of hydrogen deficiency) does its molecular formula C8H8 correspond to?","opts":["3","4","5","6","8"],"correct":2,"why":"DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2 = (16 + 2 - 8)/2 = 5. This accounts for the benzene ring (3 pi bonds + 1 ring = 4) plus the vinyl C=C (1), totaling 5.","traps":null,"qs":"C=Cc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"own-organic:ace-0010","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":2,"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with Br2 in dichloromethane. Where do the two bromines end up?","opts":["Both attached to the same carbon","On opposite faces only if the flask is heated","One bromine adds and the other stays in solution","On the same face of the ring","On opposite faces of the ring"],"correct":4,"why":"The first bromine closes into a three-membered bridge across one face of the ring, so the only face still open when the second bromine arrives is the other one. Anti addition is not a rule to memorize, it is what the bridge leaves room for.","traps":null,"qs":"C1=CCCC1","os":false,"src":"own-organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-wj81go","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"Which reagents are required to install the acetyl group and form acetophenone from benzene (shown) by Friedel-Crafts acylation?","opts":["CH3CH2Cl + AlCl3","CH3OH + H2SO4","CH3COOH + NaOH","CH3COCl + AlCl3","CH3CH2OH + PCC"],"correct":3,"why":"Friedel-Crafts acylation needs an acyl chloride (or anhydride) plus AlCl3, which forms the resonance-stabilized acylium ion CH3CO+. CH3CH2Cl/AlCl3 gives ethylbenzene (alkylation); the alcohol/acid options do not form an acylium electrophile.","traps":null,"qs":"c1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1khayk8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"A single enantiomer of 2-phenylpropanoic acid (stereocenter shown) is heated with methanol and catalytic H2SO4. Which structure is the major product, accounting for the stereochemistry at the alpha carbon?","opts":["COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Fischer esterification cleaves the acyl carbon-oxygen bond of the acid; the bond to the alpha stereocenter is never broken, so its spatial configuration is fully retained. The inversion structure (B) is wrong because no bond to the stereocenter is broken; (C) uses ethanol; (D) loses the stereochemistry; (E) is a chain-extended skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"C[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C(=O)O","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-h6tsou","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":1,"q":"Four candidate structures share the molecular formula but differ in functional group. An IR spectrum shows a strong band at 1740 cm-1 and NO absorption in the 2500-3500 cm-1 region (no O-H, no N-H). Of the molecule drawn, which statement about why it matches is correct?","opts":["It is an amide, and its two N-H bands are hidden underneath the strong C=O absorption","It is an ester: strong C=O ~1740 cm-1 and no O-H/N-H bands in the 2500-3500 region","It is a ketone, identified only by the lack of any 3300 cm-1 band","It is a carboxylic acid, so the broad O-H is simply too weak to see","It is an alcohol, and the 1740 cm-1 band is the C-O single bond stretch of its hydroxyl group"],"correct":1,"why":"Ethyl acetate (ester): strong C=O ~1735-1745 (higher than a ketone due to inductive pull of the ester O) and no O-H/N-H, so nothing broad in 2500-3500. The clean elevated carbonyl plus no broad region distinguishes ester from acid and amide.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)OCC","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1gh36h6","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"The compound shown (a beta-keto acid) is heated. What reaction occurs and what carbonyl product results?","opts":["No reaction; beta-keto acids are thermally stable","Decarboxylation, losing CO2 to give acetone","Reduction to a 1,3-diol","Decarboxylation to give acetic acid","Aldol condensation to a larger ring"],"correct":1,"why":"A beta-keto acid undergoes thermal decarboxylation through a six-membered cyclic transition state, expelling CO2. 3-oxobutanoic (acetoacetic) acid loses CO2 to give acetone.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)CC(=O)O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-105eytk","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"You isolate the product shown below from treating an alkene with a halogen source. The product is a vicinal chlorohydrin (OH and Cl on adjacent carbons). Which single reagent/condition set would produce it from cyclohexene?","opts":["Cl2, H2O","NaCl, H2O","HCl (gas)","Cl2, NaOH (concentrated)","Cl2, CCl4 (no water)"],"correct":0,"why":"A chlorohydrin (anti Cl + OH) comes from Cl2/H2O (chloronium opened by water). Dry Cl2/CCl4 gives dichloride; HCl gives only chloride; NaCl/H2O is unreactive; conc. NaOH would eliminate.","traps":null,"qs":"OC1CCCCC1Cl","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-12rz7w0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-8","topic":"The reactivity ladder: acid derivatives","nights":[8],"d":1,"q":"The carboxylic acid shown is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","opts":["The unchanged carboxylic acid (no reaction)","A primary alcohol (1-hexanol)","A methyl ketone","An aldehyde (hexanal)","A secondary alcohol"],"correct":1,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a carboxylic acid all the way to a primary alcohol (it does not stop at the aldehyde). Hexanoic acid gives 1-hexanol.","traps":null,"qs":"CCCCCC(=O)O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-8s0vx8","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-11","topic":"The ring that refuses: aromatics","nights":[11],"d":1,"q":"The molecule shown (p-nitrotoluene) is treated with hot KMnO4, then acidic workup. Which product results?","opts":["4-nitrobenzaldehyde","no reaction; the ring resists oxidation","4-nitrobenzoic acid","4-aminobenzoic acid","4-nitrobenzyl alcohol"],"correct":2,"why":"Hot KMnO4 oxidizes a methyl bearing benzylic H all the way to COOH; the ring and nitro are untouched, giving 4-nitrobenzoic acid. It does not stop at aldehyde/alcohol nor reduce the nitro.","traps":null,"qs":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1gf08ol","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-3","topic":"Molecules in 3D","nights":[3],"d":1,"q":"Assign the geometry (E or Z) of the carbon-carbon double bond in the alkene shown (a 2-bromo-3-chloropentene). Watch the priority comparisons carefully.","opts":["The geometry is undefined as drawn","Both an E and a Z descriptor apply equally","Z","E","The alkene has no E/Z isomerism"],"correct":3,"why":"Left carbon: Cl > CH2CH3; right carbon: Br > CH3. The two higher-priority groups (Cl, Br) are on opposite sides = E.","traps":null,"qs":"CC/C(Cl)=C(/C)Br","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-16yhmrn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane (drawn) is placed in warm methanol containing no added strong base. Classify the competing reactions and identify which substitution mechanism is operative.","opts":["Only SN2 occurs, with inversion of configuration at the tertiary carbon","SN1 and E2 compete, pairing unimolecular substitution with a bimolecular elimination in which methanol removes a beta-hydrogen as bromide leaves in the same step","Only E2 occurs, giving exclusively the Hofmann alkene","SN2 and E2 compete (bimolecular), because methanol is a strong enough base and nucleophile to attack the substrate directly in one concerted step","SN1 and E1 compete (unimolecular), because the tertiary substrate ionizes to a carbocation in a polar protic solvent with a weak nucleophile"],"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary halide in warm methanol (polar protic, weak nucleophile, no strong base) = solvolysis: SN1 and E1 compete; substitution via SN1. SN2 blocked (tertiary), E2 needs strong base (absent). Correct=0.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-btti9k","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":1,"q":"2-Cyclohexenone (drawn) reacts with lithium dimethylcuprate, (CH3)2CuLi, followed by aqueous workup. Which statement correctly describes the outcome?","opts":["Two methyl groups add, one at the carbonyl and one at the beta-carbon","The enone is reduced to cyclohexanone with no methyl incorporation","A methyl group adds to the carbonyl carbon (1,2-addition) to give 1-methyl-2-cyclohexenol","A methyl group adds to the beta-carbon (1,4-addition) to give 3-methylcyclohexanone","The cuprate deprotonates the alpha-carbon and no addition occurs"],"correct":3,"why":"Gilman reagents (lithium dialkylcuprates) are soft and selectively do 1,4-conjugate addition, delivering the alkyl group to the beta-carbon. After tautomerization of the enol, the product is 3-methylcyclohexanone. Organolithiums/Grignards favor 1,2-addition instead.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CCCC=C1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1a882gd","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with HBr (no peroxides). Which structure is the major product?","opts":["CC1CCCC(Br)C1","CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","BrC1CCCCCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br"],"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov addition: protonating C2 gives a tertiary cation at the methyl-bearing C1; Br- bonds there, giving 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane. Distractors: secondary anti-Markovnikov bromide, vic-dibromide (Br2), ring-expanded bromocycloheptane, remote-Br isomer. Keyed structure verified correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1=CCCCC1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1s6ocho","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Aniline (drawn below) has its nitrogen attached directly to a benzene ring. Compared with the nitrogen in a simple sp3 alkylamine, the aniline nitrogen is best described as:","opts":["Closer to sp2/planar, because its lone pair partially conjugates with the aromatic ring","sp2, with the ring carbon bearing the nitrogen rehybridizing to sp3","Purely sp3 and identical in geometry to a simple alkylamine","sp3, with the lone pair fully delocalized into the aromatic ring the way pyridine's lone pair is","Purely sp and linear at nitrogen"],"correct":0,"why":"The aniline N lone pair conjugates (donates) into the ring, flattening the nitrogen toward planarity and giving it sp2 character relative to an sp3 amine, which weakens its basicity. It is not sp/linear, and the ipso carbon stays sp2. Distractor 4 confuses aniline (exocyclic donor N) with pyridine (ring N).","traps":null,"qs":"Nc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-19ll1h0","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"The chiral epoxide (R)-2-methyloxirane (propylene oxide) is opened by sodium azide (NaN3, basic conditions). Considering BOTH the regiochemistry of basic opening AND the stereochemistry of the SN2 step, which statement is correct about the carbon that the azide attacks?","opts":["Azide attacks the less-substituted CH2 carbon, and the backside displacement inverts the configuration at the neighboring stereocenter as the ring opens","Azide attacks the more-substituted carbon with retention of configuration","Azide attacks the more-substituted carbon under basic conditions, and backside attack at that carbon inverts the configuration of the original stereocenter","Azide attacks both carbons, racemizing the product","Azide attacks the less-substituted CH2 carbon, which is not a stereocenter, so the original stereocenter is untouched and retains its configuration"],"correct":4,"why":"Under basic conditions azide does SN2 at the less-substituted terminal CH2. That carbon is not a stereocenter, so SN2 inversion there has no stereochemical consequence; the methyl-bearing stereocenter is never bonded to the nucleophile and retains its configuration. Layers basic-condition regioselectivity with the recognition that inversion only matters at the carbon actually attacked. Keyed answer (option 1) is correct.","traps":null,"qs":"CC1CO1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-vw99iv","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), a small unhindered base. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","opts":["CCC(C)=C","CCOC(C)(C)CC","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)C","C=CC(C)C"],"correct":2,"why":"A small base follows Zaitsev, giving the more-substituted alkene 2-methyl-2-butene (trisubstituted) over the disubstituted 2-methyl-1-butene. Choice B is the Hofmann product (needs a bulky base), C is wrong regiochemistry, D is an ether from substitution, and E is the reduced skeleton.","traps":null,"qs":"CCC(C)(Br)C","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"originals:ab-0a1ca31176","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-1","topic":"The only reaction in organic chemistry","nights":[1],"d":2,"q":"Which of these acids has the lowest pKa?","opts":["2-chlorobutanoic acid","3-chlorobutanoic acid","Butanoic acid","They are all equally strong","4-chlorobutanoic acid"],"correct":0,"why":"The inductive effect works through sigma bonds and drops off with every bond it has to cross. Put the chlorine on the carbon next to the carboxyl group and it stabilizes the carboxylate the most.","traps":[null,"The chlorine is one carbon further from the carboxyl group, so its pull is weaker.","With no electron-withdrawing group this is the weakest of the four acids.","The inductive effect depends on distance, so position matters.","The inductive effect fades quickly with distance. This chlorine is too far away to help much."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"originals"},{"id":"organic:oc-jwcdpe","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-13","topic":"Reading the machines: spectroscopy, lab and synthesis","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Acetic acid (drawn) is heated with excess ethanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (Fischer conditions). The product is then identified by IR. Which single answer correctly pairs the product with its diagnostic IR change relative to the starting acid?","opts":["Product is ethyl acetate; a new broad O-H stretch near 3300 cm-1 appears while the C=O absorption near 1740 cm-1 disappears entirely","Product is ethanol only; all carbonyl absorption is lost","Product is acetamide; a sharp N-H stretch (~3300) replaces the carbonyl","Product is acetaldehyde; the C=O moves to about 1715 cm-1 and a pair of diagnostic aldehyde C-H stretches near 2720 and 2820 cm-1 appears","Product is ethyl acetate; the broad O-H stretch (~2500-3300 cm-1) of the acid disappears while a strong C=O (~1740 cm-1) remains"],"correct":4,"why":"Fischer esterification of acetic acid with ethanol (H2SO4 cat.) gives ethyl acetate (CCOC(C)=O). Diagnostic IR: the very broad carboxylic acid O-H (2500-3300 cm-1) is lost while a strong ester C=O remains near 1740 cm-1. No new O-H, aldehyde C-H, or N-H forms.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(=O)O","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-sm043k","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,4-dibromobutane (drawn), then NaOH/H2O, then H3O+/heat. What is the major product of this intramolecular dialkylation?","opts":["OC(=O)C1(C(=O)O)CCCC1","OC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","OC(=O)C1CCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Both alpha-C-H positions are alkylated by the two ends of the dibromide, closing a ring; malonate carbon plus four CH2 units form a five-membered ring; after hydrolysis/decarboxylation one COOH remains = cyclopentanecarboxylic acid. opt[0] matches. Distractors: cyclobutane (miscount), cyclohexane (extra carbon), gem-diacid (no decarboxylation), open-chain adipic acid (no cyclization).","traps":null,"qs":"BrCCCCBr","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-10b6fdu","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-6","topic":"Pay twice, plus the acidic H: alkynes","nights":[6],"d":1,"q":"Phenylacetylene (drawn) is treated with (1) disiamylborane (Sia2BH) then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","opts":["OC=Cc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the terminal CH carbon, and oxidation/tautomerization gives the aldehyde phenylacetaldehyde. Acetophenone is the Markovnikov product; 2-phenylethanol is alkene-hydration thinking; the enol is the intermediate; 1-phenylethanol has the OH on the wrong carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"C#Cc1ccccc1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-xw1wee","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-5","topic":"The center of the universe: alkenes","nights":[5],"d":1,"q":"Styrene (shown) is treated with hot, concentrated KMnO4. What are the carbon-containing oxidation products?","opts":["Benzoic acid and CO2","Benzaldehyde and CO2","Acetophenone and formic acid","Benzoic acid and formaldehyde","Benzaldehyde and formaldehyde"],"correct":0,"why":"Hot concentrated KMnO4 is oxidative cleavage. The CH carbon on the ring becomes benzoic acid; the terminal =CH2 is fully oxidized and lost as CO2. Benzaldehyde/formaldehyde would require reductive ozonolysis, not hot KMnO4.","traps":null,"qs":"C=Cc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-p9yusn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-4","topic":"The four questions: substitution and elimination","nights":[4],"d":1,"q":"The substrate shown is treated with water (H2O) as the solvent and nucleophile, with no added base. Which mechanism best describes how this reaction proceeds?","opts":["SN1, because the substrate is tertiary and forms a stable 3 degree carbocation in the polar protic solvent","SN2, because water performs a fast backside attack on the tertiary carbon and displaces the leaving group in one step","SN2, because the bulky methyl groups accelerate backside displacement","No reaction, because tertiary halides are completely unreactive toward water unless a strong nucleophile or base is added","E2, because water is a strong enough base to force concerted elimination"],"correct":0,"why":"tert-Butyl bromide is 3 degree. A weak neutral nucleophile (water) in polar protic solvent favors SN1: ionization to the stable tertiary cation then capture by water; SN2 is sterically blocked.","traps":null,"qs":"CC(C)(C)Br","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1x3lymn","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-10","topic":"The alpha carbon wakes up","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"5,5-Dimethylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (drawn) is treated with the anion of nitromethane (-CH2NO2), a stabilized carbon nucleophile, then mild acid. Which structure is the major product?","opts":["OC1(C[N+](=O)[O-])CC(C)(C)CC=C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CC(C[N+](=O)[O-])C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CC(ON=O)C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CCC1C[N+](=O)[O-]","O=C1C(C[N+](=O)[O-])C(C)(C)CC=C1"],"correct":1,"why":"The nitronate is a soft, stabilized carbon nucleophile and adds 1,4: its carbon bonds to the beta carbon (C3), and the enolate is protonated to restore the saturated ketone = option 0 (verified: nitromethyl carbon sits at the beta position, 2 bonds from the carbonyl carbon). Option 1 is the 1,2-adduct (allylic alcohol, alkene retained). Option 2 attaches nitromethyl at the alpha carbon and keeps the alkene. Option 3 shows nitrite O-N=O (O-attachment, no C-C bond). Option 4 places the new C-C bond at the alpha carbon.","traps":null,"qs":"O=C1CC(C)(C)CC=C1","os":true,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1k62gcc","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-9","topic":"The switchboard: alcohols, ethers, epoxides","nights":[9],"d":1,"q":"Phenol (drawn below) is converted to ethyl phenyl ether (phenetole) by a Williamson ether synthesis. Which set of reagents accomplishes this transformation?","opts":["NaOH, then (CH3)3CBr (tert-butyl bromide)","NaBH4, then CH3CH2Br","CH3CH2OH with H2SO4 (acid only)","NaOH, then CH3CH2Br","CH3CH2MgBr (ethyl Grignard)"],"correct":3,"why":"Phenol is acidic enough to be deprotonated by NaOH to give phenoxide, which then does SN2 on the primary halide ethyl bromide to form ethyl phenyl ether. tert-Butyl bromide would eliminate; acid-only and Grignard do not perform Williamson coupling; NaBH4 is irrelevant. Keyed answer (option 1) is correct and unique.","traps":null,"qs":"Oc1ccccc1","os":false,"src":"organic"},{"id":"organic:oc-1fqqcym","sub":"OChem","stop":"oc-12","topic":"Different arrows: radicals and Diels-Alder","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"1-Hexene (drawn) is treated with NBS under radical allylic conditions. The allylic radical formed is delocalized, and the product that results from bromination at the terminal carbon (with double-bond transposition) predominates as the conjugated, more substituted alkene. Which structure is that product?","opts":["BrC/C=C\\CCC","C=CC(Br)CCC","BrCCCCCC=C","BrC/C=C/CCC","C=CCC(Br)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"NBS abstracts the C3 allylic H, giving an allyl radical delocalized between C1 and C3. Trapping at C1 transposes the double bond to C2=C3, giving the more substituted internal alkene (E)-1-bromo-2-hexene (option A); the E isomer is favored over Z. The non-transposed 3-bromo-1-hexene keeps a terminal alkene; the Z isomer and homoallylic placements are wrong.","traps":null,"qs":"C=CCCCC","os":true,"src":"organic"}],"qr":[{"id":"generated:qr-sci-notation-product#az79xq","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"Compute (3 × 10⁵)(7 × 10⁻⁶) and give the answer in proper scientific notation.","opts":["2.1 × 10⁻³⁰","2.1 × 10⁻²","1 × 10⁰","2.1 × 10⁻¹","2.1 × 10⁰"],"correct":4,"why":"3 × 7 = 21 and 10⁵ × 10⁻⁶ = 10⁻¹. 21 × 10⁻¹ is not proper form, so write 2.1 × 10⁰. Answer: 2.1 × 10⁰.","traps":["You multiplied the exponents. Powers of ten ADD when you multiply.","The exponent moved the wrong way. A smaller front number needs a LARGER power of ten.","You added the front numbers. Multiply them.","You moved the decimal but did not raise the exponent. Making the front number 10× smaller means the power goes UP by one.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:bnm3","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What does (a + b)² − (a − b)² simplify to?","opts":["2a² + 2b²","0","2ab","2b²","4ab"],"correct":4,"why":"(a²+2ab+b²) − (a²−2ab+b²) = 4ab. The squared terms cancel.","traps":["You added the two squares instead of subtracting, which gives 2a² + 2b².","They're not equal - check the signs.","You counted the cross term once instead of twice, so it should be 4ab.","You kept the signs inside the second bracket instead of flipping them as you subtracted.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1161","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":3,"q":"An $89 pair of shoes is discounted 22 percent. What is the sale price?","opts":["19.58","67","69.42","108.58","111"],"correct":2,"why":"New = 89 × (1 - 22/100) = 89 × 0.78 = 69.42.","traps":["That is 22 percent of 89, the size of the discount rather than the price left after it.","You subtracted 22 dollars instead of 22 percent, and 22 percent of 89 is 19.58.",null,"That puts 22 percent on instead of taking it off.","You added 22 dollars, and the problem takes 22 percent off."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-empirical-rule#12f5m7i","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"Scores are normally distributed with mean 100 and standard deviation 5. About what percent of scores fall between 95 and 110?","opts":["47.5%","68%","81.5%","84%","95%"],"correct":2,"why":"Empirical rule: 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD. Halves: 34% and 47.5% per side, tails 16% and 2.5%. Here that gives 81.5%.","traps":["That is only the upper half of the band. Add the 34% below the mean.","That only covers one SD each side. The upper limit here is TWO SD above.",null,"Close. Add 34% (one SD below) to 47.5% (two SD above).","That covers two SD each side. The lower limit here is only ONE SD below."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:fam4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A roll holds 4 1/2 meters of tubing, and 1 3/4 meters are cut from it. How many meters are left?","opts":["2 3/4","3 1/4","3 3/4","6 1/4","2 1/4"],"correct":0,"why":"4 1/2 = 9/2 = 18/4. 1 3/4 = 7/4. 18/4 − 7/4 = 11/4 = 2 3/4.","traps":[null,"You subtracted the whole numbers, then took the fraction gap as a positive 1/4 instead of borrowing.","You subtracted only the whole numbers and carried the 3/4 down untouched.","You added instead of subtracting; 4 1/2 plus 1 3/4 is 6 1/4.","You took 4 minus 1 to get 3 and then subtracted the full 3/4, without adding back the 1/2 you were holding."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:tri1660","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":3,"q":"Three dowels measure 15, 7, and 7 centimeters. Can the three dowels form a triangle?","opts":["Yes","No","Only if right triangle","Only if it is obtuse","Cannot determine"],"correct":1,"why":"|15−7| = 8 and 15+7 = 22. 7 is ≤ min, so invalid.","traps":["The two shorter sides, 7 and 7, reach only 14, which cannot span the 15, so the ends never meet.",null,"No angle rescues these lengths: 7 and 7 still cover only 14, short of 15.","Angles come after the sides close, and 7 plus 7 never reaches 15, so no obtuse version exists either.","Three side lengths are all the rule needs: 7 plus 7 is 14, and that has to beat 15."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:g2dm4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-9","topic":"Geometry: area, perimeter, volume","nights":[9],"d":2,"q":"A circle's radius triples. Area changes by what factor?","opts":["×6","×81","×3","×9","×27"],"correct":3,"why":"Area scales as r². Tripling r gives 3² = 9× the area.","traps":["Multiplied the factor of 3 by the 2 in the exponent instead of squaring it.","Squared the scale factor twice over, once more than an area calls for.","Linear scaling, but area is squared.",null,"That's cubed, for volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-b:QR_xp003","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":3,"q":"A bag contains 5 red marbles and 7 blue marbles. Three marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that exactly 2 are red and 1 is blue?","opts":["7/22","5/12","21/44","7/12","15/44"],"correct":0,"why":"Use combinations: P = C(5,2) · C(7,1) / C(12,3) = (10)(7) / 220 = 70/220 = 7/22.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-b"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1476","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A shift lasts 3.6 hours. How many minutes is that?","opts":["0.06","21.6","63.6","186","216"],"correct":4,"why":"1 hr = 60 min, so 3.6 hr × 60 = 216 min.","traps":["You divided the 3.6 by 60 instead of multiplying; minutes are smaller than hours, so the count grows.","You multiplied by 6 instead of 60, dropping a zero.","You added 60 to the 3.6 instead of multiplying the 3.6 by it.","You read 3.6 hours as 3 hours and 6 minutes; the 0.6 is six tenths of an hour, which is 36 minutes.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:uni1478","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":3,"q":"How many seconds are in 0.6 minutes?","opts":["0.01","3.6","6","36","60.6"],"correct":3,"why":"1 min = 60 sec, so 0.6 min × 60 = 36 sec.","traps":["You divided the 0.6 by 60 instead of multiplying; seconds are smaller, so the count has to grow.","You multiplied by 6 instead of 60, dropping a zero.","You read the 0.6 as six seconds; six tenths of a minute is 36 seconds.",null,"You added 60 to the 0.6 instead of multiplying the 0.6 by it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1058","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"One beaker holds 4/12 of a liter and a second holds 15/12 of a liter. The first amount is what fraction of the second?","opts":["12/5","60/144","4/15","19/144","60/24"],"correct":2,"why":"Flip the divisor and multiply: 4/12 times 12/15. The two 12s cancel on sight, which leaves 4/15 without any heavy arithmetic. Cancelling before you multiply is what keeps numbers like 180 off your page.","traps":["That flips the first fraction instead of the second. Check the size: dividing by something bigger than one gives an answer smaller than what you started with.","You multiplied straight across without flipping the divisor first.",null,"You added the tops (4 + 15) over the multiplied bottoms, treating the division sign as a times sign.","You multiplied straight across without flipping, then added the bottoms (12 + 12)."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:bnm4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"Two numbers have a sum of 7 and a product of 10. What is the sum of their squares?","opts":["19","29","39","49","69"],"correct":1,"why":"(x+y)² = x² + 2xy + y² → 49 = x² + y² + 20, so x² + y² = 29.","traps":["You subtracted xy one time too many, taking 49 down by 30 rather than 20.",null,"You subtracted xy once instead of 2xy.","That's (x+y)².","You added 2xy instead of subtracting it."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:est1211","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"A bottling line fills 2,984 vials an hour. How many vials does it fill in 80 hours?","opts":["23872","238720","239000","240000","241000"],"correct":1,"why":"Work it out: 2984 × 80 = 238720. Rounding first is fine for picking between far apart choices, but here the exact value is offered.","traps":["That is 2984 times 8, so the zero in the 80 went missing.",null,"That is the product rounded to the nearest thousand, when the exact 238,720 is offered.","That is 3000 times 80, the rounded estimate rather than the exact product.","That is more than 2,000 above the product, so the rounding ran past even 3000 times 80."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:abs1740","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"What values of x satisfy |x − 3| = 7?","opts":["-4 or -10","4 or -10","10 or -4","4 or -4","10"],"correct":2,"why":"|x − 3| = 7 means x − 3 = ±7, so x = 10 or -4.","traps":["That solves the positive branch correctly and then subtracts the constant on the negative branch instead of adding it. Both branches end the same way: move the constant to the other side.","You subtracted the 3 instead of adding it back on both branches.",null,"That pairs 4 with its negative, and the branches give 3 plus 7 and 3 minus 7, which are 10 and -4.","You solved x - 3 = 7 and stopped, and the branch x - 3 = -7 gives -4 as well."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1392","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One assistant can stock a supply room in 5 hours and another can stock it in 8 hours. Working together, how many hours does the job take?","opts":["3.08","5","6.15","6.5","13"],"correct":0,"why":"1/T = 1/5 + 1/8 = 13/40. T = 40/13 = 3.08 hr.","traps":[null,"Reporting the faster worker's solo time; the help pulls it lower still.","Using 2ab/(a+b), the average speed shortcut, where shared work calls for ab/(a+b).","Averaging the two times instead of combining the rates.","Adding the two times; together they finish sooner than either one alone."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:lin1263","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":3,"q":"What value of x satisfies 11x + 9 = 3x + 3?","opts":["-1.75","-0.75","0.25","0.75","1.5"],"correct":1,"why":"Move x: (11-3)x = 3-9 → 8x = -6, so x = -0.75.","traps":["Substituting -1.75 gives -10.25 on the left and -2.25 on the right.",null,"Substituting 0.25 gives 11.75 on the left and 3.75 on the right.","Dividing -6 by 8 gives -0.75, so this answer drops the minus sign.","The 9 was added to the 3 instead of subtracted, leaving 8x = 12."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-successive-percent#1ff0sqj","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $50 item is marked UP by 20% and then the new price is marked DOWN by 25%. What is the final price?","opts":["$37.50","$45.00","$46.00","$47.50","$60.00"],"correct":1,"why":"Multiply the factors: 50 × 1.2 × 0.75 = $45.00. Percents stack by multiplying, never by adding.","traps":["You skipped the mark-up and only applied the discount.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You added and subtracted the percents. The second percent is taken of the NEW price, so multiply the two factors.","You stopped after the mark-up. Apply the mark-down to that new price."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:mix1418","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":3,"q":"A photo lab combines 200 mL of a 10% developer solution with 150 mL of a 20% developer solution. What is the approximate percent concentration of the combined solution?","opts":["14.29","15","15.71","50","52.5"],"correct":0,"why":"Solute total = 20 + 30 = 50 mL in 350 mL → 14.29%.","traps":[null,"Averaged the two percents without weighting them by how much of each solution went in.","Paired each percent with the other solution's volume, putting the 20 percent on the 200 mL pour.","That is the total milliliters of solute, not the percent of the mixture.","Took 15 percent, the plain average of the two strengths, of the 350 mL total, which gives a volume rather than a concentration."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-1gxpf2c","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":2,"q":"An angle θ in standard position has tan θ greater than 0 and sin θ less than 0. In which quadrant does θ lie?","opts":["Quadrant III","Quadrant IV","Quadrant I or Quadrant III, since the sine does not narrow it further","Quadrant II","Quadrant I"],"correct":0,"why":"Take the two conditions one at a time. Tangent is positive wherever sine and cosine share a sign, which is the first and third quadrants, and sine is negative in the third and fourth. The only quadrant on both lists is the third. Running each condition separately and overlapping the lists beats trying to recall one combined rule.","traps":[null,"Sine is negative here, which fits, but tangent is negative here too, so the first condition fails.","The sine condition does rule out the first quadrant, because sine is positive there.","Sine is positive and tangent is negative here, so both conditions fail.","Both sine and tangent are positive here, so this misses the requirement that sine be negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"qr-a:fra1029","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"What is the sum of 8/14 and 8/12?","opts":["6/21","64/168","26/21","16/26","37/28"],"correct":2,"why":"Reduce first: 8/14 is 4/7 and 8/12 is 2/3. Sevenths and thirds meet at 21, so 12/21 plus 14/21 is 26/21. Reducing before you pick a denominator keeps you out of the big numbers.","traps":["You put the reduced tops, 4 and 2, over 21 without rescaling either one.","You multiplied the two fractions instead of adding them.",null,"You added the tops and the bottoms straight across, 16 over 26.","You forced both into twenty-eighths and turned 2/3 into 21/28, which is three quarters."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-two-draws#1nnrf4b","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-12","topic":"Probability and counting","nights":[12],"d":2,"q":"A bag holds 6 red and 6 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn WITHOUT replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","opts":["1/2","5/24","1/4","5/22","5/11"],"correct":3,"why":"First red: 6/12. Second red: 5/11. Multiply: 5/22.","traps":["That is the chance for ONE draw. Both draws must be red, so multiply two probabilities.","You lowered the reds but not the total. The second draw comes from one fewer marble.","That is WITH replacement. After one red leaves, there is one fewer red and one fewer marble.",null,"That is only the second draw. Multiply by the first draw too."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1330","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"If -3x < 1, which inequality must be true of x?","opts":["x < 0.33","x < -0.33","x > 0.33","x > -0.33","x = -0.33"],"correct":3,"why":"Divide both sides by -3 (negative) and FLIP: x > -0.33.","traps":["You divided by 3 rather than -3, so neither the sign nor the direction changed.","You kept the inequality pointing the same way after dividing by a negative.","You flipped the direction but dropped the negative on 1/-3.",null,"You solved the boundary equation -3x = 1 instead of the range of values."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:coo1452","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-10","topic":"Triangles and the coordinate plane","nights":[10],"d":2,"q":"A greenhouse layout places one bench at (3, 7) and another at (9, 12) on a unit grid. What is the approximate straight-line distance between the benches in units?","opts":["3.32","6","7.81","11","61"],"correct":2,"why":"d = √((6)² + (5)²) = √61 = 7.81.","traps":null,"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-compound-interest#1kcjvh","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"$2000 is invested at 10% annual interest, compounded once a year. What is the balance after 3 years?","opts":["$662.00","$2420.00","$2600.00","$2662.00","$2928.20"],"correct":3,"why":"Balance = 2000 × (1.1)³ = $2662.00.","traps":["That is the interest earned. The question asks for the BALANCE.","One year short. Count the compounding periods.","That is SIMPLE interest. Compounding earns interest on the interest, so multiply by the growth factor each year.",null,"One year too many. Count the compounding periods."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:zsc1639","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":3,"q":"A delivery fleet averages 50 drop-offs a day with a standard deviation of 10. What is the z score of a day with 35 drop-offs?","opts":["-15","-1.5","-0.67","-0.3","1.5"],"correct":1,"why":"z = (x − μ)/σ = (35 − 50)/10 = -1.5.","traps":["This is the raw gap in points, one step short of dividing by the standard deviation.",null,"Flipped the ratio and divided 10 by the 15-point gap.","Divided the 15-point gap by the mean of 50 instead of by the standard deviation of 10.","Right size, wrong side: 35 sits below the mean, so the z-score is negative."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"written:qr-11-1nphbtr","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-11","topic":"Trigonometry, just enough","nights":[11],"d":3,"q":"In triangle ABC, angle A = 40°, side a = 6, and side b = 8. Using sin 40° = 0.643, what is sin B?","opts":["0.482","0.643","0.766","0.802","0.857"],"correct":4,"why":"The Law of Sines rearranges to sin B equals b times sin A divided by a, so it is 8 times 0.643 divided by 6, which is about 0.857. Because side b is longer than side a, angle B has to be the larger angle, so sin B should land above sin 40 rather than below it. That size check catches the most common version of this slip immediately.","traps":["This puts the sides in the wrong spots, computing 6 times sin 40 divided by 8.","This reuses sin 40 without scaling by the ratio of the sides.","This is cos 40 rather than any sine.","This scales the angle itself by 8 over 6 and then takes the sine, but the Law of Sines scales the sines, not the angles.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"written"},{"id":"generated:qr-missing-value-mean#521vni","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-13","topic":"Statistics and reading data","nights":[13],"d":2,"q":"A student scored 79, 71, 88 on 3 quizzes. What score is needed on the next quiz to bring the average to exactly 81?","opts":["5","81","83","86","90"],"correct":3,"why":"Needed total = 81 × 4 = 324. Current total = 238. Next score = 324 − 238 = 86.","traps":["You used 3 quizzes. After the next one there are 4.","Scoring the target only works if you were already AT the target. You have to make up the gap on every earlier quiz too.","You averaged the old average with the new score. The old average counts for several quizzes, not one.",null,"Arithmetic slip. Required total = target × number of quizzes; subtract what you already have."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-mixture#2r0aa","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-8","topic":"Word problems: mixtures, interest, money","nights":[8],"d":2,"q":"4 L of a 5% acid solution is mixed with 1 L of a 35% acid solution. What is the concentration of the mixture?","opts":["11%","13.8%","20%","29%","55%"],"correct":0,"why":"Acid: 5% of 4 plus 35% of 1 = 0.55 L. Total volume = 5 L. Concentration = 11%.","traps":[null,"You divided by the product of the volumes. Divide by the SUM: total volume.","You averaged the two percents. The mixture leans toward whichever solution you used MORE of, so weight by volume.","You swapped the volumes. Pair each concentration with its own volume.","You found the total acid but never divided by the total volume."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"generated:qr-roots-sum-product#skajfl","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":2,"q":"What is the SUM of the solutions of x² − 5x + 4 = 0?","opts":["4","3","−5","5","−4"],"correct":3,"why":"It factors as (x − 1)(x − 4), so the roots are 1 and 4. Shortcut without solving: sum = −b/a = 5, product = c/a = 4.","traps":["That is the PRODUCT of the roots (c/a). The sum is −b/a.","That is the distance between the roots. The question asks for their sum.","Sign slip. The sum of the roots is the OPPOSITE of the x coefficient: −b/a.",null,"You mixed up which coefficient goes with which. Sum = −b/a, product = c/a."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1154","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $245 airline seat rises 67 percent over a holiday week. What is the new price?","opts":["80.85","164.15","178","312","409.15"],"correct":4,"why":"New = 245 × (1 + 67/100) = 245 × 1.67 = 409.15.","traps":["You took the 67% off the price instead of putting it on.","You stopped on the increase itself, 164.15, instead of adding it to the 245.","You subtracted 67 dollars when the price is going up.","You added 67 dollars instead of adding 67 percent of the 245.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pem1310","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":3,"q":"A dental office books 5 cleanings and 2 fillings each day. Over 3 days, how many appointments is that?","opts":["10","11","13","21","30"],"correct":3,"why":"Bracket first: 5 + 2 = 7, then 7 times 3 = 21. Without the bracket this line would be 5 + 6 = 11, which is exactly the trap sitting in the choices. The bracket is the whole question here.","traps":["You added everything, so the times sign never did any work.","You ignored the bracket and multiplied 2 by 3 before adding the 5.","You multiplied the two numbers inside the bracket and then added the 3.",null,"You multiplied all three numbers, treating the plus sign as another times sign."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-combined-work#feqvm6","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"One painter paints a fence in 4 hours. A second painter does the same job in 12 hours. Working together, how long do they take?","opts":["4 hours","8 hours","0.333 hours","16 hours","3 hours"],"correct":4,"why":"Rates add: 1/4 + 1/12 = 1/3 of the job per hour. Time = 3 = 3 hours. Sanity check: it must be less than 4 hours.","traps":["Arithmetic slip. Redo the last step slowly and carry the units.","You averaged the times. Two working together must be FASTER than the faster one alone.","That is the combined RATE (jobs per hour). Flip it to get the time.","You added the times. Adding helpers cannot make the job take longer. Add the RATES: 1/a + 1/b.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:wor1500","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-1","topic":"The setup mindset","nights":[1],"d":3,"q":"The sum of a number and 3 times the number is 40. Find the number.","opts":["9","10","11","13","30"],"correct":1,"why":"The number itself counts as one piece, so a number plus 3 times the number is 4 of them, not 3. That gives 4x = 40 and x = 10. Any time a sum holds the number and a multiple of it, add the 1 for the number before you divide.","traps":["Four 9s make 36, four short of the 40 given.",null,"Four 11s make 44, four past the 40 in the stem.","Divided 40 by 3 and rounded, which leaves out the number itself as one of the pieces.","That is 3 times the number, the larger piece of the sum, not the number itself."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:pct1183","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-4","topic":"Percentages and percent change","nights":[4],"d":2,"q":"A $250 nightly room rate falls 10% in the off season and then rises 25% for the holidays. What is the holiday rate in dollars?","opts":["168.75","225","281.25","287.5","312.5"],"correct":2,"why":"Multiply factors: 250 × 0.9 × 1.25 = 281.25. Naïvely adding gives 287.5, which is wrong.","traps":["That cuts the second step by 25 percent instead of raising by it.","That is the price after the 10 percent cut, one step short of the answer.",null,"You added the two changes into a net 15 percent rise, and sequential percents multiply instead.","That applies only the 25 percent rise and leaves the cut out."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:neg1326","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-5","topic":"Algebra: one and two unknowns","nights":[5,6],"d":2,"q":"Which inequality gives all values of x for which -x < 10?","opts":["x > -10","x > 10","x < 10","x < -10","x = -10"],"correct":0,"why":"Divide both sides by -1 (negative) and FLIP: x > -10.","traps":[null,"You flipped the direction but dropped the negative on 10/-1.","You divided by 1 rather than -1, so neither the sign nor the direction changed.","You kept the inequality pointing the same way after dividing by a negative.","You solved the boundary equation -x = 10 instead of the range of values."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-unit-rate-conversion#x7vu8n","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-3","topic":"Ratios and proportions","nights":[3],"d":2,"q":"A cyclist travels at 126 kilometers per hour. What is that speed in meters per second?","opts":["1.26 × 10⁵ m/s","454 m/s","35 m/s","0.035 m/s","2100 m/s"],"correct":2,"why":"126 km/h × 1000 m/km ÷ 3600 s/h = 35 m/s. Shortcut: divide km/h by 3.6.","traps":["That is meters per HOUR. Divide by 3600 to get per second.","Both factors are upside down. Set the units up so kilometers and hours cancel.",null,"That is kilometers per second. Multiply by 1000 to get meters.","You converted hours to MINUTES. There are 3600 seconds in an hour."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:pem1306","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"A square tile panel measures 9 tiles along each side, and 2 spare strips of 3 tiles each are set beside it. How many tiles are there in all?","opts":["15","24","33","54","87"],"correct":4,"why":"Square first: 9 squared is 81. Then 2 times 3 = 6, and 81 + 6 = 87. The size of the answer is set by that 81, so any total under 80 means the square got skipped somewhere.","traps":["You dropped the square and used a plain 9, giving 9 + 6.","You read 9 squared as 9 times 2 = 18 and added the 6 to that.","You added 9 and 2 first and then multiplied by 3, which skips both the square and the ranking.","You dropped the square and multiplied all three numbers instead.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"qr-a:exp1719","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-6","topic":"Powers, roots and quadratics","nights":[],"d":3,"q":"Expressed as a decimal, what is the value of 3^5 ÷ 3^6?","opts":["0.33","3","972","177147","205891132094649"],"correct":0,"why":"Same base, so the exponents subtract: 3^(5-6) = 3^-1. A negative exponent means one over that power, so this is 1/3, which rounds to 0.33. When the bottom exponent is the bigger one, expect an answer below 1.","traps":[null,"You subtracted the exponents the wrong way round, as 6 - 5 rather than 5 - 6.","You added 243 and 729 instead of dividing them.","You added the exponents instead of subtracting, which gives 3^11.","You multiplied the exponents, which gives 3^30."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"},{"id":"generated:qr-average-speed#18ntb5a","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-7","topic":"Word problems: rates, distance, work","nights":[7],"d":2,"q":"A driver covers 180 miles at 30 mph and returns the same 180 miles at 60 mph. What is the average speed for the whole trip?","opts":["45 mph","20 mph","42.4 mph","52.5 mph","40 mph"],"correct":4,"why":"Time out = 180/30 = 6 h, time back = 180/60 = 3 h. Average = 360 / 9 = 40 mph. The distance cancels, so it is always 2ab/(a+b).","traps":["You averaged the two speeds. More TIME is spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits below the midpoint. Use total distance over total time.","You forgot the distance is covered twice. Total distance is 2d.","That is the geometric mean. Average speed is total distance divided by total time.","Estimate slip. Compute the two times, add them, then divide the total distance by that.",null],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"generated"},{"id":"qr-a:st4","sub":"QR","stop":"qr-2","topic":"Fractions, decimals and number sense","nights":[2],"d":2,"q":"At a college, 25 students take biology and 20 take chemistry, and 35 students take at least one of the two. How many take both?","opts":["5","10","15","20","45"],"correct":1,"why":"Rearrange inclusion-exclusion: |A ∩ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∪ B| = 25 + 20 − 35 = 10.","traps":["You took the difference between the two set sizes instead of working back from the union.",null,"You subtracted |B| from the union, which gives the part of A outside the overlap.","That is |B| on its own, which would only be the overlap if all of B sat inside A.","That's the sum of |A| + |B|."],"qs":null,"os":false,"src":"qr-a"}]},"pat":{"seed":"F20261004"},"rc":{"section":"rc-s12"}}]};
